window.BANK={"ch1":{"id":"ch1","label":"Ch 1 · Investment Landscape","short":"Ch 1","kind":"chapter","weight":8,"qs":[{"t":"Goals","q":"A financial objective becomes a financial goal when it is assigned:","o":["A risk profile and an asset allocation","An amount and a timeline","A benchmark and a fund manager","A tax rate and a lock-in period"],"a":1,"e":"The workbook: \"When we assign amounts and timelines to these objectives, we convert these into financial goals.\""},{"t":"Goals","q":"Events such as hospitalisation, accident, theft or fire should be provided for through:","o":["A long-term equity SIP","Tactical asset allocation","Real estate investment","An emergency fund and insurance policies"],"a":3,"e":"Undesirable events cannot be planned for through investments. The workbook suggests an emergency fund built from savings and investment products, plus insurance to cover such risks."},{"t":"Goals","q":"What is the main drawback of the \"pool approach\" of meeting all financial needs from a single pool of investments?","o":["It is not permitted by SEBI","It always has higher costs","It cannot hold equity","The investment horizon is not known"],"a":3,"e":"Under the pool approach \"you are not sure of the horizon for the investments\", and knowing the horizon is important for investment decisions."},{"t":"Inflation","q":"Shalini's higher education costs Rs 50 lakh today and she will go to college in 10 years. If education inflation is 8% p.a., roughly how much will her parents need?","o":["Rs 54 lakh","Rs 90 lakh","Rs 1.50 crore","Rs 1.08 crore"],"a":3,"e":"FV = 50,00,000 × (1.08)^10 = Rs 1,07,94,625, about Rs 1.08 crore. Planning for only Rs 50 lakh would be grossly insufficient."},{"t":"Inflation","q":"A family spends Rs 30,000 a month today. If inflation stays at 6% p.a., its monthly expenses after 10 years will be approximately:","o":["Rs 48,000","Rs 60,000","Rs 96,214","Rs 53,725"],"a":3,"e":"30,000 × (1.06)^10 ≈ Rs 53,725. Rs 96,214 is the figure after 20 years."},{"t":"Saving vs investing","q":"Which statement best describes the relationship between saving and investing?","o":["They are two steps of the same process, and saving precedes investing","They are unrelated activities","Investing always precedes saving","Saving is riskier than investing"],"a":0,"e":"Saving means reducing consumption. It is this saved money that is invested, so saving precedes investing."},{"t":"Evaluating investments","q":"According to the workbook, the three most important factors to evaluate an investment are:","o":["Safety, liquidity and returns","Convenience, ticket size and taxability","Brand, past returns and fund size","Tax deduction, lock-in and ticket size"],"a":0,"e":"\"The three most important factors to evaluate investments are safety, liquidity, and returns.\" The others (convenience, ticket size, taxability, tax deduction) are additional parameters."},{"t":"Evaluating investments","q":"A penalty for early withdrawal from an investment primarily:","o":["Removes liquidity completely","Increases credit risk","Reduces the ticket size","Reduces the investment return"],"a":3,"e":"\"While such a penalty does not hamper liquidity, it only lowers the investment returns.\" It is a trade-off between liquidity and returns."},{"t":"Asset classes","q":"The four broad asset classes described in the workbook are:","o":["Equity, Debt, Mutual funds and Gold","Stocks, Bonds, Cash and Insurance","Real estate, Commodities, Equity and Fixed income","Equity, Hybrid, Solution-oriented and Others"],"a":2,"e":"The four broad categories are Real estate, Commodities, Equity and Fixed income. Mutual funds are a way of investing, not an asset class."},{"t":"Asset classes","q":"Which is the MOST important factor impacting the performance of a real estate investment?","o":["Location","Size of the property","Age of the builder","Interest rate on home loans"],"a":0,"e":"\"Location is the most important factor impacting the performance of an investment in real estate.\""},{"t":"Asset classes","q":"According to the workbook, a house bought for self-occupation should be treated as:","o":["An investment, since it appreciates","A commodity","An expense rather than an investment","A fixed income asset"],"a":2,"e":"It is described as the largest \"expense\" in life, not an investment, since selling it may hurt one's lifestyle."},{"t":"Asset classes","q":"Why does the workbook say commodity derivatives may not be wise to call \"investments\"?","o":["They are leveraged and normally short-term contracts","They are not regulated","They generate taxable dividends","They can only be bought in physical form"],"a":0,"e":"Two reasons: they are leveraged (large exposure for small money, so highly risky) and they are short-term contracts while investor needs are longer."},{"t":"Asset classes","q":"Which asset class does NOT generate any intermittent (periodic) cash flow?","o":["Equity","Real estate","Commodities such as gold and silver","Bonds"],"a":2,"e":"Equity can pay dividends, real estate can earn rent and bonds pay interest. \"It is the commodities where such intermittent cash flow is not generated.\""},{"t":"Asset classes","q":"An investor buys a bond at the time of issue and holds it till maturity. In almost all cases the investor will earn:","o":["Only capital gains","Interest but no capital gains","Neither interest nor capital gains","Dividends and capital gains"],"a":1,"e":"Held from issue to maturity, there would be no capital gains in almost all cases. Gains or losses arise when the bond is bought or sold in the secondary market."},{"t":"Asset classes","q":"Since its base year of 1979 (value 100) to 30 January 2026 (82,269), the Sensex has grown at approximately:","o":["8% p.a.","12% p.a.","15.4% p.a.","24% p.a."],"a":2,"e":"The workbook quotes an appreciation of around 15.4% p.a. compounded annually, before adding dividends."},{"t":"Asset classes","q":"Which asset classes can be held ONLY in financial form?","o":["Real estate and commodities","Equity and bonds","Gold and silver","Real estate and equity"],"a":1,"e":"Equity and bonds can be held only in financial form. Real estate and commodities can be held in physical or financial form."},{"t":"Asset classes","q":"In Table 1.1 of the workbook, Public Provident Fund (PPF) is classified under:","o":["Equity","Hybrid","Fixed Income","Others"],"a":2,"e":"PPF, SSY, SCSS, Post Office MIS, endowment and money-back policies are all listed under Fixed Income."},{"t":"Asset classes","q":"In Table 1.1, Infrastructure Investment Trusts (InvITs) are placed under:","o":["Fixed Income","Commodities","Hybrid","Real Estate / Infrastructure (financial asset)"],"a":3,"e":"REMFs, REITs and InvITs appear as financial assets under Real Estate / Infrastructure."},{"t":"Asset classes","q":"Rare coins, art and rare stamps are classified in Table 1.1 as:","o":["Others","Commodities","Real estate","Hybrid"],"a":0,"e":"They are listed in the \"Others\" category."},{"t":"Inflation risk","q":"Assuming inflation of 8% p.a., how much money would you need after 10 years to buy what Rs 10,000 buys today?","o":["Rs 21,589","Rs 14,693","Rs 18,000","Rs 46,610"],"a":0,"e":"10,000 × (1.08)^10 = Rs 21,589. Rs 14,693 is after 5 years and Rs 46,610 after 20 years."},{"t":"Inflation risk","q":"A fixed deposit earns 7% p.a. while inflation is 8% p.a. The investor is earning:","o":["A positive real rate of return","A real return equal to the nominal return","A negative real rate of return","A tax-free real return"],"a":2,"e":"Real return adjusts the nominal return for inflation. Returns below inflation mean falling purchasing power, a negative real rate."},{"t":"Inflation risk","q":"The return on an investment WITHOUT adjusting for inflation is called the:","o":["Real rate","Nominal rate","Risk-free rate","Effective rate"],"a":1,"e":"\"The returns on investment without factoring inflation is known as the nominal rate.\""},{"t":"Liquidity risk","q":"Per the workbook, which investment is NOT appropriate for funding short-term liquidity needs even though it is easy to sell?","o":["Savings bank account","Overnight fund","Short-term fixed deposit","Listed equity shares"],"a":3,"e":"Listed shares can be sold easily, but their prices fluctuate, so \"such investments are not appropriate for funding short term liquidity needs\"."},{"t":"Credit risk","q":"A delay or default in repayment by a borrower may arise from problems with the borrower's:","o":["Ability or intention to pay","Tax status or residency","Ticket size or lock-in","Market capitalisation only"],"a":0,"e":"The workbook names two reasons: the ability of the borrower or the intention of the borrower."},{"t":"Credit risk","q":"For citizens of a country, the safest bonds (with the lowest interest rates) are generally those issued by:","o":["AAA-rated private companies","Their own country's government","Banks","Foreign governments"],"a":1,"e":"Bonds of their own country's government are considered safest and normally offer the lowest interest rates. All other bonds offer higher rates."},{"t":"Market risk","q":"The disappearance of the pager industry after mobile phones became popular illustrates:","o":["Interest rate risk","Inflation risk","Industry-specific risk","Credit risk"],"a":2,"e":"A new technology affecting every firm using the old one is an industry-specific factor, which sits between market-wide and company-specific risk."},{"t":"Market risk","q":"Which risk CANNOT be reduced through diversification?","o":["Company-specific risk","Market-wide risk","Risk of a single company's product failure","Risk of a single company's management failure"],"a":1,"e":"\"The risk specific to the security can be reduced through diversification across unrelated securities, but the one that is market-wide cannot.\""},{"t":"Interest rate risk","q":"A 1-year bond was issued at Rs 1,000 with an 8% coupon. Immediately after, new bonds start offering 8.5%. If the investor sells the old bond now, it will sell:","o":["At a premium","At par","Only on maturity","At a discount"],"a":3,"e":"Rates and bond prices move inversely. The older 8% bond is less attractive than new 8.5% bonds, so it can be sold only at a discount."},{"t":"Interest rate risk","q":"For the same change in interest rates, which bond will see the larger price fluctuation?","o":["A bond with shorter maturity","Both fluctuate equally","A bond with longer maturity","Neither; only credit ratings move prices"],"a":2,"e":"\"Those with longer maturity would witness higher price fluctuations in comparison to those with shorter maturities.\""},{"t":"Managing risk","q":"For a lay investor without superior market knowledge, the workbook recommends which risk management strategy?","o":["Diversifying across investment options","Taking a position on expected events","Avoiding all risky assets","Timing the market"],"a":0,"e":"Taking positions needs superior knowledge and is risky. \"For a lay investor a prudent approach would be to diversify across various investment options.\""},{"t":"Managing risk","q":"Credit risk is measured through:","o":["Credit rating and credit spreads","Beta and standard deviation","Modified duration only","Sharpe ratio"],"a":0,"e":"\"Measurement of credit risk is undertaken through credit rating and credit spreads.\" Volatility is measured by variance, SD, beta and modified duration."},{"t":"Behavioural biases","q":"An investor believes that not losing Rs 5,000 matters more than gaining Rs 5,000. This bias is:","o":["Overconfidence","Herd mentality","Loss aversion","Availability heuristic"],"a":2,"e":"Loss aversion, first identified by Kahneman and Tversky, is the tendency to prefer avoiding losses over acquiring equivalent gains."},{"t":"Behavioural biases","q":"An investor decides on a stock first and then looks only for information that supports that view. This is:","o":["Recency bias","Confirmation bias","Familiarity bias","Loss aversion"],"a":1,"e":"Confirmation bias: looking for information that confirms already-held beliefs. \"Investors decide first and then look for data to support their views.\""},{"t":"Behavioural biases","q":"Someone working in the IT industry holds only technology stocks, concentrating risk. The workbook lists this under:","o":["Herd mentality","Recency bias","Interest of the investors","Loss aversion"],"a":2,"e":"Under \"Interest of the investors\", decisions follow the investor's interest rather than suitability, e.g. an IT professional with only tech stocks."},{"t":"Behavioural biases","q":"After a strong bull market, investors allocate far more to equities than advised. This is an example of:","o":["Loss aversion","Recency bias","Confirmation bias","Availability heuristic"],"a":1,"e":"Recency bias: recent experience overrides analysis. A bull market makes people over-allocate to risky assets; a crash makes them avoid them."},{"t":"Risk profiling","q":"In risk profiling, the \"ability to take risk\" refers to the investor's:","o":["Psychological comfort with volatility","Need for higher returns to meet goals","Past investment experience only","Financial ability and investment horizon"],"a":3,"e":"Need = higher returns to reach goals; ability = financial ability and investment horizon; willingness = psychological capacity to handle risk."},{"t":"Asset allocation","q":"The primary reason for adopting tactical (dynamic) asset allocation is to:","o":["Eliminate market risk","Improve the risk-adjusted return of the portfolio","Avoid rebalancing","Save tax"],"a":1,"e":"\"The primary reason for doing so is to improve the risk-adjusted return of the portfolio.\" It suits seasoned investors with a large investible surplus."},{"t":"Asset allocation","q":"Target allocation is 50:50 with Rs 1,000 each in assets A and B. After a year A rises 20% and B falls 5%. To restore 50:50, the investor should:","o":["Sell Rs 125 of A and buy B","Buy Rs 125 more of A","Sell Rs 250 of B","Do nothing; rebalancing applies only to tactical allocation"],"a":0,"e":"A = 1,200, B = 950, total 2,150. Target each = 1,075. Sell Rs 125 of A and buy Rs 125 of B: rebalancing sells what rose and buys what fell."},{"t":"Asset allocation","q":"After a passive breach of a scheme's mandated asset allocation, SEBI requires rebalancing (for schemes other than index funds and ETFs) within:","o":["15 business days","30 business days","30 calendar days","60 business days"],"a":1,"e":"The mandated rebalancing period is 30 business days. It does not apply to overnight funds."},{"t":"DIY vs professional","q":"When comparing mutual fund costs with managing money oneself, people often miss the hidden costs of doing it yourself, which are:","o":["Stamp duty and GST","Exit load and STT","Brokerage and demat charges","One's time and the potential mistakes one may make"],"a":3,"e":"The hidden costs of DIY are the value of one's time and the emotional mistakes an individual investor is likely to make."},{"t":"Financial goals","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A financial goal is defined by an objective, an amount and a timeline.\nII. Needs arising from hospitalisation, theft or fire are best met by building a larger investment portfolio.\nIII. The main flaw of the pool approach to investing is that the investment horizon is unknown.","o":["Only I and III","Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: objective + amount + timeline make a financial goal. II is false: such events are covered by an emergency fund plus insurance, not by investments. III is true: under the pool approach the horizon is unknown."},{"t":"Saving vs investing","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Saving precedes investing.\nStatement 2: A penalty for exiting an investment early affects its liquidity rather than its returns.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: saving comes before investing. Statement 2 is false: an exit penalty hits returns, not liquidity."},{"t":"Real estate","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["The four asset classes are real estate, commodities, equity and fixed income","Real estate is an illiquid asset","Unlike shares, a real estate asset is not divisible","A self-occupied home is treated as an investment"],"a":3,"e":"The first three are true. A self-occupied home is treated as an expense, not an investment."},{"t":"Asset features","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Commodity derivatives are not treated as investments because they are leveraged and short-term.\nII. An investor who buys a bond at issue and holds it to maturity earns a capital gain.\nIII. In real estate, location is the key factor.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and II"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: commodity derivatives are leveraged and short-term, so they are not investments. II is false: a bond bought at issue and held to maturity gives no capital gain. III is true: location is key in real estate."},{"t":"Asset classification","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements about the workbook's classification of investment products (Table 1.1):\nStatement 1: REITs and InvITs are classified under Equity.\nStatement 2: Coins, art and stamps are classified under Others.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: REITs, InvITs and REMF are classified under Real Estate. Statement 2 is true: coins, art and stamps fall under Others."},{"t":"Equity features","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Equity and bonds exist only in financial form.\nStatement 2: Listed equity is liquid but is not suitable for short-term needs.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: equity and bonds exist only in financial form, and listed equity, though liquid, is unfit for short-term needs."},{"t":"Credit risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Credit risk arises only from a borrower's inability to pay, not from its intention to pay","Bonds of one's own government are the safest and pay the lowest interest rate","Bonds of one's own government pay the highest interest rate because they are the safest","Bonds of one's own government carry the highest credit risk in the domestic market"],"a":1,"e":"Bonds of one's own government are the safest and therefore pay the lowest rate. Credit risk can arise from the borrower's ability or its intention to pay, so the first option is wrong, and the last two reverse the safety-rate link."},{"t":"Diversification","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Market-wide risk can be removed through diversification.\nStatement 2: A business hit by the decline of the pager industry illustrates market-wide risk.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":1,"e":"Both are false: market-wide risk cannot be diversified (only company-specific risk can), and the pager example illustrates industry-specific risk."},{"t":"Interest rate risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. When interest rates rise, bond prices fall.\nII. The shorter the maturity of a bond, the bigger its price swing when interest rates change.\nIII. Volatility can be measured by variance, standard deviation, beta and modified duration.","o":["Only I","Only III","Only II","Only I and II"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: rates up, bond prices down. II is false: a longer maturity means a bigger price swing. III is true: these are the measures of volatility."},{"t":"Risk measures","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Credit risk is measured mainly through standard deviation and beta.\nStatement 2: The three strategies for handling risk are to avoid it, take a position, or diversify.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: credit risk is measured by credit rating and spreads; SD and beta measure volatility. Statement 2 is true: avoid, take a position, or diversify."},{"t":"Behavioural biases","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Taking a decision first and then looking for evidence to support it is confirmation bias.\nStatement 2: Feeling a Rs 5,000 loss more sharply than a Rs 5,000 gain is called recency bias.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: deciding first and seeking proof later is confirmation bias. Statement 2 is false: that is loss aversion (Kahneman and Tversky)."},{"t":"Investor emotions","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Fear, greed and hope are the dominant emotions in investing.\nStatement 2: For a lay investor, diversification is the appropriate way to handle risk.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: fear, greed and hope are the dominant emotions, and a lay investor should diversify rather than avoid risk or take a position."},{"t":"Risk profile","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A risk profile considers the investor's need, ability and willingness to take risk.\nII. A useful remedy for behavioural biases is a third opinion from an RIA or an MFD.\nIII. Tactical asset allocation is best suited to new investors with a small surplus.","o":["Only I","Only III","Only I and II","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: risk profile = need + ability + willingness. II is true: a third opinion from an RIA or MFD helps counter biases. III is false: tactical/dynamic allocation suits seasoned investors with a large surplus."},{"t":"Passive breach","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A passive breach of asset allocation must be rebalanced within 30 business days.\nII. The passive-breach rebalancing rule applies to index funds and ETFs.\nIII. The passive-breach rebalancing rule is not applicable to overnight funds.","o":["Only II","Only I","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: the rebalancing period is 30 business days. II is false: index funds and ETFs are excluded. III is true: it does not apply to overnight funds."},{"t":"Risk profile","y":"concept","q":"In an investor's risk profile, the ABILITY to take risk depends on:","o":["The investor's psychology","The investor's finances and investment horizon","The investor's need for returns to reach a goal","The recent performance of the market"],"a":1,"e":"Ability to take risk depends on finances and horizon. Willingness depends on psychology, and need is a separate component of the risk profile."},{"t":"Rebalancing","y":"concept","q":"Rebalancing a portfolio back to its target asset allocation means the investor:","o":["Buys more of the asset class that has risen the most","Shifts the whole portfolio into the better-performing asset","Sells what has risen and buys what has fallen","Changes the target mix whenever markets move"],"a":2,"e":"Rebalancing means selling what rose and buying what fell to restore the target mix; it does not chase the winner or change the target."}]},"ch2":{"id":"ch2","label":"Ch 2 · Concept and Role of a Mutual Fund","short":"Ch 2","kind":"chapter","weight":6,"qs":[{"t":"Concept","q":"Which statement about mutual funds is technically correct?","o":["A mutual fund is a new asset class competing with equity and bonds","A mutual fund guarantees returns because it is regulated","An investor invests through a mutual fund to access securities","A mutual fund is set up as a company"],"a":2,"e":"One does not invest \"in\" a mutual fund but \"through\" it. It is a different way of investing, not a different product, and it is set up as a trust."},{"t":"Concept","q":"Who shares in the profits or losses of a mutual fund scheme?","o":["The sponsor and AMC","The trustees and custodian","All constituents in proportion to their fee","The unitholders only"],"a":3,"e":"Profits or losses belong to the investors (unitholders). Every other entity is paid a fee or commission for its role."},{"t":"Concept","q":"In which year did mutual fund inflows act as a counterforce to heavy selling by foreign portfolio investors, as cited in the workbook?","o":["2022","2008","2016","2020"],"a":0,"e":"In 2022, when FPIs sold heavily, fresh MF inflows created demand for equities, showing MFs acting as a market stabiliser."},{"t":"Investment policy","q":"A scheme's investment policy discloses which two aspects?","o":["NAV and AUM","Exit load and expense ratio","Benchmark and fund manager","Asset allocation and investment style"],"a":3,"e":"The investment policy is arrived at after the objective is fixed, and it covers asset allocation and investment style."},{"t":"Key concepts","q":"A scheme has issued 5 crore units with a face value of Rs 10 each. Its unit capital is:","o":["Rs 5 crore","Rs 50 crore","Rs 10 crore","It depends on the current NAV"],"a":1,"e":"Unit capital = number of units × face value = 5 crore × Rs 10 = Rs 50 crore. NAV is used for AUM, not unit capital."},{"t":"Key concepts","q":"A scheme has 2 crore units outstanding and its NAV is Rs 25. Its AUM is:","o":["Rs 20 crore","Rs 25 crore","Rs 50 crore","Rs 250 crore"],"a":2,"e":"AUM = current NAV × units outstanding = Rs 25 × 2 crore = Rs 50 crore."},{"t":"Key concepts","q":"The dividend option of mutual fund schemes is now called:","o":["IDCW option","Payout option","Bonus option","Reinvestment plan"],"a":0,"e":"The dividend option is now called the Income Distribution cum Capital Withdrawal (IDCW) option."},{"t":"Key concepts","q":"Valuing each security in a scheme's portfolio at its current market value every day is called:","o":["Amortisation","Mark to market","Book valuing","Indexation"],"a":1,"e":"Mark to market (MTM) values each security at its current market value daily for the NAV, which is why NAVs fluctuate daily."},{"t":"Key concepts","q":"Higher recurring expenses charged to a scheme result in:","o":["Lower NAV and lower investor returns","Higher NAV","No effect, as expenses are paid by the AMC","Higher unit capital"],"a":0,"e":"Expenses are charged as a percentage of AUM and deducted while calculating NAV, so higher expenses mean lower NAV and returns."},{"t":"Advantages","q":"According to the workbook, the three sources an investor can use for informed decisions (transparency) are:","o":["Advertisements, fact sheets and news","Distributor advice, ratings and rankings","Scheme documents (SID, SAI, KIM), portfolio disclosures and NAV","Annual report, AGM and tax returns"],"a":2,"e":"The three essential places are scheme related documents (SID, SAI, KIM), portfolio disclosures and the NAV. Prospective investors can access them too."},{"t":"Advantages","q":"How do mutual funds offer \"tax deferral\"?","o":["They pay tax on behalf of investors","All MF gains are tax free","Investors pay tax only once every five years","They are not liable to tax on their income, so growth options let investors defer tax"],"a":3,"e":"Mutual funds are not liable to pay tax on the income they earn. By choosing growth-type options, investors defer tax until redemption."},{"t":"Advantages","q":"ELSS investments qualify for deduction up to what amount, under which provision?","o":["Rs 1,00,000 under Section 80D","Rs 1,50,000 under Section 80C of the Old Tax Regime","Rs 2,00,000 under Section 80CCD","Rs 1,50,000 under the New Tax Regime"],"a":1,"e":"ELSS gives a deduction of up to Rs 1,50,000 in a financial year under Section 80C of the Old Tax Regime."},{"t":"Limitations","q":"An investor wants control over which securities are bought for him. Which option suits this better than a mutual fund?","o":["Index fund","Portfolio Management Services (PMS)","ETF","Interval fund"],"a":1,"e":"A mutual fund unitholder cannot influence the portfolio. PMS offers a customised portfolio and more control for large investors."},{"t":"Limitations","q":"Which step did SEBI take to address the \"choice overload\" limitation?","o":["Introduced categorisation and rationalisation of schemes","Capped the number of AMCs","Banned new fund offers","Made direct plans mandatory"],"a":0,"e":"SEBI's scheme categorisation brings uniformity to similar schemes across fund houses, helping investors compare and choose."},{"t":"Limitations","q":"Of the factors deciding a scheme's returns, which one can the fund manager actually work on?","o":["Movement of the market","Performance of individual securities","His own investment skills","Interest rates set by RBI"],"a":2,"e":"Returns depend on the market, individual securities and the team's skill. Only skill is within the fund manager's control."},{"t":"Structure","q":"In an open-ended fund, unit capital:","o":["Stays fixed after the NFO","Changes only at maturity","Changes only when listed","Keeps changing as units are created and cancelled"],"a":3,"e":"New units are created on purchase and cancelled on redemption, so unit capital of an open-ended fund changes regularly."},{"t":"Structure","q":"Which close-ended scheme is NOT required to be listed on a stock exchange?","o":["Fixed Maturity Plan","Capital Protection Oriented Fund","Infrastructure Debt Fund","Equity Linked Savings Scheme"],"a":3,"e":"Every close-ended scheme other than an ELSS must be listed on a recognised stock exchange."},{"t":"Structure","q":"Units of close-ended schemes on stock exchanges most often trade:","o":["At a premium to NAV","Exactly at NAV","At face value","At a discount to NAV"],"a":3,"e":"Experience shows they usually trade at a discount to NAV. The buyer has money and many options, so has the bargaining edge."},{"t":"Structure","q":"Per the workbook, the minimum duration of an interval fund's transaction period is:","o":["1 day","2 days","5 days","15 days"],"a":1,"e":"The workbook states the minimum transaction period is 2 days, with the interval period stated as 15 days."},{"t":"Structure","q":"What sets an ETF apart from a regular open-ended fund?","o":["It has a single end-of-day NAV for all transactions","It trades on an exchange throughout the day at multiple prices","It cannot track an index","It has a 3-year lock-in"],"a":1,"e":"An ETF trades on the exchange all day, so there are many prices during the day. A normal open-ended fund transacts at one end-of-day NAV."},{"t":"Active vs passive","q":"MF Lite, now set out in Chapter X of the SEBI MF Regulations 2026, is a framework for:","o":["Small AMCs running active equity funds","Passively managed schemes such as index funds, ETFs and FoFs","Specialised Investment Funds","Close-ended debt schemes"],"a":1,"e":"MF Lite is a framework for passively managed schemes: index funds, ETFs, fund of funds and other schemes SEBI specifies."},{"t":"Categorisation","q":"Under SEBI's 2026 categorisation circular, how many sub-categories do Debt schemes have?","o":["7","13","17","16"],"a":2,"e":"Equity 13, Debt 17, Hybrid 7, Life Cycle Funds, Other 2. The new debt category is the Sectoral (debt) Fund."},{"t":"Categorisation","q":"A company ranked 180th by full market capitalisation is classified as:","o":["Mid cap","Large cap","Small cap","Micro cap"],"a":0,"e":"Large cap = 1st to 100th, Mid cap = 101st to 250th, Small cap = 251st onwards, by full market capitalisation."},{"t":"Equity schemes","q":"A Multi Cap fund must invest a minimum of:","o":["65% in equity with no sub-limits","80% in large caps","35% each in large and mid caps","75% in equity, with at least 25% each in large, mid and small caps"],"a":3,"e":"Multi cap: at least 75% in equity, split as a minimum of 25% each in large cap, mid cap and small cap stocks."},{"t":"Equity schemes","q":"Which scheme can hold a maximum of 30 stocks?","o":["Focused fund","Flexi cap fund","Large cap fund","Dividend yield fund"],"a":0,"e":"A focused fund invests in a maximum of 30 stocks, with at least 80% in equity, and must state its market-cap focus."},{"t":"Equity schemes","q":"A fund house wants to launch both a Value fund and a Contra fund. Under SEBI rules:","o":["It can launch both, if portfolio overlap between them is not more than 50%","It needs special SEBI approval for both","It can offer only one of the two","Contra funds are banned"],"a":0,"e":"Value and Contra are now separate categories (80% equity each). An AMC may offer both, provided their portfolio overlap is not more than 50%."},{"t":"Debt schemes","q":"A Liquid fund invests in debt and money market securities with maturity of up to:","o":["1 day","6 months","1 year","91 days"],"a":3,"e":"Overnight = 1 day, Liquid = up to 91 days, Money market = up to 1 year."},{"t":"Debt schemes","q":"Under the 2026 categorisation, a debt scheme with portfolio Macaulay duration between 1 and 3 years is called a:","o":["Ultra Short to Short Term Fund","Medium Term Fund","Short Term Fund","Ultra-Short-Term Fund"],"a":2,"e":"Ultra-Short-Term 3–6 months; Ultra Short to Short Term 6–12 months; Short Term 1–3 years; Medium Term 3–4 years; Medium to Long Term 4–7 years; Long Term >7 years."},{"t":"Debt schemes","q":"A Credit Risk fund must invest at least 65% of total assets in corporate bonds rated:","o":["AA and below (excluding AA+)","AAA only","AA+ and above","Unrated only"],"a":0,"e":"Credit risk funds: minimum 65% in AA (excluding AA+) and below rated bonds. Corporate bond funds need 80% in AA+ and above."},{"t":"Hybrid schemes","q":"In a Balanced Hybrid fund:","o":["Equity is 65-80% and arbitrage is allowed","Debt is 75-90%","Equity allocation is fully dynamic","Equity is 40-60% and no arbitrage is permitted"],"a":3,"e":"Balanced hybrid: equity 40–60%, debt 40–60%, and no arbitrage. The category is now 'Balanced Hybrid and Aggressive Hybrid Fund'."},{"t":"Hybrid schemes","q":"A Multi Asset Allocation fund must invest in:","o":["At least 3 asset classes, at least 10% each; foreign securities are not a separate asset class","At least 2 asset classes, 25% each","At least 3 asset classes, at least 10% each, with foreign securities counted as a separate class","Only equity, debt and gold in equal parts"],"a":0,"e":"At least three asset classes with a minimum 10% in each. Foreign securities are not treated as a separate asset class."},{"t":"Life Cycle Funds","q":"Under the 2026 categorisation, a Life Cycle Fund can be launched with a tenure of:","o":["5 to 30 years, in multiples of 5 years","3 to 10 years","Any tenure the AMC chooses","Only till the investor's retirement age"],"a":0,"e":"Minimum 5 and maximum 30 years, in multiples of 5; at most 6 Life Cycle Funds per MF can be open for subscription at a time. They replace the old solution-oriented (retirement and children's) funds."},{"t":"Other schemes","q":"Index funds and ETFs must invest at least what share of total assets in the securities of the index they track?","o":["65%","80%","90%","95%"],"a":3,"e":"Index funds/ETFs: minimum 95% in securities of the index. FoFs: minimum 95% in the underlying fund."},{"t":"Other schemes","q":"Which is NOT an exception to SEBI's \"one scheme per category\" rule?","o":["Index funds tracking different indices","Two large cap funds with different fund managers","Fund of funds with different underlying schemes","Sectoral funds in different sectors"],"a":1,"e":"Only index funds/ETFs on different indices, FoFs with different underlyings and sector/thematic funds on different sectors are exceptions."},{"t":"Other categories","q":"Which statement about Real Estate Mutual Funds (REMFs) is correct per the workbook?","o":["They are open-ended","They are close-ended and must be listed on a stock exchange","They are the same as REITs","They cannot invest in real estate assets"],"a":1,"e":"REMFs invest in real estate assets in cities named in SEBI's circular of 2 May 2008. They are close-ended and listed. REITs and InvITs are not MF schemes."},{"t":"Other categories","q":"Which statement about REITs and InvITs is correct?","o":["They are mutual fund schemes","They are unlisted","They are SEBI-registered trusts but not mutual fund schemes","Their minimum subscription is Rs 1 lakh"],"a":2,"e":"REITs and InvITs are SEBI-registered trusts, listed, with a trading lot of 1 unit and minimum subscription now Rs 10,000-15,000. They are not MF schemes."},{"t":"Other categories","q":"A Capital Protection Oriented fund typically takes equity exposure through:","o":["Equity derivatives","Direct equity shares","Gold ETFs","REITs"],"a":0,"e":"A portion goes into debt that grows to the principal by maturity (e.g. Rs 90 to Rs 100); the rest takes equity exposure via derivatives."},{"t":"New fund types","q":"A fund that weights the index constituents equally instead of by market capitalisation is a:","o":["Quant fund","Smart beta fund","Focused fund","Arbitrage fund"],"a":1,"e":"Smart beta funds rely less on market-cap weighting and use alternatives such as equal weight to improve returns and diversification."},{"t":"SIF","q":"What is the minimum investment required in a Specialized Investment Fund (SIF)?","o":["Rs 10 lakh","Rs 1 lakh","Rs 5 lakh","Rs 50 lakh"],"a":0,"e":"SIF bridges the gap between MFs and PMS. The minimum investment is Rs 10 lakh across all investment strategies of the same AMC."},{"t":"SIF","q":"The maximum short exposure through unhedged derivative positions permitted in SIF strategies is:","o":["10% of NAV","50% of NAV","25% of NAV","No limit"],"a":2,"e":"Every SIF strategy restricts unhedged short exposure to a maximum of 25% of NAV (net assets)."},{"t":"Industry growth","q":"Industry AUM grew from Rs 11.89 lakh crore in March 2015 to about what level in February 2026?","o":["Rs 82.03 lakh crore","Rs 66.70 lakh crore","Rs 50.00 lakh crore","Rs 95.20 lakh crore"],"a":0,"e":"AUM reached Rs 82.03 lakh crore in February 2026, an 11-year growth of 19.3% p.a. compounded."},{"t":"MF basics","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A mutual fund is set up as a trust.\nII. Profits and losses of a scheme belong only to its unitholders; everyone else earns a fee or commission.\nIII. In 2022 and 2025, mutual fund inflows countered heavy selling by retail investors.","o":["Only I and II","Only II","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: a mutual fund is set up as a trust. II is true: only unitholders bear profits and losses. III is false: MF inflows countered heavy FPI selling, acting as a market stabiliser."},{"t":"Unit capital","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Unit capital of a scheme equals the number of units multiplied by the NAV.\nStatement 2: AUM equals the NAV multiplied by the number of units outstanding.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: unit capital = units x face value (typically Rs 10). Statement 2 is true: AUM = NAV x units outstanding, and AMCs are ranked by AUM."},{"t":"MF benefits","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["Mark to market means valuing each security at its market price daily","The ELSS deduction of up to Rs 1,50,000 under Sec 80C is available under both the Old and New Tax Regimes","Recurring expenses are charged as a percentage of AUM and lower the NAV","SID, SAI, KIM, portfolio disclosures and NAV are available to prospective investors too"],"a":1,"e":"The ELSS deduction of up to Rs 1,50,000 under Sec 80C is available under the Old Tax Regime only. The other three statements are correct."},{"t":"MF limitations","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Mutual funds offer no customisation; a PMS does.\nII. SEBI's categorisation of schemes was the answer to choice overload.\nIII. As a pass-through vehicle, a mutual fund lets the fund manager control the performance of the securities held.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and II","Only III"],"a":3,"e":"I is true: no customisation in MFs, PMS gives it. II is true: categorisation answered choice overload. III is false: the fund manager controls only his own skill, not the market or the securities."},{"t":"Close-ended schemes","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Close-ended schemes, including ELSS, must be compulsorily listed.\nStatement 2: Units of close-ended schemes usually trade at a discount to NAV.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: close-ended schemes are compulsorily listed except ELSS. Statement 2 is true: the buyer has the bargaining power, so units usually trade at a discount to NAV."},{"t":"Market cap","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Large cap companies are the 1st to 100th companies by full market capitalisation.\nII. Mid cap companies are the 101st to 200th companies by full market capitalisation.\nIII. Small cap companies are the 251st company onwards by full market capitalisation.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: large cap = 1-100. II is false: mid cap = 101-250. III is true: small cap = 251 onwards."},{"t":"Equity categories","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: A multi cap fund must invest at least 75% in equity, with at least 25% each in large, mid and small cap stocks.\nStatement 2: A large & mid cap fund must invest at least 35% each in large cap and mid cap stocks.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: multi cap needs at least 75% equity with at least 25% each in large, mid and small; large & mid cap needs 35% + 35%."},{"t":"Equity categories","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Value and Contra are now separate categories, each requiring at least 80% in equity","A focused fund can hold a maximum of 20 stocks","Sectoral and thematic funds together form a single equity category","ELSS - Tax Saver Fund must invest at least 65% in equity with a 3-year lock-in"],"a":0,"e":"Value and Contra are now separate categories at 80% each. A focused fund may hold up to 30 stocks, sectoral and thematic are now separate categories, and ELSS needs 80% in equity with a 3-year lock-in."},{"t":"Debt durations","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A liquid fund invests in instruments with maturity of up to 91 days.\nII. A money market fund invests in instruments with maturity of up to 1 year.\nIII. An Ultra-Short-Term fund has a Macaulay duration of 6 to 12 months.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: liquid = up to 91 days. II is true: money market = up to 1 year. III is false: Ultra-Short-Term is 3-6 months; 6-12 months is the Ultra Short to Short Term fund (formerly low duration)."},{"t":"Credit quality","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: A corporate bond fund must invest at least 80% in papers rated AA+ and above.\nStatement 2: A credit risk fund must invest at least 65% in papers rated AA+ and below.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: corporate bond = 80% in AA+ and above. Statement 2 is false: a credit risk fund puts 65% in AA and below, with AA+ excluded."},{"t":"Hybrid funds","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A conservative hybrid fund invests 75-90% in debt and 10-25% in equity.\nII. An aggressive hybrid fund invests 65-80% in equity.\nIII. In a multi asset allocation fund, foreign securities count as a separate asset class.","o":["Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III","Only I and II"],"a":3,"e":"I is true: conservative hybrid = 75-90% debt, 10-25% equity. II is true: aggressive hybrid = 65-80% equity. III is false: multi asset needs at least 3 asset classes with at least 10% each, and foreign securities are not a separate class."}]},"ch3":{"id":"ch3","label":"Ch 3 · Legal Structure of Mutual Funds","short":"Ch 3","kind":"chapter","weight":4,"qs":[{"t":"Structure","q":"Mutual funds in India are constituted as trusts and are therefore governed by:","o":["Companies Act, 2013","SEBI Act, 1992 only","Indian Trusts Act, 1882","Indian Partnership Act, 1932"],"a":2,"e":"Mutual funds are constituted as trusts and governed by the Indian Trusts Act, 1882. The trust deed is registered under the Indian Registration Act, 1908."},{"t":"Structure","q":"Who are the beneficiaries of a mutual fund trust?","o":["The unitholders","The sponsors","The trustees","The AMC employees"],"a":0,"e":"Every trust has beneficiaries. For a mutual fund trust they are the investors, i.e. the unitholders."},{"t":"Structure","q":"The trust deed of a mutual fund is executed between:","o":["The AMC and the trustees","The sponsor and SEBI","The sponsor and the trustees","The trustees and the custodian"],"a":2,"e":"The trust deed is executed by the sponsor in favour of the trustees named in it. SEBI prescribes clauses it must contain."},{"t":"Structure","q":"The Investment Management Agreement is signed between:","o":["Sponsor and AMC","AMC and custodian","Trustees and AMC","SEBI and AMC"],"a":2,"e":"The trustees execute an investment management agreement with the AMC, setting out its responsibilities."},{"t":"Structure","q":"Who appoints the custodian of a mutual fund?","o":["The AMC","The trustees","The sponsor","SEBI"],"a":1,"e":"The custodian is appointed by the trustees and a custodial agreement is signed between them."},{"t":"Sponsor","q":"Who applies to SEBI for registration of a mutual fund?","o":["The sponsor","The AMC","The trustees","AMFI"],"a":0,"e":"The application to SEBI for registration is made by the sponsor(s), who then invest in the capital of the AMC."},{"t":"Sponsor","q":"Which is one of the eligibility criteria for a sponsor?","o":["Business in financial services for at least 3 years","Net worth of at least Rs 500 crore","Average net annual profit after tax of at least Rs 10 crore over the preceding 5 years","Listing on a recognised stock exchange"],"a":2,"e":"Sponsor must have 5 years in financial services, positive net worth and net profit in each of the last 5 years, and average net profit of at least Rs 10 crore."},{"t":"Trustees","q":"Under Regulation 11 of the SEBI MF Regulations 2026, a person can be appointed a trustee only if he or she:","o":["Meets the fit and proper criteria in Schedule II of the SEBI (Intermediaries) Regulations, 2008","Is a director of the AMC","Holds at least 10% of the sponsor","Is a trustee of another mutual fund"],"a":0,"e":"Regulation 11: fit and proper; not a director, officer or employee of an AMC; not a trustee of another MF; furnishes information SEBI seeks. SEBI's prior approval is needed."},{"t":"Trustees","q":"At least what proportion of the trustee board must be independent directors?","o":["At least two-thirds","At least one-third","At least half","All of them"],"a":0,"e":"At least two-thirds must be independent directors, i.e. not associates of the sponsor, the AMC or the trustee company."},{"t":"Trustees","q":"Who is eligible to be appointed a trustee of a mutual fund?","o":["An independent director of the AMC","A fit and proper person who is not a director, officer or employee of an AMC","A trustee of another mutual fund","An employee of the AMC"],"a":1,"e":"Regulation 11: the person must be fit and proper, must not be an AMC director (including independent director), officer or employee, and must not be a trustee of another MF."},{"t":"Trustees","q":"Which of these is one of the trustee responsibilities listed in the SEBI MF Regulations 2026?","o":["Appointment and termination of the AMC","Calculating the daily NAV","Selling units to investors","Issuing ARNs to distributors"],"a":0,"e":"Six heads: appointing/terminating the AMC; conflicts of interest and broker empanelment; regulatory compliance; oversight for investor interests; additional oversight; due diligence on matters SEBI specifies."},{"t":"AMC","q":"Under the SEBI MF Regulations 2026, a director of an AMC (including its Chairman):","o":["May also sit on the board of the same MF's trustee company","Cannot simultaneously serve on the board of any MF's trustee company","Must be an employee of the sponsor","Needs AMFI's approval to be appointed"],"a":1,"e":"AMC directors cannot sit on any MF's trustee board, and no one can be a director of more than one AMC. Trustees give prior approval to AMC director appointments."},{"t":"AMC","q":"The minimum net worth that an AMC must maintain (effective 1 January 2024) is:","o":["Rs 10 crore","Rs 25 crore","Rs 100 crore","Rs 50 crore"],"a":3,"e":"AMC net worth must be at least Rs 50 crore, maintained continuously. Ensuring this is the sponsor's responsibility."},{"t":"AMC","q":"At least what share of the AMC's board must be directors who are not associates of the sponsor, its subsidiaries or the trustees?","o":["50%","One-third","Two-thirds","75%"],"a":0,"e":"At least 50% of AMC directors must not be associates of the sponsor, its subsidiaries or the trustees. Trustees give prior approval to AMC director appointments."},{"t":"AMC","q":"When control of an AMC changes, unitholders get the option to exit without exit load within at least:","o":["15 calendar days","21 days","30 calendar days","45 days"],"a":2,"e":"Unitholders may exit at prevailing NAV without exit load within a period of not less than 30 calendar days from the communication."},{"t":"AMC","q":"A change in control of the AMC requires prior approval of:","o":["The sponsor only","AMFI","75% of the distributors","The trustees and SEBI"],"a":3,"e":"Change in control directly or indirectly needs prior approval of the trustees and SEBI, plus a letter to unitholders and newspaper ads."},{"t":"AMC","q":"The appointment of an AMC can be terminated by:","o":["The custodian","A majority of trustees or 75% of unitholders","AMFI","A simple majority of distributors"],"a":1,"e":"A majority of the trustees or 75% of unitholders can terminate the AMC. Any change of AMC needs prior approval of SEBI and unitholders."},{"t":"Custodian","q":"Who tracks corporate actions such as bonus, dividends and rights in companies where a scheme has invested?","o":["Custodian","RTA","Scheme auditor","Unitholders"],"a":0,"e":"The custodian holds assets, settles transactions and tracks corporate actions (workbook sample Q2)."},{"t":"Custodian","q":"A custodian generally cannot be appointed for a sponsor's mutual fund if the sponsor or its associates hold at least what share of its voting rights?","o":["50%","26%","33%","75%"],"a":0,"e":"If the sponsor/associates control 50% or more of voting rights, or 50% or more of directors represent them, that custodian cannot be appointed unless specific conditions are met."},{"t":"AMC functions","q":"Who in the AMC signs the due-diligence certificate in new scheme documents?","o":["Compliance Officer","Chief Investment Officer","Head of Sales","Fund accountant"],"a":0,"e":"The Compliance Officer signs the due-diligence certificate and reports any non-compliance directly and immediately to the trustees."},{"t":"AMC functions","q":"Within the fund management team, who places orders with brokers?","o":["Analysts","Dealers","Fund managers","Compliance officers"],"a":1,"e":"Analysts identify opportunities, fund managers decide and own performance, and dealers place orders with brokers."},{"t":"AMC functions","q":"Under SEBI's Risk Management Framework, dedicated risk officers are designated for:","o":["Sales, marketing, HR and admin","Investment, compliance, operational and cyber security risk","Only market risk","Only credit risk"],"a":1,"e":"The RMF makes risk management an independent function with dedicated officers for investment, compliance, operational and cyber security risk."},{"t":"Service providers","q":"Which service provider does NOT need SEBI registration?","o":["RTA","Custodian","Fund accountant","KYC Registration Agency"],"a":2,"e":"The fund accountant calculates NAV and needs no SEBI registration; the AMC may do it in-house. RTAs, custodians and KRAs must register."},{"t":"Service providers","q":"Which statement about RTAs is correct?","o":["They are appointed by trustees and need no registration","Appointment is compulsory for every AMC","They are appointed by the AMC, must register with SEBI, but appointing one is optional","They hold the scheme's securities"],"a":2,"e":"The AMC appoints the RTA, though it may handle the work in-house. All RTAs must be registered with SEBI."},{"t":"Service providers","q":"Which is true about the auditors of a mutual fund?","o":["The same auditor audits the scheme and the AMC","Both auditors are appointed by the AMC","SEBI appoints the scheme auditor","The scheme auditor is appointed by the trustees and must differ from the AMC auditor"],"a":3,"e":"Scheme accounts are separate from AMC accounts. Trustees appoint the scheme auditor; the AMC appoints its own, different auditor."},{"t":"Service providers","q":"A new cadre of distributors allowed to sell only a limited set of schemes must clear:","o":["NISM Series V-A","NISM Series X-A","NISM Series V-B","NISM Series VIII"],"a":2,"e":"The new cadre must clear NISM Series V-B (Mutual Fund Foundation) and register with AMFI under that category."},{"t":"Service providers","q":"Who appoints the collecting bankers of a scheme?","o":["The trustees","The AMC","The sponsor","The RTA"],"a":1,"e":"The bank accounts of schemes are maintained with collection bankers appointed by the AMC."},{"t":"Service providers","q":"For fair valuation of non-traded or thinly traded debt securities, AMFI has appointed:","o":["CRISIL and ICRA","NSDL and CDSL","CARE and Fitch","BSE and NSE"],"a":0,"e":"SEBI requires at least two valuation agencies. AMFI appointed CRISIL Ltd. and ICRA Ltd., and AMCs use their valuation level for fair value."},{"t":"Service providers","q":"An FMP disclosed a proposed rating profile of AA. It may then invest in debt securities rated:","o":["Only AA","Any rating","AA or higher (less risky)","AA or lower"],"a":2,"e":"It must invest only within the proposed rating profile or in less risky papers with a higher credit rating."},{"t":"Depositories","q":"Units of which mutual fund must compulsorily be held in demat form?","o":["Liquid fund","ELSS","Balanced advantage fund","ETF"],"a":3,"e":"ETF units are held compulsorily in demat form since only demat securities can trade on exchanges."},{"t":"Platforms","q":"MF Utilities India is:","o":["A SEBI-owned regulator","An industry transaction platform initiated under AMFI","A stock exchange segment of BSE","A depository"],"a":1,"e":"MF Utilities is an AMFI industry initiative aggregating transactions across multiple mutual funds on one platform. BSE StAR MF and NSE NMF-II are exchange segments."},{"t":"Platforms","q":"A Category 1 Execution Only Platform (EOP) registers with ___ and acts as agent of ___.","o":["SEBI as stock broker; the investor","AMFI; the investor","RBI; the AMCs","AMFI; the AMCs"],"a":3,"e":"Category 1 EOP: registered with AMFI, body corporate, agent of AMCs. Category 2: registered as stock broker in the exchange EOP segment, agent of investor."},{"t":"Platforms","q":"Execution Only Platforms facilitate transactions in:","o":["Regular plans only","Both direct and regular plans","Only ETFs","Direct plans only"],"a":3,"e":"An EOP is a digital platform for subscription, redemption and switches in direct plans. Platforms given by RIAs and brokers to their own clients are not EOPs."},{"t":"AMFI","q":"Which statement about AMFI is correct?","o":["It is the market regulator for mutual funds","It is a Self-Regulatory Organisation","It is a department of SEBI","It is neither a regulator nor an SRO"],"a":3,"e":"AMFI is the association of registered AMCs. It issues ARNs and can cancel them for code violations, but it is neither a regulator nor an SRO."},{"t":"KYC","q":"Central KYC Registration Agencies (KRAs) were introduced through SEBI regulations in:","o":["1996","2011","2008","2017"],"a":1,"e":"SEBI issued KRA regulations in 2011, creating a common KYC across the securities market under PMLA."},{"t":"Trust structure","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A mutual fund trust is governed by the Indian Trusts Act, 1882.\nII. The trust deed is registered under the Indian Registration Act, 1908.\nIII. The trust deed is executed between the sponsor and the AMC.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I and II are true: the trust is governed by the Indian Trusts Act, 1882 and the deed is registered under the Indian Registration Act, 1908. III is false: the trust deed is executed between the sponsor and the trustees."},{"t":"Appointments","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The custodian is appointed by the AMC.\nStatement 2: The RTA is appointed by the AMC.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: the custodian is appointed by the trustees. Statement 2 is true: the RTA (and the collecting banker) is appointed by the AMC."},{"t":"Sponsor eligibility","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. The sponsor must have been in financial services for 5 years.\nII. The sponsor must have made a net profit in each of the last 3 years.\nIII. The sponsor's average profit over 5 years must be at least Rs 10 crore.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: 5 years in financial services. II is false: net profit is required in each of the last 5 years. III is true: average profit of at least Rs 10 crore over 5 years."},{"t":"Trustees","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["A person may be a trustee of two different mutual funds at the same time.","At least two-thirds of the trustee board must be independent directors.","Appointment of a trustee requires prior SEBI approval.","Trustees can be individuals forming a Board of Trustees, or a trustee company."],"a":0,"e":"A trustee cannot be a trustee of another mutual fund, so the first statement is wrong. Two-thirds independence, prior SEBI approval, and individual or company trustees are all correct."},{"t":"AMC board","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: An AMC's net worth must be at least Rs 50 crore and maintained continuously.\nStatement 2: At least 50% of an AMC's directors must not be associates of the sponsor, its subsidiaries or the trustees.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: AMC net worth of at least Rs 50 crore must be maintained continuously (the sponsor's responsibility), and at least 50% of AMC directors must be non-associates."},{"t":"Change of control","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A change in control of the AMC needs prior approval of the trustees and SEBI.\nII. Unitholders must be given an exit at NAV without exit load for at least 30 calendar days.\nIII. The change must be advertised in two English national dailies.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and II","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I and II are true: prior approval of trustees and SEBI is needed, and an exit at NAV with no exit load for at least 30 calendar days is given. III is false: the ad goes in 1 English national daily and 1 regional-language paper."},{"t":"Service providers","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A fund accountant does not need SEBI registration.\nII. Appointment of an RTA by a mutual fund is mandatory.\nIII. The scheme auditor is appointed by the trustees and must be different from the AMC's auditor.","o":["Only I","Only III","Only II","Only I and II"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: a fund accountant needs no SEBI registration and can be in-house. II is false: an RTA must register with SEBI but appointing one is optional. III is true: the scheme auditor is appointed by trustees and differs from the AMC auditor."},{"t":"AMFI and EOP","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["AMFI is a self-regulatory organisation for mutual funds.","Platforms run by RIAs for their own clients are Execution Only Platforms.","An Execution Only Platform can offer both Direct and Regular plans.","An EOP Category 1 registers with AMFI and acts as an agent of AMCs."],"a":3,"e":"EOP Category 1 is a body corporate registered with AMFI acting as agent of AMCs. AMFI is neither a regulator nor an SRO, RIA platforms for their own clients are not EOPs, and EOPs are only for Direct plans."}]},"ch4":{"id":"ch4","label":"Ch 4 · Legal and Regulatory Framework","short":"Ch 4","kind":"chapter","weight":10,"qs":[{"t":"Regulators","q":"Which body regulates mutual funds in India?","o":["AMFI","SEBI","The AMCs","The Board of Trustees"],"a":1,"e":"SEBI regulates the securities markets including mutual funds. AMFI is an industry association, not a regulator."},{"t":"Regulators","q":"The money market in India is regulated by:","o":["SEBI","PFRDA","IRDAI","RBI"],"a":3,"e":"RBI regulates the banking system and money markets. MFs must follow RBI rules for money market, overseas investment and forex."},{"t":"Regulators","q":"All four financial sector regulators (RBI, SEBI, IRDAI, PFRDA) come under the purview of:","o":["Ministry of Corporate Affairs","Prime Minister's Office","Reserve Bank of India","Ministry of Finance"],"a":3,"e":"The workbook states these regulators come under the purview of the Ministry of Finance."},{"t":"Reforms","q":"SEBI requires mutual fund schemes to be benchmarked against:","o":["Total Return Index","Price Return Index","Fixed deposit rates","The category average"],"a":0,"e":"A TRI includes dividends/interest (notionally reinvested) plus capital gains, matching how scheme returns are computed. A PRI captures only price movement."},{"t":"Reforms","q":"The Potential Risk Class (PRC) matrix for debt schemes is based on:","o":["Macaulay duration and Credit Risk Value","Standard deviation and beta","AUM and expense ratio","Yield to maturity and exit load"],"a":0,"e":"PRC uses maximum interest rate risk (Macaulay duration) and maximum credit risk (Credit Risk Value). Effective 1 December 2021."},{"t":"Reforms","q":"SEBI introduced segregated portfolios in debt schemes in the context of:","o":["The 2008 global liquidity crisis","The 2018 credit crisis","The 2020 pandemic","The 2022 FPI outflows"],"a":1,"e":"During the 2018 credit crisis SEBI allowed segregated portfolios to separate distressed assets after a credit event."},{"t":"Reforms","q":"Which reform did SEBI bring after the 2008 global liquidity crisis?","o":["Disallowing premature redemption in FMPs","Segregated portfolios","Total Return Index benchmarking","The riskometer"],"a":0,"e":"In 2008 SEBI disallowed premature redemption in FMPs and banned the phrase \"liquid plus\" in scheme names."},{"t":"Investment limits","q":"Mutual funds can buy and sell securities only on delivery basis. True or false?","o":["False","True","True only for equity","True only for debt"],"a":1,"e":"The mutual fund buys and sells securities on delivery basis (workbook sample Q2)."},{"t":"Investment limits","q":"Under Regulation 39 of the SEBI MF Regulations 2026, an AMC may invest scheme money only in:","o":["Real estate held directly","Loans to companies","Securities, money market instruments, privately placed debentures, securitised debt, gold and silver (and others SEBI specifies)","Unit-linked insurance plans"],"a":2,"e":"Regulation 39 lists securities, money market instruments, privately placed debentures, securitised debt (asset or mortgage backed), gold, silver and any other asset SEBI specifies."},{"t":"Investment limits","q":"A (non-FoF) scheme investing in another mutual fund scheme:","o":["Can charge its normal fees on it","Cannot charge any fees, and is subject to limits SEBI specifies","Is prohibited","Needs AMFI's approval"],"a":1,"e":"Sixth Schedule: a scheme may invest in another scheme of the same or any other MF without charging fees, within SEBI's limits. This does not apply to fund of funds."},{"t":"Investment limits","q":"Can a mutual fund scheme invest in a fund of funds scheme?","o":["Yes, freely","No, no MF scheme can invest in a fund of funds scheme","Only with trustee approval","Up to 5% of NAV"],"a":1,"e":"Sixth Schedule: no MF scheme can invest in a FoF, and a FoF cannot invest in another FoF."},{"t":"Investment limits","q":"Pending deployment, a gold or silver ETF scheme may invest its money in:","o":["Short-term deposits of scheduled commercial banks","Equity shares","Corporate bonds","Real estate"],"a":0,"e":"Regulation 41: gold/silver ETFs invest only in gold/silver (or related instruments), except for redemption liquidity; idle money can go into short-term deposits of scheduled commercial banks."},{"t":"Investment limits","q":"The single-issuer debt limit does NOT apply to investment in:","o":["AAA corporate bonds","Government securities and T-bills","Commercial paper","Bank certificates of deposit"],"a":1,"e":"The limit is not applicable to Government Securities, treasury bills and triparty repo on G-secs/T-bills, nor to debt ETFs."},{"t":"Investment limits","q":"Under the Sixth Schedule, which unlisted instruments can a mutual fund scheme invest in?","o":["Unlisted equity of any company","Unlisted G-secs, money market instruments other than CPs, and unlisted NCDs as SEBI specifies","Unlisted commercial papers","None at all"],"a":1,"e":"Equity and debt must be listed or to be listed, except unlisted G-secs, money market instruments other than commercial papers, and unlisted NCDs to the extent SEBI specifies."},{"t":"Investment limits","q":"Under Regulation 41, mutual funds must settle their transactions:","o":["Only in dematerialised securities, except instruments SEBI specifies","In cash","Only in physical form","Through the AMC's own account"],"a":0,"e":"Regulation 41(iii): transactions are settled only in dematerialised securities, except for instruments SEBI specifies."},{"t":"Investment limits","q":"Short selling by a mutual fund scheme is:","o":["Prohibited","Allowed on a recognised stock exchange under SEBI's short selling and securities lending framework","Allowed off-exchange with any counterparty","Allowed only for fund of funds"],"a":1,"e":"Regulation 41(vii): short selling is executed on a recognised stock exchange under SEBI's framework for short selling and SLB."},{"t":"Investment limits","q":"Transfers of investments between schemes of the same mutual fund are:","o":["Banned","Permitted subject to conditions SEBI specifies","Allowed only for fund of funds","Allowed without any conditions"],"a":1,"e":"Regulation 41(viii): inter-scheme transfers are permitted subject to SEBI's conditions."},{"t":"Investment limits","q":"Money of a mutual fund scheme can be used in carry forward transactions:","o":["Freely","Only in the manner specified by SEBI","Only with trustee approval","Up to 10% of NAV"],"a":1,"e":"Regulation 41(v): scheme funds cannot be used in carry forward transactions except in the manner SEBI specifies."},{"t":"Investment limits","q":"A gold exchange traded fund scheme must invest its money in:","o":["Only gold or gold-related instruments, except as needed for redemption liquidity","Any commodity","Gold mining company shares","At least 50% in debt"],"a":0,"e":"Regulation 41(iv): gold/silver ETFs invest only in gold/silver or related instruments as per their objective, except for redemption liquidity. They may also use exchange-traded commodity derivatives."},{"t":"Investment limits","q":"A fund of funds scheme may invest its assets in:","o":["Other fund of funds schemes","Only schemes of mutual funds, apart from money needed for redemption liquidity","Direct equity shares","Real estate"],"a":1,"e":"Sixth Schedule: a FoF cannot invest in another FoF, and holds only MF scheme units except for liquidity needed for redemptions."},{"t":"Advertisement code","q":"Which is permitted in a mutual fund scheme advertisement?","o":["Investor testimonials","Rankings of the scheme","Celebrities promoting the scheme","The standard warning with no words added or deleted"],"a":3,"e":"Ads cannot carry testimonials, rankings or celebrities. They must carry the standard warning verbatim."},{"t":"Advertisement code","q":"In an audio-visual ad, the standard warning (visual and voice-over) of 14 words is considered clear if it runs for at least:","o":["3 seconds","10 seconds","5 seconds","14 seconds"],"a":2,"e":"Both visual and voice-over of the 14-word standard warning running for at least 5 seconds may be considered clear and understandable."},{"t":"Advertisement code","q":"Advertisements by mutual funds must be in terms of which schedule of the SEBI MF Regulations?","o":["Fifth Schedule","Seventh Schedule","Sixth Schedule","Eleventh Schedule"],"a":0,"e":"Ads follow the Fifth Schedule of the MF Regulations 2026. The Fourth Schedule is the code of conduct for AMCs and trustees."},{"t":"Advertisement code","q":"An ad announcing an IDCW payout must disclose:","o":["IDCW in Rs per unit, face value and prevailing NAV at declaration","Only the dividend yield %","Only the total amount distributed","The fund manager's track record"],"a":0,"e":"IDCW ads show the payout per unit in rupees with face value and prevailing NAV, and must state just below the payout that NAV will fall by the payout and statutory levy."},{"t":"Advertisement code","q":"A scheme launched 4 months ago wants to advertise its performance. What is allowed?","o":["CAGR since inception","Simple annualised return","No past performance can be shown","Point-to-point return only"],"a":2,"e":"Under 6 months: no past performance. 6 months to 1 year: simple annualised growth rate for the past 6 months."},{"t":"Advertisement code","q":"A scheme in existence for more than 3 years must show performance as CAGR for:","o":["1 year only","3 and 5 years only","Calendar year returns for 10 years","1, 3, 5 years and since inception, plus point-to-point on Rs 10,000"],"a":3,"e":"CAGR for 1, 3, 5 years and since inception (from allotment date), plus point-to-point returns on a standard Rs 10,000 investment."},{"t":"Advertisement code","q":"Liquid and money market funds may advertise simple annualised yields if performance is available for at least:","o":["1, 3 and 7 days","7, 15 and 30 days","30, 60 and 90 days","6 months"],"a":1,"e":"For overnight, liquid and money market funds, simple annualisation is allowed if figures for at least 7, 15 and 30 days exist."},{"t":"Advertisement code","q":"The standard additional benchmark for an Arbitrage fund's performance advertisement is:","o":["Sensex/Nifty","10-year GoI security","Gold price","1-year T-Bill"],"a":3,"e":"Debt schemes with duration up to 1 year and arbitrage funds use the 1-year T-Bill."},{"t":"Advertisement code","q":"Which scheme uses the 10-year GoI security as its additional benchmark in advertisements?","o":["Equity savings fund","Aggressive hybrid fund","Retirement fund","Multi asset allocation fund"],"a":0,"e":"Conservative hybrid, equity savings and debt schemes above 1 year use the 10-year GoI security. Aggressive hybrid, multi-asset, retirement and children's funds use Sensex/Nifty."},{"t":"Advertisement code","q":"A fund manager manages 9 schemes. A performance ad for one of them must show the other schemes as:","o":["All 9 schemes in full","Total count plus top 3 and bottom 3 schemes","Only the best 3","Nothing, if the ad is on the internet"],"a":1,"e":"If a fund manager manages more than six schemes, the AMC may disclose the total number plus the top 3 and bottom 3 schemes."},{"t":"Advertisement code","q":"Which statement about celebrity endorsements is correct?","o":["SEBI permits celebrity endorsement at industry level to raise awareness, with prior SEBI approval","Celebrities may endorse individual schemes","Celebrities may endorse only NFOs","Celebrity ads need AMFI approval only"],"a":0,"e":"Allowed only at industry level for awareness, not for a scheme or AMC brand, with prior SEBI approval, paid from the investor education pool."},{"t":"Advertisement code","q":"An AMC launching an SIF may use its mutual fund brand name in SIF materials for how long?","o":["1 year","3 years","5 years from SEBI approval","Indefinitely"],"a":2,"e":"The SIF needs a distinct brand, logo and website/page. The MF or sponsor brand may be used for 5 years from SEBI approval."},{"t":"Advertisement code","q":"An employee of an intermediary receives market-related news by email. Per SEBI guidelines, he may forward it only after:","o":["Checking it on social media","Approval from the intermediary's Compliance Officer","His manager agrees","24 hours have passed"],"a":1,"e":"Market news may be forwarded only after the Compliance Officer has seen and approved it. Otherwise both employee and Compliance Officer can be liable."},{"t":"Investor rights","q":"An investor wants to change his distributor. What is needed?","o":["An NOC from the existing distributor","SEBI permission","A written request to the AMC; no NOC needed","Redeeming and reinvesting the units"],"a":2,"e":"The investor submits a written request and the AMC complies without insisting on a No Objection Certificate."},{"t":"Investor rights","q":"How many nominees can an investor appoint for mutual fund units?","o":["10","1","3","5"],"a":0,"e":"Up to 10 nominees (workbook sample Q4). If no percentage split is given, equal distribution is presumed."},{"t":"Investor rights","q":"Which document is NOT listed among those unitholders have a right to inspect?","o":["Trust Deed","Board minutes of the sponsor company","Investment Management Agreement","Custodial Services Agreement"],"a":1,"e":"Unitholders can inspect the Trust Deed, IMA, Custodial Services Agreement, RTA agreement and the AMC's Memorandum & Articles."},{"t":"Investor rights","q":"Units held in demat form are freely transferable, except:","o":["Liquid fund units","Index fund units","Close-ended fund units","ELSS units during the statutory lock-in"],"a":3,"e":"Free transferability of ELSS units is curtailed during the statutory minimum holding period."},{"t":"Investor rights","q":"When a scheme's fundamental attributes change, unitholders get an exit window without load of at least:","o":["15 days","21 days","30 days","60 days"],"a":2,"e":"Unitholders get at least 30 days to exit at prevailing NAV without exit load, plus a written communication (email/SMS allowed) and details on the AMC website."},{"t":"Investor rights","q":"What share of unitholders can pass a resolution to wind up a scheme?","o":["51%","66%","75%","90%"],"a":2,"e":"75% of unitholders (in practice, unitholding) can terminate the AMC or wind up a scheme."},{"t":"Investor rights","q":"An investor who feels trustees have failed in their duties can:","o":["Sue the trust","Sue AMFI","File a suit against the trustees for breach of trust","Only complain to the distributor"],"a":2,"e":"A trust is a notional entity and cannot be sued. Investors can file suits against the trustees."},{"t":"Investor rights","q":"An investor claims an unclaimed redemption amount 4 years after it became due. He will be paid based on:","o":["NAV at the end of 3 years","Prevailing NAV","Original redemption amount without income","NAV on the date of claim plus interest"],"a":0,"e":"Claimed within 3 years: prevailing NAV. Claimed after 3 years: NAV at the end of the 3 years."},{"t":"Investor rights","q":"Unclaimed dividend and redemption amounts are deployed in a separate liquid plan on which the AMC can charge a maximum fee of:","o":["0.25% p.a.","0.50% p.a.","1.00% p.a.","No fee"],"a":1,"e":"Management fee up to 0.50% p.a. on unclaimed amounts, and no exit load on this plan."},{"t":"Investor rights","q":"A written-off security is recovered 3 years after the scheme's maturity. The recovered amount goes to:","o":["The old investors","The Investor Education Fund","The AMC's profit","The current unitholders of other schemes"],"a":1,"e":"Only substantial amounts recovered within 2 years go to old investors. Other cases go to the Investor Education Fund."},{"t":"Investor rights","q":"Mutual funds disclose details of debt and money market transactions, including inter-scheme transfers, at what frequency and lag?","o":["Monthly, 10-day lag","Weekly, 7-day lag","Quarterly, 30-day lag","Daily, 15-day lag"],"a":3,"e":"SEBI mandates daily disclosure of debt and money market securities transacted, with a time lag of 15 days."},{"t":"Grievances","q":"An AMC must redress investor grievances within:","o":["7 working days","21 calendar days","15 days","30 calendar days"],"a":1,"e":"The AMC shall redress grievances promptly but not later than 21 calendar days from receipt."},{"t":"Grievances","q":"SCORES is:","o":["AMFI's distributor rating system","A credit rating scale","An NSE trading platform","SEBI's web-based centralised complaint redress system"],"a":3,"e":"SCORES lets investors lodge and track complaints online. Physical complaints are scanned and uploaded."},{"t":"Grievances","q":"Which principle means a unitholder cannot claim protection for not being aware of matters fairly disclosed in scheme documents?","o":["Caveat venditor","Caveat emptor","Pari passu","Res judicata"],"a":1,"e":"Caveat emptor (let the buyer beware) applies to MF investments. A prospective investor does not have the rights of a unitholder."},{"t":"Cyber security","q":"The five cyber resiliency goals in SEBI's CSCRF are:","o":["Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond, Recover","Plan, Do, Check, Act, Review","Prevent, Detect, Correct, Report, Audit","Anticipate, Withstand, Contain, Recover, Evolve"],"a":3,"e":"Adopted from CERT-In's Cyber Crisis Management Plan: Anticipate, Withstand, Contain, Recover, Evolve."},{"t":"Codes of conduct","q":"The code of conduct for AMCs and trustees under the SEBI MF Regulations is in the:","o":["Fourth Schedule","Sixth Schedule","Seventh Schedule","AGNI guidelines"],"a":0,"e":"The Fourth Schedule of the MF Regulations 2026. The AMFI Code of Ethics (ACE) supplements it with higher, more explicit standards."},{"t":"Codes of conduct","q":"AGNI stands for:","o":["AMC Governance and National Index","Association of Global Net Investors","AMFI Grievance and Notice Index","AMFI Guidelines & Norms for Intermediaries"],"a":3,"e":"AGNI is AMFI's code of conduct for intermediaries: agents, brokers, distribution houses and banks."},{"t":"Codes of conduct","q":"When a distributor breaches the AMFI code, AMFI first asks for an explanation within:","o":["3 weeks","7 days","2 weeks","30 days"],"a":0,"e":"AMFI asks for an explanation within 3 weeks. If none or unsatisfactory, a warning letter follows. A second proven violation cancels the registration."},{"t":"Codes of conduct","q":"What happens after a second proven violation of the code by a distributor?","o":["A fine of Rs 1 lakh","Another warning letter","AMFI registration is cancelled and all AMCs are informed","Suspension for 3 months"],"a":2,"e":"On a proven second violation the registration is cancelled and all AMCs are informed. The intermediary can appeal to AMFI."},{"t":"MFD code","q":"Under the MFD code of conduct, which is prohibited?","o":["Disclosing commissions of competing schemes","Explaining risk factors","Asking investors about their financial status","Passing back part of the commission to investors"],"a":3,"e":"MFDs shall not rebate or pass back commission, or attract investors with rebates, gifts or vouchers."},{"t":"MFD code","q":"An MFD's own mobile number is filled in a client's application form at the client's request. This is:","o":["Fine if the client consents","Not allowed, even if requested by the investor","Allowed for senior citizens","Allowed if the EUIN is also given"],"a":1,"e":"Whether requested or not, contact details of the MFD's representatives or any third party must not be filled in as the investor's."},{"t":"MFD code","q":"An MFD who is not registered with SEBI as an Investment Adviser may call himself:","o":["Wealth Manager","Financial Consultant","AMFI-registered Mutual Fund Distributor","Investment Advisor"],"a":2,"e":"Terms like adviser, wealth manager or consultant need SEBI RIA registration. MFDs must display \"AMFI-registered Mutual Fund Distributor\" (font at least 12 in print)."},{"t":"MFD code","q":"Which is correct about MFDs and direct plans?","o":["MFDs may sell direct plans for a flat fee","MFDs earn lower trail on direct plans","Direct plans need the MFD's ARN","MFDs cannot deal in direct plans"],"a":3,"e":"MFDs cannot deal in direct plans. Their platforms must disclose that the investor is buying a regular plan that pays commission."},{"t":"MFD code","q":"An MFD wants to design his own brochure using an AMC's logo. He needs:","o":["Nothing; logos are public","AMFI approval only","SEBI approval","Prior written approval of the AMC"],"a":3,"e":"MFDs should use AMC-provided material and must not display an AMC's name, logo or mark without its prior written approval."},{"t":"Regulators","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. PFRDA regulates pension, and all four financial-sector regulators come under the Ministry of Finance.\nII. AMFI regulates mutual funds jointly with SEBI.\nIII. \"Securities\" are defined in Section 2(h) of the Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: RBI, SEBI, IRDAI and PFRDA (pension) all sit under the Ministry of Finance. II is false: SEBI regulates mutual funds; AMFI does not. III is true: securities are defined in Sec 2(h) of the SCRA, 1956."},{"t":"Benchmarks and PRC","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Mutual fund schemes are benchmarked to a Total Return Index, which includes dividends and interest.\nStatement 2: The Potential Risk Class matrix for debt schemes plots modified duration against Credit Risk Value.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: schemes use a TRI, not a Price Return Index. Statement 2 is false: the PRC matrix uses Macaulay duration against Credit Risk Value."},{"t":"Crisis reforms","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Segregated portfolios were introduced after the 2018 credit crisis.\nII. After the 2008 crisis, premature redemption in FMPs was stopped.\nIII. After the 2008 crisis, the word \"liquid\" was banned from scheme names.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: segregation came after the 2018 credit crisis. II is true: the 2008 reforms ended premature redemption in FMPs. III is false: only the name \"liquid plus\" was banned, not \"liquid\"."},{"t":"Investment rules","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["Regulation 39 permits mutual funds to invest in privately placed debentures and securitised debt","Under Regulation 41, mutual fund investments must be settled only in demat form","Under Regulation 41, derivatives and short selling are allowed only on a recognised stock exchange","Gold and silver ETFs must follow the Sixth Schedule restrictions like every other scheme"],"a":3,"e":"Options A, B and C are correct statements of Regulations 39 and 41. D is not correct: Regulation 41 applies the Sixth Schedule to all investments except gold and silver ETFs."},{"t":"Fund of funds","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A fund of funds can invest in another fund of funds provided no fees are charged.\nStatement 2: A scheme may invest in another mutual fund scheme, but no fees can be charged on that investment.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: a FoF cannot invest in another FoF at all. Statement 2 is true: inter-scheme investment is allowed with no fees (this rule is not for FoFs)."},{"t":"Advertisement code","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A mutual fund advertisement may show the scheme's ranking if the source is disclosed.\nII. In audio-visual ads, the standard warning must appear as visual plus voice-over for at least 5 seconds.\nIII. A vernacular advertisement may carry the standard warning in English.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is false: rankings are not allowed in MF ads. II is true: the 14-word warning runs visually and as voice-over for at least 5 seconds. III is false: vernacular ads must carry the warning in the vernacular."},{"t":"Performance in ads","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A scheme that is less than 6 months old cannot show performance in an advertisement.\nII. A scheme between 6 and 12 months old shows its CAGR since inception.\nIII. A scheme older than 3 years shows CAGR for 1, 3, 5 years and since inception, plus point-to-point returns on Rs 10,000.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: no performance under 6 months. II is false: a 6 to 12 month old scheme shows a simple annualised 6-month return. III is true: that is the rule for schemes over 3 years."},{"t":"Additional benchmark","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE about the additional benchmark shown in performance advertisements?","o":["An arbitrage fund uses the 10-year GoI security","An equity savings fund uses the 10-year GoI security","A conservative hybrid fund uses the Sensex or Nifty","A debt scheme with maturity of up to 1 year uses the 10-year GoI security"],"a":1,"e":"B is true: equity savings, conservative hybrid and other debt schemes use the 10-year GoI. A and D are false: arbitrage funds and debt schemes up to 1 year use the 1-year T-bill. C is false: conservative hybrid uses the 10-year GoI, not Sensex/Nifty."},{"t":"Celebrity endorsement","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Celebrity endorsement is allowed only at the industry level and needs prior SEBI approval.\nStatement 2: The cost of an industry-level celebrity campaign is met from the AMC's own marketing budget.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: celebrities are allowed only for industry-level campaigns with prior SEBI approval, not for a scheme or AMC brand. Statement 2 is false: the cost comes from the investor education pool."},{"t":"Investor rights","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Units held in demat are freely transferable, except ELSS units during the lock-in.\nII. To switch to another distributor, the investor needs an NOC from the existing distributor.\nIII. An investor can pledge mutual fund units.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: demat units are freely transferable except ELSS in lock-in. II is false: a written request is enough and no NOC is needed. III is true: units can be pledged."},{"t":"Unclaimed amounts","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Unclaimed money claimed within 3 years is paid at the prevailing NAV.\nII. Unclaimed money claimed after 3 years is paid at the NAV at the end of the third year.\nIII. Unclaimed amounts are deployed in a separate liquid plan that can charge up to 1.00% p.a.","o":["Only III","Only II and III","Only I and III","None of the above"],"a":0,"e":"I is true and II is true, as stated. III is false: the separate liquid plan for unclaimed amounts can charge at most 0.50% p.a., with no exit load."},{"t":"Unitholder powers","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A simple majority of unitholders can remove the AMC of a scheme.\nStatement 2: An investor seeking a legal remedy for breach of trust proceeds against the trustees, not the trust.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: 75% of unitholders are needed to remove the AMC or wind up a scheme. Statement 2 is true: the trust is a notional entity, so the investor sues the trustees."},{"t":"Grievances","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["An AMC must resolve investor complaints within 21 calendar days","SCORES is SEBI's online complaints redressal system","Physical complaints received are scanned and uploaded to SCORES","The status of investor complaints is disclosed only in the AMC's annual report"],"a":3,"e":"A, B and C are correct. D is not correct: complaint status is disclosed in the annual report, on the mutual fund's website and on the AMFI website."},{"t":"Codes of conduct","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The Fourth Schedule of the MF Regulations 2026 is SEBI's code of conduct for AMCs and trustees.\nII. The AMFI Code of Ethics (ACE) sets higher and more explicit standards than the SEBI code.\nIII. AGNI is the set of AMFI guidelines and norms for AMCs.","o":["Only I and II","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: the Fourth Schedule covers AMCs and trustees. II is true: ACE sets higher, more explicit standards. III is false: AGNI stands for AMFI Guidelines and Norms for Intermediaries."},{"t":"Breach process","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A distributor accused of violating the code must give an explanation within 3 weeks.\nStatement 2: On a second proven violation, the ARN is cancelled and all AMCs are informed.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: explanation within 3 weeks, then a warning letter, and a second proven violation leads to ARN cancellation with all AMCs informed; an appeal to AMFI is allowed."},{"t":"MFD code","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. An MFD may pass back part of the commission to the investor if the investor asks for it.\nII. An MFD may transact in direct plans when the client prefers them.\nIII. An MFD must disclose all commissions payable across competing schemes.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is false: rebates and pass-back are prohibited. II is false: MFDs cannot deal in direct plans. III is true: all commissions across competing schemes must be disclosed."},{"t":"MFD conduct","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE about an MFD's conduct?","o":["The MFD may enter his own contact details on the client's form to give faster service","The MFD may prepare his own marketing material carrying the AMC logo if the content is accurate","The MFD may call himself a wealth manager once he holds a valid ARN","The MFD must always mention the EUIN on the client's application form"],"a":3,"e":"D is true: the EUIN must always be added. A is false: the MFD's own contact details must never be entered on a client form. B is false: only AMC-provided material may be used, and the logo needs written approval. C is false: only a SEBI RIA may use titles such as adviser, wealth manager or consultant."},{"t":"Cyber and due diligence","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The cyber framework CSCRF draws its goals of Anticipate, Withstand, Contain, Recover and Evolve from CERT-In.\nStatement 2: Due diligence on distributors is solely AMFI's job, and AMCs have no role in it.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: those are the CSCRF goals, taken from CERT-In. Statement 2 is false: AMCs carry out due diligence on distributors; AMFI often runs a common process for them."}]},"ch10":{"id":"ch10","label":"Ch 10 · Risk, Return and Performance","short":"Ch 10","kind":"chapter","weight":7,"qs":[{"t":"Risk factors","q":"The two broad categories of risk highlighted in the SID are:","o":["Equity and debt risk","Market and credit risk only","Standard (general) and specific risk factors","Legal and tax risk"],"a":2,"e":"SID lists standard risk factors applicable to all schemes and specific ones by asset type."},{"t":"Risk factors","q":"The risk that interim cash flows are reinvested at a lower rate than originally assumed is:","o":["Political risk","Reinvestment risk","Credit risk","Basis risk"],"a":1,"e":"Reinvestment risk concerns the \"interest on interest\" component."},{"t":"Risk factors","q":"For FPIs investing in INR-denominated schemes, currency risk is managed by:","o":["The trustees","SEBI","The FPI itself","The AMC"],"a":2,"e":"The AMC does not manage currency risk for FPIs; it is their sole responsibility."},{"t":"Risk factors","q":"Risk of mispricing or improper valuation of derivatives is called:","o":["Basis risk","Liquidity risk","Model risk","Counterparty risk"],"a":2,"e":"Model risk is the risk of mis-pricing or improper valuation."},{"t":"Risk factors","q":"Basis risk in derivatives arises from:","o":["Rating downgrade","Difference in price movement of the derivative and the hedged security","Change in tax law","Default of the exchange"],"a":1,"e":"Basis risk: the derivative and the hedged security move differently."},{"t":"Risk factors","q":"A one-notch downgrade of an issuer from AAA to AA+ will generally cause its bond price to:","o":["Stay the same","Fall","Rise","Become zero"],"a":1,"e":"Rating migration risk: downgrades hurt prices; upgrades help."},{"t":"Risk factors","q":"In a securitisation, which Loan-to-Value (LTV) ratio indicates lower default risk?","o":["LTV does not matter","Higher LTV","LTV of 100%","Lower LTV"],"a":3,"e":"Lower LTV means more of the borrower's own money is in the asset, so less propensity to default."},{"t":"Risk factors","q":"Credit enhancement in a securitisation transaction allows:","o":["The PTC to be rated higher than the originator","Tax exemption","The originator to avoid repayment","Unlimited recourse to the originator"],"a":0,"e":"Credit enhancement helps achieve a target rating higher than the originator's own."},{"t":"Systematic risk","q":"Unsystematic risk can be reduced through diversification. True or false?","o":["Only in index funds","True","Only in debt funds","False"],"a":1,"e":"Workbook sample Q1: company-specific risk is diversifiable."},{"t":"Systematic risk","q":"A rise in inflation affecting all businesses in the country is an example of:","o":["Diversifiable risk","Company-specific risk","Unsystematic risk","Systematic risk"],"a":3,"e":"Economy-wide risks are systematic (market) risks."},{"t":"Systematic risk","q":"According to finance theory, investors are rewarded for taking:","o":["Diversifiable risk","Non-diversifiable (systematic) risk only","Both equally","Neither"],"a":1,"e":"Only non-diversifiable risk earns a reward."},{"t":"Analysis","q":"Which analysis studies price and volume data of a security?","o":["Fundamental","Situation","Quantitative","Technical"],"a":3,"e":"Workbook sample Q2: technical analysts (\"chartists\") study price-volume charts."},{"t":"Analysis","q":"A company has net profit of Rs 50 crore and 10 crore shares. Its share price is Rs 100. The P/E ratio is:","o":["5","10","20","50"],"a":2,"e":"EPS = 50 ÷ 10 = Rs 5; P/E = 100 ÷ 5 = 20."},{"t":"Analysis","q":"A PEG ratio of less than one indicates the share is:","o":["Undervalued","Overvalued","Illiquid","Fairly valued"],"a":0,"e":"PEG = 1 fair; <1 undervalued; >1 overvalued."},{"t":"Analysis","q":"Dividend yield across stocks tends to:","o":["Stay constant","Rise in bull markets","Rise with P/E","Fall in bull markets and rise in bear markets"],"a":3,"e":"Prices rise in bull markets, so yields fall; the reverse in bear markets."},{"t":"Styles","q":"Growth stocks typically feature:","o":["Low P/E and high dividend yield","Low PEG","High P/E and PEG, and low dividend yield","High book value only"],"a":2,"e":"Growth stocks are richly valued and fall more in corrections."},{"t":"Styles","q":"In a bottom-up approach, sector allocation is:","o":["Irrelevant","Decided first","A result of stock selection decisions","Fixed by SEBI"],"a":2,"e":"Stock selection is the key; sector allocation follows from it."},{"t":"Styles","q":"The EIC framework stands for:","o":["Earnings, Interest, Cash","Equity, Income, Capital","Economy, Industry, Company","Exchange, Index, Currency"],"a":2,"e":"Analysts consider economy, industry and company-specific factors."},{"t":"Debt","q":"Treasury Bills are issued by:","o":["RBI on behalf of the Government of India","Banks","SEBI","Companies"],"a":0,"e":"T-bills are short-term instruments issued by RBI for the GoI."},{"t":"Debt","q":"Certificates of Deposit issued by banks have tenors of:","o":["1 day to 3 months","Up to 10 years","7 days to 1 year","1 to 3 years"],"a":2,"e":"Banks: 7 days to 1 year; Financial Institutions: 1 to 3 years."},{"t":"Debt","q":"The difference between the yield on a corporate bond and a gilt of the same tenor is the:","o":["YTM","Duration","Coupon","Credit spread"],"a":3,"e":"Credit spread reflects extra yield for credit risk."},{"t":"Debt","q":"A fund manager expects interest rates to rise. He should:","o":["Increase portfolio duration","Buy long-dated gilts","Do nothing","Shift to floaters or shorter-tenor bonds"],"a":3,"e":"Lower modified duration reduces losses when rates rise."},{"t":"Gold","q":"If the rupee weakens against the dollar while global gold prices are unchanged, gold fund returns in India will:","o":["Rise","Remain unchanged","Fall","Become negative"],"a":0,"e":"A weaker rupee raises the rupee value of gold."},{"t":"Returns","q":"An investor bought at NAV Rs 120 and redeemed at Rs 135. Simple return is:","o":["11.11%","15.00%","12.50%","10.00%"],"a":2,"e":"Workbook sample Q3: (135 − 120) ÷ 120 = 12.5%."},{"t":"Returns","q":"An investment earned 3% in 4 months. The annualised return is:","o":["6%","9%","3%","12%"],"a":1,"e":"Annualised = 3% × 12 ÷ 4 = 9%."},{"t":"Returns","q":"Rs 10,000 grows to Rs 14,400 in 2 years. The compounded annual return is:","o":["20%","44%","22%","12%"],"a":0,"e":"(14,400 ÷ 10,000)^(1/2) − 1 = 1.2 − 1 = 20%."},{"t":"Returns","q":"When a scheme has paid dividends during the period, SEBI prescribes computing returns using:","o":["Simple return","CAGR assuming reinvestment at ex-dividend NAV","Average of NAVs","Price return index"],"a":1,"e":"The reinvestment method assumes dividends are reinvested at ex-dividend NAV."},{"t":"Returns","q":"Rolling returns are used to:","o":["Remove the bias of specific start and end points","Calculate taxes","Maximise reported returns","Compute exit load"],"a":0,"e":"Averages many consecutive period returns to avoid point-to-point bias."},{"t":"Returns","q":"Mutual funds may promise a return only when:","o":["The scheme is a debt fund","The AMC is large","SEBI approves","It is an assured returns scheme with a guarantor named in the SID"],"a":3,"e":"Assured returns schemes need a named guarantor; so far no assured-return product has been launched in India, only capital protection-oriented funds."},{"t":"Risk measures","q":"Which is a measure of fluctuation in periodic returns of an equity scheme?","o":["Sharpe ratio","Variance","Jensen's alpha","Modified duration"],"a":1,"e":"Workbook sample Q4: variance."},{"t":"Risk measures","q":"Standard deviation is a measure of:","o":["Total risk","Interest rate risk only","Systematic risk only","Credit risk"],"a":0,"e":"SD measures total risk; beta measures systematic risk."},{"t":"Risk measures","q":"A scheme's beta is 1.2. If the market falls 10%, the scheme is expected to fall:","o":["8%","10%","20%","12%"],"a":3,"e":"Beta 1.2 → 12% movement for a 10% market move."},{"t":"Risk measures","q":"Beta as a risk measure is relevant only for:","o":["Debt schemes","Equity schemes","Gold funds","Liquid funds"],"a":1,"e":"Beta applies to equity schemes."},{"t":"Risk measures","q":"A debt scheme holds 60% in a 5-year bond and 40% in a 6-month paper. Its weighted average maturity is:","o":["3.5 years","3.2 years","2.75 years","5 years"],"a":1,"e":"0.6 × 5 + 0.4 × 0.5 = 3.0 + 0.2 = 3.2 years."},{"t":"Gating","q":"Restriction on redemption (gating) can be imposed for at most:","o":["10 working days in any 90-day period","5 working days in 30 days","30 days in a year","15 days in 60 days"],"a":0,"e":"Max 10 working days in any 90-day period, with AMC Board and trustee approval."},{"t":"Risk factors","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. One of the standard risk statements is that the sponsor is not liable beyond its initial contribution.\nII. Reinvestment risk relates to \"interest on interest\".\nIII. Foreign currency risk faced by FPIs is managed by the AMC.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the sponsor is not liable beyond its initial contribution. II is true: reinvestment risk is the \"interest on interest\" risk. III is false: foreign currency risk applies to FPIs and the AMC doesn't manage it."},{"t":"Systematic risk","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Inflation is an unsystematic risk that can be diversified away.\nStatement 2: Investors are rewarded only for bearing systematic risk.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: inflation is a systematic (market, non-diversifiable) risk; a strike is an example of unsystematic risk. Statement 2 is true: investors are rewarded only for systematic risk."},{"t":"Securitisation","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. In securitisation, the originator sells receivables to an SPV (trust), which issues PTCs.\nII. Credit enhancement can allow PTCs to be rated above the originator.\nIII. PTC investors have recourse to the originator if the pool defaults.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: receivables go to an SPV (trust) that issues PTCs. II is true: credit enhancement lets PTCs be rated above the originator. III is false: PTCs are non-recourse."},{"t":"Derivative risks","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Basis risk is the risk that a derivative is mispriced by a wrong model","Model risk is the risk that the hedge and the underlying do not move together","Derivatives are leveraged instruments","Settlement of exchange-traded derivatives is guaranteed by SEBI"],"a":2,"e":"Only 'derivatives are leveraged' is true. Mispricing is model risk, not basis risk; hedge-vs-underlying mismatch is basis risk, not model risk; and settlement is guaranteed by the exchange, not SEBI."},{"t":"Equity analysis","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Technical analysis is best suited to long-term investing.\nII. Dividend yield tends to fall in bull markets and rise in bear markets.\nIII. Growth stocks typically have a high P/E and a low dividend yield.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":3,"e":"I is false: technical analysis suits the short term and timing; fundamental analysis suits the long term. II is true: dividend yield falls in bull markets and rises in bear markets. III is true: growth stocks have high P/E and PEG and low dividend yield."},{"t":"Top-down/bottom-up","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The EIC framework stands for Economy, Industry, Company.\nStatement 2: In the bottom-up approach, sector allocation is decided first and stocks are then picked within those sectors.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: EIC = Economy, Industry, Company. Statement 2 is false: bottom-up starts with the stock, and sector allocation is the result; top-down starts with the economy."},{"t":"Money market","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. T-bills are issued by the RBI on behalf of the Government of India.\nII. Banks issue CDs for 7 days to 1 year, while FIs issue them for 1 to 3 years.\nIII. Companies issue CP for up to 3 years.","o":["Only I","Only III","Only I and II","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: T-bills are issued by the RBI for the GoI. II is true: CDs are 7 days–1 year for banks and 1–3 years for FIs. III is false: CP is issued for up to 1 year."},{"t":"Rate calls","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: When interest rates are expected to rise, a debt fund manager should lengthen portfolio duration.\nStatement 2: A floater's coupon is fixed for its life, so its price is more sensitive to rate changes than a fixed-rate bond.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":1,"e":"Statement 1 is false: when rates are expected up, shorten duration or buy floaters; buy long duration when rates are expected down. Statement 2 is false: floater coupon = base + spread, so it holds its value when rates move."},{"t":"Investor returns","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["Investor return always equals scheme return since both use the same NAV","Advertised scheme returns ignore loads","Returns for holding periods under 1 year are absolute; over 1 year, CAGR","Rolling returns remove start/end-date bias"],"a":0,"e":"The first is wrong: investor return differs from scheme return because of loads, taxes and cash-flow timing. The others are correct: advertised returns ignore loads; <1 year absolute, >1 year CAGR; rolling returns remove start/end-date bias."},{"t":"Beta","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Beta comes from the CAPM, and the diversified market index has a beta of zero.\nII. A scheme with a beta of 1.2 is expected to move 12% when the market moves 10%.\nIII. G-secs and cash carry no credit risk.","o":["Only I and II","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is false: beta comes from the CAPM, but the diversified index has a beta of 1, not zero. II is true: beta 1.2 moves 12% for a 10% market move. III is true: G-secs and cash have no credit risk."},{"t":"Duration measures","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Modified duration measures interest rate sensitivity and is the professional's measure.\nStatement 2: Weighted average maturity (WAM) is a simpler measure used with lay investors.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 1 is true: modified duration is the professional's interest-rate sensitivity measure. Statement 2 is true: WAM is simpler and used with lay investors."},{"t":"Gating","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Gating can be imposed when a single security in the portfolio defaults.\nII. Gating can last at most 10 working days in any 90 days.\nIII. During gating, the first Rs 2 lakh of every redemption is paid normally.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is false: gating is only for market-wide crises, never for one bad security. II is true: maximum 10 working days in any 90 days. III is true: the first Rs 2 lakh of each redemption is paid normally."},{"t":"Segregated portfolio","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Units of a segregated portfolio can be redeemed with the AMC on any business day","New investors after segregation get units in both the main and segregated portfolios","The segregation policy is approved by the AMC Board alone","Units of a segregated portfolio are listed within 10 working days"],"a":3,"e":"Only the listing statement is true (within 10 working days). There is no subscription or redemption in a segregated portfolio, new investors get main-portfolio units only, and the segregation policy is approved by the trustees."},{"t":"SD vs beta","y":"concept","q":"Standard deviation and beta differ in that:","o":["SD measures total risk; beta measures only systematic risk","SD measures systematic risk; beta measures total risk","Both measure total risk, but beta uses daily data","SD applies only to equity; beta applies to debt and equity"],"a":0,"e":"SD (√variance) is total risk and applies to debt and equity; beta (from CAPM) is systematic risk against a diversified index and applies to equity only."},{"t":"CAGR","y":"concept","q":"In the formula CAGR = (Later value ÷ Initial value)^(1/n) − 1, the term 'n' is computed as:","o":["Number of days ÷ 360","Number of days ÷ 365","Number of NAV observations ÷ 52","Number of IDCW payouts during the period"],"a":1,"e":"n is the period in years, computed as days ÷ 365."},{"t":"Credit spread","y":"concept","q":"Credit spread is the difference between:","o":["The coupon rate and the YTM of the same bond","Long-term and short-term gilt yields","Corporate bond yield and gilt yield","The repo rate and the T-bill yield"],"a":2,"e":"Credit spread = corporate yield − gilt (government security) yield; rating agencies include CRISIL, ICRA, CARE and India Ratings (Fitch)."},{"t":"Book value","y":"concept","q":"In the P/BV ratio, the book value per share used in the denominator is:","o":["PAT ÷ number of shares","Market price × number of shares","Dividend per share ÷ market price","Net worth ÷ number of shares"],"a":3,"e":"Book value per share = net worth ÷ shares, and P/BV = price ÷ BV. PAT ÷ shares is EPS, and DPS ÷ price is dividend yield."},{"t":"Annualised SD","y":"concept","q":"An SD computed from monthly returns is annualised by multiplying it by:","o":["12","√12","√52","√252"],"a":1,"e":"Annualise SD by ×√12 for monthly, ×√52 for weekly and ×√252 for daily data."}]},"ch11":{"id":"ch11","label":"Ch 11 · Scheme Performance","short":"Ch 11","kind":"chapter","weight":7,"qs":[{"t":"Benchmarks","q":"Which is a measure of risk-adjusted returns?","o":["Standard deviation","Beta","Sharpe ratio","Variance"],"a":2,"e":"Workbook sample Q1: Sharpe ratio."},{"t":"Benchmarks","q":"Which can NOT be a basis for selecting a scheme's benchmark?","o":["Scheme's past returns","Investment strategy","Investment objective","Asset allocation pattern"],"a":0,"e":"Workbook sample Q2."},{"t":"Benchmarks","q":"An index that includes dividends from constituents in addition to capital gains is the:","o":["Dividend Return Index","Total Return Index","Composite Index","Price Return Index"],"a":1,"e":"Workbook sample Q3."},{"t":"Benchmarks","q":"The most appropriate measure of how closely an index fund tracks its benchmark is:","o":["Treynor ratio","AUM","TER","Tracking error"],"a":3,"e":"Workbook sample Q4."},{"t":"Benchmarks","q":"Who decides the benchmark of a scheme?","o":["AMFI","The AMC in consultation with the trustees","The fund manager alone","SEBI alone"],"a":1,"e":"The AMC decides with the trustees and discloses it in the SID."},{"t":"Benchmarks","q":"Mutual fund schemes have been benchmarked to TRI since:","o":["1 April 2020","1 April 2017","1 February 2018","1 January 2022"],"a":2,"e":"From February 1, 2018."},{"t":"Benchmarks","q":"Switching from PRI to TRI benchmarks has made it look as if:","o":["Expenses fell","Fewer schemes beat benchmarks","More schemes beat benchmarks","NAVs increased"],"a":1,"e":"TRI includes dividends, raising the bar; reality has not changed."},{"t":"Benchmarks","q":"Historically, index dividend yields in India (the PRI–TRI gap) have mostly ranged between:","o":["4% and 6%","0.1% and 0.5%","8% and 10%","1.5% and 2.5%"],"a":3,"e":"Mostly 1.5% to 2.5%."},{"t":"Benchmarks","q":"If TRI data is unavailable for an earlier period, mutual funds should use:","o":["PRI only","Peer average","No benchmark","A composite CAGR of PRI (until TRI available) and TRI thereafter"],"a":3,"e":"SEBI circular of January 4, 2018."},{"t":"Equity benchmarks","q":"A focused equity fund with few stocks would find an appropriate benchmark in:","o":["Nifty 500","Sensex or Nifty 50 (narrow indices)","A debt index","Gold price"],"a":1,"e":"Fewer stocks suit narrow indices; broader portfolios suit Nifty 100/200/500."},{"t":"Equity benchmarks","q":"A banking sector fund would most appropriately be benchmarked to:","o":["S&P 500","Nifty 50","S&P BSE Bankex","CRISIL Liquid Fund Index"],"a":2,"e":"Sectoral funds use sectoral indices."},{"t":"Debt benchmarks","q":"In ICICI Securities' I-Bex family, Mi-Bex covers maturities of:","o":["3–7 years","Up to 1 year","More than 7 years","1–3 years"],"a":0,"e":"Si-Bex 1–3, Mi-Bex 3–7, Li-Bex >7 years."},{"t":"Debt benchmarks","q":"Per the workbook's benchmark table, the CRISIL index for a Liquid Fund is:","o":["CRISIL Liquid Debt A-I Index","CRISIL Short Duration Debt A-II Index","CRISIL Low Duration Debt A-I Index","CRISIL Liquid Overnight Index"],"a":0,"e":"Overnight: CRISIL Liquid Overnight; Liquid: CRISIL Liquid Debt A-I; Short duration: CRISIL Short Duration Debt A-II."},{"t":"Debt benchmarks","q":"Benchmarks for debt and balanced schemes, per SEBI, should be developed by:","o":["The AMC itself","Research and rating agencies recommended by AMFI","Trustees","RBI"],"a":1,"e":"CRISIL, ICICI Securities and NSE have developed such indices."},{"t":"Other benchmarks","q":"Per Table 11.2, the NSE benchmark for a Conservative Hybrid Fund is:","o":["NIFTY 50 Hybrid Composite Debt 15:85 Index","NIFTY 50 Hybrid Composite Debt 65:35 Index","NIFTY 50 Arbitrage","NIFTY 50 Hybrid Composite Debt 50:50 Index"],"a":0,"e":"Conservative 15:85; balanced and BAF 50:50; aggressive 65:35; arbitrage NIFTY 50 Arbitrage."},{"t":"Other benchmarks","q":"The benchmark for a Gold ETF is:","o":["Sensex","Nifty 50","Price of gold","10-year G-sec"],"a":2,"e":"Gold price is the benchmark."},{"t":"Other benchmarks","q":"A scheme investing mainly in the US market might use as benchmark:","o":["Nifty 50","S&P 500","Shanghai Composite","Bankex"],"a":1,"e":"The benchmark follows where the scheme invests."},{"t":"Two-tier","q":"Under SEBI's two-tier benchmarking, Tier 1 reflects:","o":["The fund manager's style","The scheme's category","The AMC brand","The distributor channel"],"a":1,"e":"Tier 1: category; Tier 2: investment style/strategy."},{"t":"Two-tier","q":"Which schemes have a single benchmark rather than two tiers?","o":["Ultra-short duration funds","Short duration funds","Large cap funds","Sectoral/thematic and index funds"],"a":3,"e":"Hybrid/solution, sectoral/thematic, index/ETF have a single benchmark."},{"t":"Two-tier","q":"A FoF investing in multiple underlying schemes is benchmarked to:","o":["Gold","The best underlying scheme's benchmark","A broad market index","No benchmark"],"a":2,"e":"Single-fund FoF uses the underlying's benchmark; multi-fund FoF uses a broad market index."},{"t":"Measures","q":"Relative return comparisons have the weakness that they:","o":["Ignore returns","Are too complex","Need beta","Do not differentiate between schemes taking different levels of risk"],"a":3,"e":"Hence risk-adjusted measures are used."},{"t":"Measures","q":"Risk-free return is 5%, scheme return is 7%, SD is 0.5%. Sharpe ratio is:","o":["4","14","0.25","2"],"a":0,"e":"(7 − 5) ÷ 0.5 = 4."},{"t":"Measures","q":"Risk-free return 6%, scheme return 12%, SD 15%. Sharpe ratio is:","o":["6","2.5","0.4","0.8"],"a":2,"e":"(12 − 6) ÷ 15 = 0.4."},{"t":"Measures","q":"Sharpe ratio uses which measure of risk?","o":["Beta","Tracking error","Standard deviation","Duration"],"a":2,"e":"Sharpe uses SD (total risk); Treynor uses beta."},{"t":"Measures","q":"Risk-free return 5%, scheme return 8%, beta 1.2. Treynor ratio is:","o":["2.5","3.6","0.4","6.67"],"a":0,"e":"(8 − 5) ÷ 1.2 = 2.5."},{"t":"Measures","q":"Treynor ratio and alpha are most relevant for:","o":["Gold funds","Debt funds","Liquid funds","Diversified equity schemes"],"a":3,"e":"Beta-based measures suit diversified equity schemes."},{"t":"Measures","q":"Can the Sharpe ratio of an equity scheme be compared with that of a debt scheme?","o":["Only annually","No; only comparable schemes should be compared","Yes","Only for index funds"],"a":1,"e":"Sharpe comparisons should be within comparable schemes."},{"t":"Measures","q":"A positive alpha indicates:","o":["Lower expenses","Higher risk","Out-performance relative to beta-adjusted expected return","Under-performance"],"a":2,"e":"Alpha = actual − optimal return."},{"t":"Measures","q":"Which risk-adjusted measure is mandatory for AMCs to disclose?","o":["Information ratio","Sharpe ratio","Treynor ratio","Sortino ratio"],"a":0,"e":"Information Ratio is the only mandatory one, disclosed daily."},{"t":"Measures","q":"The Information Ratio uses excess return over which benchmark?","o":["Tier-1 benchmark in the SID","Tier-2 benchmark","Risk-free rate","Peer average"],"a":0,"e":"Tier-I benchmark, with SD of daily excess returns."},{"t":"Measures","q":"A portfolio returned 12%, its Tier-1 benchmark 10%, and SD of excess return is 4%. IR is:","o":["0.2","0.5","2","3"],"a":1,"e":"(12 − 10) ÷ 4 = 0.5."},{"t":"Measures","q":"Tracking error is calculated as:","o":["Beta minus 1","TER divided by AUM","Standard deviation of excess returns","Average excess return"],"a":2,"e":"Tracking error = SD of excess returns over the benchmark."},{"t":"Disclosures","q":"Is publishing a monthly factsheet mandatory?","o":["Yes","Only for NFOs","Only for equity funds","No, but most AMCs do it and ad rules apply"],"a":3,"e":"Factsheets are voluntary."},{"t":"Disclosures","q":"Discrete annual returns help an investor assess the fund's:","o":["Consistency across market conditions","Exit load","Expense ratio","Tax"],"a":0,"e":"They show performance in different market scenarios."},{"t":"Disclosures","q":"A high fiscal deficit, as analysed in factsheets, tends to:","o":["Have no effect","Lower interest rates","Reduce inflation","Push up interest rates through higher government borrowing"],"a":3,"e":"Higher borrowing raises interest costs and limits fiscal impetus."},{"t":"Benchmark choice","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A scheme's benchmark must match its investment objective, asset allocation and investment strategy.\nII. A scheme's past returns can be a basis for choosing its benchmark.\nIII. The benchmark of an index fund is the index it tracks.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: the benchmark must match objective, asset allocation and strategy. II is false: past returns are never a basis for choosing a benchmark. III is true: an index fund is benchmarked to the index it tracks."},{"t":"TRI","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. The benchmark is chosen by the AMC with the trustees and is stated in the SID.\nII. Scheme performance has been compared with the Total Return Index (TRI) since 1 Feb 2018.\nIII. For periods before the TRI existed, performance is compared with the Price Return Index (PRI) alone.","o":["Only I","Only III","Only II and III","Only I and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the AMC with the trustees chooses the benchmark and states it in the SID. II is true: TRI replaced PRI from 1 Feb 2018. III is false: periods before TRI existed use a composite CAGR (PRI, then TRI)."},{"t":"Two-tier benchmarks","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: SEBI introduced two-tier benchmarking in Oct 2021, effective 1 Jan 2022.\nStatement 2: Under two-tier benchmarking, Tier 1 reflects the fund manager's style and Tier 2 reflects the scheme category.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: SEBI's Oct 2021 circular took effect from 1 Jan 2022. Statement 2 is false: Tier 1 is the category benchmark and Tier 2 reflects the fund manager's style."},{"t":"Single benchmark","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["Hybrid and solution-oriented schemes have a single benchmark","Sectoral schemes have a single benchmark","All benchmarks under the two-tier structure must be TRI","Index funds and ETFs follow the two-tier benchmark structure"],"a":3,"e":"Hybrid/solution, sectoral and index/ETF schemes all have a single benchmark, so the statement on index funds and ETFs is false. Two-tier benchmarks apply to debt and equity schemes and all must be TRI."},{"t":"FoF and gold","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A Fund of Funds investing in a single fund uses the benchmark of that underlying fund.\nII. A Fund of Funds investing in multiple funds uses the benchmark of its largest underlying fund.\nIII. A gold ETF is benchmarked against the price of gold.","o":["Only I and II","Only I and III","Only II and III","Only III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: a single-fund FoF uses the underlying's benchmark. II is false: a multi-fund FoF uses a broad market index. III is true: a gold ETF's benchmark is the price of gold."},{"t":"I-Bex","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Mi-Bex is the I-Bex sub-index for maturities of more than 7 years.\nStatement 2: Si-Bex is the I-Bex sub-index for maturities of 1 to 3 years.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: Mi-Bex covers 3–7 years; Li-Bex covers more than 7 years. Statement 2 is true: Si-Bex covers 1–3 years."},{"t":"Equity benchmarks","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A banking sectoral fund may be benchmarked to a sector index such as Bankex.\nII. A concentrated scheme holding fewer stocks is better compared with a broad index such as Nifty 500 than with Sensex 30 or Nifty 50.\nIII. Large cap schemes are benchmarked to NIFTY 100 / BSE 100.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II","Only II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: sectoral schemes use a sector index such as Bankex. II is false: a scheme with fewer stocks is compared with Sensex 30 / Nifty 50; schemes with more stocks use Nifty 100/200/500. III is true: large cap uses NIFTY 100 / BSE 100."},{"t":"Debt indices","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Debt indices come from CRISIL, ICICI Securities and NSE, and are recommended by AMFI","Debt indices are recommended by SEBI and published only by CRISIL","The list of scheme benchmarks is published on SEBI's website","Debt Tier-1 benchmarks follow PRC from 1 Jan 2022"],"a":0,"e":"Debt indices come from CRISIL, ICICI Securities and NSE, recommended by AMFI, and the benchmark list is on AMFI's website (not SEBI's). Debt Tier-1 benchmarks follow PRC from 1 Apr 2022, not 1 Jan 2022."},{"t":"Hybrid benchmarks","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Conservative hybrid schemes: NIFTY 50 Hybrid Composite Debt 15:85 Index.\nII. Balanced advantage / dynamic asset allocation schemes: NIFTY 50 Hybrid Composite Debt 65:35 Index.\nIII. Arbitrage schemes: NIFTY 50 Arbitrage Index.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: conservative hybrid uses the 15:85 index. II is false: balanced and BAF/DAA schemes use the 50:50 index; 65:35 is for aggressive hybrid. III is true: arbitrage uses NIFTY 50 Arbitrage."},{"t":"Relative return","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Relative return, measured against a benchmark or peers, ignores risk.\nStatement 2: AMCs and trustees review a scheme's relative returns periodically.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 1 is true: relative return vs benchmark or peers ignores risk. Statement 2 is true: AMCs and trustees review relative returns periodically."},{"t":"Sharpe and Treynor","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A higher Sharpe ratio indicates better risk-adjusted performance.\nII. The Sharpe ratio can be used to compare an equity scheme directly with a debt scheme.\nIII. The Treynor ratio is meant for diversified equity schemes.","o":["Only II","Only III","Only I and II","Only II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: higher Sharpe is better. II is false: Sharpe must compare like with like, never equity vs debt. III is true: Treynor is used for diversified equity schemes."},{"t":"Information Ratio","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["The Information Ratio is the only mandatory risk-adjusted performance measure","Under the Jan 2025 circular, the Information Ratio is disclosed monthly in the factsheet","The Information Ratio is calculated using daily data","For an index fund, tracking error should ideally be zero"],"a":1,"e":"IR is the only mandatory risk-adjusted measure, uses daily data, and under the Jan 2025 circular is disclosed daily on the AMC and AMFI websites, not monthly in the factsheet. An index fund's tracking error is ideally zero."},{"t":"Performance disclosure","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A scheme's product label shows its objective, asset class, holding period and risk-o-meter.\nII. Discrete annual returns help judge a scheme's consistency across market conditions.\nIII. The AMFI website has performance data for all schemes.","o":["Only I and II","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"e":"I is true: the label shows objective, asset class, holding period and risk-o-meter. II is true: discrete annual returns show consistency across market conditions. III is true: AMFI's website carries performance data for all schemes."},{"t":"Sharpe vs Treynor","y":"concept","q":"The Sharpe ratio and the Treynor ratio:","o":["Share the numerator (scheme return minus benchmark return) and differ in the denominator: Sharpe uses SD, Treynor uses beta","Share the denominator (SD) and differ in the numerator: Sharpe uses the T-bill return, Treynor uses the benchmark return","Share the numerator (scheme return minus T-bill return) and differ in the denominator: Sharpe uses SD, Treynor uses beta","Share the numerator (scheme return minus T-bill return) and differ in the denominator: Sharpe uses beta, Treynor uses SD"],"a":2,"e":"Both have the risk premium (scheme return minus risk-free T-bill return) as numerator. Sharpe divides by SD (total risk); Treynor divides by beta (systematic risk)."},{"t":"Information Ratio","y":"concept","q":"In the Information Ratio, the numerator and the denominator are respectively:","o":["Excess return over the Tier-1 benchmark; SD of that excess return","Scheme return minus T-bill return; SD of scheme returns","Excess return over the Tier-1 benchmark; beta of the scheme","Scheme return minus beta-expected return; SD of scheme returns"],"a":0,"e":"IR = excess return over the Tier-1 benchmark divided by the SD of that excess return, computed on daily data. Scheme return minus T-bill return over SD is the Sharpe ratio."},{"t":"Tracking error","y":"concept","q":"Tracking error is the standard deviation of:","o":["The scheme's daily NAV","The benchmark's returns","The scheme's returns","The scheme's excess returns over its benchmark"],"a":3,"e":"Tracking error = SD of excess returns. It shows how closely an index fund tracks its index (ideally zero) and how consistently an active fund beats its benchmark (lower = more consistent)."},{"t":"Alpha","y":"concept","q":"Alpha measures the difference between:","o":["The scheme's actual return and the return expected from its beta (optimal return)","The scheme's return and the risk-free T-bill return","The scheme's return and the average return of its peers","The benchmark's TRI return and its PRI return"],"a":0,"e":"Alpha = actual return minus the beta-expected (optimal) return; a positive alpha means outperformance. Scheme return minus T-bill return is the risk premium."},{"t":"TRI effect","y":"concept","q":"Benchmarking against a TRI instead of a PRI makes it look like fewer schemes beat the benchmark because:","o":["The TRI deducts the scheme's expenses from the benchmark return","The TRI adds the dividends/interest of the index, raising the benchmark return by about the index dividend yield (~1.5–2.5%)","The TRI excludes dividends, lowering the benchmark return","The TRI is computed on beta-adjusted returns"],"a":1,"e":"The TRI adds dividends/interest to the price index, so the benchmark return rises by roughly the index dividend yield (~1.5–2.5%), making it harder for schemes to beat."}]},"ch12":{"id":"ch12","label":"Ch 12 · Scheme Selection","short":"Ch 12","kind":"chapter","weight":15,"qs":[{"t":"Sample","q":"Which scheme category is least risky in terms of credit risk?","o":["Dynamic bond","Corporate bond fund","Gilt fund","Credit risk fund"],"a":2,"e":"Workbook sample Q1: gilt funds hold government securities with no credit risk."},{"t":"Sample","q":"For a quick sense of a scheme's risk level, SEBI introduced:","o":["Risk-o-meter","Factsheet","Risk factors","Investor's risk profile"],"a":0,"e":"Workbook sample Q2."},{"t":"Sample","q":"Passive funds are safe because their NAV does not fall when markets fall. True or false?","o":["True for ETFs only","True for index funds only","True","False"],"a":3,"e":"Workbook sample Q3: if the index falls, the index fund NAV falls too."},{"t":"Sample","q":"Which scheme has the lowest concentration risk?","o":["Multi-cap fund","Focused fund","Sector fund","Thematic fund"],"a":0,"e":"Workbook sample Q4."},{"t":"Sample","q":"___ are close-ended debt funds.","o":["Overnight funds","Arbitrage funds","Fixed Maturity Plans","Dynamic debt funds"],"a":2,"e":"Workbook sample Q5."},{"t":"Sample","q":"A focused fund is riskier than a diversified fund. True or false?","o":["False","Only for small caps","True","Only in bear markets"],"a":2,"e":"Workbook sample Q6."},{"t":"Sample","q":"Which is true about the risk-return profile of MF schemes?","o":["Liquid to debt funds: risk increases","Liquid to equity funds: potential return increases","Both of these","Neither"],"a":2,"e":"Workbook sample Q7."},{"t":"Investor fit","q":"The first step in building a portfolio of mutual funds is to:","o":["Pick the top-rated fund","Set financial goals","Choose a distributor","Check NAVs"],"a":1,"e":"Set goals first; then assess the investor's situation and risk appetite."},{"t":"Investor fit","q":"An investor seeks growth and accepts risk but needs the money in 1 year. Equity is:","o":["Ideal","Not suitable due to short horizon","Tax-free","Required"],"a":1,"e":"Equity is volatile in the short term; it needs an adequately long horizon."},{"t":"Investor fit","q":"According to the workbook, using age alone to judge risk profile is:","o":["The best method","Required by SEBI","Mandatory","Not prudent; the horizon to each goal matters more"],"a":3,"e":"Investors of the same age can have very different goals and situations."},{"t":"Investor fit","q":"The need for regular income is best matched by which asset class?","o":["Equity","Gold","Real estate","Debt"],"a":3,"e":"Growth → equity; income → debt."},{"t":"Core-satellite","q":"Which fund is most suited to the satellite portfolio?","o":["Diversified equity fund","Sector fund","Large cap fund","Short duration fund for a low-risk investor"],"a":1,"e":"Sector funds do well cyclically and need timing."},{"t":"Core-satellite","q":"Exposure to gold funds may be increased tactically when:","o":["Interest rates fall","The rupee strengthens","Equity markets boom","Inflation is high or there is political/economic uncertainty"],"a":3,"e":"Gold is a tactical hedge in such conditions."},{"t":"Core-satellite","q":"A long-term gilt fund is a tactical (satellite) choice when interest rates are expected to:","o":["Rise","Be volatile","Fall","Stay unchanged"],"a":2,"e":"Long gilts gain when rates decline."},{"t":"Core-satellite","q":"In the workbook's illustration, a moderate investor may be comfortable with:","o":["100% satellite","80% core and 20% satellite","50% core and 50% satellite","20% core and 80% satellite"],"a":1,"e":"Indicative only; conservative investors take less satellite exposure."},{"t":"Hierarchy","q":"Moving from left to right, which order shows increasing interest rate risk?","o":["Overnight → liquid → ultra-short → short → long duration","Gilt → credit risk","Long → medium → short → liquid","Large → small cap"],"a":0,"e":"Illustration 12.1.2."},{"t":"Hierarchy","q":"Which order shows increasing credit risk?","o":["Credit risk → corporate bond → banking & PSU → gilt","Overnight → long duration","Arbitrage → aggressive hybrid","Gilt → banking & PSU → corporate bond → credit risk"],"a":3,"e":"Illustration 12.1.3."},{"t":"Hierarchy","q":"In the workbook's equity hierarchy, which is the least risky?","o":["Small cap funds","Mid cap funds","Multi cap funds","Large cap funds"],"a":3,"e":"Risk falls from small → mid → multi → large & mid → large cap."},{"t":"Hierarchy","q":"Which order shows increasing concentration risk?","o":["Focused → diversified → sector → thematic","Diversified → focused → thematic → sector","Thematic → sector → diversified → focused","Sector → thematic → focused → diversified"],"a":1,"e":"Illustration 12.1.5."},{"t":"Hierarchy","q":"Among hybrid funds, which is the least risky per the workbook hierarchy?","o":["Aggressive hybrid","Multi asset allocation","Balanced hybrid","Arbitrage fund"],"a":3,"e":"Arbitrage → conservative hybrid → equity savings → DAAF → multi-asset → balanced → aggressive."},{"t":"Hierarchy","q":"Which hybrid category is the riskiest per the workbook hierarchy?","o":["Equity savings","Aggressive hybrid","Arbitrage","Conservative hybrid"],"a":1,"e":"Aggressive hybrid is at the right end."},{"t":"Hierarchy","q":"Comparing a large-cap sector fund with a diversified small cap fund:","o":["They are exposed to different kinds of risk (concentration vs size), so not directly comparable","The small cap fund is risk-free","The sector fund is always riskier","They have identical risk"],"a":0,"e":"One takes concentration risk; the other size risk."},{"t":"Hierarchy","q":"In the workbook's hybrid hierarchy, which category comes immediately after arbitrage funds (next riskier)?","o":["Equity savings fund","Conservative hybrid fund","Balanced hybrid fund","Multi asset allocation fund"],"a":1,"e":"Arbitrage → conservative hybrid → equity savings → DAAF → multi-asset → balanced → aggressive (Illustration 12.1.6)."},{"t":"Code of conduct","q":"Regulated entities and their agents, including MFDs, must show on social media:","o":["Their registered name and registration number on the home page and at the start of each video/content","Daily NAVs of all schemes","AMC logos without approval","Nothing; social media is exempt"],"a":0,"e":"Per the code of conduct appendix, registered name and number go on the social media home page and at the beginning of each securities-market video or content."},{"t":"Labelling","q":"Why did SEBI introduce product labelling in 2013?","o":["To raise TER","To promote NFOs","To rank AMCs","To address mis-selling and help investors understand suitability"],"a":3,"e":"Product labels help investors understand the product and its suitability."},{"t":"Strategy","q":"An investor wanting low-risk equity exposure may consider:","o":["A small cap fund","A thematic fund","An index fund or value fund","A sector fund"],"a":2,"e":"Growth funds suit those willing to take more risk."},{"t":"Strategy","q":"An index fund has no:","o":["Selection risk from the fund manager","Expenses","Market risk","NAV fluctuation"],"a":0,"e":"The fund manager has no role in choosing securities."},{"t":"Strategy","q":"An investor who wants equity growth rather than beating the benchmark would be better off in:","o":["An FMP","A credit risk fund","An index fund","A sector fund"],"a":2,"e":"The modest objective suits index funds."},{"t":"Strategy","q":"In open-ended equity funds, keeping part of the portfolio in liquid assets:","o":["Can dilute returns compared with full equity exposure","Removes all risk","Increases returns","Is prohibited"],"a":0,"e":"Liquidity holdings dilute equity returns."},{"t":"Strategy","q":"The price of close-ended scheme units on an exchange relative to NAV:","o":["Is always at face value","Is fixed at NAV","May be at a discount with no limit, converging to NAV near maturity","Is always at a premium"],"a":2,"e":"There is no limit to the discount."},{"t":"Strategy","q":"A close-ended equity fund allows the fund manager to:","o":["Guarantee returns","Charge higher TER","Invest in illiquid or long-gestation stocks without redemption pressure","Avoid SEBI rules"],"a":2,"e":"No redemption pressure permits such strategies."},{"t":"Strategy","q":"\"Diversification is a protection against ignorance\" implies:","o":["If unsure, diversify","Only experts should diversify","Concentrate if unsure","Diversification lowers returns always"],"a":0,"e":"If one is not sure, diversification is a good idea."},{"t":"Strategy","q":"Thematic funds such as MNC, PSU or infrastructure funds are:","o":["Debt funds","Between diversified funds and sector funds","Index funds","Riskier than sector funds"],"a":1,"e":"They invest across a theme, not a single sector."},{"t":"Strategy","q":"When the economy recovers and front-line stocks get expensive, which funds may offer attractive opportunities?","o":["Gilt funds","Mid-cap and small-cap funds","Liquid funds","Arbitrage funds"],"a":1,"e":"Mid/small caps benefit in recoveries."},{"t":"Strategy","q":"In which fund does the fund manager decide the market-cap mix freely?","o":["Flexi cap","Large cap","Multi cap","Large & mid cap"],"a":0,"e":"Flexi-cap gives full freedom; multi-cap has minimum 25% each."},{"t":"Strategy","q":"Growth funds tend to outperform in:","o":["Bull markets","Falling markets","Sideways markets","Only at NFO"],"a":0,"e":"Value orientation helps in falling markets."},{"t":"Strategy","q":"An Indian investor in a US equity fund benefits from currency movement when:","o":["The rupee strengthens","The US dollar strengthens","Both currencies are stable","Interest rates rise"],"a":1,"e":"A stronger dollar raises rupee value of US holdings."},{"t":"Strategy","q":"International equity funds are best suited as:","o":["An emergency fund","A liquid fund substitute","Satellite or a small diversifying core allocation","The entire core portfolio"],"a":2,"e":"As per the workbook."},{"t":"Debt selection","q":"An FMP is ideal when:","o":["The investor's horizon matches the scheme maturity and more predictable returns are wanted","The investor wants equity growth","Interest rates are expected to fall sharply","The investor may need money anytime"],"a":0,"e":"Also consider its credit risk."},{"t":"Debt selection","q":"An advantage of Target Maturity Funds over FMPs is:","o":["Higher credit risk","Guaranteed returns","Liquidity, as they can be bought or redeemed with the AMC","Equity exposure"],"a":2,"e":"TMFs following an index are open-ended in practice."},{"t":"Debt selection","q":"TMF portfolios typically comprise:","o":["Credit risk bonds","G-secs, SDLs and AAA PSU bonds","Small cap stocks","Gold"],"a":1,"e":"Top-notch quality paper."},{"t":"Debt selection","q":"If interest rates are expected to rise, it is safer to increase allocation to:","o":["Short duration funds","Gilt funds with 10-year constant maturity","Dynamic bond funds only","Long duration funds"],"a":0,"e":"Maturing short-term bonds get reinvested at higher rates."},{"t":"Debt selection","q":"The comparable for a liquid scheme for retail investors is:","o":["Savings bank account","PPF","Equity fund","Real estate"],"a":0,"e":"Liquid funds are for parking money up to 91 days."},{"t":"Debt selection","q":"Using a liquid fund for long periods is not advised because:","o":["The investor needlessly loses returns","It is risky","It is taxed at 30%","It has a lock-in"],"a":0,"e":"Better options exist for longer horizons."},{"t":"Debt selection","q":"A debt fund that manages duration based on rate expectations will have returns that are:","o":["Tax free","Guaranteed","Very stable","More volatile"],"a":3,"e":"Rate calls may be wrong, causing losses."},{"t":"Hybrid selection","q":"The word \"conservative\" in conservative hybrid fund refers to:","o":["The debt credit quality","The overall risk","The fund manager's style","The equity allocation only"],"a":3,"e":"Investors must still check the equity and debt quality."},{"t":"Hybrid selection","q":"The main benefit of choosing a hybrid fund over separate equity and debt funds is:","o":["No risk","Fewer scheme selection decisions","Higher returns always","No taxes"],"a":1,"e":"Simpler, but the investor must accept the fund's mix."},{"t":"Gold selection","q":"A gold sector fund invests in:","o":["Physical gold","Shares of gold mining and processing companies","Gold bonds","Gold futures only"],"a":1,"e":"Its returns depend on company profitability, not just gold price."},{"t":"Gold selection","q":"When gold prices rise, gold mining shares can:","o":["Rise much more than gold","Stay flat","Fall","Track gold exactly"],"a":0,"e":"They can amplify gains (and losses)."},{"t":"Within category","q":"A \"value fund\" holding mostly fully-valued front-line stocks indicates:","o":["The fund manager is not true to the promised style","Low risk","Excellent value discipline","Tax efficiency"],"a":0,"e":"Portfolio should match the stated objective and style."},{"t":"Within category","q":"Equity fund performance should be evaluated over at least:","o":["6 months","5 years","3 years","1 year"],"a":1,"e":"Equity: at least 5 years; long-term debt: 3 years."},{"t":"Within category","q":"For liquid funds, which return periods are most relevant?","o":["Since inception only","5 and 10 years","3 years","7 days, 15 days and 1 month"],"a":3,"e":"Short periods match their holding horizon."},{"t":"Within category","q":"A fund's ideal performance pattern is to:","o":["Have the highest 1-month return","Match peers exactly","Only beat the benchmark in bull markets","Outperform in rising markets and protect the downside in falling markets"],"a":3,"e":"Consistency and downside protection matter."},{"t":"Within category","q":"Fund age matters most in categories where:","o":["Schemes perform very similarly","There is only one scheme","Performance diverges widely, like multi-cap funds","Returns are fixed"],"a":2,"e":"Large-cap funds show less divergence."},{"t":"Within category","q":"A very large fund size may be a disadvantage for:","o":["A liquid fund","A mid-cap or sector fund with limited investment options","An index fund","A large cap fund"],"a":1,"e":"Limited universe makes deploying large money harder."},{"t":"Within category","q":"Purchases and sales total Rs 10,000 crore in a year; average net assets are Rs 5,000 crore. Portfolio turnover is:","o":["100%","500%","200%","50%"],"a":2,"e":"10,000 ÷ 5,000 = 2 = 200%."},{"t":"Within category","q":"With a portfolio turnover of 200%, the average holding period of investments is:","o":["3 months","6 months","12 months","24 months"],"a":1,"e":"12 months ÷ 2 = 6 months."},{"t":"Within category","q":"Purchases and sales of Rs 3,000 crore against average net assets of Rs 6,000 crore imply an average holding of about:","o":["12 months","6 months","36 months","24 months"],"a":3,"e":"Turnover 0.5 → 12 ÷ 0.5 = 24 months."},{"t":"Within category","q":"A six-month average holding period is too short for which style?","o":["Momentum","Arbitrage","Value investing","Liquid fund"],"a":2,"e":"Value investing needs longer holding."},{"t":"Within category","q":"Investors should be particularly careful about costs in:","o":["Sector funds","International funds","Small cap funds","Debt and index funds"],"a":3,"e":"Debt returns are lower; index funds are passive."},{"t":"Within category","q":"Star ratings of mutual funds by research agencies are generally:","o":["Assessments of past performance with no predictive ability","Guarantees","Predictive of future returns","Issued by SEBI"],"a":0,"e":"Understand the methodology; ratings reflect the past."},{"t":"Within category","q":"In a category with many schemes, the workbook suggests staying invested in schemes that are consistently in the:","o":["Bottom half","Middle","Top 10–15 percent","Top 1"],"a":2,"e":"Top 3–5 in small categories; top 10–15% in large ones."},{"t":"Within category","q":"Why is switching every quarter to the best-ranked fund not advisable?","o":["SEBI bans it","It is illegal","Top rankers change and switching costs (loads, taxes) hurt returns","Funds refuse switches"],"a":2,"e":"Neither ideal nor feasible."},{"t":"Within category","q":"Investors in non-gilt debt schemes should track:","o":["Distributor rating","Only NAV","Only AUM","Credit quality and sector concentration of the portfolio"],"a":3,"e":"Even highly rated papers may be concentrated in a sector."},{"t":"Options","q":"For an investor who needs regular income, the workbook prefers:","o":["ELSS","IDCW payout option","Growth without withdrawal","Systematic Withdrawal Plan"],"a":3,"e":"IDCW depends on distributable surplus; SWP gives the needed amount."},{"t":"Options","q":"A monthly IDCW option guarantees monthly payouts. True or false?","o":["True","False; payouts depend on distributable surplus","True for debt funds","True only for NRIs"],"a":1,"e":"No surplus, no dividend."},{"t":"Options","q":"The IDCW option is preferable mainly for:","o":["High-income investors","Tax-exempt investors","Minors","NRIs"],"a":1,"e":"Dividends are taxed at slab rates for others."},{"t":"Options","q":"The growth option's main benefit is:","o":["No exit load","Guaranteed return","Money grows on a gross basis without annual taxation","Regular cash flow"],"a":2,"e":"Tax deferral allows pre-tax compounding."},{"t":"Options","q":"An investor may redeem units to book a capital loss mainly to:","o":["Increase units","Set it off against capital gains where permitted","Avoid KYC","Get a dividend"],"a":1,"e":"Set-off rules were covered in Chapter 8."},{"t":"Do's and don'ts","q":"Under which regulation must a distributor ensure scheme suitability?","o":["RBI Act","SEBI (PFUTP) Regulations","Income Tax Act","Companies Act"],"a":1,"e":"Fraudulent and Unfair Trade Practices regulations cover mis-selling."},{"t":"Do's and don'ts","q":"Which is a \"don't\" while selecting schemes?","o":["Checking taxes and loads","Chasing recent past performance","Understanding strategy","Sticking to asset allocation"],"a":1,"e":"Past performance may not be sustained."},{"t":"Do's and don'ts","q":"Before a redemption, a distributor should check:","o":["Capital gains tax and exit load implications","The NFO calendar","The fund manager's age","The AMC's share price"],"a":0,"e":"Both reduce investor returns."},{"t":"Do's and don'ts","q":"The workbook suggests a distributor's scheme selection methodology should be:","o":["Consistent and written down","Kept secret","Based on commission","Changed monthly"],"a":0,"e":"A written consistent approach keeps one on course."},{"t":"Hybrid selection","q":"Tax treatment of a balanced scheme depends on:","o":["The distributor","The investor's age","Its investment portfolio (equity share)","Its name"],"a":2,"e":"It may be equity or non-equity for tax."},{"t":"Debt selection","q":"Floater fund NAVs tend to be:","o":["Linked to equity","Highly volatile","Steady","Linked to gold"],"a":2,"e":"Floating coupons adjust with rates."},{"t":"Investor fit","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The first step in scheme selection is to set goals; the second is to assess the investor's situation and risk appetite.\nII. Risk appetite is assessed through the investor's need, ability and willingness to take risk.\nIII. An investor who is willing to take risk can use equity funds even for a short horizon.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: step 1 is setting goals, step 2 is assessing situation and risk appetite. II is true: risk appetite = need + ability + willingness. III is false: equity needs a long horizon even for an investor willing to take risk."},{"t":"Needs mapping","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Growth needs map to equity funds and income needs map to debt funds.\nStatement 2: Parking needs map to short duration funds.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: growth maps to equity and income to debt. Statement 2 is false: parking needs map to liquid funds."},{"t":"Age vs horizon","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["The time horizon to each goal is a better guide to fund choice than age alone.","Age is the most reliable guide to an investor's equity allocation.","The core portfolio is meant for tactical bets such as sector funds and gold.","The satellite portfolio is meant for long-term goals."],"a":0,"e":"Age alone is a poor guide; the time horizon to each goal should be used. Core holdings serve long-term goals, while sector funds and gold are tactical satellite holdings."},{"t":"Core-satellite","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. The core portfolio serves long-term goals through diversified, large cap and mid cap funds.\nII. A long gilt fund is a satellite holding when interest rates are expected to rise.\nIII. Gold is a satellite holding at times of high inflation or uncertainty.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: core = long-term goals (diversified, large, mid cap). II is false: long gilt is a satellite holding when rates are expected to come down. III is true: gold suits the satellite for high inflation or uncertainty."},{"t":"Overall risk","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Overall risk and return rise in the order liquid, hybrid, debt, equity.\nStatement 2: In the workbook illustration, a moderate investor holds 80% in the core portfolio and 20% in the satellite portfolio.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: the order is liquid, debt, hybrid, equity. Statement 2 is true: the workbook illustrates a moderate investor at 80% core / 20% satellite."},{"t":"Interest rate risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A liquid fund carries less interest rate risk than an ultra-short duration fund.\nII. A low duration fund carries more interest rate risk than a short duration fund.\nIII. An overnight fund carries the least interest rate risk among the debt fund categories in the hierarchy.","o":["Only I and III","Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true and III is true: interest rate risk rises overnight, liquid, ultra-short, low, short, medium, medium-long, long. II is false: a short duration fund carries more interest rate risk than a low duration fund."},{"t":"Credit risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["Gilt funds carry the lowest credit risk.","Credit risk funds carry the highest credit risk.","Credit risk funds carry more credit risk than banking & PSU funds.","Gilt funds carry more credit risk than corporate bond funds."],"a":3,"e":"Credit risk rises gilt, banking & PSU, corporate bond, credit risk fund; so gilt funds carry less credit risk than corporate bond funds, and the other three statements are true."},{"t":"Equity risk","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Among equity categories, large cap funds carry the lowest risk and small cap funds the highest.\nStatement 2: Large & mid cap funds are less risky than multi cap funds.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: equity risk falls in the order small, mid, multi, large & mid, large, so large cap is lowest, small cap is highest, and large & mid is below multi cap."},{"t":"Concentration risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Focused funds are riskier than diversified funds but less risky than sector funds.\nII. Focused funds carry more concentration risk than thematic funds.\nIII. Thematic funds such as MNC, PSU and infrastructure funds sit between diversified and sector funds in risk.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: focused funds sit between diversified and sector funds. II is false: concentration risk rises diversified, focused, thematic, sector, so thematic funds carry more concentration risk than focused funds. III is true: thematic funds (MNC, PSU, infra) sit between diversified and sector."},{"t":"Hybrid risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Arbitrage funds are the lowest-risk hybrid category.\nII. Equity savings funds are riskier than conservative hybrid funds.\nIII. Balanced hybrid funds are riskier than aggressive hybrid funds.","o":["Only II","Only III","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I and II are true: hybrid risk rises arbitrage, conservative, equity savings, dynamic asset allocation, multi-asset, balanced, aggressive. III is false: aggressive hybrid funds are the riskiest, above balanced."},{"t":"Hybrid risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Dynamic asset allocation funds are less risky than equity savings funds.","Multi-asset funds are riskier than dynamic asset allocation funds.","Balanced hybrid funds are less risky than dynamic asset allocation funds.","Multi-asset funds are riskier than balanced hybrid funds."],"a":1,"e":"In the hybrid hierarchy (arbitrage, conservative, equity savings, dynamic asset allocation, multi-asset, balanced, aggressive) multi-asset sits above dynamic asset allocation. Dynamic asset allocation is above equity savings, balanced is above dynamic asset allocation, and balanced is above multi-asset, so the other three are false."},{"t":"Labelling, IDCW","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Product labelling, introduced in 2013, added a disclaimer asking investors to consult a financial adviser if unsure about suitability.\nStatement 2: For regular income, the IDCW option is better than an SWP.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: 2013 product labelling added a consult-your-adviser disclaimer. Statement 2 is false: for regular income an SWP is better than IDCW."},{"t":"Active vs passive","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A low-risk equity investor may choose an index fund or a value fund.\nII. An investor willing to take extra risk for extra return may choose a growth fund.\nIII. Active funds have lower costs than passive funds.","o":["Only I and II","Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: index or value funds suit low-risk equity investors. II is true: growth funds are for extra risk and extra return. III is false: active funds have higher cost and risk; passive funds cost less."},{"t":"Open vs active","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["Active funds must beat their benchmark to justify being chosen.","Large cap funds carry less risk than mid cap funds.","Open-ended funds offer liquidity at NAV less load.","Open-ended funds need not hold any cash, so their returns are not diluted."],"a":3,"e":"Open-ended funds must hold some cash, which dilutes returns, so the last statement is wrong. Active funds must beat the benchmark to justify the choice, large cap funds are less risky than mid cap funds, and open-ended funds offer liquidity at NAV less load."},{"t":"Close-ended","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Units of a close-ended scheme can be exited only through the stock exchange, often at a discount to NAV.\nII. The discount on close-ended units on the exchange is capped by regulation.\nIII. The market price of close-ended units converges to NAV near maturity.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and II"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: exit is only via the exchange, often at a discount. II is false: the discount has no limit. III is true: the price converges to NAV near maturity."},{"t":"Open vs close","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Close-ended funds can invest in illiquid or long-gestation stocks.\nStatement 2: Open-ended funds face flow pressure from investor inflows and outflows.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: close-ended funds can hold illiquid or long-gestation stocks, and open-ended funds face flow pressure."},{"t":"Market cycles","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Mid and small cap funds tend to shine in recoveries, when large caps turn expensive.\nII. Many mid and small cap stocks fail in downturns.\nIII. Value funds outperform mainly in bull markets.","o":["Only II","Only I and III","I, II and III","Only I and II"],"a":3,"e":"I and II are true: mid/small caps shine in recoveries but many fail in downturns. III is false: growth outperforms in bull markets; value outperforms in falling markets or over a long period."},{"t":"Satellite funds","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. International fund returns come from foreign equity plus currency movement.\nII. International funds suit the core portfolio.\nIII. Sector funds depend on timing and so belong in the satellite portfolio.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: international funds = foreign equity + currency. II is false: international funds suit the satellite portfolio. III is true: sector funds depend on timing and belong in the satellite."},{"t":"FMP","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: An FMP can be exited before maturity by redeeming units with the AMC.\nStatement 2: In an FMP, the investor's horizon must match the scheme's maturity.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: exit from an FMP before maturity is only via the exchange. Statement 2 is true: the horizon must match the FMP's maturity."},{"t":"Debt fund choice","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Short duration funds (1-3 years) are used when interest rates are expected to rise.\nII. Liquid funds suit parking money for up to 182 days.\nIII. Using a liquid fund for longer than the parking need wastes return.","o":["Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III","Only I and III"],"a":3,"e":"I is true: short duration funds suit an expected rate rise. II is false: liquid funds are for parking up to 91 days. III is true: using a liquid fund longer wastes return."},{"t":"Hybrid evaluation","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Hybrid funds should be judged on the quality of the equity portion and the credit/duration of the debt portion separately.\nStatement 2: The word 'conservative' in a conservative hybrid fund refers to both its equity and debt allocation.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: equity quality and debt credit/duration are judged separately. Statement 2 is false: 'conservative' refers only to the equity allocation."},{"t":"Within category","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A 'value' fund whose portfolio is full of expensive front-line stocks is not true to label.\nII. Performance should be judged on consistent outperformance and downside protection, against benchmark and peers, over rolling periods.\nIII. For equity funds, a look-back period of 3 years is sufficient.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: the portfolio must be true to label. II is true: that is the workbook's performance test. III is false: equity funds need a look-back of at least 5 years."},{"t":"Fund age, size","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Fund age matters more for large cap funds than for multi cap funds.\nStatement 2: A large fund size helps large-cap funds but can hurt mid-cap or sector funds that have a limited universe of stocks.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: age matters most where scheme returns diverge, e.g. multi cap more than large cap. Statement 2 is true: size helps large-cap funds and hurts mid-cap or sector funds."},{"t":"Portfolio turnover","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Portfolio turnover uses the lower of purchases or sales as the numerator.\nII. A turnover of 200% implies an average holding period of about 6 months.\nIII. An average holding period of 6 months is too short for a momentum-style fund.","o":["Only I and II","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: turnover = lower of purchases or sales / average net assets. II is true: 200% turnover means about 6 months' holding. III is false: 6 months is fine for momentum but too short for a value style."},{"t":"Ratings and costs","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["Costs matter most in debt funds and index funds.","Star ratings reflect past performance and have no predictive power.","In small categories, investors should target the top 10-15% of funds.","This quarter's best fund is rarely next quarter's best."],"a":2,"e":"In small categories the target is the top 3-5 funds; top 10-15% applies to big categories. The other three statements are true."}]},"stmt":{"id":"stmt","label":"Statement drill · all chapters","short":"Statements","kind":"drill","weight":0,"qs":[{"t":"Ch 4 · Unclaimed amounts","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Unclaimed money claimed within 3 years is paid at the prevailing NAV.\nII. Unclaimed money claimed after 3 years is paid at the NAV at the end of the third year.\nIII. Unclaimed amounts are deployed in a separate liquid plan that can charge up to 1.00% p.a.","o":["Only III","Only II and III","Only I and III","None of the above"],"a":0,"e":"I is true and II is true, as stated. III is false: the separate liquid plan for unclaimed amounts can charge at most 0.50% p.a., with no exit load."},{"t":"Ch 6 · MFCentral","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. MFCentral was built by CAMS and KFintech under a SEBI mandate.\nII. MFCentral gives a consolidated view of demat and non-demat folios.\nIII. MFCentral allows family portfolio tracking.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"e":"I is true: CAMS and KFintech built MFCentral under SEBI mandate. II is true: it consolidates demat and non-demat folios. III is true: it supports family portfolio tracking."},{"t":"Ch 7 · Dilution","y":"concept","q":"Mark-to-market pricing protects existing investors from dilution. If a scheme issued new units at a price below NAV, the effect on existing investors would be that:","o":["Their NAV rises","Their NAV is unchanged because net assets also rise","Their NAV falls","Only the new investors' NAV falls"],"a":2,"e":"Issuing units below NAV lowers the NAV for existing investors (and redeeming below NAV raises it); pricing at NAV prevents this dilution."},{"t":"Ch 3 · AMC board","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: An AMC's net worth must be at least Rs 50 crore and maintained continuously.\nStatement 2: At least 50% of an AMC's directors must not be associates of the sponsor, its subsidiaries or the trustees.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: AMC net worth of at least Rs 50 crore must be maintained continuously (the sponsor's responsibility), and at least 50% of AMC directors must be non-associates."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Cut-off purchase","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. For equity scheme purchases, the cut-off is 3:00 pm and the NAV applied is of the day the funds are available for use.\nII. For liquid scheme purchases, the cut-off is 1:30 pm.\nIII. Cut-off timings also apply to NFO applications.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: equity and debt (non-liquid) purchases have a 3:00 pm cut-off and get the NAV of the day funds are available, for any amount. II is true: liquid and overnight purchases have a 1:30 pm cut-off. III is false: cut-offs don't apply to NFOs (or international schemes)."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Asset classification","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements about the workbook's classification of investment products (Table 1.1):\nStatement 1: REITs and InvITs are classified under Equity.\nStatement 2: Coins, art and stamps are classified under Others.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: REITs, InvITs and REMF are classified under Real Estate. Statement 2 is true: coins, art and stamps fall under Others."},{"t":"Ch 7 · Valuation policy","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. If the fair valuation principles conflict with the Valuation Guidelines, the principles prevail.\nII. The valuation policy must be reviewed by an independent auditor at least once every two years.\nIII. The valuation policy is disclosed in the SAI and on the website.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the 10 principles prevail over the Valuation Guidelines. II is false: the policy is reviewed by an independent auditor at least once a year. III is true: it is disclosed in the SAI and on the website."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Annualised SD","y":"concept","q":"An SD computed from monthly returns is annualised by multiplying it by:","o":["12","√12","√52","√252"],"a":1,"e":"Annualise SD by ×√12 for monthly, ×√52 for weekly and ×√252 for daily data."},{"t":"Ch 11 · TRI","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. The benchmark is chosen by the AMC with the trustees and is stated in the SID.\nII. Scheme performance has been compared with the Total Return Index (TRI) since 1 Feb 2018.\nIII. For periods before the TRI existed, performance is compared with the Price Return Index (PRI) alone.","o":["Only I","Only III","Only II and III","Only I and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the AMC with the trustees chooses the benchmark and states it in the SID. II is true: TRI replaced PRI from 1 Feb 2018. III is false: periods before TRI existed use a composite CAGR (PRI, then TRI)."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Book value","y":"concept","q":"In the P/BV ratio, the book value per share used in the denominator is:","o":["PAT ÷ number of shares","Market price × number of shares","Dividend per share ÷ market price","Net worth ÷ number of shares"],"a":3,"e":"Book value per share = net worth ÷ shares, and P/BV = price ÷ BV. PAT ÷ shares is EPS, and DPS ÷ price is dividend yield."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Benchmark choice","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A scheme's benchmark must match its investment objective, asset allocation and investment strategy.\nII. A scheme's past returns can be a basis for choosing its benchmark.\nIII. The benchmark of an index fund is the index it tracks.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: the benchmark must match objective, asset allocation and strategy. II is false: past returns are never a basis for choosing a benchmark. III is true: an index fund is benchmarked to the index it tracks."},{"t":"Ch 7 · Non-traded securities","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. An equity share with no trade for 30 days is treated as non-traded.\nII. Non-traded equity is valued by capitalisation of earnings, with an illiquidity premium added.\nIII. Warrants are valued as share value minus exercise price minus a discount.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: no trade for 30 days makes a security non-traded. II is false: an illiquidity discount is deducted, not a premium added. III is true: warrants = share value − exercise price − discount."},{"t":"Ch 5 · Offer and acceptance","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. The scheme documents are the offer.\nII. The signed application form with payment is the acceptance.\nIII. The contract is complete when the investor's payment is realised.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I and II are true: documents are the offer, and the signed form plus payment is the acceptance. III is false: the contract completes on allotment of units."},{"t":"Ch 8 · Bonus stripping","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Under bonus stripping rules, a loss is disallowed if units were bought within 9 months before the record date and sold within 3 months after it.\nStatement 2: A loss disallowed under bonus stripping becomes the cost of the bonus units.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: the window is bought up to 3 months before the record date and sold up to 9 months after. Statement 2 is true: the disallowed loss becomes the cost of the bonus units."},{"t":"Ch 6 · Exchange platforms","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. The exchange platforms for mutual fund transactions are NSE NMF-II and BSE StAR MF.\nII. Depository participants can process both purchases and redemptions on exchange platforms.\nIII. Exchange platforms replace the RTA and give a settlement guarantee.","o":["Only II","Only III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: the platforms are NSE NMF-II and BSE StAR MF. II is false: DPs can only process redemptions. III is false: exchanges are an order-routing system, don't replace RTAs and give no settlement guarantee (the AMC is responsible)."},{"t":"Ch 7 · TER slabs","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements about open-ended scheme TER slabs is/are TRUE?\nI. On the first Rs 500 cr, an equity scheme can charge up to 2.10%.\nII. On the first Rs 500 cr, a non-equity scheme can charge up to 1.85%.\nIII. On the next Rs 250 cr, the equity limit is 1.60%.","o":["Only I and II","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I and II are true: the first Rs 500 cr slab is 2.10% equity and 1.85% other. III is false: the next Rs 250 cr slab is 1.90% equity (1.65% other); 1.60% applies to the following Rs 1,250 cr."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Securitisation","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. In securitisation, the originator sells receivables to an SPV (trust), which issues PTCs.\nII. Credit enhancement can allow PTCs to be rated above the originator.\nIII. PTC investors have recourse to the originator if the pool defaults.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: receivables go to an SPV (trust) that issues PTCs. II is true: credit enhancement lets PTCs be rated above the originator. III is false: PTCs are non-recourse."},{"t":"Ch 5 · Factsheet","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["A factsheet is mandatory under SEBI rules and must be published every month","Publishing the factsheet monthly is an AMFI best practice","SEBI's disclosure rules still apply to a factsheet","A factsheet typically shows PE, beta, standard deviation and Sharpe ratio"],"a":0,"e":"A is not correct: the factsheet is voluntary, not mandatory. B, C and D are correct: monthly publication is an AMFI best practice, SEBI disclosure rules apply, and it shows PE, beta, SD and Sharpe."},{"t":"Ch 3 · Service providers","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A fund accountant does not need SEBI registration.\nII. Appointment of an RTA by a mutual fund is mandatory.\nIII. The scheme auditor is appointed by the trustees and must be different from the AMC's auditor.","o":["Only I","Only III","Only II","Only I and II"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: a fund accountant needs no SEBI registration and can be in-house. II is false: an RTA must register with SEBI but appointing one is optional. III is true: the scheme auditor is appointed by trustees and differs from the AMC auditor."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Alpha","y":"concept","q":"Alpha measures the difference between:","o":["The scheme's actual return and the return expected from its beta (optimal return)","The scheme's return and the risk-free T-bill return","The scheme's return and the average return of its peers","The benchmark's TRI return and its PRI return"],"a":0,"e":"Alpha = actual return minus the beta-expected (optimal) return; a positive alpha means outperformance. Scheme return minus T-bill return is the risk premium."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Market cycles","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Mid and small cap funds tend to shine in recoveries, when large caps turn expensive.\nII. Many mid and small cap stocks fail in downturns.\nIII. Value funds outperform mainly in bull markets.","o":["Only II","Only I and III","I, II and III","Only I and II"],"a":3,"e":"I and II are true: mid/small caps shine in recoveries but many fail in downturns. III is false: growth outperforms in bull markets; value outperforms in falling markets or over a long period."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Satellite funds","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. International fund returns come from foreign equity plus currency movement.\nII. International funds suit the core portfolio.\nIII. Sector funds depend on timing and so belong in the satellite portfolio.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: international funds = foreign equity + currency. II is false: international funds suit the satellite portfolio. III is true: sector funds depend on timing and belong in the satellite."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Third-party payment","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["A parent or related person may pay for a minor's investment up to Rs 50,000 per purchase or SIP instalment.","An employer may pay for employees' investments through payroll deductions.","A custodian may make payment on behalf of an FPI.","A spouse may pay for the other spouse's investment up to Rs 50,000 per purchase."],"a":3,"e":"There are only three third-party payment exceptions: a parent or related person for a minor up to Rs 50,000 per purchase or SIP instalment, an employer through payroll, and a custodian for an FPI. A spouse paying is not an exception."},{"t":"Ch 9 · E-wallet and cards","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Credit cards are not an allowed mode of payment for mutual fund purchases.\nII. Investments through e-wallet and cash together are capped at Rs 50,000 per investor per mutual fund per financial year.\nIII. E-wallet money loaded through a credit card can be used to buy mutual fund units.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: credit cards are not allowed. II is true: the Rs 50,000 umbrella limit covers e-wallet and cash per investor per MF per FY. III is false: e-wallet money is accepted only if loaded via cash, debit card or net banking."},{"t":"Ch 7 · IDCW process","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The AMC Board decides the IDCW amount and the record date.\nII. A public notice of the IDCW must be issued within 1 day of the decision.\nIII. The record date is 5 calendar days from the issue of the notice.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is false: the trustees decide the amount and record date. II is true: notice within 1 day of the decision. III is true: record date is 5 calendar days from the notice."},{"t":"Ch 7 · Distributable surplus","y":"concept","q":"While computing the distributable surplus from which IDCW can be paid, valuation gains and valuation losses are treated as follows:","o":["Valuation gains are ignored; valuation losses are deducted","Both valuation gains and losses are ignored","Valuation gains are added; valuation losses are deducted","Valuation gains are added; valuation losses are ignored"],"a":0,"e":"IDCW is paid only from distributable surplus, which ignores valuation gains but deducts valuation losses."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Tracking error","y":"concept","q":"Tracking error is the standard deviation of:","o":["The scheme's daily NAV","The benchmark's returns","The scheme's returns","The scheme's excess returns over its benchmark"],"a":3,"e":"Tracking error = SD of excess returns. It shows how closely an index fund tracks its index (ideally zero) and how consistently an active fund beats its benchmark (lower = more consistent)."},{"t":"Ch 9 · NFO allotment","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Units must be allotted, or money refunded, within 5 business days of the NFO closing.\nStatement 2: If the refund is delayed, interest at 15% p.a. is paid out of the scheme.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: allotment or refund is due within 5 business days of NFO close. Statement 2 is false: the 15% p.a. late-refund interest is paid by the AMC, not the scheme."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Equity analysis","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Technical analysis is best suited to long-term investing.\nII. Dividend yield tends to fall in bull markets and rise in bear markets.\nIII. Growth stocks typically have a high P/E and a low dividend yield.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":3,"e":"I is false: technical analysis suits the short term and timing; fundamental analysis suits the long term. II is true: dividend yield falls in bull markets and rises in bear markets. III is true: growth stocks have high P/E and PEG and low dividend yield."},{"t":"Ch 6 · Due diligence","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A distributor with presence in more than 20 locations is covered by the commission disclosure and due diligence requirements.\nStatement 2: A distributor earning more than Rs 50 lakh commission a year from a single AMC is covered by the commission disclosure and due diligence requirements.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 1 is true: more than 20 locations is a trigger. Statement 2 is true: more than Rs 50 lakh from one AMC is a trigger; any one of the four conditions is enough."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Investor returns","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["Investor return always equals scheme return since both use the same NAV","Advertised scheme returns ignore loads","Returns for holding periods under 1 year are absolute; over 1 year, CAGR","Rolling returns remove start/end-date bias"],"a":0,"e":"The first is wrong: investor return differs from scheme return because of loads, taxes and cash-flow timing. The others are correct: advertised returns ignore loads; <1 year absolute, >1 year CAGR; rolling returns remove start/end-date bias."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Risk measures","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Credit risk is measured mainly through standard deviation and beta.\nStatement 2: The three strategies for handling risk are to avoid it, take a position, or diversify.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: credit risk is measured by credit rating and spreads; SD and beta measure volatility. Statement 2 is true: avoid, take a position, or diversify."},{"t":"Ch 2 · Market cap","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Large cap companies are the 1st to 100th companies by full market capitalisation.\nII. Mid cap companies are the 101st to 200th companies by full market capitalisation.\nIII. Small cap companies are the 251st company onwards by full market capitalisation.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: large cap = 1-100. II is false: mid cap = 101-250. III is true: small cap = 251 onwards."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Sharpe vs Treynor","y":"concept","q":"The Sharpe ratio and the Treynor ratio:","o":["Share the numerator (scheme return minus benchmark return) and differ in the denominator: Sharpe uses SD, Treynor uses beta","Share the denominator (SD) and differ in the numerator: Sharpe uses the T-bill return, Treynor uses the benchmark return","Share the numerator (scheme return minus T-bill return) and differ in the denominator: Sharpe uses SD, Treynor uses beta","Share the numerator (scheme return minus T-bill return) and differ in the denominator: Sharpe uses beta, Treynor uses SD"],"a":2,"e":"Both have the risk premium (scheme return minus risk-free T-bill return) as numerator. Sharpe divides by SD (total risk); Treynor divides by beta (systematic risk)."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Service timelines","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The half-yearly portfolio statement must be disclosed within 10 days of the end of the half-year.\nStatement 2: The annual report must be provided within 4 months.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: the portfolio statement is due within 10 days of the half-year and the annual report within 4 months."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Holders and PoA","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["In a joint folio, the first holder receives all benefits such as dividends and redemption proceeds.","A PoA holder can do everything on the investor's behalf, including making a nomination.","Once a PoA is granted, the grantor can no longer operate the folio.","Joint holders can be added to an existing folio on request."],"a":0,"e":"The first holder gets all benefits (dividends, redemptions, tax). A PoA holder can do everything except nominate, and the grantor can still operate; joint holders cannot be added or removed except deletion on death."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Relative return","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Relative return, measured against a benchmark or peers, ignores risk.\nStatement 2: AMCs and trustees review a scheme's relative returns periodically.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 1 is true: relative return vs benchmark or peers ignores risk. Statement 2 is true: AMCs and trustees review relative returns periodically."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Close-ended","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Units of a close-ended scheme can be exited only through the stock exchange, often at a discount to NAV.\nII. The discount on close-ended units on the exchange is capped by regulation.\nIII. The market price of close-ended units converges to NAV near maturity.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and II"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: exit is only via the exchange, often at a discount. II is false: the discount has no limit. III is true: the price converges to NAV near maturity."},{"t":"Ch 9 · NFO period","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. An NFO must remain open for at least 3 working days.\nII. An ELSS NFO is exempt from the maximum NFO period of 15 days.\nIII. A close-ended scheme's NFO announces an opening date, a closing date and a re-opening date.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the minimum NFO period is 3 working days. II is true: the 15-day maximum does not apply to ELSS. III is false: only open-ended schemes have a re-opening date; a close-ended scheme has none."},{"t":"Ch 2 · Unit capital","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Unit capital of a scheme equals the number of units multiplied by the NAV.\nStatement 2: AUM equals the NAV multiplied by the number of units outstanding.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: unit capital = units x face value (typically Rs 10). Statement 2 is true: AUM = NAV x units outstanding, and AMCs are ranked by AUM."},{"t":"Ch 7 · Equity valuation","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A traded equity share is valued at its last closing price on the principal stock exchange.\nStatement 2: If the share is not traded on the valuation day, the price of the earliest previous day may be used provided it is within 60 days.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: traded equity uses the last closing price on the principal exchange. Statement 2 is false: another exchange, or the earliest previous day within 30 days (not 60), is used."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Regulators","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. PFRDA regulates pension, and all four financial-sector regulators come under the Ministry of Finance.\nII. AMFI regulates mutual funds jointly with SEBI.\nIII. \"Securities\" are defined in Section 2(h) of the Securities Contracts (Regulation) Act, 1956.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: RBI, SEBI, IRDAI and PFRDA (pension) all sit under the Ministry of Finance. II is false: SEBI regulates mutual funds; AMFI does not. III is true: securities are defined in Sec 2(h) of the SCRA, 1956."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Behavioural biases","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Taking a decision first and then looking for evidence to support it is confirmation bias.\nStatement 2: Feeling a Rs 5,000 loss more sharply than a Rs 5,000 gain is called recency bias.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: deciding first and seeking proof later is confirmation bias. Statement 2 is false: that is loss aversion (Kahneman and Tversky)."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Beta","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Beta comes from the CAPM, and the diversified market index has a beta of zero.\nII. A scheme with a beta of 1.2 is expected to move 12% when the market moves 10%.\nIII. G-secs and cash carry no credit risk.","o":["Only I and II","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is false: beta comes from the CAPM, but the diversified index has a beta of 1, not zero. II is true: beta 1.2 moves 12% for a 10% market move. III is true: G-secs and cash have no credit risk."},{"t":"Ch 6 · Advice and nomination","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. There are only two transaction types for an MFD: advisory and execution only.\nII. An MFD must register as an RIA to give advice incidental to distribution.\nIII. The nominee of a deceased distributor must hold an ARN to receive trail commission.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only II and III","Only I and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: only advisory and execution-only transactions exist, with no third category. II is false: advice incidental to distribution needs no RIA registration. III is false: the nominee need not hold an ARN and receives commission as trustee for the legal heirs."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Investor fit","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The first step in scheme selection is to set goals; the second is to assess the investor's situation and risk appetite.\nII. Risk appetite is assessed through the investor's need, ability and willingness to take risk.\nIII. An investor who is willing to take risk can use equity funds even for a short horizon.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: step 1 is setting goals, step 2 is assessing situation and risk appetite. II is true: risk appetite = need + ability + willingness. III is false: equity needs a long horizon even for an investor willing to take risk."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Advertisement code","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A mutual fund advertisement may show the scheme's ranking if the source is disclosed.\nII. In audio-visual ads, the standard warning must appear as visual plus voice-over for at least 5 seconds.\nIII. A vernacular advertisement may carry the standard warning in English.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is false: rankings are not allowed in MF ads. II is true: the 14-word warning runs visually and as voice-over for at least 5 seconds. III is false: vernacular ads must carry the warning in the vernacular."},{"t":"Ch 2 · Credit quality","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: A corporate bond fund must invest at least 80% in papers rated AA+ and above.\nStatement 2: A credit risk fund must invest at least 65% in papers rated AA+ and below.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: corporate bond = 80% in AA+ and above. Statement 2 is false: a credit risk fund puts 65% in AA and below, with AA+ excluded."},{"t":"Ch 11 · I-Bex","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Mi-Bex is the I-Bex sub-index for maturities of more than 7 years.\nStatement 2: Si-Bex is the I-Bex sub-index for maturities of 1 to 3 years.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: Mi-Bex covers 3–7 years; Li-Bex covers more than 7 years. Statement 2 is true: Si-Bex covers 1–3 years."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Risk profile","y":"concept","q":"In an investor's risk profile, the ABILITY to take risk depends on:","o":["The investor's psychology","The investor's finances and investment horizon","The investor's need for returns to reach a goal","The recent performance of the market"],"a":1,"e":"Ability to take risk depends on finances and horizon. Willingness depends on psychology, and need is a separate component of the risk profile."},{"t":"Ch 9 · CAS","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A Consolidated Account Statement (CAS) is sent monthly if there is any transaction in that month.\nII. If there is no transaction, a CAS is sent half-yearly.\nIII. Investors are matched across mutual funds for the CAS on the basis of their Aadhaar number.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and II","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I and II are true: CAS is monthly when there is a transaction and half-yearly when there is none. III is false: investors are matched by PAN."},{"t":"Ch 5 · Update timelines","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["The SAI is updated within 3 months of the end of the financial year","The KIM is updated at least once every half-year, within 1 month","After its first update, the SID is updated within 1 month of each half-year","The SAI is updated once in 3 years, and material changes wait for that update"],"a":3,"e":"A, B and C are correct. D is not correct: the SAI is updated within 3 months of each FY end, and material changes are updated on an ongoing basis."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Nomination","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A folio can have up to 10 nominees.\nII. A minor can be appointed as a nominee.\nIII. A company can be appointed as a nominee.","o":["Only III","Only II","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: up to 10 nominees per folio. II is true: a nominee can be an individual, even a minor or NRI. III is false: a company cannot be a nominee."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Grievances","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["An AMC must resolve investor complaints within 21 calendar days","SCORES is SEBI's online complaints redressal system","Physical complaints received are scanned and uploaded to SCORES","The status of investor complaints is disclosed only in the AMC's annual report"],"a":3,"e":"A, B and C are correct. D is not correct: complaint status is disclosed in the annual report, on the mutual fund's website and on the AMFI website."},{"t":"Ch 7 · Exit load","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Exit load can differ according to the amount invested.\nII. Changes in exit load apply only prospectively.\nIII. Exit load is not charged on bonus units or IDCW-reinvestment units.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is false: exit load makes no distinction by amount. II is true: changes apply only prospectively. III is true: no exit load on bonus or IDCW-reinvestment units."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Application form","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The address in the KYC records overrides the address given on the application form.\nII. Bank details of the first holder are optional if the investor gives proof of a different payout account.\nIII. A bank passbook or statement given as proof of a payout account must not be more than 3 months old.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the KYC address overrides the form address. II is false: bank details of the first holder are mandatory. III is true: a passbook or statement used as proof must be not more than 3 months old."},{"t":"Ch 5 · SID vs SAI","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The SID contains details of one scheme.\nII. Each scheme of a mutual fund has its own separate SAI.\nIII. Legally, the SAI is part of the SID.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the SID covers one scheme. II is false: one SAI serves all schemes of the mutual fund. III is true: legally the SAI is part of the SID."},{"t":"Ch 7 · IDCW notice","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: No public notice is needed for daily-to-monthly IDCW payouts in equity schemes.\nStatement 2: Interest on a late IDCW payment runs from the date the public notice was issued.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":1,"e":"Statement 1 is false: the no-notice relief for daily-to-monthly payouts applies to liquid and debt schemes. Statement 2 is false: interest on late payment runs from the record date."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Age vs horizon","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["The time horizon to each goal is a better guide to fund choice than age alone.","Age is the most reliable guide to an investor's equity allocation.","The core portfolio is meant for tactical bets such as sector funds and gold.","The satellite portfolio is meant for long-term goals."],"a":0,"e":"Age alone is a poor guide; the time horizon to each goal should be used. Core holdings serve long-term goals, while sector funds and gold are tactical satellite holdings."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Equity risk","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Among equity categories, large cap funds carry the lowest risk and small cap funds the highest.\nStatement 2: Large & mid cap funds are less risky than multi cap funds.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: equity risk falls in the order small, mid, multi, large & mid, large, so large cap is lowest, small cap is highest, and large & mid is below multi cap."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Hybrid risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Arbitrage funds are the lowest-risk hybrid category.\nII. Equity savings funds are riskier than conservative hybrid funds.\nIII. Balanced hybrid funds are riskier than aggressive hybrid funds.","o":["Only II","Only III","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I and II are true: hybrid risk rises arbitrage, conservative, equity savings, dynamic asset allocation, multi-asset, balanced, aggressive. III is false: aggressive hybrid funds are the riskiest, above balanced."},{"t":"Ch 6 · Certification and KYD","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A distributor aged 50 or more, or with at least 10 years' experience, as of 31 May 2010 could be exempted from the NISM V-A exam through CPE.\nStatement 2: For KYD, the distributor's left thumb impression is captured at a CAMS PoS.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: the CPE exemption applies at age 50+ or 10+ years' experience as of 31 May 2010. Statement 2 is false: KYD captures the right index finger biometric at a CAMS PoS."},{"t":"Ch 9 · IDCW options","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Under the IDCW payout option, the number of units held does not change.\nII. Under the IDCW reinvestment option, the new units are allotted at the cum-dividend NAV.\nIII. The IDCW amount reinvested is still taxed at the investor's slab rate in the year it is received.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: payout sends cash to the bank and units stay unchanged. II is false: reinvestment is at the ex-dividend NAV. III is true: the reinvested IDCW is taxed at slab in the year received."},{"t":"Ch 8 · Stamp duty","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Stamp duty of 0.005% applies on issue of units, including SIP, STP, switch-in and IDCW reinvestment.","Stamp duty on transfer of units is 0.005%.","Units are allotted on the investment amount before deducting stamp duty.","Stamp duty on mutual fund units applies from 1 April 2020."],"a":0,"e":"Stamp duty is 0.005% on issue and 0.015% on transfer, applies from 1 July 2020, and units are allotted on the amount after stamp duty."},{"t":"Ch 9 · DTP and triggers","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["A dividend transfer plan requires the investor to be in the IDCW option.","Under a dividend transfer plan, the dividend is invested in the target scheme on the record date itself.","Triggers such as stop-loss or appreciation triggers are one-time in nature.","A minor's SIP is registered only till the minor attains majority."],"a":1,"e":"Under a DTP the dividend is invested on the next business day after the record date, so the second statement is false. A DTP needs the IDCW option, triggers are one-time, and a minor's SIP is registered only till majority."},{"t":"Ch 5 · Risk-o-meter review","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The risk-o-meter of a scheme is reviewed quarterly.\nII. A change in the risk-o-meter is communicated by a notice-cum-addendum and email/SMS.\nIII. The scheme's risk-o-meter is shown together with the benchmark's risk-o-meter.","o":["Only I","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is false: the risk-o-meter is reviewed monthly. II is true. III is true: scheme and benchmark risk-o-meters are shown together."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Open vs close","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Close-ended funds can invest in illiquid or long-gestation stocks.\nStatement 2: Open-ended funds face flow pressure from investor inflows and outflows.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: close-ended funds can hold illiquid or long-gestation stocks, and open-ended funds face flow pressure."},{"t":"Ch 8 · Equity-oriented","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: A fund is equity-oriented for tax if it invests more than 65% in listed Indian equity.\nStatement 2: A fund of funds investing in equity mutual funds is treated as equity-oriented for tax.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: equity-oriented = >65% in listed Indian equity. Statement 2 is false: a FoF investing in equity MFs is non-equity for tax."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Performance in ads","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A scheme that is less than 6 months old cannot show performance in an advertisement.\nII. A scheme between 6 and 12 months old shows its CAGR since inception.\nIII. A scheme older than 3 years shows CAGR for 1, 3, 5 years and since inception, plus point-to-point returns on Rs 10,000.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: no performance under 6 months. II is false: a 6 to 12 month old scheme shows a simple annualised 6-month return. III is true: that is the rule for schemes over 3 years."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Equity features","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Equity and bonds exist only in financial form.\nStatement 2: Listed equity is liquid but is not suitable for short-term needs.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: equity and bonds exist only in financial form, and listed equity, though liquid, is unfit for short-term needs."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Cheques and NRI","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: An NRI investing on a non-repatriable basis can pay only from an NRE account.\nStatement 2: A cheque more than 3 months old is stale and is not accepted.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: non-repatriable investments can also be paid from an NRO account (repatriable ones come from NRE/FCNR). Statement 2 is true: a cheque older than 3 months is stale and not accepted."},{"t":"Ch 6 · Bank distribution","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: PSU banks were the first banks to enter mutual fund distribution.\nStatement 2: Distribution companies and banks are institutional distributors.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: multinational banks entered first; PSU banks give non-urban reach. Statement 2 is true: distribution companies and banks are institutional distributors."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Asset features","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Commodity derivatives are not treated as investments because they are leveraged and short-term.\nII. An investor who buys a bond at issue and holds it to maturity earns a capital gain.\nIII. In real estate, location is the key factor.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and II"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: commodity derivatives are leveraged and short-term, so they are not investments. II is false: a bond bought at issue and held to maturity gives no capital gain. III is true: location is key in real estate."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Codes of conduct","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The Fourth Schedule of the MF Regulations 2026 is SEBI's code of conduct for AMCs and trustees.\nII. The AMFI Code of Ethics (ACE) sets higher and more explicit standards than the SEBI code.\nIII. AGNI is the set of AMFI guidelines and norms for AMCs.","o":["Only I and II","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: the Fourth Schedule covers AMCs and trustees. II is true: ACE sets higher, more explicit standards. III is false: AGNI stands for AMFI Guidelines and Norms for Intermediaries."},{"t":"Ch 7 · NAV formula","y":"concept","q":"In the NAV formula, the liabilities deducted from total assets to arrive at net assets are:","o":["All liabilities, including amounts owed to unitholders","Liabilities other than those to unitholders","Only short-term borrowings","Only accrued management fees"],"a":1,"e":"NAV = (total assets − liabilities other than to unitholders) ÷ units outstanding. Amounts owed to unitholders are what net assets represent, so they are not deducted."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Foreign investors","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A QFI must be from a FATF member country and a signatory to the IOSCO MMOU.\nStatement 2: An FPI investing through the indirect route holds its units in demat form.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: a QFI must be from a FATF member and an IOSCO MMOU signatory. Statement 2 is false: the direct route uses demat; the indirect route uses a Unit Confirmation Receipt (UCR)."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Systematic risk","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Inflation is an unsystematic risk that can be diversified away.\nStatement 2: Investors are rewarded only for bearing systematic risk.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: inflation is a systematic (market, non-diversifiable) risk; a strike is an example of unsystematic risk. Statement 2 is true: investors are rewarded only for systematic risk."},{"t":"Ch 7 · AT1 and AT2","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. From 1 April 2023, the deemed maturity of AT1 bonds is 100 years from the date of issue.\nII. The deemed maturity of AT2 bonds is also 100 years from the date of issue.\nIII. Close-ended debt schemes can invest in perpetual bonds.","o":["Only II","Only III","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":3,"e":"I is true: AT1 deemed maturity is 100 years from issue. II is false: AT2 deemed maturity is its contractual maturity. III is false: close-ended debt schemes can't buy perpetuals."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Information Ratio","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["The Information Ratio is the only mandatory risk-adjusted performance measure","Under the Jan 2025 circular, the Information Ratio is disclosed monthly in the factsheet","The Information Ratio is calculated using daily data","For an index fund, tracking error should ideally be zero"],"a":1,"e":"IR is the only mandatory risk-adjusted measure, uses daily data, and under the Jan 2025 circular is disclosed daily on the AMC and AMFI websites, not monthly in the factsheet. An index fund's tracking error is ideally zero."},{"t":"Ch 10 · CAGR","y":"concept","q":"In the formula CAGR = (Later value ÷ Initial value)^(1/n) − 1, the term 'n' is computed as:","o":["Number of days ÷ 360","Number of days ÷ 365","Number of NAV observations ÷ 52","Number of IDCW payouts during the period"],"a":1,"e":"n is the period in years, computed as days ÷ 365."},{"t":"Ch 5 · TER and BER","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["TER is the Base Expense Ratio plus brokerage, transaction cost and statutory levies including GST","The Base Expense Ratio is set under Regulation 22(9)(c)","The direct plan has a higher BER because of direct servicing costs","Ensuring that every expense is identified and disclosed in the offer document is the responsibility of the AMC alone"],"a":0,"e":"A is true. B is false: BER is under Regulation 66(7). C is false: the direct plan BER is lower, as there is no distribution cost or commission. D is false: both the trustees and the AMC must ensure every expense is identified and disclosed."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Labelling, IDCW","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Product labelling, introduced in 2013, added a disclaimer asking investors to consult a financial adviser if unsure about suitability.\nStatement 2: For regular income, the IDCW option is better than an SWP.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: 2013 product labelling added a consult-your-adviser disclaimer. Statement 2 is false: for regular income an SWP is better than IDCW."},{"t":"Ch 5 · Periodic reports","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Unaudited half-yearly results are hosted on the website within 2 months of 31 March and 30 September.\nStatement 2: The annual report is published within 4 months of the end of the financial year.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: half-yearly results go up within 1 month of 31 March / 30 September. Statement 2 is true: the annual report comes within 4 months of FY end."},{"t":"Ch 5 · KIM","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Every application form must carry a Key Information Memorandum.\nStatement 2: The KIM is a summary of the SID only.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: every application form must carry a KIM. Statement 2 is false: the KIM summarises both the SID and the SAI."},{"t":"Ch 6 · ARN and EUIN","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. The minimum age to obtain an ARN is 18.\nII. Employees of a distribution firm must each obtain a separate ARN.\nIII. ARN and EUIN applications are accepted online only from 1 Oct 2024.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and II"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: ARN minimum age is 18. II is false: employees get an EUIN under the firm's ARN, not a separate ARN. III is true: ARN/EUIN applications are online only from 1 Oct 2024."},{"t":"Ch 5 · SAI contents","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The SAI carries condensed financial information of schemes for the last 5 financial years.\nII. The SAI covers valuation norms.\nIII. All SAIs are available on AMFI's website.","o":["Only II","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is false: condensed financials cover the last 3 financial years. II is true: valuation norms are in the SAI. III is true: all SAIs are on AMFI's site."},{"t":"Ch 5 · SEBI's role","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: SEBI approves the SID before a scheme is launched.\nStatement 2: Draft scheme documents are on SEBI's website, while final documents are on AMFI's website and the mutual fund's own site.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: SEBI does not approve the documents; it only gives observations, so they are vetted, not approved. Statement 2 is true: drafts on SEBI's site, finals on AMFI's and the MF's site."},{"t":"Ch 7 · NAV","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Net assets used for NAV include unrealised gains.\nII. Under the accrual principle, income is counted only when it is actually received.\nIII. A scheme may borrow short term for liquidity for up to 6 months.","o":["Only I and II","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: net assets include unrealised gains. II is false: under accrual, income and expenses count when due, not when paid or received. III is true: short-term borrowing for liquidity is limited to 6 months."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Breach process","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A distributor accused of violating the code must give an explanation within 3 weeks.\nStatement 2: On a second proven violation, the ARN is cancelled and all AMCs are informed.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: explanation within 3 weeks, then a warning letter, and a second proven violation leads to ARN cancellation with all AMCs informed; an appeal to AMFI is allowed."},{"t":"Ch 8 · Holding periods","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["Equity-oriented funds become long-term after 12 months.","A hybrid fund with 35-65% domestic equity becomes long-term after 12 months.","Equity-oriented funds: STCG is taxed at 20% and LTCG at 12.5%.","Gains on debt-oriented funds are taxed at the marginal slab rate."],"a":1,"e":"A hybrid with 35-65% domestic equity becomes long-term after 24 months (taxed MSR / 12.5%); the other three statements match the workbook table."},{"t":"Ch 6 · Additional commission","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. For a lump sum by a new investor, the additional commission is 1% of the first application, up to Rs 2,000, if the investor stays at least 1 year.\nII. The additional commission is paid from the 2 bps set aside for investor education, with claw back.\nIII. A distributor can earn the additional commission twice for the same investor, once on a lump sum and once on a SIP.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: lump sum incentive is 1% of the first application, max Rs 2,000, if the investor stays 1 year or more. II is true: it is paid from the 2 bps investor education money with claw back. III is false: there is no dual incentive for the same investor."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Debt fund choice","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Short duration funds (1-3 years) are used when interest rates are expected to rise.\nII. Liquid funds suit parking money for up to 182 days.\nIII. Using a liquid fund for longer than the parking need wastes return.","o":["Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III","Only I and III"],"a":3,"e":"I is true: short duration funds suit an expected rate rise. II is false: liquid funds are for parking up to 91 days. III is true: using a liquid fund longer wastes return."},{"t":"Ch 5 · Attribute change process","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Under Regulation 22(9)(c), the written communication to unitholders may be sent by email or SMS.\nII. Unitholders get an exit at the prevailing NAV with a reduced exit load.\nIII. Details of the change are also put on the AMC website.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and II","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: email or SMS is allowed. II is false: the exit is at prevailing NAV with no exit load. III is true: details go on the AMC website."},{"t":"Ch 3 · Trustees","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["A person may be a trustee of two different mutual funds at the same time.","At least two-thirds of the trustee board must be independent directors.","Appointment of a trustee requires prior SEBI approval.","Trustees can be individuals forming a Board of Trustees, or a trustee company."],"a":0,"e":"A trustee cannot be a trustee of another mutual fund, so the first statement is wrong. Two-thirds independence, prior SEBI approval, and individual or company trustees are all correct."},{"t":"Ch 6 · MF Utilities","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Bank and nomination details registered with MF Utilities override those in the folio","An investor can hold only one Common Account Number (CAN)","A CAN can be obtained without completing KYC","MF Utilities requires a separate payment for each scheme invested in"],"a":0,"e":"MFU bank/nomination details override folio details. An investor can have multiple CANs, a CAN needs KYC, and MFU allows one form and one payment for multiple schemes."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Portfolio turnover","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Portfolio turnover uses the lower of purchases or sales as the numerator.\nII. A turnover of 200% implies an average holding period of about 6 months.\nIII. An average holding period of 6 months is too short for a momentum-style fund.","o":["Only I and II","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: turnover = lower of purchases or sales / average net assets. II is true: 200% turnover means about 6 months' holding. III is false: 6 months is fine for momentum but too short for a value style."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Crisis reforms","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Segregated portfolios were introduced after the 2018 credit crisis.\nII. After the 2008 crisis, premature redemption in FMPs was stopped.\nIII. After the 2008 crisis, the word \"liquid\" was banned from scheme names.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: segregation came after the 2018 credit crisis. II is true: the 2008 reforms ended premature redemption in FMPs. III is false: only the name \"liquid plus\" was banned, not \"liquid\"."},{"t":"Ch 2 · MF limitations","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Mutual funds offer no customisation; a PMS does.\nII. SEBI's categorisation of schemes was the answer to choice overload.\nIII. As a pass-through vehicle, a mutual fund lets the fund manager control the performance of the securities held.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and II","Only III"],"a":3,"e":"I is true: no customisation in MFs, PMS gives it. II is true: categorisation answered choice overload. III is false: the fund manager controls only his own skill, not the market or the securities."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Additional benchmark","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE about the additional benchmark shown in performance advertisements?","o":["An arbitrage fund uses the 10-year GoI security","An equity savings fund uses the 10-year GoI security","A conservative hybrid fund uses the Sensex or Nifty","A debt scheme with maturity of up to 1 year uses the 10-year GoI security"],"a":1,"e":"B is true: equity savings, conservative hybrid and other debt schemes use the 10-year GoI. A and D are false: arbitrage funds and debt schemes up to 1 year use the 1-year T-bill. C is false: conservative hybrid uses the 10-year GoI, not Sensex/Nifty."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Hybrid benchmarks","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Conservative hybrid schemes: NIFTY 50 Hybrid Composite Debt 15:85 Index.\nII. Balanced advantage / dynamic asset allocation schemes: NIFTY 50 Hybrid Composite Debt 65:35 Index.\nIII. Arbitrage schemes: NIFTY 50 Arbitrage Index.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: conservative hybrid uses the 15:85 index. II is false: balanced and BAF/DAA schemes use the 50:50 index; 65:35 is for aggressive hybrid. III is true: arbitrage uses NIFTY 50 Arbitrage."},{"t":"Ch 5 · Portfolio disclosure","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Scheme portfolios are disclosed monthly and half-yearly, with ISIN, within 10 days.\nII. Debt schemes disclose their portfolio fortnightly, within 5 days, along with yield.\nIII. Portfolio disclosures are hosted only on the AMC's website.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and II","Only III"],"a":3,"e":"I and II are true. III is false: portfolios are disclosed on both the AMC and AMFI websites."},{"t":"Ch 2 · Equity categories","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Value and Contra are now separate categories, each requiring at least 80% in equity","A focused fund can hold a maximum of 20 stocks","Sectoral and thematic funds together form a single equity category","ELSS - Tax Saver Fund must invest at least 65% in equity with a 3-year lock-in"],"a":0,"e":"Value and Contra are now separate categories at 80% each. A focused fund may hold up to 30 stocks, sectoral and thematic are now separate categories, and ELSS needs 80% in equity with a 3-year lock-in."},{"t":"Ch 5 · Addendum","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. An addendum records an interim change and is part of the scheme documents.\nII. An addendum must accompany the KIM.\nIII. When a fundamental attribute changes, the SID is revised immediately, before the exit window opens.","o":["Only I and II","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I and II are true. III is false: an addendum is issued immediately, and the SID is revised after the exit window (of at least 30 days)."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Interest rate risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A liquid fund carries less interest rate risk than an ultra-short duration fund.\nII. A low duration fund carries more interest rate risk than a short duration fund.\nIII. An overnight fund carries the least interest rate risk among the debt fund categories in the hierarchy.","o":["Only I and III","Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true and III is true: interest rate risk rises overnight, liquid, ultra-short, low, short, medium, medium-long, long. II is false: a short duration fund carries more interest rate risk than a low duration fund."},{"t":"Ch 8 · IDCW taxation","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. DDT was abolished by the Budget of February 2020; since April 2020, IDCW is taxed at the investor's slab rate.\nII. An IDCW reduces the NAV by less than the investor's post-tax receipt.\nIII. The growth option is more tax-efficient than IDCW because of tax deferral.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and II"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: DDT went in Feb 2020 and IDCW is taxed at slab from April 2020. II is false: IDCW reduces NAV by more than the post-tax receipt. III is true: growth is more tax-efficient due to deferral."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Within category","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A 'value' fund whose portfolio is full of expensive front-line stocks is not true to label.\nII. Performance should be judged on consistent outperformance and downside protection, against benchmark and peers, over rolling periods.\nIII. For equity funds, a look-back period of 3 years is sufficient.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: the portfolio must be true to label. II is true: that is the workbook's performance test. III is false: equity funds need a look-back of at least 5 years."},{"t":"Ch 5 · NAV declaration","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: NAV is declared every business day on the AMC and AMFI websites.\nStatement 2: Liquid and overnight schemes do not declare NAV for Sundays and holidays.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false: liquid and overnight NAVs are also declared for Sundays and holidays."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Cut-off redemption","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["An equity scheme redemption request received after 3 pm gets the same day's NAV.","For online redemption in an overnight scheme, the cut-off is 7 pm.","The cut-off for redemption in a liquid scheme is 1:30 pm.","Liquid funds calculate NAV only on business days."],"a":1,"e":"Overnight scheme redemption has a 3 pm cut-off, extended to 7 pm online. An equity redemption after 3 pm gets the next business day's NAV, liquid redemption cut-off is 3 pm, and liquid funds calculate NAV for every calendar day."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Gating","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Gating can be imposed when a single security in the portfolio defaults.\nII. Gating can last at most 10 working days in any 90 days.\nIII. During gating, the first Rs 2 lakh of every redemption is paid normally.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is false: gating is only for market-wide crises, never for one bad security. II is true: maximum 10 working days in any 90 days. III is true: the first Rs 2 lakh of each redemption is paid normally."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Top-down/bottom-up","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The EIC framework stands for Economy, Industry, Company.\nStatement 2: In the bottom-up approach, sector allocation is decided first and stocks are then picked within those sectors.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: EIC = Economy, Industry, Company. Statement 2 is false: bottom-up starts with the stock, and sector allocation is the result; top-down starts with the economy."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Single benchmark","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["Hybrid and solution-oriented schemes have a single benchmark","Sectoral schemes have a single benchmark","All benchmarks under the two-tier structure must be TRI","Index funds and ETFs follow the two-tier benchmark structure"],"a":3,"e":"Hybrid/solution, sectoral and index/ETF schemes all have a single benchmark, so the statement on index funds and ETFs is false. Two-tier benchmarks apply to debt and equity schemes and all must be TRI."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Rate calls","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: When interest rates are expected to rise, a debt fund manager should lengthen portfolio duration.\nStatement 2: A floater's coupon is fixed for its life, so its price is more sensitive to rate changes than a fixed-rate bond.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":1,"e":"Statement 1 is false: when rates are expected up, shorten duration or buy floaters; buy long duration when rates are expected down. Statement 2 is false: floater coupon = base + spread, so it holds its value when rates move."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Risk factors","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. One of the standard risk statements is that the sponsor is not liable beyond its initial contribution.\nII. Reinvestment risk relates to \"interest on interest\".\nIII. Foreign currency risk faced by FPIs is managed by the AMC.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the sponsor is not liable beyond its initial contribution. II is true: reinvestment risk is the \"interest on interest\" risk. III is false: foreign currency risk applies to FPIs and the AMC doesn't manage it."},{"t":"Ch 2 · Close-ended schemes","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Close-ended schemes, including ELSS, must be compulsorily listed.\nStatement 2: Units of close-ended schemes usually trade at a discount to NAV.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: close-ended schemes are compulsorily listed except ELSS. Statement 2 is true: the buyer has the bargaining power, so units usually trade at a discount to NAV."},{"t":"Ch 11 · FoF and gold","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A Fund of Funds investing in a single fund uses the benchmark of that underlying fund.\nII. A Fund of Funds investing in multiple funds uses the benchmark of its largest underlying fund.\nIII. A gold ETF is benchmarked against the price of gold.","o":["Only I and II","Only I and III","Only II and III","Only III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: a single-fund FoF uses the underlying's benchmark. II is false: a multi-fund FoF uses a broad market index. III is true: a gold ETF's benchmark is the price of gold."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Instant access","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Under the Instant Access Facility, an investor can redeem the higher of Rs 50,000 or 90% of the value of the investment per day.\nStatement 2: The Instant Access Facility is offered in liquid and overnight schemes.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: the limit is the lower of Rs 50,000 or 90% of value, per day per scheme per investor. Statement 2 is true: IAF applies to liquid and overnight schemes."},{"t":"Ch 2 · Hybrid funds","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A conservative hybrid fund invests 75-90% in debt and 10-25% in equity.\nII. An aggressive hybrid fund invests 65-80% in equity.\nIII. In a multi asset allocation fund, foreign securities count as a separate asset class.","o":["Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III","Only I and II"],"a":3,"e":"I is true: conservative hybrid = 75-90% debt, 10-25% equity. II is true: aggressive hybrid = 65-80% equity. III is false: multi asset needs at least 3 asset classes with at least 10% each, and foreign securities are not a separate class."},{"t":"Ch 5 · TER disclosure","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. TER is disclosed daily on the AMC and AMFI websites for all schemes except IDFs.\nII. TER is disclosed in Format 7E, downloadable and machine-readable.\nIII. Any expense item above 5% of total expenditure must be disclosed separately in the scheme accounts.","o":["Only III","Only I and III","Only II and III","None of them"],"a":0,"e":"I and II are true. III is false: the threshold for separate disclosure is any expense item above 10% of total expenditure."},{"t":"Ch 5 · SID cover page","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["The SID cover disclaimer says SEBI has approved and certified the document","The Due Diligence Certificate filed with the SID is given by the trustees","The risk-o-meter and suitability statement sit on the front page of the SID","Eligible and ineligible investors are listed in the SAI's \"How to Apply\" section"],"a":2,"e":"C is true. A is false: the disclaimer says SEBI has not approved or certified it. B is false: the SID is filed with an AMC Due Diligence Certificate. D is false: eligible and ineligible investors are in the SID's NFO and Ongoing Offer section."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Risk profile","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A risk profile considers the investor's need, ability and willingness to take risk.\nII. A useful remedy for behavioural biases is a third opinion from an RIA or an MFD.\nIII. Tactical asset allocation is best suited to new investors with a small surplus.","o":["Only I","Only III","Only I and II","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: risk profile = need + ability + willingness. II is true: a third opinion from an RIA or MFD helps counter biases. III is false: tactical/dynamic allocation suits seasoned investors with a large surplus."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Open vs active","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["Active funds must beat their benchmark to justify being chosen.","Large cap funds carry less risk than mid cap funds.","Open-ended funds offer liquidity at NAV less load.","Open-ended funds need not hold any cash, so their returns are not diluted."],"a":3,"e":"Open-ended funds must hold some cash, which dilutes returns, so the last statement is wrong. Active funds must beat the benchmark to justify the choice, large cap funds are less risky than mid cap funds, and open-ended funds offer liquidity at NAV less load."},{"t":"Ch 8 · Loss set-off","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A capital loss on mutual fund units can be set off against salary income.\nII. A short-term capital loss can be set off against STCG or LTCG.\nIII. Unabsorbed capital loss can be carried forward for up to 8 years.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is false: capital loss can't be set off against salary or other heads. II is true: STCL sets off against STCG or LTCG. III is true: carry forward is up to 8 years."},{"t":"Ch 5 · Risk-o-meter colours","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The Moderately High level of the risk-o-meter is shown in Caramel.\nStatement 2: The Low level of the risk-o-meter is shown in Chartreuse.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false: Low is Irish Green; Chartreuse is Low to Moderate."},{"t":"Ch 3 · Change of control","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A change in control of the AMC needs prior approval of the trustees and SEBI.\nII. Unitholders must be given an exit at NAV without exit load for at least 30 calendar days.\nIII. The change must be advertised in two English national dailies.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and II","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I and II are true: prior approval of trustees and SEBI is needed, and an exit at NAV with no exit load for at least 30 calendar days is given. III is false: the ad goes in 1 English national daily and 1 regional-language paper."},{"t":"Ch 11 · TRI effect","y":"concept","q":"Benchmarking against a TRI instead of a PRI makes it look like fewer schemes beat the benchmark because:","o":["The TRI deducts the scheme's expenses from the benchmark return","The TRI adds the dividends/interest of the index, raising the benchmark return by about the index dividend yield (~1.5–2.5%)","The TRI excludes dividends, lowering the benchmark return","The TRI is computed on beta-adjusted returns"],"a":1,"e":"The TRI adds dividends/interest to the price index, so the benchmark return rises by roughly the index dividend yield (~1.5–2.5%), making it harder for schemes to beat."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Direct plan","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["Apart from the Direct and Regular plans, a scheme may offer a third plan with a different expense structure.","A Direct plan has a lower TER than the Regular plan of the same scheme.","A Direct plan has a NAV separate from the Regular plan.","An investor applying under the Direct plan writes \"Direct\" in the ARN box."],"a":0,"e":"Only two plans, Direct and Regular, can differ by expense, so the first statement is false. The Direct plan has a lower TER and no commission, has a separate NAV, and is chosen by writing \"Direct\" in the ARN box."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Bank accounts","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. An individual investor can register up to 5 bank accounts in a folio.\nII. A non-individual investor can register up to 10 bank accounts.\nIII. A redemption payout requested to an unregistered bank account is paid into the default bank account.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"e":"All are true: up to 5 accounts for individuals and 10 for non-individuals, one marked default, and a payout to an unregistered account goes to the default account."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Passive breach","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A passive breach of asset allocation must be rebalanced within 30 business days.\nII. The passive-breach rebalancing rule applies to index funds and ETFs.\nIII. The passive-breach rebalancing rule is not applicable to overnight funds.","o":["Only II","Only I","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: the rebalancing period is 30 business days. II is false: index funds and ETFs are excluded. III is true: it does not apply to overnight funds."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Fund of funds","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A fund of funds can invest in another fund of funds provided no fees are charged.\nStatement 2: A scheme may invest in another mutual fund scheme, but no fees can be charged on that investment.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: a FoF cannot invest in another FoF at all. Statement 2 is true: inter-scheme investment is allowed with no fees (this rule is not for FoFs)."},{"t":"Ch 12 · FMP","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: An FMP can be exited before maturity by redeeming units with the AMC.\nStatement 2: In an FMP, the investor's horizon must match the scheme's maturity.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: exit from an FMP before maturity is only via the exchange. Statement 2 is true: the horizon must match the FMP's maturity."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Credit risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Credit risk arises only from a borrower's inability to pay, not from its intention to pay","Bonds of one's own government are the safest and pay the lowest interest rate","Bonds of one's own government pay the highest interest rate because they are the safest","Bonds of one's own government carry the highest credit risk in the domestic market"],"a":1,"e":"Bonds of one's own government are the safest and therefore pay the lowest rate. Credit risk can arise from the borrower's ability or its intention to pay, so the first option is wrong, and the last two reverse the safety-rate link."},{"t":"Ch 7 · TER limits","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. For TER purposes, an equity-oriented scheme is one with at least 65% in equity, excluding index funds and ETFs.\nII. The TER of an index fund or ETF cannot exceed 0.90%.\nIII. A close-ended equity scheme can charge a TER of up to 1.25%.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: equity-oriented for TER means ≥65% in equity, excluding index funds and ETFs. II is true: index fund/ETF TER ≤0.90%. III is false: close-ended and interval equity schemes are capped at 1.00% (others 0.80%)."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Eligible investors","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A minor's folio can have joint holders along with the minor.\nII. An HUF invests in mutual funds through its Karta.\nIII. An individual folio can have up to 3 joint holders.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and II"],"a":0,"e":"I is false: a minor invests through a guardian and there are no joint holders. II is true: an HUF invests via the Karta. III is true: individuals can have up to 3 joint holders."},{"t":"Ch 7 · Gold/silver ETF","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Gold held by a Gold ETF is valued using the LBMA PM fixing","Gold held by a Gold ETF is valued at 999.0 fineness","Silver held by a Silver ETF is valued using the LBMA AM fixing at 999.0 fineness","Gold held by a Gold ETF is valued in rupees per 10 grams"],"a":2,"e":"Only the Silver ETF statement is true (LBMA AM fixing, 999.0 fineness). Gold ETF uses the LBMA AM (not PM) fixing, at 995.0 (not 999.0) fineness, in USD per troy ounce (not rupees per 10 grams)."},{"t":"Ch 3 · Trust structure","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A mutual fund trust is governed by the Indian Trusts Act, 1882.\nII. The trust deed is registered under the Indian Registration Act, 1908.\nIII. The trust deed is executed between the sponsor and the AMC.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I and II are true: the trust is governed by the Indian Trusts Act, 1882 and the deed is registered under the Indian Registration Act, 1908. III is false: the trust deed is executed between the sponsor and the trustees."},{"t":"Ch 8 · Grandfathering","y":"concept","q":"Under grandfathering, the cost of acquisition of equity fund units bought before 31 Jan 2018 is taken as:","o":["The actual purchase cost only","The NAV on 31 Jan 2018 only","The higher of the actual cost or the NAV on 31 Jan 2018","The lower of the actual cost or the NAV on 31 Jan 2018"],"a":2,"e":"Gains up to 31 Jan 2018 are exempt, so cost = higher of actual cost or 31 Jan 2018 NAV (e.g. cost Rs 1 lakh, 31 Jan 2018 value Rs 2 lakh, sold at Rs 1.8 lakh = zero gain)."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Credit spread","y":"concept","q":"Credit spread is the difference between:","o":["The coupon rate and the YTM of the same bond","Long-term and short-term gilt yields","Corporate bond yield and gilt yield","The repo rate and the T-bill yield"],"a":2,"e":"Credit spread = corporate yield − gilt (government security) yield; rating agencies include CRISIL, ICRA, CARE and India Ratings (Fitch)."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Cyber and due diligence","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The cyber framework CSCRF draws its goals of Anticipate, Withstand, Contain, Recover and Evolve from CERT-In.\nStatement 2: Due diligence on distributors is solely AMFI's job, and AMCs have no role in it.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: those are the CSCRF goals, taken from CERT-In. Statement 2 is false: AMCs carry out due diligence on distributors; AMFI often runs a common process for them."},{"t":"Ch 9 · SIP and STP","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Under rupee cost averaging in an SIP, the average cost per unit is below the average NAV.\nII. An SIP can be started during an NFO.\nIII. In an STP, the minimum investment amount of the target scheme applies to each transfer.","o":["Only I and II","Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: rupee cost averaging keeps average cost below average NAV. II is true: an SIP can start during an NFO. III is false: scheme minimums don't apply to an STP, though loads and tax do."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Time stamping","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: An application is time-stamped when the distributor receives it from the investor.\nStatement 2: For online transactions, the time considered is the web server time.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: time stamping happens at the Official Point of Acceptance (OPoA), not when the distributor receives the form. Statement 2 is true: online time is the web server time."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Investor emotions","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Fear, greed and hope are the dominant emotions in investing.\nStatement 2: For a lay investor, diversification is the appropriate way to handle risk.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: fear, greed and hope are the dominant emotions, and a lay investor should diversify rather than avoid risk or take a position."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Bonus units","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: In a 1:3 bonus, the investor receives 3 free units for every 1 unit held.\nStatement 2: After a bonus issue, the value of the investor's holding remains unchanged.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: a 1:3 bonus means 1 free unit for every 3 units held. Statement 2 is true: NAV falls proportionately, so the value of the holding is unchanged."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Hybrid evaluation","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Hybrid funds should be judged on the quality of the equity portion and the credit/duration of the debt portion separately.\nStatement 2: The word 'conservative' in a conservative hybrid fund refers to both its equity and debt allocation.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: equity quality and debt credit/duration are judged separately. Statement 2 is false: 'conservative' refers only to the equity allocation."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Unitholder powers","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A simple majority of unitholders can remove the AMC of a scheme.\nStatement 2: An investor seeking a legal remedy for breach of trust proceeds against the trustees, not the trust.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: 75% of unitholders are needed to remove the AMC or wind up a scheme. Statement 2 is true: the trust is a notional entity, so the investor sues the trustees."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Credit risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["Gilt funds carry the lowest credit risk.","Credit risk funds carry the highest credit risk.","Credit risk funds carry more credit risk than banking & PSU funds.","Gilt funds carry more credit risk than corporate bond funds."],"a":3,"e":"Credit risk rises gilt, banking & PSU, corporate bond, credit risk fund; so gilt funds carry less credit risk than corporate bond funds, and the other three statements are true."},{"t":"Ch 8 · IDCW split","y":"concept","q":"From 1 April 2021, for tax purposes an IDCW payout is split into:","o":["Income distribution and capital distribution","Dividend and bonus","Short-term and long-term capital gains","Taxable dividend and exempt dividend"],"a":0,"e":"From 1 Apr 2021, IDCW is split into income distribution and capital distribution for tax."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Benchmarks and PRC","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Mutual fund schemes are benchmarked to a Total Return Index, which includes dividends and interest.\nStatement 2: The Potential Risk Class matrix for debt schemes plots modified duration against Credit Risk Value.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: schemes use a TRI, not a Price Return Index. Statement 2 is false: the PRC matrix uses Macaulay duration against Credit Risk Value."},{"t":"Ch 2 · MF benefits","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["Mark to market means valuing each security at its market price daily","The ELSS deduction of up to Rs 1,50,000 under Sec 80C is available under both the Old and New Tax Regimes","Recurring expenses are charged as a percentage of AUM and lower the NAV","SID, SAI, KIM, portfolio disclosures and NAV are available to prospective investors too"],"a":1,"e":"The ELSS deduction of up to Rs 1,50,000 under Sec 80C is available under the Old Tax Regime only. The other three statements are correct."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Money market","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. T-bills are issued by the RBI on behalf of the Government of India.\nII. Banks issue CDs for 7 days to 1 year, while FIs issue them for 1 to 3 years.\nIII. Companies issue CP for up to 3 years.","o":["Only I","Only III","Only I and II","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: T-bills are issued by the RBI for the GoI. II is true: CDs are 7 days–1 year for banks and 1–3 years for FIs. III is false: CP is issued for up to 1 year."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Needs mapping","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Growth needs map to equity funds and income needs map to debt funds.\nStatement 2: Parking needs map to short duration funds.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: growth maps to equity and income to debt. Statement 2 is false: parking needs map to liquid funds."},{"t":"Ch 7 · Brokerage","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Over and above the expense limits, brokerage is capped at 0.06% of trade value for cash market trades and 0.02% for derivatives.\nStatement 2: Apart from the base expense limit, brokerage, transaction cost and statutory levies, the only other charge to investors is exit load.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 1 is true: brokerage ≤0.06% (cash) and ≤0.02% (derivatives). Statement 2 is true: total charged = base limit + brokerage + transaction cost + statutory levies, and nothing else except exit load."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Sharpe and Treynor","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A higher Sharpe ratio indicates better risk-adjusted performance.\nII. The Sharpe ratio can be used to compare an equity scheme directly with a debt scheme.\nIII. The Treynor ratio is meant for diversified equity schemes.","o":["Only II","Only III","Only I and II","Only II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: higher Sharpe is better. II is false: Sharpe must compare like with like, never equity vs debt. III is true: Treynor is used for diversified equity schemes."},{"t":"Ch 9 · KYC coverage","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. For a minor's investment, the guardian (not the minor) must be KYC-compliant.\nII. A PoA holder must also complete KYC.\nIII. Joint holders other than the first holder are exempt from KYC.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I and II are true: KYC is needed by the guardian (not the minor) and by the PoA holder and issuer. III is false: everyone, including all joint holders, needs KYC."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Ratings and costs","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["Costs matter most in debt funds and index funds.","Star ratings reflect past performance and have no predictive power.","In small categories, investors should target the top 10-15% of funds.","This quarter's best fund is rarely next quarter's best."],"a":2,"e":"In small categories the target is the top 3-5 funds; top 10-15% applies to big categories. The other three statements are true."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Rebalancing","y":"concept","q":"Rebalancing a portfolio back to its target asset allocation means the investor:","o":["Buys more of the asset class that has risen the most","Shifts the whole portfolio into the better-performing asset","Sells what has risen and buys what has fallen","Changes the target mix whenever markets move"],"a":2,"e":"Rebalancing means selling what rose and buying what fell to restore the target mix; it does not chase the winner or change the target."},{"t":"Ch 4 · MFD conduct","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE about an MFD's conduct?","o":["The MFD may enter his own contact details on the client's form to give faster service","The MFD may prepare his own marketing material carrying the AMC logo if the content is accurate","The MFD may call himself a wealth manager once he holds a valid ARN","The MFD must always mention the EUIN on the client's application form"],"a":3,"e":"D is true: the EUIN must always be added. A is false: the MFD's own contact details must never be entered on a client form. B is false: only AMC-provided material may be used, and the logo needs written approval. C is false: only a SEBI RIA may use titles such as adviser, wealth manager or consultant."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Derivative risks","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Basis risk is the risk that a derivative is mispriced by a wrong model","Model risk is the risk that the hedge and the underlying do not move together","Derivatives are leveraged instruments","Settlement of exchange-traded derivatives is guaranteed by SEBI"],"a":2,"e":"Only 'derivatives are leveraged' is true. Mispricing is model risk, not basis risk; hedge-vs-underlying mismatch is basis risk, not model risk; and settlement is guaranteed by the exchange, not SEBI."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Real estate","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["The four asset classes are real estate, commodities, equity and fixed income","Real estate is an illiquid asset","Unlike shares, a real estate asset is not divisible","A self-occupied home is treated as an investment"],"a":3,"e":"The first three are true. A self-occupied home is treated as an expense, not an investment."},{"t":"Ch 8 · Scheme income","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Mutual fund scheme income is tax exempt under Sec 10(23D) because mutual funds are trusts.\nII. The growth option gives only capital gains, while the IDCW option gives dividend plus capital gains.\nIII. In a joint holding, income is taxed equally in the hands of all the holders.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: Sec 10(23D) exempts MF income as MFs are trusts. II is true: growth = capital gains only; IDCW = dividend + capital gains. III is false: income is taxed in the first holder's hands."},{"t":"Ch 6 · Commission model","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["A distributor earns commission on his own (self) investments","Under the full trail model there is no upfront commission","Trail commission is calculated on daily net assets","Under Regulation 66(6) of the MF Regulations 2026, the AMC pays distribution costs"],"a":0,"e":"There is no commission on self-investment. The full trail model allows no upfront commission, trail is calculated on daily net assets, and the AMC pays distribution costs under Regulation 66(6)."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Investor rights","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Units held in demat are freely transferable, except ELSS units during the lock-in.\nII. To switch to another distributor, the investor needs an NOC from the existing distributor.\nIII. An investor can pledge mutual fund units.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: demat units are freely transferable except ELSS in lock-in. II is false: a written request is enough and no NOC is needed. III is true: units can be pledged."},{"t":"Ch 3 · AMFI and EOP","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["AMFI is a self-regulatory organisation for mutual funds.","Platforms run by RIAs for their own clients are Execution Only Platforms.","An Execution Only Platform can offer both Direct and Regular plans.","An EOP Category 1 registers with AMFI and acts as an agent of AMCs."],"a":3,"e":"EOP Category 1 is a body corporate registered with AMFI acting as agent of AMCs. AMFI is neither a regulator nor an SRO, RIA platforms for their own clients are not EOPs, and EOPs are only for Direct plans."},{"t":"Ch 7 · Scheme accounting","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["Scheme accounts are kept separate from the AMC's accounts, with different auditors","NAV of index, liquid and debt funds is computed to 4 decimal places","NAV of equity and balanced funds is computed to at least 2 decimal places","Fractional units are not allowed, so units are always allotted in whole numbers"],"a":3,"e":"The first three are correct: separate accounts with different auditors; 4 decimals for index, liquid and debt funds; at least 2 for equity and balanced funds. The last is wrong: fractional units are allowed, though exchanges may trade whole units only."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Performance disclosure","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A scheme's product label shows its objective, asset class, holding period and risk-o-meter.\nII. Discrete annual returns help judge a scheme's consistency across market conditions.\nIII. The AMFI website has performance data for all schemes.","o":["Only I and II","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"e":"I is true: the label shows objective, asset class, holding period and risk-o-meter. II is true: discrete annual returns show consistency across market conditions. III is true: AMFI's website carries performance data for all schemes."},{"t":"Ch 9 · PAN exemption","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Residents of Sikkim are exempt from the PAN requirement.\nII. The Micro-SIP PAN exemption applies where Micro-SIP plus lump sum is up to Rs 50,000 per year per mutual fund.\nIII. A photo credit card is accepted as identity proof for Micro-SIP.","o":["Only III","Only I","Only II","Only I and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: Sikkim residents are PAN-exempt. II is true: Micro-SIP plus lump sum up to Rs 50,000 per year per MF is exempt (PEKRN is quoted). III is false: a photo debit card is accepted, not a credit card."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Core-satellite","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. The core portfolio serves long-term goals through diversified, large cap and mid cap funds.\nII. A long gilt fund is a satellite holding when interest rates are expected to rise.\nIII. Gold is a satellite holding at times of high inflation or uncertainty.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: core = long-term goals (diversified, large, mid cap). II is false: long gilt is a satellite holding when rates are expected to come down. III is true: gold suits the satellite for high inflation or uncertainty."},{"t":"Ch 4 · MFD code","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. An MFD may pass back part of the commission to the investor if the investor asks for it.\nII. An MFD may transact in direct plans when the client prefers them.\nIII. An MFD must disclose all commissions payable across competing schemes.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is false: rebates and pass-back are prohibited. II is false: MFDs cannot deal in direct plans. III is true: all commissions across competing schemes must be disclosed."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Fund age, size","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Fund age matters more for large cap funds than for multi cap funds.\nStatement 2: A large fund size helps large-cap funds but can hurt mid-cap or sector funds that have a limited universe of stocks.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: age matters most where scheme returns diverge, e.g. multi cap more than large cap. Statement 2 is true: size helps large-cap funds and hurts mid-cap or sector funds."},{"t":"Ch 6 · Distribution channels","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A distributor analyses the investor, while a fund manager analyses the market.\nII. LIC and UTI built the individual agent force.\nIII. The employee model of distribution is a variable cost, while the sub-agent model is a fixed cost.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the distributor analyses the investor and the fund manager the market. II is true: LIC and UTI built the individual agent force. III is false: employees are a fixed cost (salary); sub-agents are a variable cost (commission)."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Saving vs investing","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Saving precedes investing.\nStatement 2: A penalty for exiting an investment early affects its liquidity rather than its returns.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: saving comes before investing. Statement 2 is false: an exit penalty hits returns, not liquidity."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Diversification","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Market-wide risk can be removed through diversification.\nStatement 2: A business hit by the decline of the pager industry illustrates market-wide risk.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":1,"e":"Both are false: market-wide risk cannot be diversified (only company-specific risk can), and the pager example illustrates industry-specific risk."},{"t":"Ch 3 · Sponsor eligibility","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. The sponsor must have been in financial services for 5 years.\nII. The sponsor must have made a net profit in each of the last 3 years.\nIII. The sponsor's average profit over 5 years must be at least Rs 10 crore.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: 5 years in financial services. II is false: net profit is required in each of the last 5 years. III is true: average profit of at least Rs 10 crore over 5 years."},{"t":"Ch 2 · MF basics","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A mutual fund is set up as a trust.\nII. Profits and losses of a scheme belong only to its unitholders; everyone else earns a fee or commission.\nIII. In 2022 and 2025, mutual fund inflows countered heavy selling by retail investors.","o":["Only I and II","Only II","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: a mutual fund is set up as a trust. II is true: only unitholders bear profits and losses. III is false: MF inflows countered heavy FPI selling, acting as a market stabiliser."},{"t":"Ch 2 · Equity categories","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: A multi cap fund must invest at least 75% in equity, with at least 25% each in large, mid and small cap stocks.\nStatement 2: A large & mid cap fund must invest at least 35% each in large cap and mid cap stocks.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: multi cap needs at least 75% equity with at least 25% each in large, mid and small; large & mid cap needs 35% + 35%."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Investment rules","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["Regulation 39 permits mutual funds to invest in privately placed debentures and securitised debt","Under Regulation 41, mutual fund investments must be settled only in demat form","Under Regulation 41, derivatives and short selling are allowed only on a recognised stock exchange","Gold and silver ETFs must follow the Sixth Schedule restrictions like every other scheme"],"a":3,"e":"Options A, B and C are correct statements of Regulations 39 and 41. D is not correct: Regulation 41 applies the Sixth Schedule to all investments except gold and silver ETFs."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Concentration risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Focused funds are riskier than diversified funds but less risky than sector funds.\nII. Focused funds carry more concentration risk than thematic funds.\nIII. Thematic funds such as MNC, PSU and infrastructure funds sit between diversified and sector funds in risk.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: focused funds sit between diversified and sector funds. II is false: concentration risk rises diversified, focused, thematic, sector, so thematic funds carry more concentration risk than focused funds. III is true: thematic funds (MNC, PSU, infra) sit between diversified and sector."},{"t":"Ch 8 · STT and ELSS","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: STT applies on both purchase and redemption of units of equity-oriented funds.\nStatement 2: The Sec 80C deduction of up to Rs 1.5 lakh for ELSS is available only under the Old Tax Regime.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: STT applies only on sale or redemption of equity-oriented funds, none on purchase. Statement 2 is true: ELSS 80C up to Rs 1.5 lakh is Old Tax Regime only."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Financial goals","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A financial goal is defined by an objective, an amount and a timeline.\nII. Needs arising from hospitalisation, theft or fire are best met by building a larger investment portfolio.\nIII. The main flaw of the pool approach to investing is that the investment horizon is unknown.","o":["Only I and III","Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: objective + amount + timeline make a financial goal. II is false: such events are covered by an emergency fund plus insurance, not by investments. III is true: under the pool approach the horizon is unknown."},{"t":"Ch 10 · SD vs beta","y":"concept","q":"Standard deviation and beta differ in that:","o":["SD measures total risk; beta measures only systematic risk","SD measures systematic risk; beta measures total risk","Both measure total risk, but beta uses daily data","SD applies only to equity; beta applies to debt and equity"],"a":0,"e":"SD (√variance) is total risk and applies to debt and equity; beta (from CAPM) is systematic risk against a diversified index and applies to equity only."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Equity benchmarks","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A banking sectoral fund may be benchmarked to a sector index such as Bankex.\nII. A concentrated scheme holding fewer stocks is better compared with a broad index such as Nifty 500 than with Sensex 30 or Nifty 50.\nIII. Large cap schemes are benchmarked to NIFTY 100 / BSE 100.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II","Only II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: sectoral schemes use a sector index such as Bankex. II is false: a scheme with fewer stocks is compared with Sensex 30 / Nifty 50; schemes with more stocks use Nifty 100/200/500. III is true: large cap uses NIFTY 100 / BSE 100."},{"t":"Ch 7 · Recurring expenses","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Spending above the base expense limits may be charged to the scheme if the trustees approve it.\nStatement 2: Storage of gold and silver and listing fees are allowed recurring expenses of a scheme.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: spending above the base limits is borne by the AMC, trustees or sponsors, not the scheme. Statement 2 is true: storage of gold and silver and listing fee are in the allowed list (a)–(o)."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Interest rate risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. When interest rates rise, bond prices fall.\nII. The shorter the maturity of a bond, the bigger its price swing when interest rates change.\nIII. Volatility can be measured by variance, standard deviation, beta and modified duration.","o":["Only I","Only III","Only II","Only I and II"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: rates up, bond prices down. II is false: a longer maturity means a bigger price swing. III is true: these are the measures of volatility."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Information Ratio","y":"concept","q":"In the Information Ratio, the numerator and the denominator are respectively:","o":["Excess return over the Tier-1 benchmark; SD of that excess return","Scheme return minus T-bill return; SD of scheme returns","Excess return over the Tier-1 benchmark; beta of the scheme","Scheme return minus beta-expected return; SD of scheme returns"],"a":0,"e":"IR = excess return over the Tier-1 benchmark divided by the SD of that excess return, computed on daily data. Scheme return minus T-bill return over SD is the Sharpe ratio."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Debt indices","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Debt indices come from CRISIL, ICICI Securities and NSE, and are recommended by AMFI","Debt indices are recommended by SEBI and published only by CRISIL","The list of scheme benchmarks is published on SEBI's website","Debt Tier-1 benchmarks follow PRC from 1 Jan 2022"],"a":0,"e":"Debt indices come from CRISIL, ICICI Securities and NSE, recommended by AMFI, and the benchmark list is on AMFI's website (not SEBI's). Debt Tier-1 benchmarks follow PRC from 1 Apr 2022, not 1 Jan 2022."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Two-tier benchmarks","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: SEBI introduced two-tier benchmarking in Oct 2021, effective 1 Jan 2022.\nStatement 2: Under two-tier benchmarking, Tier 1 reflects the fund manager's style and Tier 2 reflects the scheme category.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: SEBI's Oct 2021 circular took effect from 1 Jan 2022. Statement 2 is false: Tier 1 is the category benchmark and Tier 2 reflects the fund manager's style."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Segregated portfolio","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Units of a segregated portfolio can be redeemed with the AMC on any business day","New investors after segregation get units in both the main and segregated portfolios","The segregation policy is approved by the AMC Board alone","Units of a segregated portfolio are listed within 10 working days"],"a":3,"e":"Only the listing statement is true (within 10 working days). There is no subscription or redemption in a segregated portfolio, new investors get main-portfolio units only, and the segregation policy is approved by the trustees."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Active vs passive","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A low-risk equity investor may choose an index fund or a value fund.\nII. An investor willing to take extra risk for extra return may choose a growth fund.\nIII. Active funds have lower costs than passive funds.","o":["Only I and II","Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: index or value funds suit low-risk equity investors. II is true: growth funds are for extra risk and extra return. III is false: active funds have higher cost and risk; passive funds cost less."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Hybrid risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Dynamic asset allocation funds are less risky than equity savings funds.","Multi-asset funds are riskier than dynamic asset allocation funds.","Balanced hybrid funds are less risky than dynamic asset allocation funds.","Multi-asset funds are riskier than balanced hybrid funds."],"a":1,"e":"In the hybrid hierarchy (arbitrage, conservative, equity savings, dynamic asset allocation, multi-asset, balanced, aggressive) multi-asset sits above dynamic asset allocation. Dynamic asset allocation is above equity savings, balanced is above dynamic asset allocation, and balanced is above multi-asset, so the other three are false."},{"t":"Ch 3 · Appointments","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The custodian is appointed by the AMC.\nStatement 2: The RTA is appointed by the AMC.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: the custodian is appointed by the trustees. Statement 2 is true: the RTA (and the collecting banker) is appointed by the AMC."},{"t":"Ch 7 · Repurchase price","y":"concept","q":"When a scheme charges an exit load, the repurchase price paid to the investor is computed as:","o":["NAV × (1 + exit load)","NAV + stamp duty − exit load","NAV − (exit load × face value)","NAV × (1 − exit load)"],"a":3,"e":"Repurchase price = NAV × (1 − load); e.g. NAV Rs 11 with 1% exit load = Rs 10.89. The load is credited back to the scheme."},{"t":"Ch 2 · Debt durations","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A liquid fund invests in instruments with maturity of up to 91 days.\nII. A money market fund invests in instruments with maturity of up to 1 year.\nIII. An Ultra-Short-Term fund has a Macaulay duration of 6 to 12 months.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: liquid = up to 91 days. II is true: money market = up to 1 year. III is false: Ultra-Short-Term is 3-6 months; 6-12 months is the Ultra Short to Short Term fund (formerly low duration)."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Overall risk","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Overall risk and return rise in the order liquid, hybrid, debt, equity.\nStatement 2: In the workbook illustration, a moderate investor holds 80% in the core portfolio and 20% in the satellite portfolio.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: the order is liquid, debt, hybrid, equity. Statement 2 is true: the workbook illustrates a moderate investor at 80% core / 20% satellite."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Duration measures","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Modified duration measures interest rate sensitivity and is the professional's measure.\nStatement 2: Weighted average maturity (WAM) is a simpler measure used with lay investors.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 1 is true: modified duration is the professional's interest-rate sensitivity measure. Statement 2 is true: WAM is simpler and used with lay investors."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Celebrity endorsement","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Celebrity endorsement is allowed only at the industry level and needs prior SEBI approval.\nStatement 2: The cost of an industry-level celebrity campaign is met from the AMC's own marketing budget.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: celebrities are allowed only for industry-level campaigns with prior SEBI approval, not for a scheme or AMC brand. Statement 2 is false: the cost comes from the investor education pool."},{"t":"Ch 9 · CKYC and UBO","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: CERSAI is the Central KYC Records Registry and issues a KYC Identifier.\nStatement 2: For a company, the beneficial owner (UBO) is a person with more than 15% ownership.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: CERSAI is the Central KYC Records Registry and issues a KYC Identifier. Statement 2 is false: the UBO threshold for a company is more than 25% (more than 15% applies to partnerships and bodies of individuals)."},{"t":"Ch 5 · Fundamental attributes","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The type of scheme (open-ended, close-ended or interval) is a fundamental attribute.\nStatement 2: Any safety net or guarantee, as part of the terms of issue, is a fundamental attribute.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: fundamental attributes are the type of scheme, the investment objective with min/max asset allocation, and the terms of issue (liquidity, aggregate fees and expenses, any safety net or guarantee)."}]},"ch5":{"id":"ch5","label":"Ch 5 · Scheme Related Information","short":"Ch 5","kind":"chapter","weight":10,"qs":[{"t":"Documents","q":"\"Please read the scheme related documents carefully\" refers to:","o":["SID and audited AMC balance sheet","Trust deed and KIM","SID and SAI","SAI and factsheet"],"a":2,"e":"Scheme related documents are the SID and SAI (with the KIM as summary)."},{"t":"Documents","q":"In whose format are the SID and SAI prepared?","o":["AMFI Code of Conduct","AMFI Best Practices","SEBI's prescribed format","Each AMC's own format"],"a":2,"e":"Both follow the SEBI-prescribed format and sequence."},{"t":"Documents","q":"Which document contains statutory information about the mutual fund or AMC and applies to all its schemes?","o":["SAI","SID","Factsheet","KIM"],"a":0,"e":"A single SAI covers all schemes of the mutual fund."},{"t":"Documents","q":"Legally, the SAI is:","o":["Part of the trust deed","Optional","A separate contract","Part of the SID"],"a":3,"e":"In practice they are separate, but legally the SAI is part of the SID."},{"t":"Documents","q":"SEBI's role regarding scheme related documents is to:","o":["Draft them","Approve them","Certify their accuracy","Give observations that the MF must incorporate"],"a":3,"e":"SEBI does not approve or disapprove; the documents are vetted, not approved."},{"t":"Documents","q":"Draft SIDs and SAIs are available for public viewing on:","o":["Only at AMC offices","AMFI's website","SEBI's website","RBI's website"],"a":2,"e":"Drafts are on SEBI's site; final documents are hosted on AMFI's site and the MF's own site."},{"t":"Documents","q":"The investor accepts the offer made through scheme documents by:","o":["Receiving the account statement","Registering with AMFI","Reading the KIM","Signing the application form and paying"],"a":3,"e":"The documents are the offer; signing and paying is acceptance; allotment completes the contract."},{"t":"Documents","q":"Under caveat emptor, an investor who did not read a fact disclosed in the SID:","o":["Cannot later claim to be unaware of it","Can claim compensation","Can cancel the investment anytime","Can sue SEBI"],"a":0,"e":"The investor is presumed to have read and understood the documents."},{"t":"SID","q":"The SID cover page states that the units:","o":["Are guaranteed by SEBI","Are insured","Have not been approved or recommended by SEBI","Are approved by AMFI"],"a":2,"e":"The standard clause says SEBI has not approved, recommended or certified the SID."},{"t":"SID","q":"The Due Diligence Certificate filed with the SID is issued by:","o":["AMFI","The AMC","SEBI","The custodian"],"a":1,"e":"It is filed along with a Due Diligence Certificate from the AMC (signed by the Compliance Officer)."},{"t":"SID","q":"Where does the SID show the Risk-o-meter and suitability statement?","o":["Only on the website","Last page","Front page","Only in the KIM"],"a":2,"e":"They are on the front page of the SID."},{"t":"SID","q":"A standard risk factor states that the sponsor is not liable for losses beyond:","o":["The AMC's net worth","Its contribution towards setting up the scheme","Rs 1 lakh","10% of AUM"],"a":1,"e":"Sponsors are not liable beyond their initial contribution to set up the scheme."},{"t":"SID","q":"The list of investors eligible and not eligible to invest is found in which SID section?","o":["Fees and expenses","Risk factors","Penalties and litigation","NFO and Ongoing Offer details"],"a":3,"e":"The NFO and Ongoing Offer section lists eligible and ineligible investors."},{"t":"SID","q":"The asset allocation disclosed in the SID is the proportion of:","o":["Expenses charged","Units issued","Investors by category","Net assets invested in different asset classes"],"a":3,"e":"It shows how net assets will be split across asset classes, with the risk level of each."},{"t":"SID","q":"Pre-payment and reinvestment risks are scheme-specific risks of investing in:","o":["Equity shares","Real estate","Fixed income securities","Gold"],"a":2,"e":"Fixed income investments carry credit, interest rate, liquidity, pre-payment and reinvestment risks."},{"t":"SID","q":"Details of how to apply, PAN and KYC requirements and third-party payment rules are mainly found in:","o":["The SAI (read with the SID)","The annual report","The KIM only","The factsheet"],"a":0,"e":"SAI and SID are read together; the SAI details how to apply."},{"t":"SID","q":"Loads differ from recurring expenses in that loads are:","o":["Paid by the AMC","Paid by the distributor","Charged to the scheme and reflected in NAV","Paid by the individual investor at the time of exit"],"a":3,"e":"Fees are charged to the scheme (via NAV); loads are paid by each investor out of redemption value."},{"t":"Fundamental attributes","q":"Which is NOT a fundamental attribute of a scheme?","o":["Terms of issue","Investment objective","Name of the fund manager","Type of scheme"],"a":2,"e":"Fundamental attributes are type, investment objective (with asset allocation) and terms of issue."},{"t":"Fundamental attributes","q":"Under \"terms of issue\", which item is a fundamental attribute?","o":["Aggregate fees and expenses charged to the scheme","The distributor list","Colour of the risk-o-meter","Office address of the RTA"],"a":0,"e":"Terms of issue include liquidity provisions, aggregate fees and expenses, and any safety net or guarantee."},{"t":"Fundamental attributes","q":"Changes to fundamental attributes are governed by which regulation?","o":["Regulation 49(3)","Regulation 22(9)(c)","Sixth Schedule","Regulation 66(7)"],"a":1,"e":"Regulation 22(9)(c) of the SEBI (Mutual Funds) Regulations, 2026."},{"t":"Fundamental attributes","q":"Before changing a fundamental attribute, the AMC must ensure unitholders get:","o":["Written communication (email/SMS allowed), details on the AMC website and an option to exit at prevailing NAV without exit load","Only a newspaper advertisement","AMFI's approval","A bonus issue"],"a":0,"e":"All three conditions apply; the exit window is at least 30 days."},{"t":"SAI","q":"Condensed financial information in the SAI is for schemes launched in the last:","o":["10 financial years","1 financial year","5 financial years","3 financial years"],"a":3,"e":"SAI gives condensed financials for schemes launched in the last 3 financial years."},{"t":"SAI","q":"Investment valuation norms are described in the:","o":["KIM","SAI","Factsheet","SID"],"a":1,"e":"The SAI details SEBI-standardised valuation rules."},{"t":"SAI","q":"Information on nomination, transmission, minors and pledge of units is in the SAI section on:","o":["Tax, Legal & General Information","Constituents","Investor grievance","Rights of unitholders"],"a":0,"e":"Legal aspects like nomination, transfer, transmission, minors and pledge are in Tax, Legal & General Information."},{"t":"SAI","q":"An investor wants the SAI of every mutual fund in one place. He should visit:","o":["RBI's website","AMFI's website","SEBI's website","NSDL's website"],"a":1,"e":"Investors can access the SAI of all mutual funds on the AMFI website."},{"t":"KIM","q":"Why is the KIM circulated with application forms?","o":["It lists distributor commissions","It is a SEBI approval letter","Circulating full SID and SAI with every form is too costly, so a summary goes instead","It replaces the SID"],"a":2,"e":"The KIM summarises SID and SAI and must accompany every application form."},{"t":"KIM","q":"Which item is NOT typically in the KIM?","o":["Detailed valuation norms for each security","Performance over 1, 3, 5 years and since inception","Investment objective","Benchmark index"],"a":0,"e":"Detailed valuation norms are in the SAI; the KIM has key points only."},{"t":"KIM","q":"How often must the KIM be updated?","o":["At least once every six months","Monthly","Annually","Never after issue"],"a":0,"e":"KIM is updated at least once a half-year, within one month of the half-year end."},{"t":"Addendum","q":"An addendum to scheme documents:","o":["Is part of the scheme documents and must accompany the KIM","Needs SEBI approval each time","Replaces the SID","Is optional reading"],"a":0,"e":"Interim changes are issued as addenda, which form part of the documents."},{"t":"Updates","q":"After the first update, the SID of an open-ended scheme must be updated within:","o":["Every quarter","3 months from year end","1 month from the end of each half-year","6 months from year end"],"a":2,"e":"Subsequently, within one month of each half-year end, using September and March data."},{"t":"Updates","q":"When the load structure changes, the AMC must:","o":["Reprint all SIDs immediately","Only inform SEBI","Wait for the next half-yearly update","Issue an addendum, display it on the website and attach it to KIM and SID in stock"],"a":3,"e":"The addendum goes to distributors and ISCs and is attached to stock; account statements also show the load structure."},{"t":"Updates","q":"The SAI must be regularly updated within:","o":["1 month of FY end","3 months of FY end","Every quarter","6 months of FY end"],"a":1,"e":"By the end of 3 months of every financial year; material changes on an ongoing basis."},{"t":"NAV disclosure","q":"The NAV of a scheme is disclosed on the website of:","o":["RBI","The AMC","Both RBI and AMC","Neither"],"a":1,"e":"NAV is published on the AMC and AMFI websites."},{"t":"NAV disclosure","q":"For which schemes are NAVs declared even on Sundays and holidays?","o":["Equity funds","Liquid and overnight funds","Close-ended funds","ELSS"],"a":1,"e":"Liquid and overnight fund NAVs reflect accrual for every day, including holidays."},{"t":"NAV disclosure","q":"A close-ended scheme must calculate its NAV at least:","o":["Weekly","Quarterly","Daily","Monthly"],"a":0,"e":"Close-ended scheme NAV is calculated at least once a week."},{"t":"TER disclosure","q":"An AMC plans to change the base expense ratio (BER) of a scheme. It must notify investors at least:","o":["30 days before","7 days before","1 working day before","3 working days before"],"a":3,"e":"Notice by email or SMS and on the website at least three working days before (e.g. effective 8 Jan 2026 = notice by 2 Jan 2026)."},{"t":"TER disclosure","q":"Under the March 2026 workbook, the Total Expense Ratio (TER) equals:","o":["Base expense ratio only","BER + brokerage + transaction cost + statutory levies (incl. GST)","BER + exit load","Management fee + GST only"],"a":1,"e":"TER = BER + brokerage cost + transaction cost for executing trades + statutory levies (including GST). BER is under Regulation 66(7)."},{"t":"TER disclosure","q":"AMCs disclose TER daily for all schemes EXCEPT:","o":["Liquid funds","Infrastructure debt fund (IDF) schemes","Index funds","ELSS"],"a":1,"e":"Daily TER disclosure covers all schemes except IDFs, in Format 7E, downloadable and machine-readable."},{"t":"Risk-o-meter","q":"How many risk levels does the mutual fund Risk-o-meter have?","o":["5","6","4","7"],"a":1,"e":"Six: Low, Low to Moderate, Moderate, Moderately High, High, Very High."},{"t":"Risk-o-meter","q":"On the Risk-o-meter, \"Moderately High\" is shown in which colour?","o":["Dark Orange","Caramel","Chartreuse","Neon Yellow"],"a":1,"e":"Low: Irish Green; Low-Moderate: Chartreuse; Moderate: Neon Yellow; Moderately High: Caramel; High: Dark Orange; Very High: Red."},{"t":"Risk-o-meter","q":"The Risk-o-meter is evaluated:","o":["Annually","Quarterly","Monthly","Daily"],"a":2,"e":"Risk-o-meter is evaluated monthly; changes are notified by notice-cum-addendum and email/SMS."},{"t":"Risk-o-meter","q":"A commodity has annualised volatility of 17%. Its risk value is:","o":["5 (High)","4 (Moderately High)","3 (Moderate)","6 (Very High)"],"a":0,"e":"<10% = 3, 10-15% = 4, 15-20% = 5, >20% = 6."},{"t":"Risk-o-meter","q":"Commodity volatility for risk scoring is computed quarterly based on how many years of benchmark prices?","o":["10","15","20","5"],"a":1,"e":"Past 15 years of the commodity benchmark index prices."},{"t":"Risk-band","q":"SIF strategies depict risk using a Risk-Band with how many levels?","o":["6","3","5","10"],"a":2,"e":"Five levels, from 1 (lowest) to 5 (highest)."},{"t":"Risk-band","q":"An AMC must publish the Risk-Band of all SIF strategies within ___ of each month end.","o":["5 days","7 days","15 days","10 days"],"a":3,"e":"Within 10 days from the close of each month."},{"t":"Portfolio disclosure","q":"Monthly portfolio disclosure for equity schemes must be made within:","o":["10 days","30 days","15 days","5 days"],"a":0,"e":"Monthly and half-yearly portfolios with ISIN within 10 days on AMC and AMFI sites."},{"t":"Portfolio disclosure","q":"Debt schemes disclose portfolios:","o":["Fortnightly within 5 days, with yields","Weekly within 2 days","Monthly within 10 days","Quarterly"],"a":0,"e":"Debt scheme portfolios are disclosed fortnightly within 5 days, including yield of instruments."},{"t":"Financial results","q":"The annual report must be emailed to investors within:","o":["6 months of FY end","1 month of FY end","4 months of FY end","3 months of FY end"],"a":2,"e":"No later than four months from 31 March; ad in English and Hindi dailies."},{"t":"Financial results","q":"Unaudited half-yearly financial results must be displayed within:","o":["15 days","1 month","2 months","3 months"],"a":1,"e":"Within one month from the close of each half-year (31 March and 30 September)."},{"t":"Factsheet","q":"Which is NOT a statutory document?","o":["SID","Fund factsheet","SAI","KIM"],"a":1,"e":"The factsheet is voluntary, though SEBI disclosure rules apply to it."},{"t":"SID vs SAI","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The SID contains details of one scheme.\nII. Each scheme of a mutual fund has its own separate SAI.\nIII. Legally, the SAI is part of the SID.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the SID covers one scheme. II is false: one SAI serves all schemes of the mutual fund. III is true: legally the SAI is part of the SID."},{"t":"SEBI's role","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: SEBI approves the SID before a scheme is launched.\nStatement 2: Draft scheme documents are on SEBI's website, while final documents are on AMFI's website and the mutual fund's own site.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: SEBI does not approve the documents; it only gives observations, so they are vetted, not approved. Statement 2 is true: drafts on SEBI's site, finals on AMFI's and the MF's site."},{"t":"Offer and acceptance","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. The scheme documents are the offer.\nII. The signed application form with payment is the acceptance.\nIII. The contract is complete when the investor's payment is realised.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I and II are true: documents are the offer, and the signed form plus payment is the acceptance. III is false: the contract completes on allotment of units."},{"t":"SID cover page","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["The SID cover disclaimer says SEBI has approved and certified the document","The Due Diligence Certificate filed with the SID is given by the trustees","The risk-o-meter and suitability statement sit on the front page of the SID","Eligible and ineligible investors are listed in the SAI's \"How to Apply\" section"],"a":2,"e":"C is true. A is false: the disclaimer says SEBI has not approved or certified it. B is false: the SID is filed with an AMC Due Diligence Certificate. D is false: eligible and ineligible investors are in the SID's NFO and Ongoing Offer section."},{"t":"Fundamental attributes","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The type of scheme (open-ended, close-ended or interval) is a fundamental attribute.\nStatement 2: Any safety net or guarantee, as part of the terms of issue, is a fundamental attribute.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: fundamental attributes are the type of scheme, the investment objective with min/max asset allocation, and the terms of issue (liquidity, aggregate fees and expenses, any safety net or guarantee)."},{"t":"Attribute change process","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Under Regulation 22(9)(c), the written communication to unitholders may be sent by email or SMS.\nII. Unitholders get an exit at the prevailing NAV with a reduced exit load.\nIII. Details of the change are also put on the AMC website.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and II","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: email or SMS is allowed. II is false: the exit is at prevailing NAV with no exit load. III is true: details go on the AMC website."},{"t":"SAI contents","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The SAI carries condensed financial information of schemes for the last 5 financial years.\nII. The SAI covers valuation norms.\nIII. All SAIs are available on AMFI's website.","o":["Only II","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is false: condensed financials cover the last 3 financial years. II is true: valuation norms are in the SAI. III is true: all SAIs are on AMFI's site."},{"t":"KIM","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Every application form must carry a Key Information Memorandum.\nStatement 2: The KIM is a summary of the SID only.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: every application form must carry a KIM. Statement 2 is false: the KIM summarises both the SID and the SAI."},{"t":"Update timelines","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["The SAI is updated within 3 months of the end of the financial year","The KIM is updated at least once every half-year, within 1 month","After its first update, the SID is updated within 1 month of each half-year","The SAI is updated once in 3 years, and material changes wait for that update"],"a":3,"e":"A, B and C are correct. D is not correct: the SAI is updated within 3 months of each FY end, and material changes are updated on an ongoing basis."},{"t":"Addendum","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. An addendum records an interim change and is part of the scheme documents.\nII. An addendum must accompany the KIM.\nIII. When a fundamental attribute changes, the SID is revised immediately, before the exit window opens.","o":["Only I and II","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I and II are true. III is false: an addendum is issued immediately, and the SID is revised after the exit window (of at least 30 days)."},{"t":"NAV declaration","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: NAV is declared every business day on the AMC and AMFI websites.\nStatement 2: Liquid and overnight schemes do not declare NAV for Sundays and holidays.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false: liquid and overnight NAVs are also declared for Sundays and holidays."},{"t":"TER disclosure","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. TER is disclosed daily on the AMC and AMFI websites for all schemes except IDFs.\nII. TER is disclosed in Format 7E, downloadable and machine-readable.\nIII. Any expense item above 5% of total expenditure must be disclosed separately in the scheme accounts.","o":["Only III","Only I and III","Only II and III","None of them"],"a":0,"e":"I and II are true. III is false: the threshold for separate disclosure is any expense item above 10% of total expenditure."},{"t":"TER and BER","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["TER is the Base Expense Ratio plus brokerage, transaction cost and statutory levies including GST","The Base Expense Ratio is set under Regulation 22(9)(c)","The direct plan has a higher BER because of direct servicing costs","Ensuring that every expense is identified and disclosed in the offer document is the responsibility of the AMC alone"],"a":0,"e":"A is true. B is false: BER is under Regulation 66(7). C is false: the direct plan BER is lower, as there is no distribution cost or commission. D is false: both the trustees and the AMC must ensure every expense is identified and disclosed."},{"t":"Risk-o-meter colours","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The Moderately High level of the risk-o-meter is shown in Caramel.\nStatement 2: The Low level of the risk-o-meter is shown in Chartreuse.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false: Low is Irish Green; Chartreuse is Low to Moderate."},{"t":"Risk-o-meter review","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The risk-o-meter of a scheme is reviewed quarterly.\nII. A change in the risk-o-meter is communicated by a notice-cum-addendum and email/SMS.\nIII. The scheme's risk-o-meter is shown together with the benchmark's risk-o-meter.","o":["Only I","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is false: the risk-o-meter is reviewed monthly. II is true. III is true: scheme and benchmark risk-o-meters are shown together."},{"t":"Portfolio disclosure","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Scheme portfolios are disclosed monthly and half-yearly, with ISIN, within 10 days.\nII. Debt schemes disclose their portfolio fortnightly, within 5 days, along with yield.\nIII. Portfolio disclosures are hosted only on the AMC's website.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and II","Only III"],"a":3,"e":"I and II are true. III is false: portfolios are disclosed on both the AMC and AMFI websites."},{"t":"Periodic reports","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Unaudited half-yearly results are hosted on the website within 2 months of 31 March and 30 September.\nStatement 2: The annual report is published within 4 months of the end of the financial year.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: half-yearly results go up within 1 month of 31 March / 30 September. Statement 2 is true: the annual report comes within 4 months of FY end."},{"t":"Factsheet","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["A factsheet is mandatory under SEBI rules and must be published every month","Publishing the factsheet monthly is an AMFI best practice","SEBI's disclosure rules still apply to a factsheet","A factsheet typically shows PE, beta, standard deviation and Sharpe ratio"],"a":0,"e":"A is not correct: the factsheet is voluntary, not mandatory. B, C and D are correct: monthly publication is an AMFI best practice, SEBI disclosure rules apply, and it shows PE, beta, SD and Sharpe."}]},"ch6":{"id":"ch6","label":"Ch 6 · Distribution and Channel Management","short":"Ch 6","kind":"chapter","weight":6,"qs":[{"t":"Role","q":"Only individuals are allowed to distribute mutual funds in India. True or false?","o":["False","True","True only in B-30 cities","True only for equity funds"],"a":0,"e":"Banks, NBFCs, brokers, national and regional distributors and others also distribute MFs."},{"t":"Role","q":"The main difference between the job of a distributor and a fund manager is that the distributor analyses:","o":["Market factors","Company balance sheets","Interest rates","The investor's needs, resources and goals"],"a":3,"e":"The distributor analyses the investor; the fund manager analyses the market."},{"t":"Channels","q":"Which banks were the first to enter mutual fund distribution in India?","o":["Regional rural banks","Co-operative banks","Public sector banks","Multinational banks"],"a":3,"e":"Multinational banks entered first; private and PSU banks came later."},{"t":"Channels","q":"Compared with the employee model, a sub-agent network model has:","o":["No costs","Higher variable costs","Higher fixed costs","Salary-based costs"],"a":1,"e":"Employees earn salary (fixed cost); sub-agents earn commission linked to business (variable cost)."},{"t":"Exchange platforms","q":"On the stock exchange MF platforms, depository participants can process:","o":["Purchases only","Redemptions only","Both purchases and redemptions","SIPs only"],"a":1,"e":"DPs can process redemption requests only."},{"t":"Exchange platforms","q":"Who is responsible for settlement of MF transactions routed through stock exchange platforms?","o":["The clearing member","The AMC","SEBI","The stock exchange"],"a":1,"e":"Exchanges only route orders and give no settlement guarantee; settlement responsibility lies with the AMC."},{"t":"Exchange platforms","q":"Open-ended schemes eligible for transactions on exchange platforms are:","o":["Converted to ETFs","Not listed on the exchange","Listed on the exchange","Close-ended"],"a":1,"e":"Eligible schemes are notified by the MF but not listed."},{"t":"Exchange platforms","q":"Since which SEBI circular can investors directly access stock exchange infrastructure to buy and redeem MF units?","o":["February 26, 2020","August 22, 2011","May 31, 2010","March 15, 2017"],"a":0,"e":"SEBI circular dated 26 February 2020 allowed direct access."},{"t":"MF Utilities","q":"An investor registered on MF Utilities is allotted a:","o":["PAN","CAN (Common Account Number)","ARN","EUIN"],"a":1,"e":"MFU allots a Common Account Number under which holdings are consolidated."},{"t":"MF Utilities","q":"If bank details on the MFU CAN differ from those in the folio:","o":["Folio details prevail","CAN details override the folio details","Both are rejected","The RTA decides"],"a":1,"e":"Bank and nomination details given for the CAN override the folio details."},{"t":"Prerequisites","q":"The certification required to become a mutual fund distributor is conducted by:","o":["SEBI","NSDC","AMFI","NISM"],"a":3,"e":"NISM Series V-A is conducted by the National Institute of Securities Markets."},{"t":"Prerequisites","q":"Who could obtain certification through CPE instead of passing the exam?","o":["Bank employees","Those aged 50+ or with 10+ years of MF distribution experience as of 31 May 2010","Anyone above 40","Graduates in finance"],"a":1,"e":"The grandfathered category could qualify via CPE classroom credits."},{"t":"Prerequisites","q":"In the KYD biometric process, which fingerprint is taken?","o":["All ten fingers","Right thumb","Index finger of the right hand","Left thumb"],"a":2,"e":"The impression of the right-hand index finger is taken at the CAMS PoS."},{"t":"Prerequisites","q":"The minimum age to obtain an ARN is:","o":["18","21","25","16"],"a":0,"e":"Minimum age for ARN is 18 years."},{"t":"Prerequisites","q":"Employees of an institutional distributor selling mutual funds need:","o":["Their own ARN","NISM certification and an EUIN, operating under the institution's ARN","Only a PAN","SEBI RIA registration"],"a":1,"e":"Employees need NISM V-A and an EUIN; no separate ARN."},{"t":"Prerequisites","q":"From which date are ARN and EUIN applications accepted only online?","o":["1 January 2025","1 August 2025","1 April 2023","1 October 2024"],"a":3,"e":"AMFI moved to online-only applications from 1 October 2024."},{"t":"Commission","q":"Under current SEBI regulations, AMCs pay distributors:","o":["Trail commission only, in monetary terms","Upfront commission only","Gifts and incentives","Upfront plus transaction charges"],"a":0,"e":"Full trail model: no upfront, no upfronting of trail, monetary only."},{"t":"Commission","q":"Mutual fund distributors earn no commission when the investor invests in direct plans. True or false?","o":["True","True only for ETFs","False for SIPs","False"],"a":0,"e":"Commission is paid only on Regular Plan investments."},{"t":"Commission","q":"Rs 20 lakh is invested through a distributor at a trail rate of 0.73% p.a. What is the daily trail commission if NAV is unchanged?","o":["Rs 400","Rs 146","Rs 40","Rs 20"],"a":2,"e":"Daily trail = 20,00,000 × 0.73% ÷ 365 = Rs 40."},{"t":"Commission","q":"If the NAV of units sold by a distributor rises, trail commission:","o":["Falls","Rises, since it is on current net assets","Stays the same","Stops"],"a":1,"e":"Trail is calculated on the current value of assets, so it rises with NAV."},{"t":"Commission","q":"A distributor invests his own money in a regular plan under his ARN. Commission payable is:","o":["Only upfront","Double trail","Nil","Normal trail"],"a":2,"e":"No commission is payable on self-business."},{"t":"Commission","q":"The transaction charges framework (for subscriptions above Rs 10,000) was withdrawn by SEBI in:","o":["August 2025","2022","November 2025","2019"],"a":0,"e":"SEBI withdrew transaction charges in August 2025; distributors are paid by AMCs."},{"t":"GST","q":"Under the reverse charge mechanism, GST on supplies from an unregistered supplier is paid by:","o":["The registered recipient","Nobody","The investor","The unregistered supplier"],"a":0,"e":"Section 9(4) of the CGST Act and 5(4) of the IGST Act: the registered recipient pays GST on procurements from unregistered suppliers."},{"t":"Disclosure","q":"Which distributor triggers AMC commission disclosure and due diligence?","o":["One earning Rs 80 lakh across the industry","One with 15 locations","One with Rs 50 crore AUM","One earning Rs 60 lakh from a single AMC"],"a":3,"e":"Triggers: >20 locations, >Rs 100 cr AUM, >Rs 1 cr commission industry-wide, or >Rs 50 lakh from one AMC."},{"t":"Disclosure","q":"AMCs conduct additional due diligence of a distributor whose portfolio turnover ratio exceeds:","o":["Five times the industry average","The industry average","Two times the industry average","1.5 times the industry average"],"a":2,"e":"More than two times the industry average is treated as excessive."},{"t":"Advisory vs execution","q":"How many categories of customer relationship/transaction are allowed for distributors?","o":["Unlimited","Three: advisory, execution only, and discretionary","One: advisory","Two: advisory and execution only"],"a":3,"e":"Only advisory or execution only; no third categorisation."},{"t":"Advisory vs execution","q":"A client insists on a product the distributor believes is unsuitable. The distributor should:","o":["Give a written unsuitability communication, get it acknowledged and get an \"execution only\" confirmation","Execute silently","Charge extra","Refuse outright"],"a":0,"e":"Written communication acknowledged by the investor, plus confirmation that the transaction is execution only."},{"t":"Advisory vs execution","q":"Under PFUTP regulations, which is mis-selling of MF units?","o":["Explaining risks","Recommending an index fund","Not taking reasonable care to ensure suitability","Disclosing commission"],"a":2,"e":"Mis-selling includes false statements, hiding facts or risks, and not ensuring suitability."},{"t":"Nomination","q":"After a distributor's death, commission is paid to the nominee until:","o":["1 year","The nominee gets an ARN","AUM under the deceased's ARN becomes nil or investors change the ARN","The ARN's original expiry date"],"a":2,"e":"Paid until AUM is nil (or investor changes code); future ARN expiry is not considered."},{"t":"Change of distributor","q":"An investor changes his distributor code (not due to the old distributor ceasing business). Trail commission goes to:","o":["The old distributor","The new distributor","Neither","Both equally"],"a":2,"e":"No commission is payable to either, to discourage luring clients."},{"t":"Distribution channels","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A distributor analyses the investor, while a fund manager analyses the market.\nII. LIC and UTI built the individual agent force.\nIII. The employee model of distribution is a variable cost, while the sub-agent model is a fixed cost.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the distributor analyses the investor and the fund manager the market. II is true: LIC and UTI built the individual agent force. III is false: employees are a fixed cost (salary); sub-agents are a variable cost (commission)."},{"t":"Bank distribution","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: PSU banks were the first banks to enter mutual fund distribution.\nStatement 2: Distribution companies and banks are institutional distributors.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: multinational banks entered first; PSU banks give non-urban reach. Statement 2 is true: distribution companies and banks are institutional distributors."},{"t":"Exchange platforms","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. The exchange platforms for mutual fund transactions are NSE NMF-II and BSE StAR MF.\nII. Depository participants can process both purchases and redemptions on exchange platforms.\nIII. Exchange platforms replace the RTA and give a settlement guarantee.","o":["Only II","Only III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: the platforms are NSE NMF-II and BSE StAR MF. II is false: DPs can only process redemptions. III is false: exchanges are an order-routing system, don't replace RTAs and give no settlement guarantee (the AMC is responsible)."},{"t":"MF Utilities","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Bank and nomination details registered with MF Utilities override those in the folio","An investor can hold only one Common Account Number (CAN)","A CAN can be obtained without completing KYC","MF Utilities requires a separate payment for each scheme invested in"],"a":0,"e":"MFU bank/nomination details override folio details. An investor can have multiple CANs, a CAN needs KYC, and MFU allows one form and one payment for multiple schemes."},{"t":"MFCentral","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. MFCentral was built by CAMS and KFintech under a SEBI mandate.\nII. MFCentral gives a consolidated view of demat and non-demat folios.\nIII. MFCentral allows family portfolio tracking.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"e":"I is true: CAMS and KFintech built MFCentral under SEBI mandate. II is true: it consolidates demat and non-demat folios. III is true: it supports family portfolio tracking."},{"t":"Certification and KYD","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A distributor aged 50 or more, or with at least 10 years' experience, as of 31 May 2010 could be exempted from the NISM V-A exam through CPE.\nStatement 2: For KYD, the distributor's left thumb impression is captured at a CAMS PoS.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: the CPE exemption applies at age 50+ or 10+ years' experience as of 31 May 2010. Statement 2 is false: KYD captures the right index finger biometric at a CAMS PoS."},{"t":"ARN and EUIN","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. The minimum age to obtain an ARN is 18.\nII. Employees of a distribution firm must each obtain a separate ARN.\nIII. ARN and EUIN applications are accepted online only from 1 Oct 2024.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and II"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: ARN minimum age is 18. II is false: employees get an EUIN under the firm's ARN, not a separate ARN. III is true: ARN/EUIN applications are online only from 1 Oct 2024."},{"t":"Commission model","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["A distributor earns commission on his own (self) investments","Under the full trail model there is no upfront commission","Trail commission is calculated on daily net assets","Under Regulation 66(6) of the MF Regulations 2026, the AMC pays distribution costs"],"a":0,"e":"There is no commission on self-investment. The full trail model allows no upfront commission, trail is calculated on daily net assets, and the AMC pays distribution costs under Regulation 66(6)."},{"t":"Additional commission","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. For a lump sum by a new investor, the additional commission is 1% of the first application, up to Rs 2,000, if the investor stays at least 1 year.\nII. The additional commission is paid from the 2 bps set aside for investor education, with claw back.\nIII. A distributor can earn the additional commission twice for the same investor, once on a lump sum and once on a SIP.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: lump sum incentive is 1% of the first application, max Rs 2,000, if the investor stays 1 year or more. II is true: it is paid from the 2 bps investor education money with claw back. III is false: there is no dual incentive for the same investor."},{"t":"Due diligence","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A distributor with presence in more than 20 locations is covered by the commission disclosure and due diligence requirements.\nStatement 2: A distributor earning more than Rs 50 lakh commission a year from a single AMC is covered by the commission disclosure and due diligence requirements.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 1 is true: more than 20 locations is a trigger. Statement 2 is true: more than Rs 50 lakh from one AMC is a trigger; any one of the four conditions is enough."},{"t":"Advice and nomination","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. There are only two transaction types for an MFD: advisory and execution only.\nII. An MFD must register as an RIA to give advice incidental to distribution.\nIII. The nominee of a deceased distributor must hold an ARN to receive trail commission.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only II and III","Only I and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: only advisory and execution-only transactions exist, with no third category. II is false: advice incidental to distribution needs no RIA registration. III is false: the nominee need not hold an ARN and receives commission as trustee for the legal heirs."}]},"ch7":{"id":"ch7","label":"Ch 7 · NAV, TER and Pricing of Units","short":"Ch 7","kind":"chapter","weight":8,"qs":[{"t":"Valuation","q":"Mutual fund investment valuation norms are specified in which schedule of the SEBI MF Regulations?","o":["Sixth Schedule","Fifth Schedule","Seventh Schedule","Ninth Schedule"],"a":2,"e":"AMCs value investments per the Seventh Schedule of the MF Regulations 2026."},{"t":"Valuation","q":"The valuation policy of an AMC must be disclosed in the:","o":["SAI","Factsheet","It is not made public","Account statement"],"a":0,"e":"Principle 6: disclosure in the SAI, the AMC website and elsewhere as SEBI specifies."},{"t":"Valuation","q":"Before investing in a new type of security, the AMC must:","o":["Establish its valuation methodology with AMC Board approval","Get AMFI approval","Get unitholder consent","Inform the RTA"],"a":0,"e":"Principle 2: valuation method must be established and Board-approved first."},{"t":"Valuation","q":"Valuation policies must be reviewed by an independent auditor at least:","o":["Quarterly","Once a financial year","Monthly","Once in three years"],"a":1,"e":"Principle 4: at least once a financial year."},{"t":"Valuation","q":"If there is a conflict between the Principles of Fair Valuation and the Valuation Guidelines:","o":["The AMC decides","SEBI decides case by case","The Guidelines prevail","The Principles prevail"],"a":3,"e":"The Principles of Fair Valuation prevail."},{"t":"Valuation","q":"Who is responsible for true and fair valuation and correct NAV?","o":["The auditor","The trustees","The custodian","The AMC"],"a":3,"e":"Principle 7: the AMC, irrespective of the disclosed policy."},{"t":"Valuation","q":"A listed share traded on two exchanges is valued at:","o":["The highest price","The purchase cost","The last closing price on the exchange where it is principally traded","The average price of both"],"a":2,"e":"Use the principal exchange; reasons for selecting it are recorded in writing."},{"t":"Valuation","q":"A share becomes a \"non-traded\" security when it has not traded on any exchange for:","o":["90 days","7 days","30 days","15 days"],"a":2,"e":"No trade for 30 days before the valuation date makes it non-traded."},{"t":"Valuation","q":"Non-traded equity shares are generally valued on the basis of:","o":["Face value","Capitalisation of earnings using P/E of comparables, with an illiquidity discount","Last traded price a year ago","Book value only"],"a":1,"e":"Earnings capitalisation (alone or with net asset value) using comparable P/Es, less a discount for illiquidity."},{"t":"Valuation","q":"Gold held by a gold ETF is valued using:","o":["MCX closing price","Jeweller rates","LBMA AM fixing price in USD per troy ounce for 995.0 fineness","RBI reference rate"],"a":2,"e":"LBMA AM fixing, USD/troy ounce, 995.0 fineness (silver: 999.0)."},{"t":"NAV","q":"Investors bought 20 crore units at Rs 10. Rs 140 crore is in equities and Rs 60 crore in bank deposits. Accrued income is Rs 8 crore and accrued expenses Rs 4 crore. NAV per unit is:","o":["Rs 20.00","Rs 10.20","Rs 20.40","Rs 10.00"],"a":1,"e":"(140 + 60 + 8 − 4) ÷ 20 = 204 ÷ 20 = Rs 10.20 (workbook sample Q2)."},{"t":"NAV","q":"Stocks Rs 300 cr, bonds Rs 80 cr, money market Rs 15 cr, accrued interest Rs 3 cr, fees payable Rs 2 cr, units 20 cr. NAV is:","o":["Rs 19.90","Rs 19.80","Rs 19.70","Rs 20.00"],"a":1,"e":"(300 + 80 + 15 + 3 − 2) ÷ 20 = 396 ÷ 20 = Rs 19.80."},{"t":"NAV","q":"Stocks Rs 250 cr, cash Rs 6 cr, receivable on shares sold Rs 4 cr, payable on shares bought Rs 10 cr, units 12.5 cr. NAV is:","o":["Rs 20.80","Rs 19.20","Rs 21.60","Rs 20.00"],"a":3,"e":"(250 + 6 + 4 − 10) ÷ 12.5 = 250 ÷ 12.5 = Rs 20.00."},{"t":"NAV","q":"Which change will increase a scheme's NAV, all else equal?","o":["Higher dividend income","Realised capital losses","Valuation losses","Higher expenses"],"a":0,"e":"More income and gains raise NAV; expenses and losses lower it."},{"t":"NAV","q":"Expenses relating to a period are counted even if not yet paid. This is the:","o":["Going concern principle","Accrual principle","Prudence principle","Cash principle"],"a":1,"e":"Under the accrual principle a scheme cannot inflate profits by delaying payments."},{"t":"NAV","q":"Net assets of a scheme increase when portfolio prices rise:","o":["Even if securities are not sold","Only at year end","Never","Only after the securities are sold"],"a":0,"e":"Net assets include unrealised appreciation because of mark to market."},{"t":"NAV","q":"If new units are issued below the prevailing NAV:","o":["NAV falls for existing investors","Only unit capital changes","NAV rises for existing investors","NAV is unaffected"],"a":0,"e":"Issuing below NAV dilutes existing investors (workbook Case 1: Rs 15 to Rs 14.95)."},{"t":"NAV","q":"If units are redeemed at a price below NAV, the NAV for remaining investors will:","o":["Become zero","Fall","Stay the same","Rise"],"a":3,"e":"Workbook Case 2: NAV rises from Rs 15 to Rs 15.05."},{"t":"Expenses","q":"Which of these is a permitted recurring expense in the workbook's list (a) to (o)?","o":["Costs of setting up the AMC's own office","Storage and handling of gold and silver","The sponsor's corporate advertising","Penalties paid by the AMC"],"a":1,"e":"The list includes marketing/distribution, brokerage, RTA, trustee, audit and custodian fees, investor communication, fund transfer, statements, insurance, winding up, statutory ads, storage and handling of gold/silver, and listing fees."},{"t":"Expenses","q":"For TER purposes, a scheme is treated as equity-oriented if it invests at least what share of net assets in equity?","o":["80%","65%","75%","50%"],"a":1,"e":"A minimum of 65% in equity (excluding index funds and ETFs)."},{"t":"Expenses","q":"The maximum TER for index funds and ETFs is:","o":["0.90% of daily net assets","0.50%","It depends on AUM","1.00%"],"a":0,"e":"Index funds/ETFs: TER including management fee capped at 0.90% (earlier 1.00%)."},{"t":"Expenses","q":"The maximum TER for a fund of funds investing at least 65% in equity-oriented schemes is:","o":["1.85%","2.10%","0.90%","2.25%"],"a":1,"e":"FoF (≥65% equity-oriented): 2.10% including weighted TER of underlying schemes. Other FoFs 1.85%; FoFs into liquid/index/ETFs 0.90%."},{"t":"Expenses","q":"For an open-ended equity-oriented scheme, the TER limit on the first Rs 500 crore of daily net assets is:","o":["2.10%","1.85%","2.25%","1.90%"],"a":0,"e":"Equity: 2.10% on the first Rs 500 cr; other schemes: 1.85%."},{"t":"Expenses","q":"An open-ended equity scheme has daily net assets of Rs 600 crore. Its maximum annual TER amount (excluding permitted extras) is:","o":["Rs 12.60 crore","Rs 12.40 crore","Rs 13.25 crore","Rs 12.00 crore"],"a":1,"e":"500 × 2.10% = 10.50 + 100 × 1.90% = 1.90; total Rs 12.40 crore."},{"t":"Expenses","q":"The maximum TER of a close-ended equity-oriented scheme is:","o":["1.00%","0.80%","2.10%","1.25%"],"a":0,"e":"Close-ended/interval: equity 1.00%, others 0.80%."},{"t":"Expenses","q":"Brokerage for cash market trades can be charged in addition to the TER limits up to:","o":["No limit","0.12% of trade value","0.02% of trade value","0.06% of trade value"],"a":3,"e":"0.06% for cash market and 0.02% for derivatives."},{"t":"Expenses","q":"Under the March 2026 workbook, which charges may be levied on investors of a scheme?","o":["Only base expense ratio, brokerage, transaction cost, statutory levy and exit load","Any expense the AMC incurs","Base expense ratio plus an extra 0.05% for schemes with exit load","Entry load"],"a":0,"e":"No charges other than BER, brokerage, transaction cost, statutory levy and exit load may be charged. The old extra 0.05% is no longer in the workbook."},{"t":"Expenses","q":"For the balance of assets beyond all slabs, the TER limit for non-equity open-ended schemes is:","o":["0.70%","0.95%","0.80%","1.05%"],"a":0,"e":"Balance of assets: equity 0.95%, other schemes 0.70%."},{"t":"AT1 bonds","q":"A mutual fund scheme can invest at most what share of its assets in AT1/AT2 perpetual bonds?","o":["15%","5%","10%","25%"],"a":2,"e":"Scheme limit 10% of assets, and 5% in such bonds of a single issuer."},{"t":"AT1 bonds","q":"From 1 April 2023, the deemed residual maturity for valuing AT1 bonds is:","o":["30 years","100 years","10 years","20 years"],"a":1,"e":"100 years for AT1; contractual maturity for AT2."},{"t":"AT1 bonds","q":"AT1 bonds held by mutual funds are valued on which basis?","o":["Book value","Face value","Yield to call","Yield to maturity"],"a":2,"e":"SEBI aligned AT1 valuation to yield to call."},{"t":"Dividends","q":"Dividends (IDCW) can be paid only out of:","o":["Mark-to-market profits","Distributable surplus","Unit capital","Premium reserve"],"a":1,"e":"Workbook sample Q4: distributable surplus."},{"t":"Dividends","q":"While computing distributable reserves:","o":["Valuation gains and losses are both included","Valuation gains are ignored but valuation losses are adjusted","Both are ignored","Only valuation gains are counted"],"a":1,"e":"A conservative approach so dividends come from real, realised profits."},{"t":"Dividends","q":"The record date for an IDCW payout must be how many days from the AMC's notice?","o":["10 days","1 calendar day","3 working days","5 calendar days"],"a":3,"e":"Notice within one day of trustees' decision; record date five calendar days from the notice."},{"t":"Dividends","q":"Which scheme does NOT need a public notice before declaring dividends?","o":["An ELSS","An equity fund paying quarterly","A liquid fund with daily dividend frequency","A balanced advantage fund paying annually"],"a":2,"e":"Liquid and debt schemes with daily to monthly frequency are exempt from the notice."},{"t":"Loads","q":"NAV is Rs 50 and exit load is 1%. The repurchase price is:","o":["Rs 50.00","Rs 49.00","Rs 49.50","Rs 50.50"],"a":2,"e":"Repurchase price = 50 × (1 − 0.01) = Rs 49.50."},{"t":"Loads","q":"An investor invests Rs 1,00,000 at NAV Rs 40. Ignoring stamp duty, units allotted are:","o":["2,500","2,000","4,000","40,000"],"a":0,"e":"No entry load, so units = 1,00,000 ÷ 40 = 2,500."},{"t":"Loads","q":"Exit load is NOT charged on:","o":["Large redemptions","Units held less than one year","Units of equity funds","Bonus units and IDCW reinvestment units"],"a":3,"e":"No exit load on bonus units or units from dividend reinvestment."},{"t":"Loads","q":"Exit load collected by a scheme is:","o":["Kept by the AMC for selling expenses","Paid to SEBI","Paid to distributors","Credited back to the scheme"],"a":3,"e":"Exit loads are credited back to the scheme immediately."},{"t":"Accounting","q":"NAV of liquid and debt funds is computed up to:","o":["3 decimal places","4 decimal places","2 decimal places","Whole rupees"],"a":1,"e":"4 decimals for index, liquid and debt funds; at least 2 for equity and balanced funds."},{"t":"Valuation policy","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. If the fair valuation principles conflict with the Valuation Guidelines, the principles prevail.\nII. The valuation policy must be reviewed by an independent auditor at least once every two years.\nIII. The valuation policy is disclosed in the SAI and on the website.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the 10 principles prevail over the Valuation Guidelines. II is false: the policy is reviewed by an independent auditor at least once a year. III is true: it is disclosed in the SAI and on the website."},{"t":"Equity valuation","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A traded equity share is valued at its last closing price on the principal stock exchange.\nStatement 2: If the share is not traded on the valuation day, the price of the earliest previous day may be used provided it is within 60 days.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: traded equity uses the last closing price on the principal exchange. Statement 2 is false: another exchange, or the earliest previous day within 30 days (not 60), is used."},{"t":"Non-traded securities","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. An equity share with no trade for 30 days is treated as non-traded.\nII. Non-traded equity is valued by capitalisation of earnings, with an illiquidity premium added.\nIII. Warrants are valued as share value minus exercise price minus a discount.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: no trade for 30 days makes a security non-traded. II is false: an illiquidity discount is deducted, not a premium added. III is true: warrants = share value − exercise price − discount."},{"t":"Gold/silver ETF","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Gold held by a Gold ETF is valued using the LBMA PM fixing","Gold held by a Gold ETF is valued at 999.0 fineness","Silver held by a Silver ETF is valued using the LBMA AM fixing at 999.0 fineness","Gold held by a Gold ETF is valued in rupees per 10 grams"],"a":2,"e":"Only the Silver ETF statement is true (LBMA AM fixing, 999.0 fineness). Gold ETF uses the LBMA AM (not PM) fixing, at 995.0 (not 999.0) fineness, in USD per troy ounce (not rupees per 10 grams)."},{"t":"NAV","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Net assets used for NAV include unrealised gains.\nII. Under the accrual principle, income is counted only when it is actually received.\nIII. A scheme may borrow short term for liquidity for up to 6 months.","o":["Only I and II","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: net assets include unrealised gains. II is false: under accrual, income and expenses count when due, not when paid or received. III is true: short-term borrowing for liquidity is limited to 6 months."},{"t":"Recurring expenses","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Spending above the base expense limits may be charged to the scheme if the trustees approve it.\nStatement 2: Storage of gold and silver and listing fees are allowed recurring expenses of a scheme.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: spending above the base limits is borne by the AMC, trustees or sponsors, not the scheme. Statement 2 is true: storage of gold and silver and listing fee are in the allowed list (a)–(o)."},{"t":"TER limits","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. For TER purposes, an equity-oriented scheme is one with at least 65% in equity, excluding index funds and ETFs.\nII. The TER of an index fund or ETF cannot exceed 0.90%.\nIII. A close-ended equity scheme can charge a TER of up to 1.25%.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: equity-oriented for TER means ≥65% in equity, excluding index funds and ETFs. II is true: index fund/ETF TER ≤0.90%. III is false: close-ended and interval equity schemes are capped at 1.00% (others 0.80%)."},{"t":"TER slabs","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements about open-ended scheme TER slabs is/are TRUE?\nI. On the first Rs 500 cr, an equity scheme can charge up to 2.10%.\nII. On the first Rs 500 cr, a non-equity scheme can charge up to 1.85%.\nIII. On the next Rs 250 cr, the equity limit is 1.60%.","o":["Only I and II","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I and II are true: the first Rs 500 cr slab is 2.10% equity and 1.85% other. III is false: the next Rs 250 cr slab is 1.90% equity (1.65% other); 1.60% applies to the following Rs 1,250 cr."},{"t":"Brokerage","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Over and above the expense limits, brokerage is capped at 0.06% of trade value for cash market trades and 0.02% for derivatives.\nStatement 2: Apart from the base expense limit, brokerage, transaction cost and statutory levies, the only other charge to investors is exit load.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 1 is true: brokerage ≤0.06% (cash) and ≤0.02% (derivatives). Statement 2 is true: total charged = base limit + brokerage + transaction cost + statutory levies, and nothing else except exit load."},{"t":"AT1 and AT2","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. From 1 April 2023, the deemed maturity of AT1 bonds is 100 years from the date of issue.\nII. The deemed maturity of AT2 bonds is also 100 years from the date of issue.\nIII. Close-ended debt schemes can invest in perpetual bonds.","o":["Only II","Only III","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":3,"e":"I is true: AT1 deemed maturity is 100 years from issue. II is false: AT2 deemed maturity is its contractual maturity. III is false: close-ended debt schemes can't buy perpetuals."},{"t":"IDCW process","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The AMC Board decides the IDCW amount and the record date.\nII. A public notice of the IDCW must be issued within 1 day of the decision.\nIII. The record date is 5 calendar days from the issue of the notice.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is false: the trustees decide the amount and record date. II is true: notice within 1 day of the decision. III is true: record date is 5 calendar days from the notice."},{"t":"IDCW notice","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: No public notice is needed for daily-to-monthly IDCW payouts in equity schemes.\nStatement 2: Interest on a late IDCW payment runs from the date the public notice was issued.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":1,"e":"Statement 1 is false: the no-notice relief for daily-to-monthly payouts applies to liquid and debt schemes. Statement 2 is false: interest on late payment runs from the record date."},{"t":"Exit load","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Exit load can differ according to the amount invested.\nII. Changes in exit load apply only prospectively.\nIII. Exit load is not charged on bonus units or IDCW-reinvestment units.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is false: exit load makes no distinction by amount. II is true: changes apply only prospectively. III is true: no exit load on bonus or IDCW-reinvestment units."},{"t":"Scheme accounting","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["Scheme accounts are kept separate from the AMC's accounts, with different auditors","NAV of index, liquid and debt funds is computed to 4 decimal places","NAV of equity and balanced funds is computed to at least 2 decimal places","Fractional units are not allowed, so units are always allotted in whole numbers"],"a":3,"e":"The first three are correct: separate accounts with different auditors; 4 decimals for index, liquid and debt funds; at least 2 for equity and balanced funds. The last is wrong: fractional units are allowed, though exchanges may trade whole units only."},{"t":"NAV formula","y":"concept","q":"In the NAV formula, the liabilities deducted from total assets to arrive at net assets are:","o":["All liabilities, including amounts owed to unitholders","Liabilities other than those to unitholders","Only short-term borrowings","Only accrued management fees"],"a":1,"e":"NAV = (total assets − liabilities other than to unitholders) ÷ units outstanding. Amounts owed to unitholders are what net assets represent, so they are not deducted."},{"t":"Dilution","y":"concept","q":"Mark-to-market pricing protects existing investors from dilution. If a scheme issued new units at a price below NAV, the effect on existing investors would be that:","o":["Their NAV rises","Their NAV is unchanged because net assets also rise","Their NAV falls","Only the new investors' NAV falls"],"a":2,"e":"Issuing units below NAV lowers the NAV for existing investors (and redeeming below NAV raises it); pricing at NAV prevents this dilution."},{"t":"Distributable surplus","y":"concept","q":"While computing the distributable surplus from which IDCW can be paid, valuation gains and valuation losses are treated as follows:","o":["Valuation gains are ignored; valuation losses are deducted","Both valuation gains and losses are ignored","Valuation gains are added; valuation losses are deducted","Valuation gains are added; valuation losses are ignored"],"a":0,"e":"IDCW is paid only from distributable surplus, which ignores valuation gains but deducts valuation losses."},{"t":"Repurchase price","y":"concept","q":"When a scheme charges an exit load, the repurchase price paid to the investor is computed as:","o":["NAV × (1 + exit load)","NAV + stamp duty − exit load","NAV − (exit load × face value)","NAV × (1 − exit load)"],"a":3,"e":"Repurchase price = NAV × (1 − load); e.g. NAV Rs 11 with 1% exit load = Rs 10.89. The load is credited back to the scheme."}]},"ch8":{"id":"ch8","label":"Ch 8 · Taxation","short":"Ch 8","kind":"chapter","weight":4,"qs":[{"t":"Scheme income","q":"What is the tax applicable on income earned by mutual fund schemes?","o":["Depends on type of income","Marginal rate of each investor","Exempt from tax","10% plus surcharge and cess"],"a":2,"e":"MF income is exempt under Section 10(23D) as MFs are trusts (workbook sample Q1)."},{"t":"Classification","q":"For income tax, a scheme is equity-oriented if it holds more than what share of assets in listed Indian equity?","o":["50%","80%","65%","75%"],"a":2,"e":"More than 65% in equity shares listed on recognised Indian exchanges."},{"t":"Classification","q":"A fund of funds investing in equity mutual fund schemes is treated for tax as:","o":["Exempt","Equity-oriented","Non-equity-oriented","Hybrid"],"a":2,"e":"Though it ultimately holds equity, a FoF is a non-equity-oriented scheme for tax."},{"t":"Classification","q":"In a joint holding, capital gains and dividends are taxed in the hands of:","o":["The first holder","The youngest holder","The nominee","All holders equally"],"a":0,"e":"Income is considered earned by the first holder."},{"t":"Holding period","q":"Units of an equity-oriented fund become long-term after being held for more than:","o":["12 months","36 months","6 months","24 months"],"a":0,"e":"12 months for listed securities and equity funds; 24 months for other assets."},{"t":"Capital gains","q":"An investor bought equity fund units for Rs 1,00,000 in 2017. Value on 31 Jan 2018 was Rs 2,00,000. He sold for Rs 2,20,000 in Feb 2018. Taxable LTCG is:","o":["Rs 2,20,000","Nil","Rs 1,20,000","Rs 20,000"],"a":3,"e":"Grandfathering: cost = higher of cost or 31 Jan 2018 value, so gain = 2,20,000 − 2,00,000."},{"t":"Capital gains","q":"Same investor sells for Rs 1,80,000 instead. Taxable LTCG is:","o":["Rs 80,000","A loss of Rs 20,000","Rs 1,80,000","Nil"],"a":3,"e":"Realised value is below the grandfathered value, so LTCG for tax is considered zero."},{"t":"Capital gains","q":"An investor has LTCG of Rs 1,40,000 from equity funds in a year. The amount subject to tax is:","o":["Nil","Rs 15,000","Rs 40,000","Rs 1,40,000"],"a":1,"e":"First Rs 1.25 lakh is exempt, so Rs 15,000 is taxable."},{"t":"Dividends","q":"Since the Union Budget of February 2020, dividends from mutual funds are:","o":["Taxed at a flat 10%","Exempt for all","Taxable in the investor's hands at applicable slab rates","Tax free for investors after DDT"],"a":2,"e":"DDT was removed; dividends are added to the investor's taxable income."},{"t":"Dividends","q":"Why is the growth option considered more tax-efficient than IDCW?","o":["Growth units pay no STT","Growth units are exempt from tax","Growth has no exit load","Tax is deferred until redemption, allowing pre-tax compounding"],"a":3,"e":"Schemes are tax-exempt and gains are taxed only when realised."},{"t":"Stamp duty","q":"Stamp duty on issue of mutual fund units (purchase, SIP, switch-in) is:","o":["0.001%","0.005%","0.015%","0.1%"],"a":1,"e":"0.005% on issue since 1 July 2020; transfers attract 0.015%."},{"t":"Stamp duty","q":"Stamp duty applies to which transaction?","o":["IDCW reinvestment","SWP","Payout of dividend","Redemption"],"a":0,"e":"All inflows: purchase, additional purchase, IDCW reinvestment, SIP, STP, DTP."},{"t":"Set-off","q":"A short-term capital loss on mutual fund units can be set off against:","o":["Salary income","Only short-term gains","Short-term or long-term capital gains","Any income"],"a":2,"e":"STCL can be set off against STCG or LTCG; LTCL only against LTCG; no set-off against other heads."},{"t":"Set-off","q":"A long-term capital loss can be set off against:","o":["Long-term capital gains only","House property income","Short-term capital gains","Salary"],"a":0,"e":"LTCL only against LTCG."},{"t":"Bonus stripping","q":"Under bonus stripping rules, a loss is disallowed if units were bought within ___ before the bonus record date and sold within ___ after.","o":["1 month; 3 months","6 months; 12 months","9 months; 3 months","3 months; 9 months"],"a":3,"e":"3 months before and 9 months after; the loss becomes the cost of the bonus units."},{"t":"STT","q":"Redemption from which scheme attracts STT?","o":["Liquid fund","Multi-cap fund","Gilt fund","Overnight fund"],"a":1,"e":"STT applies only on sale/redemption of equity-oriented fund units (workbook sample Q2)."},{"t":"STT","q":"STT on redemption of equity fund units to the mutual fund is:","o":["0.001%, paid by the seller","0.1%","Nil","0.025%, paid by the buyer"],"a":0,"e":"0.001% on redemption and on delivery-based exchange sale; 0.025% on non-delivery sale."},{"t":"ELSS","q":"An investor buys ELSS through monthly SIPs starting January 2024. When is the January 2025 instalment free of lock-in?","o":["January 2027","January 2028","Immediately","January 2026"],"a":1,"e":"Each SIP instalment has its own 3-year lock-in from its date."},{"t":"ELSS","q":"An investor who has opted for the new tax regime invests in ELSS. He gets:","o":["80C deduction up to Rs 1.5 lakh","Double deduction","Deduction under 80D","No 80C deduction; ELSS is just an equity fund with a lock-in"],"a":3,"e":"The new regime removes most deductions, including 80C."},{"t":"TDS","q":"For a resident investor, TDS on mutual fund dividends applies at 10% when annual dividend exceeds:","o":["Rs 50,000","Rs 5,000","Rs 10,000","Rs 2,500"],"a":2,"e":"10% TDS if IDCW exceeds Rs 10,000 in a year; no TDS on redemption proceeds for residents."},{"t":"Scheme income","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Mutual fund scheme income is tax exempt under Sec 10(23D) because mutual funds are trusts.\nII. The growth option gives only capital gains, while the IDCW option gives dividend plus capital gains.\nIII. In a joint holding, income is taxed equally in the hands of all the holders.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: Sec 10(23D) exempts MF income as MFs are trusts. II is true: growth = capital gains only; IDCW = dividend + capital gains. III is false: income is taxed in the first holder's hands."},{"t":"Equity-oriented","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: A fund is equity-oriented for tax if it invests more than 65% in listed Indian equity.\nStatement 2: A fund of funds investing in equity mutual funds is treated as equity-oriented for tax.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: equity-oriented = >65% in listed Indian equity. Statement 2 is false: a FoF investing in equity MFs is non-equity for tax."},{"t":"Holding periods","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["Equity-oriented funds become long-term after 12 months.","A hybrid fund with 35-65% domestic equity becomes long-term after 12 months.","Equity-oriented funds: STCG is taxed at 20% and LTCG at 12.5%.","Gains on debt-oriented funds are taxed at the marginal slab rate."],"a":1,"e":"A hybrid with 35-65% domestic equity becomes long-term after 24 months (taxed MSR / 12.5%); the other three statements match the workbook table."},{"t":"Bonus stripping","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Under bonus stripping rules, a loss is disallowed if units were bought within 9 months before the record date and sold within 3 months after it.\nStatement 2: A loss disallowed under bonus stripping becomes the cost of the bonus units.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: the window is bought up to 3 months before the record date and sold up to 9 months after. Statement 2 is true: the disallowed loss becomes the cost of the bonus units."},{"t":"IDCW taxation","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. DDT was abolished by the Budget of February 2020; since April 2020, IDCW is taxed at the investor's slab rate.\nII. An IDCW reduces the NAV by less than the investor's post-tax receipt.\nIII. The growth option is more tax-efficient than IDCW because of tax deferral.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and II"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: DDT went in Feb 2020 and IDCW is taxed at slab from April 2020. II is false: IDCW reduces NAV by more than the post-tax receipt. III is true: growth is more tax-efficient due to deferral."},{"t":"Stamp duty","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Stamp duty of 0.005% applies on issue of units, including SIP, STP, switch-in and IDCW reinvestment.","Stamp duty on transfer of units is 0.005%.","Units are allotted on the investment amount before deducting stamp duty.","Stamp duty on mutual fund units applies from 1 April 2020."],"a":0,"e":"Stamp duty is 0.005% on issue and 0.015% on transfer, applies from 1 July 2020, and units are allotted on the amount after stamp duty."},{"t":"Loss set-off","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A capital loss on mutual fund units can be set off against salary income.\nII. A short-term capital loss can be set off against STCG or LTCG.\nIII. Unabsorbed capital loss can be carried forward for up to 8 years.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is false: capital loss can't be set off against salary or other heads. II is true: STCL sets off against STCG or LTCG. III is true: carry forward is up to 8 years."},{"t":"STT and ELSS","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: STT applies on both purchase and redemption of units of equity-oriented funds.\nStatement 2: The Sec 80C deduction of up to Rs 1.5 lakh for ELSS is available only under the Old Tax Regime.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: STT applies only on sale or redemption of equity-oriented funds, none on purchase. Statement 2 is true: ELSS 80C up to Rs 1.5 lakh is Old Tax Regime only."},{"t":"Grandfathering","y":"concept","q":"Under grandfathering, the cost of acquisition of equity fund units bought before 31 Jan 2018 is taken as:","o":["The actual purchase cost only","The NAV on 31 Jan 2018 only","The higher of the actual cost or the NAV on 31 Jan 2018","The lower of the actual cost or the NAV on 31 Jan 2018"],"a":2,"e":"Gains up to 31 Jan 2018 are exempt, so cost = higher of actual cost or 31 Jan 2018 NAV (e.g. cost Rs 1 lakh, 31 Jan 2018 value Rs 2 lakh, sold at Rs 1.8 lakh = zero gain)."},{"t":"IDCW split","y":"concept","q":"From 1 April 2021, for tax purposes an IDCW payout is split into:","o":["Income distribution and capital distribution","Dividend and bonus","Short-term and long-term capital gains","Taxable dividend and exempt dividend"],"a":0,"e":"From 1 Apr 2021, IDCW is split into income distribution and capital distribution for tax."}]},"ch9":{"id":"ch9","label":"Ch 9 · Investor Services","short":"Ch 9","kind":"chapter","weight":15,"qs":[{"t":"NFO","q":"The decision on which scheme to take to market is based on inputs from the:","o":["CFO and HR head","CIO and CMO","Trustees and custodian","SEBI and AMFI"],"a":1,"e":"CIO gives inputs on investment objectives; CMO on market interest."},{"t":"NFO","q":"The SID for an NFO must be approved by:","o":["AMFI","SEBI","The RTA","The Trustees and the AMC Board"],"a":3,"e":"The SID is approved by the Trustees and AMC Board, then filed with SEBI."},{"t":"NFO","q":"Which date does a close-ended scheme NOT have?","o":["Allotment date","NFO open date","Scheme re-opening date","NFO close date"],"a":2,"e":"Close-ended schemes do not sell or repurchase after NFO, so there is no re-opening date."},{"t":"NFO","q":"NFOs (other than ELSS) can remain open for a maximum of:","o":["7 days","15 days","30 days","45 days"],"a":1,"e":"Minimum 3 working days; maximum 15 days (ELSS exempt from the maximum)."},{"t":"NFO","q":"The minimum period an NFO must stay open is:","o":["1 working day","7 days","5 working days","3 working days"],"a":3,"e":"All NFOs remain open for at least three working days."},{"t":"NFO","q":"Units must be allotted or money refunded within ___ of NFO closure.","o":["10 days","5 business days","2 business days","15 days"],"a":1,"e":"Allotment/refund within 5 business days."},{"t":"NFO","q":"An open-ended scheme must re-open for ongoing sale and repurchase within:","o":["2 business days of allotment","30 days of NFO close","5 business days of allotment","15 days of NFO close"],"a":2,"e":"Within 5 business days of allotment."},{"t":"NFO","q":"If an NFO refund is delayed beyond 5 business days, interest is payable at:","o":["15% p.a., borne by the AMC","No interest","6% p.a., charged to the scheme","12% p.a., borne by trustees"],"a":0,"e":"15% p.a. for the delay, charged to the AMC, not the scheme."},{"t":"NFO","q":"An investor puts Rs 50,000 in an NFO. Ignoring stamp duty, units allotted are:","o":["Depends on NAV at close","50,000","500","5,000"],"a":3,"e":"NFO units are at face value Rs 10: 50,000 ÷ 10 = 5,000."},{"t":"NFO","q":"The key benefit of ASBA for an NFO investor is:","o":["Guaranteed allotment","Money is only blocked, keeps earning interest and is debited only on allotment","No KYC needed","Higher NAV"],"a":1,"e":"With ASBA the exact amount is debited on allotment; no refund wait."},{"t":"Plans","q":"Which statement about Direct Plans is correct?","o":["Same NAV as Regular Plan","Higher TER than Regular","Available through distributors","Lower expense ratio and no commission, so a separate (higher) NAV"],"a":3,"e":"Direct plans exclude distribution costs, have lower TER and a separate NAV."},{"t":"Plans","q":"An investor routes his purchase through a distributor but asks for the Direct Plan. He will be treated as investing in:","o":["Direct Plan","Regular Plan","Both","Neither"],"a":1,"e":"Anything routed through a distributor is Regular Plan."},{"t":"Plans","q":"An investor investing directly must write what in the ARN box of the form?","o":["Direct","His PAN","AMC name","Nil"],"a":0,"e":"He writes \"Direct\" in the space for the ARN."},{"t":"Options","q":"The NAV after a dividend has been paid out is called:","o":["Lower NAV","Cum-dividend NAV","Ex-dividend NAV","Dividend NAV"],"a":2,"e":"Workbook sample Q1: ex-dividend NAV."},{"t":"Options","q":"Under the IDCW payout option, the number of units held by the investor:","o":["Increases","Decreases","Doubles","Remains unchanged"],"a":3,"e":"Cash is paid out; units stay the same while NAV falls."},{"t":"Options","q":"An investor holds 100 units. Dividend is Rs 2 per unit and ex-dividend NAV is Rs 20. Under IDCW reinvestment, additional units are:","o":["2","5","20","10"],"a":3,"e":"Rs 200 ÷ Rs 20 = 10 units."},{"t":"Options","q":"NAV rose from Rs 10 to Rs 12 and a 10% dividend (on face value) is declared. The reinvesting investor gets new units at:","o":["Rs 12","Rs 10","Rs 13","Rs 11"],"a":3,"e":"Dividend = 10% of Rs 10 = Re 1; ex-dividend NAV = 12 − 1 = Rs 11."},{"t":"Options","q":"In the same example, an investor with 100 units in the reinvestment option gets how many extra units?","o":["8.33","11.00","10.00","9.09"],"a":3,"e":"Rs 100 ÷ Rs 11 = 9.09 units."},{"t":"Options","q":"Across growth, IDCW payout and IDCW reinvestment options of one scheme, which is the same?","o":["Post-tax returns","Portfolio returns","NAV","Number of units"],"a":1,"e":"They share the same portfolio; cash flows, units, NAV and taxes differ."},{"t":"Allotment","q":"An investor invests Rs 12,000 in an open-ended scheme where the applicable NAV is Rs 12. Units allotted:","o":["1,000","1,200","100","12,000"],"a":0,"e":"No entry load: 12,000 ÷ 12 = 1,000 units."},{"t":"Allotment","q":"At what price are bonus units issued?","o":["Free of cost","Decided by AMC and trustees","Prevailing NAV","NAV divided by bonus ratio"],"a":0,"e":"Bonus units are allotted free (workbook sample Q2)."},{"t":"Allotment","q":"An investor holds 300 units at NAV Rs 40. After a 1:3 bonus, his holding and NAV will be about:","o":["400 units at Rs 30","300 units at Rs 40","400 units at Rs 40","600 units at Rs 20"],"a":0,"e":"1 new unit per 3: 100 new units; value 12,000 stays same, so NAV = 12,000 ÷ 400 = Rs 30."},{"t":"Allotment","q":"The Rs 10 lakh minimum for SIF strategies is computed:","o":["Per folio","Per scheme","Including other MF schemes","At PAN level across all SIF strategies of the SIF"],"a":3,"e":"Aggregate across SIF strategies at PAN level; other MF schemes are not counted."},{"t":"Statements","q":"A consolidated account statement (CAS) is sent monthly to investors who:","o":["Have any holding","Hold only equity funds","Have registered email only","Had a transaction in that month"],"a":3,"e":"Monthly CAS for folios with transactions; half-yearly CAS for others."},{"t":"Statements","q":"Common investors across fund houses are identified for CAS using:","o":["PAN","Folio number","Aadhaar","Mobile number"],"a":0,"e":"AMCs identify common investors by PAN."},{"t":"Statements","q":"For CAS purposes, which is NOT a \"transaction\"?","o":["SIP","Bonus","Dividend reinvestment","Change of address"],"a":3,"e":"Transactions include purchase, redemption, switch, IDCW, SIP, SWP, STP and bonus."},{"t":"Investors","q":"Which statement is true?","o":["HUFs cannot invest","Foreign investors can invest after completing KYC","NRIs cannot invest","Minors cannot invest"],"a":1,"e":"Workbook sample Q3."},{"t":"Investors","q":"The maximum number of joint holders in a mutual fund folio is:","o":["3","5","4","2"],"a":0,"e":"Up to three holders."},{"t":"Investors","q":"An HUF invests in mutual funds through its:","o":["Any coparcener","Karta","Trustee","Eldest son"],"a":1,"e":"The Karta invests on behalf of the HUF."},{"t":"Investors","q":"QFIs investing in Indian MFs must be from a country that is a member of:","o":["OECD only","SAARC","FATF and a signatory to IOSCO MMOU","WTO and IMF"],"a":2,"e":"FATF membership (or of a group that is) and IOSCO MMOU signatory."},{"t":"Investors","q":"FPIs can hold MF units through the indirect route using:","o":["Demat account","Physical certificates","Unit Confirmation Receipt (UCR)","Bank FD"],"a":2,"e":"Direct route: demat; indirect route: UCR."},{"t":"Form","q":"All benefits of a jointly held folio (dividends, redemptions, tax benefits) go to:","o":["The nominee","All holders equally","The youngest holder","The first holder"],"a":3,"e":"The folio is in the first holder's name."},{"t":"Form","q":"Which is true of an investment in the name of a minor?","o":["Cannot have joint holders","Guardian need not do KYC","Needs no proof of age","Can have up to 3 joint holders"],"a":0,"e":"A minor's folio cannot have joint holders; date of birth proof is needed."},{"t":"Form","q":"A PoA holder operating a folio can do all transactions except:","o":["Purchase","Switch","Make or change nominations","Redemption"],"a":2,"e":"The PoA holder cannot make or change nominations."},{"t":"Form","q":"In a jointly held folio, can a new joint holder be added later?","o":["Yes, with SEBI approval","Yes, anytime","Only for minors","No; only a deletion on death is allowed"],"a":3,"e":"Joint holders cannot be added or deleted except deletion on death."},{"t":"Form","q":"Once the first holder's PAN is validated for KYC, which address prevails?","o":["Bank address","Address in the KYC records","The distributor's address","Address in the application form"],"a":1,"e":"The KYC address overrides the application form address."},{"t":"Form","q":"For a different payout account, a bank statement submitted as proof should have entries not older than:","o":["3 months","1 year","6 months","1 month"],"a":0,"e":"Self-attested passbook/statement with entries not older than 3 months."},{"t":"Form","q":"Nomination for folios with joint holding is:","o":["Mandatory","Prohibited","Optional","Required only for NRIs"],"a":2,"e":"Compulsory nomination/opt-out declaration is optional for joint folios."},{"t":"Redemption","q":"Units are redeemed in which order?","o":["Highest cost first","LIFO","Investor's choice only","FIFO"],"a":3,"e":"Redemption is on a First-In-First-Out basis."},{"t":"Redemption","q":"Units held in demat form are redeemed through:","o":["The AMC branch","The distributor","The Depository Participant","SEBI"],"a":2,"e":"Demat redemption requests go through the DP (or exchange platform)."},{"t":"Payments","q":"Which payment mode is NOT allowed for mutual fund purchases?","o":["Debit card","Credit card","Net banking","UPI"],"a":1,"e":"Mutual fund purchases using credit cards are not allowed."},{"t":"Payments","q":"NACH was launched by:","o":["SEBI","RBI","NSDL","NPCI"],"a":3,"e":"National Automated Clearing House is an NPCI system replacing ECS."},{"t":"Payments","q":"The maximum subscription through e-wallets and cash together per investor per mutual fund per financial year is:","o":["Rs 20,000","Rs 2,00,000","Rs 50,000","Rs 1,00,000"],"a":2,"e":"Umbrella limit of Rs 50,000 for e-wallet and cash."},{"t":"Payments","q":"Money loaded into an e-wallet by which method can be used for MF subscription?","o":["Promotional offer","Credit card","Net banking","Cashback"],"a":2,"e":"Only cash, debit card or net banking loads are allowed."},{"t":"Payments","q":"Cash investments in MFs are allowed for:","o":["Companies","Resident individuals, sole proprietorships and minors via guardians","NRIs","Trusts"],"a":1,"e":"Up to Rs 50,000 per MF per FY for these small investors, with KYC."},{"t":"Payments","q":"A One-Time Mandate (OTM) is registered at the level of:","o":["The folio","The AMC for all investors","Each SIP","Each scheme"],"a":0,"e":"OTM is at folio level and can be used for all purchases under the folio."},{"t":"Payments","q":"A cheque becomes \"stale\" for MF purposes if it is older than:","o":["3 months","1 month","1 year","6 months"],"a":0,"e":"Cheque date should not be more than 3 months old."},{"t":"Payments","q":"An NRI wants to invest on a repatriable basis. He must pay from:","o":["Any Indian account","NRO account only","Cash","NRE or FCNR account"],"a":3,"e":"Repatriable: NRE/FCNR; non-repatriable: NRO/NRE/FCNR."},{"t":"Third party","q":"A parent or related person can pay for a minor's MF investment as a third-party payment up to:","o":["Rs 25,000 per purchase","Rs 50,000 per purchase or SIP instalment","No limit","Rs 1 lakh per year"],"a":1,"e":"Up to Rs 50,000 per regular purchase or SIP instalment, with KYC and a Third Party Declaration."},{"t":"Third party","q":"Which is an accepted third-party payment?","o":["Friend paying for an adult","Employer paying via payroll deduction for an employee","Distributor paying for a client","Company paying for a supplier"],"a":1,"e":"Employer payroll deductions and custodians for FPIs are permitted."},{"t":"Bank accounts","q":"How many bank accounts can a resident individual register with a mutual fund?","o":["1","5","2","3"],"a":1,"e":"5 for individuals, 10 for non-individuals (workbook sample Q5)."},{"t":"Bank accounts","q":"A redemption request asks for credit to a bank account not registered with the MF. The MF will:","o":["Reject the redemption","Pay by cash","Credit the default registered account","Credit that account"],"a":2,"e":"Unregistered account requests are not processed; credit goes to the default account."},{"t":"Bank accounts","q":"An NRI invested through an NRO account. Payouts can be registered to:","o":["NRO account","Any foreign account","NRE account","FCNR account"],"a":0,"e":"If paid via NRO, the registered account should be NRO."},{"t":"IAF","q":"An investor holds Rs 40,000 in a liquid fund. The maximum Instant Access Facility redemption in a day is:","o":["Rs 20,000","Rs 50,000","Rs 36,000","Rs 40,000"],"a":2,"e":"Lower of Rs 50,000 or 90% of value = 36,000."},{"t":"IAF","q":"Instant Access Facility can be offered in:","o":["All debt funds","Equity funds","ELSS","Liquid and overnight schemes"],"a":3,"e":"Only in liquid and overnight schemes."},{"t":"Cut-off","q":"The cut-off time for purchases in an equity fund is:","o":["2:00 pm","5:00 pm","3:00 pm","1:30 pm"],"a":2,"e":"3:00 pm for equity and non-liquid debt purchases."},{"t":"Cut-off","q":"For purchases in an equity fund, the applicable NAV depends on:","o":["Time of application only","The distributor's location","The day funds are available for utilisation before cut-off","The amount invested"],"a":2,"e":"For any amount, NAV of the day funds are realised before cut-off (since Feb 2021)."},{"t":"Cut-off","q":"Cut-off time for purchases in liquid and overnight funds is:","o":["7:00 pm","1:30 pm","12:00 noon","3:00 pm"],"a":1,"e":"1:30 pm for liquid and overnight fund purchases."},{"t":"Cut-off","q":"Funds for a liquid fund purchase are realised at 11 am Monday (a business day). Applicable NAV is of:","o":["Monday","Tuesday","Previous Friday","Sunday (day before receipt)"],"a":3,"e":"Before cut-off: closing NAV of the day immediately preceding the day of receipt."},{"t":"Cut-off","q":"An equity fund redemption request is submitted at 4 pm on a business day. Applicable NAV is:","o":["Same day","Next business day","Previous day","Two days later"],"a":1,"e":"After 3 pm cut-off: next business day NAV."},{"t":"Cut-off","q":"Online redemption requests in overnight funds have an extended cut-off of:","o":["11:59 pm","3:00 pm","7:00 pm","5:00 pm"],"a":2,"e":"Online applications for overnight funds: cut-off extended to 7:00 pm."},{"t":"Cut-off","q":"Cut-off timings do NOT apply to:","o":["NFOs and international schemes","Equity funds","Liquid funds","Debt funds"],"a":0,"e":"The cut-off rules are not applicable to NFOs and international schemes."},{"t":"Time stamping","q":"An investor hands his form to a distributor at 2 pm, who submits it at the OPoA at 3:30 pm. Which time counts?","o":["Midnight","The time the investor signed","3:30 pm","2 pm"],"a":2,"e":"The time of submission at the OPoA is relevant."},{"t":"Time stamping","q":"For online transactions, which time determines the NAV?","o":["Distributor's time","Web server time","Bank time","Investor's device time"],"a":1,"e":"Time as per the web server receiving the instruction."},{"t":"KYC","q":"For an application by a minor, whose KYC is required?","o":["The guardian","Any family member","The minor","No KYC"],"a":0,"e":"Workbook sample Q4: the guardian."},{"t":"KYC","q":"Which investor is exempt from producing PAN?","o":["An NRI","A company","Resident of Sikkim","A trust"],"a":2,"e":"Sikkim residents, government/court officials, UN entities and micro investments up to Rs 50,000 are PAN-exempt."},{"t":"KYC","q":"Which document is accepted as photo ID instead of PAN for micro-SIP KYC?","o":["Photo debit card","Credit card","Library card","Club membership"],"a":0,"e":"Photo debit card is accepted; credit card is not."},{"t":"KYC","q":"Which documents can be used for KYC verification: (i) Voter ID (ii) Driving licence (iii) Government/Defence ID (iv) Passport?","o":["Only i","Only ii and iii","Only ii and iv","Any of the above"],"a":3,"e":"Workbook sample Q7: any of the above."},{"t":"KYC","q":"The Central KYC Records Registry (CKYCR) function is performed by:","o":["AMFI","CDSL","CERSAI","NSDL"],"a":2,"e":"CERSAI was authorised by Government notification of 26 November 2015."},{"t":"KYC","q":"For a company investor, an Ultimate Beneficial Owner is one who holds more than:","o":["51%","15%","10% of shares or profits","25%"],"a":3,"e":"Company: >25%; partnership/body of individuals: >15%; trust: 15%+ beneficiaries plus settlor and trustees."},{"t":"KYC","q":"A change in FATCA/CRS status must be reported to the mutual fund within:","o":["15 days","30 days","7 days","90 days"],"a":1,"e":"Within 30 days."},{"t":"SIP","q":"SIP helps investors because it:","o":["Eliminates exit load","Averages the cost of acquisition below the average NAV (rupee cost averaging)","Avoids all taxes","Guarantees returns"],"a":1,"e":"More units are bought when prices fall and fewer when they rise."},{"t":"SWP","q":"An investor withdraws Rs 1,000 via SWP when NAV is Rs 10 and exit load is 1%. Units redeemed:","o":["101.0101","99.0000","110.0000","100.0000"],"a":0,"e":"Effective price 9.90; 1,000 ÷ 9.90 = 101.0101 units."},{"t":"STP","q":"An STP transfers Rs 1,00,000 from a debt fund with 0.25% exit load. The amount invested in the target equity fund is:","o":["Rs 99,000","Rs 1,00,000","Rs 99,750","Rs 97,500"],"a":2,"e":"Load = Rs 250; transferred amount = Rs 99,750."},{"t":"Top-up","q":"A Rs 2,500 monthly SIP has a 10% annual top-up. The instalment in year 3 is:","o":["Rs 3,025","Rs 3,500","Rs 3,000","Rs 2,750"],"a":0,"e":"Year 2: 2,750; year 3: 2,750 + 275 = Rs 3,025."},{"t":"Nomination","q":"Which of the following is true regarding nominees?","o":["Only individuals incl. minors and NRIs","Only governments and local authorities","Both of these","Neither"],"a":2,"e":"Workbook sample Q6."},{"t":"NFO period","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. An NFO must remain open for at least 3 working days.\nII. An ELSS NFO is exempt from the maximum NFO period of 15 days.\nIII. A close-ended scheme's NFO announces an opening date, a closing date and a re-opening date.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the minimum NFO period is 3 working days. II is true: the 15-day maximum does not apply to ELSS. III is false: only open-ended schemes have a re-opening date; a close-ended scheme has none."},{"t":"NFO allotment","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Units must be allotted, or money refunded, within 5 business days of the NFO closing.\nStatement 2: If the refund is delayed, interest at 15% p.a. is paid out of the scheme.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: allotment or refund is due within 5 business days of NFO close. Statement 2 is false: the 15% p.a. late-refund interest is paid by the AMC, not the scheme."},{"t":"Direct plan","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["Apart from the Direct and Regular plans, a scheme may offer a third plan with a different expense structure.","A Direct plan has a lower TER than the Regular plan of the same scheme.","A Direct plan has a NAV separate from the Regular plan.","An investor applying under the Direct plan writes \"Direct\" in the ARN box."],"a":0,"e":"Only two plans, Direct and Regular, can differ by expense, so the first statement is false. The Direct plan has a lower TER and no commission, has a separate NAV, and is chosen by writing \"Direct\" in the ARN box."},{"t":"IDCW options","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Under the IDCW payout option, the number of units held does not change.\nII. Under the IDCW reinvestment option, the new units are allotted at the cum-dividend NAV.\nIII. The IDCW amount reinvested is still taxed at the investor's slab rate in the year it is received.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: payout sends cash to the bank and units stay unchanged. II is false: reinvestment is at the ex-dividend NAV. III is true: the reinvested IDCW is taxed at slab in the year received."},{"t":"Bonus units","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: In a 1:3 bonus, the investor receives 3 free units for every 1 unit held.\nStatement 2: After a bonus issue, the value of the investor's holding remains unchanged.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: a 1:3 bonus means 1 free unit for every 3 units held. Statement 2 is true: NAV falls proportionately, so the value of the holding is unchanged."},{"t":"CAS","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A Consolidated Account Statement (CAS) is sent monthly if there is any transaction in that month.\nII. If there is no transaction, a CAS is sent half-yearly.\nIII. Investors are matched across mutual funds for the CAS on the basis of their Aadhaar number.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and II","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I and II are true: CAS is monthly when there is a transaction and half-yearly when there is none. III is false: investors are matched by PAN."},{"t":"Service timelines","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The half-yearly portfolio statement must be disclosed within 10 days of the end of the half-year.\nStatement 2: The annual report must be provided within 4 months.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: the portfolio statement is due within 10 days of the half-year and the annual report within 4 months."},{"t":"Eligible investors","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A minor's folio can have joint holders along with the minor.\nII. An HUF invests in mutual funds through its Karta.\nIII. An individual folio can have up to 3 joint holders.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and II"],"a":0,"e":"I is false: a minor invests through a guardian and there are no joint holders. II is true: an HUF invests via the Karta. III is true: individuals can have up to 3 joint holders."},{"t":"Holders and PoA","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["In a joint folio, the first holder receives all benefits such as dividends and redemption proceeds.","A PoA holder can do everything on the investor's behalf, including making a nomination.","Once a PoA is granted, the grantor can no longer operate the folio.","Joint holders can be added to an existing folio on request."],"a":0,"e":"The first holder gets all benefits (dividends, redemptions, tax). A PoA holder can do everything except nominate, and the grantor can still operate; joint holders cannot be added or removed except deletion on death."},{"t":"Foreign investors","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A QFI must be from a FATF member country and a signatory to the IOSCO MMOU.\nStatement 2: An FPI investing through the indirect route holds its units in demat form.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: a QFI must be from a FATF member and an IOSCO MMOU signatory. Statement 2 is false: the direct route uses demat; the indirect route uses a Unit Confirmation Receipt (UCR)."},{"t":"Application form","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The address in the KYC records overrides the address given on the application form.\nII. Bank details of the first holder are optional if the investor gives proof of a different payout account.\nIII. A bank passbook or statement given as proof of a payout account must not be more than 3 months old.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the KYC address overrides the form address. II is false: bank details of the first holder are mandatory. III is true: a passbook or statement used as proof must be not more than 3 months old."},{"t":"E-wallet and cards","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Credit cards are not an allowed mode of payment for mutual fund purchases.\nII. Investments through e-wallet and cash together are capped at Rs 50,000 per investor per mutual fund per financial year.\nIII. E-wallet money loaded through a credit card can be used to buy mutual fund units.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: credit cards are not allowed. II is true: the Rs 50,000 umbrella limit covers e-wallet and cash per investor per MF per FY. III is false: e-wallet money is accepted only if loaded via cash, debit card or net banking."},{"t":"Third-party payment","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["A parent or related person may pay for a minor's investment up to Rs 50,000 per purchase or SIP instalment.","An employer may pay for employees' investments through payroll deductions.","A custodian may make payment on behalf of an FPI.","A spouse may pay for the other spouse's investment up to Rs 50,000 per purchase."],"a":3,"e":"There are only three third-party payment exceptions: a parent or related person for a minor up to Rs 50,000 per purchase or SIP instalment, an employer through payroll, and a custodian for an FPI. A spouse paying is not an exception."},{"t":"Cheques and NRI","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: An NRI investing on a non-repatriable basis can pay only from an NRE account.\nStatement 2: A cheque more than 3 months old is stale and is not accepted.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: non-repatriable investments can also be paid from an NRO account (repatriable ones come from NRE/FCNR). Statement 2 is true: a cheque older than 3 months is stale and not accepted."},{"t":"Bank accounts","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. An individual investor can register up to 5 bank accounts in a folio.\nII. A non-individual investor can register up to 10 bank accounts.\nIII. A redemption payout requested to an unregistered bank account is paid into the default bank account.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"e":"All are true: up to 5 accounts for individuals and 10 for non-individuals, one marked default, and a payout to an unregistered account goes to the default account."},{"t":"Instant access","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Under the Instant Access Facility, an investor can redeem the higher of Rs 50,000 or 90% of the value of the investment per day.\nStatement 2: The Instant Access Facility is offered in liquid and overnight schemes.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: the limit is the lower of Rs 50,000 or 90% of value, per day per scheme per investor. Statement 2 is true: IAF applies to liquid and overnight schemes."},{"t":"Cut-off purchase","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. For equity scheme purchases, the cut-off is 3:00 pm and the NAV applied is of the day the funds are available for use.\nII. For liquid scheme purchases, the cut-off is 1:30 pm.\nIII. Cut-off timings also apply to NFO applications.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: equity and debt (non-liquid) purchases have a 3:00 pm cut-off and get the NAV of the day funds are available, for any amount. II is true: liquid and overnight purchases have a 1:30 pm cut-off. III is false: cut-offs don't apply to NFOs (or international schemes)."},{"t":"Cut-off redemption","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["An equity scheme redemption request received after 3 pm gets the same day's NAV.","For online redemption in an overnight scheme, the cut-off is 7 pm.","The cut-off for redemption in a liquid scheme is 1:30 pm.","Liquid funds calculate NAV only on business days."],"a":1,"e":"Overnight scheme redemption has a 3 pm cut-off, extended to 7 pm online. An equity redemption after 3 pm gets the next business day's NAV, liquid redemption cut-off is 3 pm, and liquid funds calculate NAV for every calendar day."},{"t":"Time stamping","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: An application is time-stamped when the distributor receives it from the investor.\nStatement 2: For online transactions, the time considered is the web server time.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: time stamping happens at the Official Point of Acceptance (OPoA), not when the distributor receives the form. Statement 2 is true: online time is the web server time."},{"t":"KYC coverage","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. For a minor's investment, the guardian (not the minor) must be KYC-compliant.\nII. A PoA holder must also complete KYC.\nIII. Joint holders other than the first holder are exempt from KYC.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I and II are true: KYC is needed by the guardian (not the minor) and by the PoA holder and issuer. III is false: everyone, including all joint holders, needs KYC."},{"t":"PAN exemption","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Residents of Sikkim are exempt from the PAN requirement.\nII. The Micro-SIP PAN exemption applies where Micro-SIP plus lump sum is up to Rs 50,000 per year per mutual fund.\nIII. A photo credit card is accepted as identity proof for Micro-SIP.","o":["Only III","Only I","Only II","Only I and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: Sikkim residents are PAN-exempt. II is true: Micro-SIP plus lump sum up to Rs 50,000 per year per MF is exempt (PEKRN is quoted). III is false: a photo debit card is accepted, not a credit card."},{"t":"CKYC and UBO","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: CERSAI is the Central KYC Records Registry and issues a KYC Identifier.\nStatement 2: For a company, the beneficial owner (UBO) is a person with more than 15% ownership.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: CERSAI is the Central KYC Records Registry and issues a KYC Identifier. Statement 2 is false: the UBO threshold for a company is more than 25% (more than 15% applies to partnerships and bodies of individuals)."},{"t":"SIP and STP","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Under rupee cost averaging in an SIP, the average cost per unit is below the average NAV.\nII. An SIP can be started during an NFO.\nIII. In an STP, the minimum investment amount of the target scheme applies to each transfer.","o":["Only I and II","Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: rupee cost averaging keeps average cost below average NAV. II is true: an SIP can start during an NFO. III is false: scheme minimums don't apply to an STP, though loads and tax do."},{"t":"DTP and triggers","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["A dividend transfer plan requires the investor to be in the IDCW option.","Under a dividend transfer plan, the dividend is invested in the target scheme on the record date itself.","Triggers such as stop-loss or appreciation triggers are one-time in nature.","A minor's SIP is registered only till the minor attains majority."],"a":1,"e":"Under a DTP the dividend is invested on the next business day after the record date, so the second statement is false. A DTP needs the IDCW option, triggers are one-time, and a minor's SIP is registered only till majority."},{"t":"Nomination","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A folio can have up to 10 nominees.\nII. A minor can be appointed as a nominee.\nIII. A company can be appointed as a nominee.","o":["Only III","Only II","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: up to 10 nominees per folio. II is true: a nominee can be an individual, even a minor or NRI. III is false: a company cannot be a nominee."}]},"mock1":{"id":"mock1","label":"Mock 1 · Full exam","short":"Mock 1","kind":"mock","weight":0,"qs":[{"t":"Ch 3 · Structure","q":"When individuals are appointed as trustees, they are jointly called the:","o":["Trust Council","Board of Directors","Board of Trustees","Investment Committee"],"a":2,"e":"Individual trustees are jointly the Board of Trustees; a trustee company acts through its Board of Directors.","c":3},{"t":"Ch 9 · NFO","q":"From the scheme re-opening date, an open-ended scheme:","o":["Stops accepting money","Declares dividend","Gets listed","Starts sale and repurchase of units at NAV-based prices"],"a":3,"e":"The AMC announces sale and repurchase prices from re-opening.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 6 · Channels","q":"Banks classify their client segments into retail and wealth/priority/private banking based on:","o":["Age","Location","Wealth or investible surplus","Occupation"],"a":2,"e":"Categorisation is based on client wealth or investible surplus.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 12 · Scenario","q":"An investor wants debt returns with low interest rate risk. Which category is best?","o":["Long duration","Ultra short duration","Medium to long duration","Gilt 10-year constant duration"],"a":1,"e":"Shorter duration means lower interest rate risk.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 5 · Documents","q":"Final scheme related documents, after incorporating SEBI's observations, must be hosted on:","o":["Stock exchange websites","SEBI's website","RBI's website","AMFI's website"],"a":3,"e":"Final documents are hosted on AMFI's website (and the MF's own website).","c":5},{"t":"Ch 7 · Valuation","q":"A share did not trade on any exchange today but traded 12 days ago. It is valued at:","o":["Zero","Face value","Book value","The price of the earliest previous day it traded (within 30 days)"],"a":3,"e":"Use the earliest previous day's traded price if within 30 days.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 9 · NFO","q":"An AMC can launch a new scheme in a category where it already has one only if the category is:","o":["ELSS","Large cap","Sector/thematic, index or fund of funds","Liquid"],"a":2,"e":"Multiple schemes allowed only in sector/theme, index and FoF categories.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 9 · Statements","q":"Investors who have not transacted in the last six months receive account statements:","o":["Weekly","Monthly","Annually (or by email)","Never"],"a":2,"e":"Annual account statement for dormant investors.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 12 · Scenario","q":"An investor wants exposure to gold that tracks the metal price closely. He should choose:","o":["Mining company shares","Gold ETF","Gold sector fund","A thematic fund"],"a":1,"e":"Gold ETFs track gold prices; sector funds track mining companies.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 5 · SID","q":"The SID's \"Investment Strategy\" section tells an investor about:","o":["Complaint procedure","Tax rates","NAV computation","The philosophy for selecting, rebalancing and selling securities"],"a":3,"e":"It explains how securities are identified, the portfolio rebalanced and holdings sold.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 9 · Options","q":"Investors A (growth), B (payout), C (reinvest) each hold 100 units bought at Rs 10. NAV rises to Rs 12 and Re 1 dividend is paid. Value of B's units after payout is:","o":["Rs 1,200","Rs 1,100","Rs 1,109","Rs 1,000"],"a":1,"e":"B holds 100 units at ex-dividend NAV Rs 11 = Rs 1,100 plus Rs 100 cash.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 9 · Allotment","q":"Rights issues are described in the workbook as:","o":["Common in MFs","Less meaningful for MF units than for shares","Banned","Mandatory"],"a":1,"e":"Rights issues are common for shares but less meaningful for MF units.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 5 · Documents","q":"Which pair of documents is the primary source of information for existing and prospective investors?","o":["Factsheet and annual report","Trust deed and IMA","KIM and account statement","SID and SAI"],"a":3,"e":"SID and SAI together are the primary source.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 12 · Investor fit","q":"An investor with a long horizon but unwilling to take equity risk may prefer:","o":["Sector funds","Lower returns from debt","Derivatives","Equity despite discomfort"],"a":1,"e":"Willingness matters.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 4 · Reforms","q":"A segregated portfolio is created to:","o":["Separate distressed, illiquid assets after a credit event","Hold cash","Separate equity from debt","Track an index"],"a":0,"e":"It separates distressed assets so all investors are treated fairly.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 7 · Valuation","q":"In valuing a convertible debenture, the non-convertible and convertible parts are:","o":["Valued at purchase cost","Valued separately, as debt and equity respectively","Ignored","Valued together at face value"],"a":1,"e":"Each component is valued on the basis applicable to its nature.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 11 · Benchmarks","q":"A credible benchmark should be calculated:","o":["By an independent agency in a transparent manner","By the fund manager","By SEBI","By the AMC"],"a":0,"e":"Independent, transparent and regularly published.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 2 · Concept","q":"Every mutual fund scheme has a pre-announced:","o":["Return","Dividend rate","Maturity bonus","Investment objective"],"a":3,"e":"Investors choose a scheme whose investment objective reflects their needs.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 6 · Channels","q":"Distribution companies and banks are sometimes called:","o":["Institutional distributors","Individual distributors","Execution-only platforms","Sub-brokers"],"a":0,"e":"They are referred to as institutional distributors.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 1 · Goals","q":"Which of the following is a financial goal rather than just a financial objective?","o":["Buying a house some day","Being financially secure","Accumulating Rs 40 lakh for a child's education in 12 years","Retiring comfortably"],"a":2,"e":"An objective becomes a goal once an amount and a timeline are attached.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 9 · Allotment","q":"In a bonus issue, the scheme's net assets:","o":["Double","Stay the same while units increase","Decrease","Increase"],"a":1,"e":"Only the number of units changes; NAV falls proportionately.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 12 · Scenario","q":"Ravi, 30, wants to build a retirement corpus over 25 years and can tolerate volatility. The most suitable core holding is:","o":["FMP","Diversified equity fund","Liquid fund","Overnight fund"],"a":1,"e":"Long horizon and willingness to take risk suit diversified equity in the core.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 10 · Risk factors","q":"In securities lending, the main risk is:","o":["Interest rate risk","Inflation","Failure of the counterparty to return securities or benefits","Currency risk"],"a":2,"e":"Risk of failure of the other party, loss of collateral rights or corporate benefits.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 9 · NFO","q":"Mutual funds must specify realistic deployment timelines for NFO money in the SID under:","o":["Regulation 18(15A)","Regulation 35(5)","Regulation 52(6A)","Sixth Schedule"],"a":1,"e":"Regulation 35(5).","c":9},{"t":"Ch 1 · Goals","q":"Goals can be classified by timeline and by:","o":["Asset class","Tax rate","Importance (needs, dreams, good-to-have)","Distributor commission"],"a":2,"e":"The workbook classifies goals by timeline and by importance.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 1 · Goals","q":"Retirement planning involves two phases. They are:","o":["NFO and redemption","Accumulation of a corpus and drawing income from it","Equity and debt","Saving and spending"],"a":1,"e":"Retirement has an accumulation phase and a distribution (income) phase.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 8 · Classification","q":"New capital gains tax rates from the July 2024 budget apply to units redeemed on or after:","o":["23 July 2024","1 April 2024","31 March 2025","1 October 2024"],"a":0,"e":"Redemptions before 23 July 2024 follow old rates; on or after, new rates.","c":8},{"t":"Ch 7 · Valuation","q":"If the AMC deviates from its disclosed valuation policy to reach fair value, it must:","o":["Get SEBI approval first","Stop NAV publication","Change the auditor","Report to both Boards (trustees and AMC) and disclose to investors"],"a":3,"e":"Deviation is allowed with reporting to both Boards and disclosure to investors.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 6 · Exchange platforms","q":"Who can offer MF transactions to investors on NSE's NMF-II platform?","o":["Only banks","Only AMCs","Any stock broker","Trading members registered with AMFI who have signed up with the MF"],"a":3,"e":"Trading members who are AMFI-registered MFDs and empanelled with the MF.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 8 · Classification","q":"Which date is relevant for the change in capital gains tax on debt funds introduced by Budget 2023?","o":["Any date","Units purchased from 1 April 2023","Units bought before 2018","Units redeemed from 1 April 2020"],"a":1,"e":"Budget 2023 changes applied to investments from 1 April 2023.","c":8},{"t":"Ch 12 · Scenario","q":"An investor wants debt exposure with the least credit risk. He should prefer:","o":["Credit risk fund","Corporate bond fund","Banking & PSU fund","Gilt fund"],"a":3,"e":"Gilt funds carry no credit risk (but have interest rate risk).","c":12},{"t":"Ch 12 · Scenario","q":"An investor wants to ride equity markets without fund manager selection risk and at low cost. Suitable:","o":["Focused fund","Sector fund","Active flexi cap fund","Index fund"],"a":3,"e":"Passive funds avoid selection risk and cost less.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 12 · Scenario","q":"An investor may need his money at short notice but is attracted to an FMP. He is better off in:","o":["A normal open-ended debt fund","ELSS","The FMP","A close-ended equity fund"],"a":0,"e":"FMPs cannot be redeemed early except via exchange.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 5 · SID","q":"Details of SIPs, STPs, switches and triggers are part of which SID discussion?","o":["Definitions","Special products and facilities","Risk factors","Fundamental attributes"],"a":1,"e":"SID discusses special products and facilities such as SIP, STP, triggers and switches.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 12 · Scenario","q":"A retired investor wants Rs 20,000 every month from his corpus. The workbook suggests:","o":["Selling all units at once","A sector fund","An SWP from a suitable scheme","Monthly IDCW option"],"a":2,"e":"SWP provides a predictable amount; IDCW is not assured.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 11 · Benchmarks","q":"The shift to TRI benchmarks was a move towards:","o":["Higher returns","Tax savings","Lower costs","Greater transparency and fair comparison"],"a":3,"e":"It made comparisons fair.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 9 · NFO","q":"Demat applicants in an NFO get units credited within:","o":["2 working days of allotment","10 days","5 business days","30 days"],"a":0,"e":"Credited to demat within 2 working days.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 12 · Scenario","q":"An investor expects interest rates to fall significantly. A tactical satellite choice is:","o":["Long-term gilt fund","Overnight fund","Floater fund","Liquid fund"],"a":0,"e":"Long gilts gain when rates fall.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 10 · Risk factors","q":"Which statement is a standard risk factor in every SID?","o":["The scheme name does not indicate quality or future prospects","Returns are assured","Sponsor bears all losses","The scheme name indicates its quality"],"a":0,"e":"The name of the scheme does not indicate its quality or future returns.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 10 · Risk factors","q":"Counterparty risk in repo lending is further mitigated by:","o":["Lower rating","Short selling","Over-collateralisation","Longer tenor"],"a":2,"e":"Collateral value exceeding the repo amount mitigates risk.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 2 · Key concepts","q":"The typical face value of a mutual fund unit is:","o":["Rs 1,000","Rs 1","Rs 100","Rs 10"],"a":3,"e":"Typically every unit has a face value of Rs 10, relevant for accounting.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 8 · Scheme income","q":"Mutual fund income is exempt from tax under which section?","o":["Section 10(23D)","Section 80C","Section 112A","Section 54"],"a":0,"e":"Section 10(23D) exempts income of mutual funds.","c":8},{"t":"Ch 2 · Concept","q":"Through a mutual fund, an investor gets:","o":["Ownership of the AMC","A different product from equity and bonds","A different way of investing in the same securities","A guaranteed return"],"a":2,"e":"MFs give access to the same securities through professional, diversified, regulated investing.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 6 · Channels","q":"Which institutions historically built the individual agent force in India?","o":["SBI and HDFC","LIC and UTI","NSE and BSE","RBI and SEBI"],"a":1,"e":"LIC and UTI depended on large forces of individual agents.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 4 · Reforms","q":"The \"instant access facility\" in liquid funds falls under which regulation category?","o":["Investor rights and obligations","New products","Governance","Valuation"],"a":0,"e":"Investor rights cover account statements, payout delays and instant access.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 4 · Investment limits","q":"Investment restrictions under SEBI MF regulations apply:","o":["At the time of making the investment","Only at year end","Only at NFO","Every day continuously"],"a":0,"e":"The restrictions apply at the time of making the investment.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 9 · Options","q":"The dividend option was renamed IDCW with effect from:","o":["1 April 2020","1 July 2020","1 April 2021","1 January 2022"],"a":2,"e":"From 1 April 2021.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 12 · Scenario","q":"A tax-exempt charitable trust wants regular income from a debt fund. The IDCW option is:","o":["Only for NRIs","Suitable, since dividends are not taxed in its hands","Illegal","Unsuitable"],"a":1,"e":"IDCW is preferable for tax-exempt investors.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 12 · Scenario","q":"Two funds in the same category: A has TER 0.4%, B 1.8%, similar risk and gross returns. For a long-term investor:","o":["A is better, as costs drag returns","Cannot compare","No difference","B is better"],"a":0,"e":"Any cost is a drag on returns.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 5 · SID","q":"The SID states the prevalent tax laws on MF investing:","o":["In full legal detail","Not at all","In brief","Only for NRIs"],"a":2,"e":"SID gives applicable tax laws in brief.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 10 · Risk factors","q":"In a floating rate bond, spread risk means:","o":["There is no risk","The coupon is fixed","The benchmark disappears","The spread over the benchmark may widen, reducing value"],"a":3,"e":"Even with the benchmark unchanged, a wider spread lowers value.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 9 · NFO","q":"Who can reject any NFO application without assigning a reason?","o":["The trustees","The RTA","The bank","The distributor"],"a":0,"e":"The trustee reserves the right to reject any application at its discretion.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 5 · Documents","q":"An MF wants to add an extra disclosure it considers important to the SID. It:","o":["Needs AMFI approval","Cannot deviate from the format","Must put it in the factsheet instead","May add any disclosure it feels is material"],"a":3,"e":"The MF may add any other disclosure it feels is material for investors.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 2 · Concept","q":"The primary role of mutual funds is to:","o":["Help investors earn income or build wealth through securities markets","Fix interest rates","Provide insurance cover","Lend to companies directly"],"a":0,"e":"The primary role is helping investors earn income or build wealth by investing in securities markets.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 12 · Duration, diversification","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Short duration funds have low volatility and can form part of the core portfolio for low-risk investors.\nStatement 2: The workbook describes diversification as protection against ignorance.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: short duration (1-3 yr) funds have low volatility and are core for low-risk investors, and the workbook quotes 'Diversification is protection against ignorance.'"},{"t":"Ch 1 · Inflation","q":"At 8% inflation, a basket costing Rs 100 today can buy roughly what share of the same basket after 10 years with the same Rs 100?","o":["About 68%","About 46%","About 25%","About 80%"],"a":1,"e":"Purchasing power at 8% falls to about 68 after 5 years and 46 after 10.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 5 · SID","q":"Minimum purchase amount, minimum balance and minimum redemption amount are found in the SID's:","o":["Due diligence certificate","Highlights/Summary and NFO/Ongoing Offer sections","Penalties section","Risk factors"],"a":1,"e":"These appear in the Highlights and Offer details.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 6 · Channels","q":"Physical application forms are deposited at:","o":["Stock exchanges","SEBI offices","Official Points of Acceptance (OPOAs)","Post offices only"],"a":2,"e":"Forms go to OPOAs of the AMC/RTA.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 4 · Reforms","q":"Governance norms include which of these?","o":["Fund manager salary caps","Distributor dress code","Minimum number of investors in a scheme","Daily AGM"],"a":2,"e":"Scheme-level governance includes minimum investors, benchmarking and systems audit.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 1 · Inflation","q":"Which categories of expenses have seen the fastest price rise according to the workbook?","o":["Food and clothing","Transport","Electronics","Education and healthcare"],"a":3,"e":"Education and healthcare costs have risen fastest, affecting those goals most.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 1 · Inflation","q":"Inflation has the greatest impact on the funding required for:","o":["Emergency funds","Short-term liquidity needs","Long-term goals","Goals due within 3 months"],"a":2,"e":"Compounding of inflation over long horizons makes long-term goals the most affected.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 10 · Risk factors","q":"Money market securities, though fairly liquid, carry risk because:","o":["They are equity","They lack a well-developed secondary market","They are unrated","They have 10-year tenors"],"a":1,"e":"The lack of a deep secondary market may restrict selling.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 2 · Concept","q":"As large investors, mutual funds can:","o":["Run the investee company","Guarantee dividends","Set share prices","Keep a check on investee companies' governance and ethics"],"a":3,"e":"Large holdings let MFs monitor the operations and governance of investee companies.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 7 · Valuation","q":"The primary objective of the fair valuation principles is:","o":["Fair treatment of all investors entering, exiting or staying","Maximising NAV","Increasing AUM","Reducing taxes"],"a":0,"e":"They ensure fairness to existing and incoming/outgoing investors and address mispricing risk.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 9 · Options","q":"In the same example, Investor C's total value after reinvestment is:","o":["Rs 1,300","Rs 1,100","Rs 1,000","Rs 1,200"],"a":3,"e":"109.09 units × Rs 11 ≈ Rs 1,200.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 9 · ASBA","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["Under ASBA, the application money is only blocked and is debited on allotment.","Money blocked under ASBA continues to earn interest in the investor's account.","The trustees can reject any application without giving a reason.","Under ASBA, an investor who is not allotted units must wait for a refund."],"a":3,"e":"With ASBA the money is only blocked, debited on allotment and keeps earning interest, so there is no refund wait; the last statement is wrong. Trustees can indeed reject any application without giving a reason."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Regulators","q":"Insurance companies are regulated by:","o":["RBI","IRDAI","AMFI","SEBI"],"a":1,"e":"IRDAI regulates the insurance market.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 7 · Valuation","q":"Principle 1 of fair valuation states that valuation should reflect:","o":["Face value","Book value","Realisable value of securities","Purchase cost"],"a":2,"e":"Valuation shall reflect the realisable value, done in good faith.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 11 · Equity benchmarks","q":"A mid-cap fund would be better benchmarked to:","o":["Nifty 50","Bankex","Nifty Midcap 50 or S&P BSE Midcap","Sensex"],"a":2,"e":"Midcap indices suit mid-cap funds.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 3 · Structure","q":"Day-to-day management of mutual fund schemes is handled by the:","o":["Sponsor","Custodian","Trustees","AMC"],"a":3,"e":"The AMC manages the schemes day to day.","c":3},{"t":"Ch 12 · Equity risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Small cap funds are riskier than mid cap funds.\nII. Large & mid cap funds are riskier than large cap funds.\nIII. Mid cap funds are less risky than multi cap funds.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":2,"e":"I and II are true: equity risk falls small, mid, multi, large & mid, large. III is false: mid cap funds are riskier than multi cap funds."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Benchmarks","q":"A valid reason for changing a scheme's benchmark is:","o":["Poor recent performance","Change in investment objective or availability of a better index","New fund manager's preference","Distributor request"],"a":1,"e":"Changes must be justified and documented.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 6 · GST and charges","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The GST registration threshold for a distributor is Rs 20 lakh.\nII. For an unregistered distributor, the registered recipient pays GST under reverse charge.\nIII. Transaction charges still apply to subscriptions above Rs 10,000.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the GST threshold is Rs 20 lakh. II is true: for unregistered suppliers the registered recipient pays under reverse charge. III is false: SEBI withdrew transaction charges in Aug 2025; they earlier applied to subscriptions above Rs 10,000."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Interest rate risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Medium duration funds carry more interest rate risk than medium to long duration funds.","Long duration funds carry the highest interest rate risk among the duration categories.","Short duration funds carry more interest rate risk than medium duration funds.","Ultra-short duration funds carry more interest rate risk than short duration funds."],"a":1,"e":"Interest rate risk rises overnight, liquid, ultra-short, low, short, medium, medium-long, long, so long duration is highest; the other three reverse the order."},{"t":"Ch 9 · NFO pricing","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. An open-ended scheme re-opens for sale and repurchase within 5 business days of allotment.\nII. Since there is no entry load, the sale price of units equals the NAV.\nIII. The redemption price equals the NAV plus any exit load.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: an open-ended scheme re-opens within 5 business days of allotment. II is true: with no entry load, sale price = NAV. III is false: the redemption price is NAV less any exit load."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Passive funds","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: The NAV of a passive fund does not fall when its index falls.\nStatement 2: Passive funds carry no selection risk.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: a passive fund's NAV still falls when the index falls. Statement 2 is true: passive funds have no selection risk."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Benchmarks","q":"Even for the IDCW option, scheme performance is computed using:","o":["Reinvestment of IDCW method","NAV only","Payout method","Price return"],"a":0,"e":"Reinvestment method; comparing with PRI would give an unfair advantage.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 9 · Scheme categories","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. As a rule, an AMC can have only one scheme per category.\nII. Index funds are an exception to the one-scheme-per-category rule.\nIII. Large cap funds are an exception to the one-scheme-per-category rule.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: one scheme per category is the rule. II is true: sector/thematic, index and FoF schemes are the exceptions. III is false: large cap funds are not among the exceptions."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Debt benchmarks","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Short duration debt schemes are benchmarked to a Short Duration Debt A-I index.\nStatement 2: Overnight schemes are benchmarked to the NIFTY 1D Rate Index / CRISIL Liquid Overnight Index.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: short duration schemes use the Short Duration Debt A-II index. Statement 2 is true: overnight schemes use NIFTY 1D Rate / CRISIL Liquid Overnight."},{"t":"Ch 2 · Hybrid funds","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["An arbitrage fund invests at least 65% in equity","A Balanced Advantage fund keeps a fixed split between equity and debt","An equity savings fund invests 65% in equity and 10% in debt, with net equity of 15-40%","A balanced hybrid fund invests 40-60% in equity and cannot use arbitrage"],"a":1,"e":"A Balanced Advantage fund manages equity and debt dynamically, not as a fixed split. The other three statements are correct."},{"t":"Ch 5 · SID","q":"An SID remains effective until:","o":["A material change occurs, after which changes are filed and communicated","The NFO closes","One year from issue","The fund manager changes"],"a":0,"e":"It remains effective until a material change, then updated and notified.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 8 · TDS","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. There is no TDS on redemption for resident investors.\nII. TDS on IDCW for residents is 10% if dividends exceed Rs 10,000 a year.\nIII. For NRIs, TDS is at the higher of the Income Tax Act rate or the DTAA rate.","o":["Only I and II","Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: no TDS on redemption for residents. II is true: 10% TDS on IDCW above Rs 10,000 a year. III is false: NRIs suffer TDS at the lower of the IT Act rate or DTAA rate (with documents)."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Regulators","q":"Listing, trading and margining rules for MF units on exchanges are set by:","o":["The stock exchange concerned","RBI","The AMC","AMFI"],"a":0,"e":"Each stock exchange has its own listing, trading and margining rules.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 7 · Valuation","q":"Documentation of valuation rationale, including inter-scheme transfers, is kept to enable:","o":["Audit trail","Marketing","Tax filing","Distributor payouts"],"a":0,"e":"Principle 9: preserved per the Seventh Schedule for audit trail.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 7 · Valuation responsibility","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The trustees are responsible for true and fair valuation, and only they may deviate from the valuation policy.\nStatement 2: Debt securities are valued using trade prices from all public platforms.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: the AMC is responsible for true and fair valuation and may deviate (reporting to both Boards and disclosing to investors). Statement 2 is true: debt valuation uses trade prices from all public platforms."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Reforms","q":"Which regulation category covers disclosure of commission payable to distributors?","o":["Advertisements","Valuation","Loads, fees and expenses","Secondary market activities"],"a":2,"e":"Loads, fees and expenses rules cover limits and commission disclosure.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 4 · Sixth Schedule","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A scheme can hold equity and debt only if listed or to be listed, subject to stated exceptions.\nII. Unlisted commercial papers are among the unlisted money market instruments a scheme may hold.\nIII. Unrated debt instruments may be held on conditions specified by SEBI.","o":["Only II","Only III","Only I and II","Only II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: the listed-or-to-be-listed rule applies. II is false: unlisted G-secs and money market instruments are allowed, but not CPs. III is true: unrated debt is allowed on SEBI's conditions."},{"t":"Ch 3 · Structure","q":"The record of investors and their unitholding may be maintained by:","o":["SEBI","The custodian only","The trustees","The AMC itself or an RTA"],"a":3,"e":"The AMC may do it in-house or appoint an RTA.","c":3},{"t":"Ch 7 · Valuation norms","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Valuation norms are contained in the Seventh Schedule of the MF Regulations 2026.\nII. Under Principle 1, securities are valued at their realisable value, in good faith.\nIII. The valuation policy is approved by the trustees.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: valuation is in the Seventh Schedule. II is true: Principle 1 is realisable value in good faith. III is false: the valuation policy is approved by the AMC Board."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Sensex","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: The Sensex has a base value of 100 set in 1979.\nStatement 2: By 30 January 2026, the Sensex had reached 82,269.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: the Sensex started at a base of 100 in 1979 and reached 82,269 by 30 January 2026."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Scheme launch","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The idea for a new scheme comes from the CIO (scheme objective) and the CMO (market interest).\nStatement 2: The SID is approved by the trustees and the AMC Board before it is filed with SEBI.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: the CIO and CMO originate the scheme idea, and the SID is approved by the trustees and AMC Board, filed with SEBI, and SEBI's observations are incorporated."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Saving and evaluation","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The word 'saving' comes from 'safe'.\nII. Safety, liquidity and returns are the top three factors for evaluating an investment.\nIII. A mutual fund is a different investment product, not a different way of investing.","o":["Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III","Only I and II"],"a":3,"e":"I is true: 'saving' comes from 'safe'. II is true: safety, liquidity and returns are the top three factors. III is false: a mutual fund is a different way of investing, not a different product."},{"t":"Ch 5 · Risks and charges","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The standard risk factors state that the sponsor is not liable beyond its initial contribution.\nStatement 2: Fees are paid by the investor at exit, while loads are charged to the NAV.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false: it is the other way round, fees hit the NAV and loads are paid by the investor at exit."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Benchmarks and SID","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A benchmark should be run by an independent agency in a transparent manner.\nII. Once stated in the SID, a scheme's benchmark can never be changed.\nIII. The SID, including scheme performance, is updated twice a year.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: benchmarks must be run by an independent agency, transparently. II is false: a benchmark can be changed, but the change must be justified and documented. III is true: the SID, with performance, is updated twice a year."},{"t":"Ch 3 · AMC appointment","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The AMC is appointed by the sponsor or the trustees, with SEBI approval.\nStatement 2: The Investment Management Agreement is signed between the sponsor and the AMC.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: the AMC is appointed by the sponsor or trustees with SEBI approval. Statement 2 is false: the trustees sign the Investment Management Agreement with the AMC."},{"t":"Ch 5 · Caveat emptor","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Under caveat emptor, the investor is presumed to have read the scheme documents, and signing the form is legal acceptance.\nII. The format of the SID is prescribed by AMFI.\nIII. A mutual fund may add other material disclosures to the prescribed content.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true. II is false: the format is prescribed by SEBI, with content in the same sequence. III is true: other material disclosures may be added."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Gold and silver ETFs","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A gold ETF may keep idle money in short-term bank deposits.\nStatement 2: Gold ETFs are not permitted to use exchange-traded commodity derivatives.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: gold/silver ETFs may park idle money in short-term bank deposits. Statement 2 is false: they may use exchange-traded commodity derivatives."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Debt risks","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A rating migration from AAA to AA+ causes the bond's price to fall.\nStatement 2: Floating rate instruments face spread risk and basis risk.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 1 is true: a downgrade such as AAA to AA+ makes the price fall. Statement 2 is true: floaters carry spread risk and basis risk."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Risk factors","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The SID lists standard and specific risk factors, along with risk mitigation.\nII. Moving from large caps to mid caps to small caps reduces liquidity risk.\nIII. A fund manager can reduce systematic risk only by holding cash, which SEBI limits.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the SID lists standard and specific risk factors plus mitigation. II is false: moving down from large to mid to small caps increases volatility and liquidity risk. III is true: holding cash is the only way to cut systematic risk, and SEBI limits it."},{"t":"Ch 4 · SEBI mandate","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The SEBI preamble is to protect investors and to develop and regulate the securities market.\nII. RBI rules also apply to mutual funds for money market, overseas investment and forex matters.\nIII. The SEBI MF Regulations 2026 replace the regulations first issued in 1996.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"e":"All three are true: SEBI's preamble is protection plus development and regulation; RBI rules apply for money market, overseas investment and forex; the 2026 Regulations replace the 1996 ones."}],"ver":2},"mock2":{"id":"mock2","label":"Mock 2 · Full exam","short":"Mock 2","kind":"mock","weight":0,"qs":[{"t":"Ch 9 · Investors","q":"Some gilt schemes have plans open only to:","o":["Retail investors","NRIs","Provident, superannuation, gratuity and pension funds and certain trusts","Minors"],"a":2,"e":"Hence check \"Who can invest\" in the SID.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 5 · Fundamental attributes","q":"Converting an open-ended scheme to close-ended changes which fundamental attribute?","o":["Terms of issue only","Type of scheme","Nothing","Benchmark"],"a":1,"e":"Open/close/interval is the type of scheme.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 3 · AMC","q":"Who is responsible for ensuring the AMC maintains its minimum net worth continuously?","o":["AMFI","Sponsor","SEBI","Trustees"],"a":1,"e":"Maintaining the Rs 50 crore net worth continuously is the sponsor's responsibility.","c":3},{"t":"Ch 4 · Investment limits","q":"The 10% per-company equity limit does NOT apply to:","o":["Large cap funds","Flexi cap funds","ELSS","Index and sector funds"],"a":3,"e":"Index, sector and industry-specific schemes are exempt.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 4 · Investment limits","q":"Under the Sixth Schedule, a scheme's investment in unrated debt instruments is:","o":["Prohibited","Subject to conditions SEBI specifies","Unlimited","Allowed only for liquid funds"],"a":1,"e":"Investment in unrated debt instruments is subject to conditions SEBI specifies from time to time.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 7 · NAV","q":"Which item reduces NAV?","o":["Scheme expenses","Interest income","Dividend income","Valuation gains"],"a":0,"e":"Expenses, realised losses and valuation losses reduce NAV.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 10 · Systematic risk","q":"A labour strike at a company's plant is an example of:","o":["Market risk","Unsystematic (company-specific) risk","Interest rate risk","Systematic risk"],"a":1,"e":"It affects only that company.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 12 · Strategy","q":"Why is an index fund allowed to charge lower costs?","o":["It is unregulated","It is tax-exempt","It has no investors","No research or active fund management expenses are needed"],"a":3,"e":"Passive management reduces costs.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 6 · MF Utilities","q":"An investor can have more than one CAN because:","o":["It is a mistake","CANs expire yearly","Each AMC needs one","CANs depend on holding pattern and mode of holding"],"a":3,"e":"Multiple CANs are possible based on holding combinations and mode.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 8 · Capital gains","q":"The \"grandfathering\" date for equity LTCG is:","o":["31 January 2018","1 April 2018","1 July 2020","23 July 2024"],"a":0,"e":"Gains till 31 January 2018 are not taxed.","c":8},{"t":"Ch 10 · Risk factors","q":"\"True sale\" legal opinion in securitisation protects investors against:","o":["Inflation","Prepayment","Interest rate risk","Bankruptcy of the originator"],"a":3,"e":"It ensures the sale to the trust is not undone in the originator's bankruptcy.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 12 · Hierarchy","q":"Between an equity savings fund and a balanced hybrid fund, which is riskier?","o":["Equity savings","Balanced hybrid","Equal","Neither"],"a":1,"e":"Hierarchy: arbitrage → conservative → equity savings → DAAF → multi-asset → balanced → aggressive.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 9 · Form","q":"If demat details in the application do not match depository records:","o":["Application is rejected outright","Units may be issued in physical (non-demat) mode if otherwise valid","Money is refunded after a year","SEBI is informed"],"a":1,"e":"Considered invalid for demat mode; units may be issued in physical mode.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 9 · Statements","q":"A half-yearly CAS is sent to investors who:","o":["Hold only ETFs","Are NRIs","Had no transaction during the half-year","Transacted every month"],"a":2,"e":"Half-yearly CAS details holdings for folios without transactions.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 5 · Fundamental attributes","q":"\"The main objective is growth, income or both\" falls under which fundamental attribute?","o":["Benchmark","Type of scheme","Investment objective","Terms of issue"],"a":2,"e":"Investment objective covers main objective and tentative portfolio break-up.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 2 · Advantages","q":"Which is NOT an advantage of mutual funds listed in the workbook?","o":["Portfolio customisation","Transparency","Economies of scale","Professional management"],"a":0,"e":"Lack of customisation is a limitation; PMS offers customisation.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 9 · Form","q":"FATCA/CRS details are required when the applicant's:","o":["Income exceeds Rs 50 lakh","Age is above 60","Investment exceeds Rs 1 crore","Country of birth, citizenship or tax residency is other than India"],"a":3,"e":"Additional information under FATCA and CRS.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 5 · Fundamental attributes","q":"A scheme adds a safety net guarantee. This change affects which fundamental attribute?","o":["Terms of issue","None","Type of scheme","Investment objective"],"a":0,"e":"Any safety net or guarantee is part of terms of issue.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 4 · Advertisement code","q":"Which slogan is NOT allowed in an MF advertisement?","o":["One in a vernacular language","An exaggerated or unwarranted one","One consistent with the product's risk profile","One carrying the standard warning"],"a":1,"e":"Exaggerated, unwarranted or inconsistent slogans are prohibited.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 4 · Investment limits","q":"Equity investments by a scheme must be in securities that are:","o":["Unlisted only","Listed or to be listed","Foreign only","Rated AAA"],"a":1,"e":"Sixth Schedule: equity (and debt) investments only in listed or to-be-listed securities.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 10 · Systematic risk","q":"A fund manager can reduce systematic risk mainly by:","o":["Moving part of the portfolio to cash, within SEBI limits","Increasing turnover","Buying small caps","Diversifying across more stocks"],"a":0,"e":"Only staying out of the market reduces systematic risk.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 12 · Hierarchy","q":"Which is riskier on the interest rate dimension?","o":["Overnight fund","Liquid fund","Medium duration fund","Low duration fund"],"a":2,"e":"Longer duration = higher interest rate risk.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 7 · Valuation","q":"Silver held by a silver ETF is valued at LBMA AM fixing for silver of fineness:","o":["999.0","916.0","995.0","925.0"],"a":0,"e":"Silver: 999.0 parts per thousand; gold: 995.0.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 6 · Platforms","q":"When an investor transacts through an AMC's own app or WhatsApp service, he is:","o":["Dealing directly with the mutual fund","Using MFU","Using an exchange","Using a distributor"],"a":0,"e":"AMC platforms mean direct dealing with the fund.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 5 · KIM","q":"The KIM is a summary of:","o":["The annual report","The factsheet","The SID and SAI","The trust deed"],"a":2,"e":"KIM summarises SID and SAI.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 9 · Form","q":"For demat applications, dividends and redemption proceeds are paid to:","o":["The bank account in the application form","The distributor","The first holder's employer","The bank account linked to the demat account"],"a":3,"e":"Payouts go to the bank linked with the demat account.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 5 · Fundamental attributes","q":"During the exit window for a change in fundamental attributes, investors exit at:","o":["Prevailing NAV without exit load","Face value","The NFO price","NAV minus exit load"],"a":0,"e":"Exit is at prevailing NAV without exit load for at least 30 days.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 2 · Advantages","q":"SIP, SWP and STP are examples of which advantage?","o":["Systematic approach to investing","Tax deferral","Economies of scale","Regulatory comfort"],"a":0,"e":"Systematic plans promote investment discipline.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 5 · SID","q":"Which risk is specific to unlisted equity securities?","o":["No risk","High liquidity risk","Only credit risk","Only interest rate risk"],"a":1,"e":"Unlisted securities carry high liquidity risk.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 10 · Analysis","q":"Fundamental analysis is generally best suited for:","o":["Timing entry only","Intraday trading","Currency trading","Long-term investment decisions"],"a":3,"e":"Long-term decisions use fundamentals; technical analysis helps timing.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 4 · Investment limits","q":"Securities purchased or transferred by a mutual fund must be held:","o":["In the name of the mutual fund on account of the concerned scheme","In the AMC's name","In the custodian's own name","In the sponsor's name"],"a":0,"e":"Sixth Schedule: securities are held in the MF's name on account of the scheme, except those SEBI specifies.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 12 · Hierarchy","q":"Between a thematic and a focused fund, which is riskier on concentration?","o":["Focused","Thematic","Equal","Neither"],"a":1,"e":"Diversified → focused → thematic → sector.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 3 · Trustees","q":"Whose critical role is it to ensure the mutual fund complies with all regulations and protects unitholders' interests?","o":["The Board of Directors of the trustee company (trustees)","The RTA","The custodian","The distributors"],"a":0,"e":"The trustees have a critical role in ensuring regulatory compliance and protecting unitholders (Section 3.2.2).","c":3},{"t":"Ch 12 · Hierarchy","q":"Moving from liquid to debt to hybrid to equity funds:","o":["Risk and potential return rise","Nothing changes","Return falls","Risk falls"],"a":0,"e":"Illustration 12.1.1.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 8 · Capital gains","q":"An investor's total equity LTCG for the year is Rs 90,000. Tax payable on LTCG is:","o":["On Rs 90,000","On Rs 10,000","Nil","On Rs 35,000"],"a":2,"e":"LTCG up to Rs 1.25 lakh is exempt.","c":8},{"t":"Ch 9 · Form","q":"Once a minor attains majority, the guardian:","o":["Becomes a joint holder","Becomes the nominee","Continues operating","Can no longer operate the folio; it is frozen until status changes"],"a":3,"e":"The account is frozen for the guardian until the change of status.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 5 · SAI","q":"Timelines for servicing investor applications and acknowledgements appear in the SAI section on:","o":["Rights of unitholders","Valuation norms","Constituents","Tax"],"a":0,"e":"Rights of unitholders includes service timelines and exit options.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 12 · Investor fit","q":"A portfolio of mutual funds is described in the workbook as:","o":["An end in itself","A trading tool","A tax shelter only","A means to an end, a vehicle to achieve goals"],"a":3,"e":"The goal must never be lost sight of.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 2 · Advantages","q":"Schemes where money can be recovered from the fund only on closure are:","o":["Never listed","Traded only through the AMC","Compulsorily listed on a stock exchange","Optionally listed"],"a":2,"e":"Such (close-ended) schemes must be listed to provide liquidity.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 11 · Debt benchmarks","q":"A gilt fund should use a benchmark based on:","o":["Gold","Corporate bonds","Government securities","Equity"],"a":2,"e":"Gilt funds invest only in G-secs.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 1 · Asset classes","q":"Fixed income securities can be classified by:","o":["Issuer and maturity","Market cap","Dividend yield","Sector and style"],"a":0,"e":"Fixed income securities are classified by issuer or by maturity.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 7 · NAV","q":"Which formula correctly gives the NAV per unit?","o":["Total assets ÷ units","Market value of equity ÷ units","Unit capital ÷ units","(Total assets − liabilities other than to unitholders) ÷ units"],"a":3,"e":"NAV = (total assets − liabilities other than to unitholders) ÷ outstanding units.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 10 · Risk factors","q":"Co-mingling risk in securitisation arises when:","o":["Interest rates rise","The rating is downgraded","Loans are prepaid","Collections are not segregated from the servicer's own funds before remittance"],"a":3,"e":"Servicer may mix collections with its funds; failure to remit hurts investors.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 11 · Debt benchmarks","q":"Li-Bex covers government securities with maturity:","o":["More than 7 years","3–7 years","Under 1 year","1–3 years"],"a":0,"e":"Li-Bex: more than 7 years.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 1 · Evaluating investments","q":"Which of these is an additional parameter, not one of the three most important factors, for evaluating an investment?","o":["Liquidity","Ticket size","Returns","Safety"],"a":1,"e":"Safety, liquidity and returns are the top three; convenience, ticket size, taxability and tax deduction are additional.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 12 · Hierarchy","q":"While comparing debt funds on credit risk, one should assume:","o":["No risk","Equal NAV","Different interest rate risks","Similar interest rate risk"],"a":3,"e":"Compare one risk at a time.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 11 · Equity benchmarks","q":"A diversified equity fund investing in 150 stocks would be better suited to:","o":["A sectoral index","A debt index","A broad index like Nifty 200 or 500","Sensex"],"a":2,"e":"Broader portfolios suit broader indices.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 9 · Form","q":"Investors subscribing on or after 30 September 2023 must:","o":["Nominate only minors","Either nominate or opt out through a signed declaration","Not nominate","Always nominate"],"a":1,"e":"Choice to nominate or opt out by declaration.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 12 · Code of conduct","q":"Under the Fourth Schedule code of conduct, decisions that favour one set of investors over another are:","o":["Allowed for large investors","Not considered equitable and fair treatment","Allowed with trustee approval","Required during NFOs"],"a":1,"e":"Schemes must give equitable and fair treatment to all classes of unitholders.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 1 · Evaluating investments","q":"Returns realised only when an investment is sold are called:","o":["Coupon","Dividend yield","Capital appreciation","Current income"],"a":2,"e":"Current income is received while holding; capital appreciation is realised on sale.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 5 · SID","q":"The performance of the scheme and its benchmark is given in the:","o":["RTA agreement","Trust deed","SID","IMA"],"a":2,"e":"The SID provides scheme and benchmark performance.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 2 · Limitations","q":"Costs incurred for managing a scheme are shared by unitholders:","o":["In proportion to their unitholding","Only by redeeming investors","Only by new investors","Equally per person"],"a":0,"e":"Costs are shared in proportion to units held, so an investor has no control over costs.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 5 · Updates and filing","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. The SID is first updated within 6 months of the half-year in which the scheme was launched.\nII. The KIM is filed with SEBI in physical form.\nIII. Account statements show the load structure.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and II"],"a":1,"e":"I is true. II is false: the KIM is filed with SEBI electronically. III is true: account statements show the load structure."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Labelling","q":"The 2013 product label also required a disclaimer that investors should:","o":["Ignore risk","Contact SEBI","Consult their financial advisers if unclear about suitability","Buy immediately"],"a":2,"e":"As per the 2013 circular.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 11 · Debt benchmarks","q":"A liquid fund is best benchmarked to:","o":["Nifty 50","CRISIL Liquid Fund Index","Gold price","10-year G-sec index"],"a":1,"e":"Short-term money market benchmarks suit liquid funds.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 12 · Core-satellite","q":"A conservative investor would typically keep the satellite portion:","o":["At 80%","At 50%","Very small","At 100%"],"a":2,"e":"Conservative investors prefer little tactical management.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 9 · Transactions","q":"For additional purchases in an existing folio, an investor typically submits:","o":["A trust deed","A transaction slip with the folio number","A full application form with KYC","A new KIM"],"a":1,"e":"Only a transaction slip and payment are needed.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 4 · Investment limits","q":"A mutual fund may enter into derivatives transactions:","o":["Over the counter with any party","On a recognised stock exchange, within SEBI's framework","Only through fund of funds","Never"],"a":1,"e":"Regulation 41(vi): derivatives only on a recognised stock exchange, subject to SEBI's framework.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 4 · SIF investments","q":"Under Regulation 51, a Specialized Investment Fund may invest in:","o":["Instruments permitted for MF schemes under Regulation 39, subject to the Sixth Schedule","Any asset without limit","Only listed equity","Only real estate"],"a":0,"e":"SIFs may invest in instruments permitted under Regulation 39, and the Sixth Schedule restrictions for MF schemes apply.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 1 · Asset classes","q":"Which asset class is described as \"risk capital\"?","o":["Equity","Fixed income","Gold","Bank deposits"],"a":0,"e":"Equity is the owner's capital and bears business risk, so it is risk capital.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 5 · Where information sits","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Details on PAN, KYC, payment modes and cut-off timing are in the SAI, read with the SID","The investment objective of a scheme can only be a single primary objective","Modes of transaction are given in the SID's \"Highlights\" section","The KIM shows performance over 1 and 3 years only"],"a":0,"e":"A is true: \"How to apply\" is in the SAI, read with the SID. B is false: the objective can be primary plus secondary. C is false: modes of transaction are in the SAI's \"How to Apply\". D is false: the KIM shows 1, 3, 5 years and since inception."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Transactions","q":"If personal data in an additional purchase form conflicts with the folio data:","o":["New data overrides","Existing folio data overrides","Both are kept","The form is sent to SEBI"],"a":1,"e":"Existing folio data applies; name mismatch may lead to rejection.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 7 · NAV","q":"Current liabilities of a scheme may include borrowings for liquidity needs for a period not exceeding:","o":["1 month","6 months","1 year","3 months"],"a":1,"e":"Borrowings not exceeding six months to meet liquidity needs.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 9 · Form","q":"If scheme selection is made but plan and option are left blank:","o":["The distributor decides","Growth is always applied","The form is rejected","Default plan/option as stated in the form applies"],"a":3,"e":"Default options mentioned in the form apply.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 3 · Trustees","q":"Who is responsible for protecting the interests of unitholders?","o":["Trustees","RTA","Sponsor","AMC"],"a":0,"e":"The trust acts through trustees, who protect beneficiaries' interests.","c":3},{"t":"Ch 6 · MF Utilities","q":"To register for a CAN on MF Utilities, an investor must be:","o":["An NRI","KYC compliant (MFU can facilitate KYC)","A demat account holder","An HNI"],"a":1,"e":"KYC compliance is needed; MFU facilitates KYC if required.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 10 · Valuation ratios","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["Forward P/E uses projected EPS","A PEG ratio above 1 indicates an overvalued stock","Value stocks are priced below intrinsic value and need a long horizon","A stock with a high price necessarily has a high valuation"],"a":3,"e":"The last is false: high valuation is not the same as high price. The others are true: forward P/E uses projected EPS, PEG >1 is overvalued, and value stocks trade below intrinsic value and need a long horizon."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Securitised pools","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. In a securitised pool, a higher LTV makes the pool safer.\nII. Seasoning is better when more instalments have already been paid.\nIII. Counterparty risk in repo in corporate bonds is mitigated by over-collateralisation.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and II"],"a":0,"e":"I is false: a lower LTV is safer. II is true: more instalments already paid means better seasoning. III is true: repo counterparty risk is mitigated by over-collateralisation."},{"t":"Ch 6 · MF Utilities","q":"MFU's Common Transaction Form allows investors to:","o":["Buy shares","Transact in multiple schemes across participating MFs with a single form and payment","Only change address","Only redeem"],"a":1,"e":"One form and one payment for multiple schemes across MFs.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 7 · NAV","q":"Mark-to-market NAV helps investors because:","o":["They can buy and sell at fair, transparently calculated prices","It guarantees returns","It removes risk","It fixes the NAV at Rs 10"],"a":0,"e":"It gives fair prices and helps assess fund manager performance.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 4 · Ad code basics","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Words may be added to the standard warning for clarity, as long as its meaning is kept.\nII. The advertisement rules are in the Fifth Schedule of the MF Regulations 2026.\nIII. Unverified market news may be forwarded only after the Compliance Officer's approval.","o":["Only II","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is false: no words may be added to or removed from the standard warning. II is true: ad rules sit in the Fifth Schedule. III is true: unverified news needs the Compliance Officer's approval."},{"t":"Ch 2 · Advantages","q":"The bond market in India is described as:","o":["Retail","Wholesale, with very large lot sizes","Unregulated","Closed to MFs"],"a":1,"e":"MFs let small investors participate in the wholesale bond market.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 9 · Statements","q":"The monthly Statement of Account shows:","o":["Only tax details","Only the closing balance","Only dividends","Each transaction with value, NAV, units, plus closing balance and value"],"a":3,"e":"It shows transaction details and the updated holding value.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 7 · NAV","q":"Investments Rs 400 cr, accrued income Rs 50 cr, other receivables Rs 5 cr, accrued expenses Rs 5 cr, units 30 cr. NAV is:","o":["Rs 14.83","Rs 15.00","Rs 15.17","Rs 13.50"],"a":1,"e":"(400 + 50 + 5 − 5) ÷ 30 = 450 ÷ 30 = Rs 15.00.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 6 · Exchange platforms","q":"Complaints about MF transactions on exchange platforms can use:","o":["The normal stock exchange redressal mechanism","Only AMFI","Only courts","No redressal"],"a":0,"e":"The exchange's investor redressal mechanism is available.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 12 · Core-satellite","q":"The core portfolio is invested according to:","o":["Long-term needs and goals","Tips","Sector cycles","Short-term market movements"],"a":0,"e":"Satellite handles tactical opportunities.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 2 · Life Cycle Funds","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements about Life Cycle Funds:\nStatement 1: Along the glide path, the equity share rises and the debt share falls as maturity nears.\nStatement 2: A mutual fund may keep up to 10 Life Cycle Funds open for subscription at a time.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":1,"e":"Both are false: equity falls and debt rises as maturity nears, and a mutual fund may have at most 6 Life Cycle Funds open for subscription at a time."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Demat applicants","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. For demat applications, the investor's details must match the DP's records.\nII. The KYC done by the DP is accepted.\nIII. For a demat application, redemption payouts go to the bank account given on the application form even if it differs from the demat-linked account.","o":["Only I and II","Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I and II are true: details must match the DP and the DP's KYC is accepted. III is false: payouts go to the bank account linked to the demat account."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Minors and NRIs","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A minor's guardian must be a natural parent or a court-appointed guardian.\nII. Payment for a minor's investment may come from a minor-guardian joint account.\nIII. NRIs can invest in mutual funds only on a repatriable basis.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: the guardian is a natural parent or court-appointed. II is true: payment can come from the minor's, the parent's/guardian's, or a minor-guardian joint account. III is false: NRI/PIO/OCI can invest on a repatriable or non-repatriable basis."},{"t":"Ch 8 · Holding period","q":"For units of a non-equity (non-listed) mutual fund, the long-term holding period threshold is:","o":["60 months","36 months","24 months","12 months"],"a":2,"e":"Other assets: 24 months.","c":8},{"t":"Ch 12 · Target maturity","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements about target maturity funds (TMFs) is TRUE?","o":["TMFs can be exited before maturity only through the stock exchange.","TMFs invest mainly in AA-rated private corporate bonds.","TMFs invest in G-secs, SDLs and AAA-rated PSU paper.","TMFs offer liquidity only at maturity."],"a":2,"e":"TMFs hold G-secs, SDLs and AAA PSU paper and are liquid via the AMC, unlike FMPs whose pre-maturity exit is only via the exchange."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Equity benchmarks","q":"Per the workbook's equity benchmark table, a Mid Cap Fund uses:","o":["NIFTY Midcap 150 / BSE Midcap 150","NIFTY 100 / BSE 100","NIFTY Smallcap 250 / BSE 250 SmallCap","NIFTY 500 / BSE 500"],"a":0,"e":"Large cap: 100; Large & mid: LargeMidcap 250; Mid cap: Midcap 150; Small cap: Smallcap 250; ELSS, flexi cap, dividend yield: 500.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 11 · Hybrid benchmarks","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Aggressive hybrid schemes are benchmarked to the NIFTY 50 Hybrid Composite Debt 50:50 Index","International funds are benchmarked to an Indian index such as Nifty 50","The two-tier benchmark structure applies only to equity schemes","A hybrid scheme's benchmark can be a blend, such as 65% Sensex + 35% I-Bex"],"a":3,"e":"A hybrid benchmark is a blend, e.g. 65% Sensex + 35% I-Bex. Aggressive hybrid uses the 65:35 index, international funds use a local index (e.g. S&P 500, Shanghai Composite), and two-tier benchmarks apply to both debt and equity schemes."},{"t":"Ch 12 · FMP and TMF","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. FMPs give more predictable returns than FDs.\nII. FMP investors need not check credit risk because the maturity is fixed.\nIII. Target maturity funds (TMFs) are replacing FMPs.","o":["Only III","Only I and II","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: FMPs are more predictable than FDs. II is false: the investor must check the FMP's credit risk. III is true: TMFs are replacing FMPs."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Evaluating investments","q":"An investment offering a tax deduction usually comes with:","o":["No risk","Guaranteed returns","A lock-in period","Higher liquidity"],"a":2,"e":"Tax deductions typically require a lock-in, trading liquidity for the tax benefit.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 3 · AMC directors","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. AMC directors, including the Chairman, cannot be on the board of any mutual fund's trustee company.\nII. A person may be a director of up to two AMCs.\nIII. Appointment of an AMC director needs prior approval of the trustees.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and II"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: AMC directors including the Chairman can't sit on any MF's trustee company board. II is false: no one can be a director of more than one AMC. III is true: prior trustee approval is needed."},{"t":"Ch 6 · Change of distributor","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: When an investor changes distributor, the old distributor continues to receive trail on the existing investment.\nStatement 2: On an investor-initiated ARN change, the AMC may pay trail to the new distributor after a 12-month cooling-off period.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: no commission is paid to either the old or new distributor, except when the old distributor voluntarily ceased business. Statement 2 is true: the AMC may pay the new distributor after a 12-month cooling-off."},{"t":"Ch 4 · SIF branding","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A Specialised Investment Fund may use the mutual fund's brand for 3 years from SEBI approval.\nStatement 2: In SIF communication, the mutual fund brand's font must be no bigger than the SIF's.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: the MF brand may be used for 5 years from SEBI approval, with phrases like \"brought to you by\". Statement 2 is true: the MF brand's font cannot be bigger than the SIF's."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Holding and deployment","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["A fund of funds may hold listed equity shares alongside mutual fund units","Securities bought for a scheme are held in the name of the AMC","Money awaiting deployment can be kept in short-term deposits of scheduled commercial banks","Any scheme may invest in a fund of funds within prudential limits"],"a":2,"e":"C is true: money awaiting deployment can sit in short-term deposits of scheduled commercial banks. A is false: a FoF holds only mutual fund units (plus liquidity). B is false: securities are held in the mutual fund's name for the scheme. D is false: no scheme can invest in a FoF."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Equity risk, value","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Multi cap funds carry more risk than large cap funds.\nStatement 2: Value funds need a long investment horizon.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: equity risk falls small, mid, multi, large & mid, large, so multi cap is riskier than large cap; and value funds outperform in falling markets or over a long period and need a long horizon."},{"t":"Ch 7 · FoF TER","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements about Fund of Funds (FoF) TER is TRUE?","o":["An FoF investing at least 65% in equity schemes can charge up to 2.10%","An FoF investing in liquid schemes, index funds or ETFs can charge up to 1.85%","The FoF's own expense layer can be up to 3 times the weighted TER of the underlying schemes","An FoF investing in other schemes can charge up to 2.10%"],"a":0,"e":"Only the first is true: ≥65% in equity schemes = 2.10%. FoFs into liquid/index/ETFs are capped at 0.90% (not 1.85%), other FoFs at 1.85% (not 2.10%), and the FoF's own layer at 2× (not 3×) the underlying's weighted TER."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Hybrid risk","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Conservative hybrid funds are less risky than multi-asset funds.\nII. Balanced hybrid funds are less risky than equity savings funds.\nIII. Investors should be cautious with dynamic asset allocation funds.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: conservative sits well below multi-asset in the hybrid hierarchy. II is false: hybrid risk rises arbitrage, conservative, equity savings, dynamic asset allocation, multi-asset, balanced, aggressive, so balanced is riskier than equity savings. III is true: the workbook advises caution with dynamic asset allocation funds."},{"t":"Ch 8 · GST","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: GST on the management fee is charged over and above the TER.\nStatement 2: GST on distributor commission can be charged to the scheme.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: GST on management fee is over and above TER. Statement 2 is false: GST on distributor commission can't be charged to the scheme."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Asset classes","q":"Real estate has which of these drawbacks as an investment?","o":["Highly divisible","Illiquid and not divisible","No capital appreciation","Very low transaction costs"],"a":1,"e":"Real estate is illiquid, not divisible and has high transaction and maintenance costs.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 9 · SIF","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: An SIF must have the same frequency for subscriptions and redemptions.\nStatement 2: The SIF minimum investment of Rs 10 lakh is computed at the PAN level, across SIF strategies.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: an SIF may have different subscription and redemption frequencies. Statement 2 is true: the Rs 10 lakh minimum is at PAN level, across SIF strategies only."},{"t":"Ch 7 · Valuation and NAV","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. The debt and equity parts of a convertible are valued separately.\nII. NAV falls when scheme expenses increase.\nIII. A valuation method for a new type of security can be fixed after the scheme has invested in it.","o":["Only I","Only III","Only I and II","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: convertibles are valued in separate debt and equity parts. II is true: NAV falls with more expenses. III is false: the valuation method for a new security type must be set first, before investing."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Equity benchmarks","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. ELSS, flexi cap and dividend yield schemes are benchmarked to NIFTY 500 / BSE 500.\nII. Small cap schemes are benchmarked to NIFTY Smallcap 100.\nIII. Mid cap schemes are benchmarked to a Midcap 150 index.","o":["Only I","Only III","Only II","Only I and II"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: ELSS, flexi cap and dividend yield use NIFTY 500 / BSE 500. II is false: small cap uses NIFTY Smallcap 250 / BSE 250 SmallCap. III is true: mid cap uses Midcap 150."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Growth and IDCW","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Under the growth option, the NAV falls each time a dividend is declared.\nStatement 2: The cum-dividend NAV is the NAV between the dividend announcement and the payout.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: the growth option declares no dividend, and its NAV captures all gains. Statement 2 is true: cum-dividend NAV applies between announcement and payout."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Compounding","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Rs 30,000 invested at 6% grows to about Rs 53,725 in 10 years","Rs 10,000 invested at 8% grows to about Rs 21,589 in 5 years","Rs 30,000 invested at 6% grows to about Rs 31,800 in 10 years","Rs 10,000 invested at 8% grows to about Rs 14,693 in 10 years"],"a":0,"e":"Rs 30,000 at 6% becomes about Rs 53,725 in 10 years (Rs 31,800 is the 1-year value). Rs 10,000 at 8% becomes about Rs 14,693 in 5 years and Rs 21,589 in 10 years, so those two options swap the periods."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Asset classification","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements about the workbook's classification of investment products (Table 1.1):\nStatement 1: Endowment and money-back policies are classified under Others.\nStatement 2: POMIS and SCSS are classified under Fixed Income.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: endowment and money-back policies are Fixed Income, along with PPF, SSY, SCSS and POMIS. Statement 2 is true."}],"ver":2},"mock3":{"id":"mock3","label":"Mock 3 · Full exam","short":"Mock 3","kind":"mock","weight":0,"qs":[{"t":"Ch 5 · KIM","q":"The KIM's risk profile gives a snapshot of:","o":["Fund manager's salary","The AMC's profits","The distributor's rating","Risk to principal, suitable horizon and type of securities"],"a":3,"e":"It covers risk to principal, investment horizon and securities type.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 9 · Payments","q":"UPI links the app to the bank account through a:","o":["PAN","Virtual Payment Address (VPA)","ARN","CAN"],"a":1,"e":"A VPA is created and linked to the account.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 1 · Liquidity risk","q":"PPF is an example of an investment with:","o":["Daily redemption","No lock-in","Full liquidity","A lock-in with partial liquidity"],"a":3,"e":"PPF has a long lock-in with only partial withdrawals allowed.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 5 · Disclosures","q":"Which item is included in the scheme dashboard?","o":["Investor PANs","Month-end AUM and TER","Fund manager's salary","Distributor names"],"a":1,"e":"Dashboard includes AUM, TER, portfolio, performance, loads, benchmark, fund manager and more.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 11 · Standard benchmarks","q":"The standard additional benchmark for a Retirement Fund is:","o":["10-year GoI security","1-year T-bill","Sensex / Nifty","Gold"],"a":2,"e":"Retirement and children's funds: Sensex/Nifty.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 8 · Stamp duty","q":"Stamp duty on transfer of MF units between demat accounts is:","o":["0.001%","0.015%","0.005%","Nil"],"a":1,"e":"Transfers attract 0.015%.","c":8},{"t":"Ch 12 · Debt selection","q":"Ultra short-term debt funds play which role compared with liquid funds?","o":["Equity role","Tax-saving role","The same role, with slightly longer tenor","Entirely different"],"a":2,"e":"Low volatility and high liquidity.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 12 · Debt selection","q":"An FMP generally offers returns:","o":["Guaranteed by SEBI","More predictable than a conventional debt fund and generally superior to FDs","Linked to equity","Lower than savings account"],"a":1,"e":"Portfolio matches maturity; not guaranteed.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 1 · Asset classes","q":"Investing in international assets adds which additional risk?","o":["Currency risk","No additional risk","Liquidity risk","Credit risk only"],"a":0,"e":"Returns on international assets are also affected by exchange rate movements.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 3 · AMC functions","q":"The Compliance Officer of an AMC reports directly to:","o":["The sales head","The RTA","The head of the AMC","The fund manager"],"a":2,"e":"To ensure independence, the Compliance Officer reports directly to the head of the AMC.","c":3},{"t":"Ch 4 · Advertisement code","q":"A vernacular language advertisement must carry the standard warning in:","o":["The vernacular language","Hindi","Any language","English only"],"a":0,"e":"Vernacular ads carry the standard warning in that vernacular language.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 10 · Styles","q":"Value investing involves buying stocks that are:","o":["Priced above intrinsic value","Priced below intrinsic value","Recent IPOs only","The highest priced"],"a":1,"e":"Value investors expect prices to rise when the market recognises intrinsic value.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 12 · Strategy","q":"Open-ended schemes face which operational risk?","o":["Listing risk","No liquidity","Maturity risk","Large fluctuations in net assets from heavy sales or redemptions"],"a":3,"e":"Flows can pressure the fund manager.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 9 · Payments","q":"An OTM with frequency \"As and when presented\" allows:","o":["Only one debit ever","Unlimited debits without limit","Only SIP debits","Multiple debits within the specified daily maximum"],"a":3,"e":"Any number of debits within the daily cap.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 12 · Strategy","q":"In an actively managed diversified fund, the fund manager adds value by:","o":["Holding only one sector","Avoiding equities","Raising exposure to better-performing sectors","Tracking an index exactly"],"a":2,"e":"Active sector allocation within diversification.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 10 · Debt","q":"Yield to maturity is the return an investor earns if the bond is:","o":["Sold after one year","Held till maturity","Called early","Traded daily"],"a":1,"e":"YTM assumes holding to maturity.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 10 · Analysis","q":"Dividend per share Rs 4, market price Rs 200. Dividend yield is:","o":["50%","2%","5%","4%"],"a":1,"e":"4 ÷ 200 = 2%.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 9 · Payments","q":"Which transfer is instantaneous?","o":["Cheque","RTGS","NEFT","SWIFT"],"a":1,"e":"RTGS is instantaneous; NEFT is batched; SWIFT may be delayed.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 9 · Transactions","q":"SEBI mandated which safeguard for subscription and redemption transactions?","o":["Two-factor authentication","Video KYC for each trade","Notarisation","Physical signature only"],"a":0,"e":"Two-factor authentication is mandatory.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 9 · Payments","q":"Bank charges on an outstation demand draft are borne by:","o":["The investor always","SEBI","The AMC within specified limits","The distributor"],"a":2,"e":"AMC bears outstation DD charges within limits.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 12 · Strategy","q":"When investing in international equity, an Indian investor takes exposure to:","o":["Foreign equity and the rupee exchange rate","Indian equity","Only currency","Only foreign equity"],"a":0,"e":"Two exposures.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 9 · Transactions","q":"Can subscription and redemption frequencies differ within a scheme?","o":["Only for ELSS","No, except for SIF strategies","Only for liquid funds","Yes, for all schemes"],"a":1,"e":"They must be the same for all MF schemes except SIFs.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 11 · Two-tier","q":"SEBI's two-tier benchmarking circular came into force from:","o":["1 October 2020","1 February 2018","1 April 2023","1 January 2022"],"a":3,"e":"Circular of October 27, 2021, effective January 1, 2022.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 5 · Disclosures","q":"An investor holds 2,500 units and the NAV is Rs 42. The current value is:","o":["Rs 1,00,000","Rs 25,420","Rs 10,500","Rs 1,05,000"],"a":3,"e":"Current value = units × NAV = 2,500 × 42 = Rs 1,05,000.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 4 · Advertisement code","q":"If the same fund manager has not managed the scheme for the whole period shown, the ad must:","o":["Show only the current manager's period","Get SEBI approval","Disclose it in a footnote","Omit that period"],"a":2,"e":"The change of fund manager must be disclosed in a footnote.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 2 · Limitations","q":"A mutual fund is a \"pass-through vehicle\". This means:","o":["The AMC keeps the profits","Risks and returns pass to investors","Returns are guaranteed","Taxes are paid by the AMC"],"a":1,"e":"MFs pass on both risk and return to unitholders.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 11 · Other benchmarks","q":"Per Table 11.2, which NSE index benchmarks an Arbitrage Fund?","o":["NIFTY 50 Arbitrage","NIFTY Liquid Index A-I","NIFTY 50 Hybrid Composite Debt 50:50 Index","NIFTY 500"],"a":0,"e":"Arbitrage funds use the NIFTY 50 Arbitrage index.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 7 · Expenses","q":"Rating fees can be charged as a recurring expense in case of:","o":["ELSS","Equity funds","Capital protection oriented schemes","Index funds"],"a":2,"e":"Rating fees are allowed for capital-oriented (protection) schemes.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 12 · Strategy","q":"The key difference between open-ended and close-ended funds is usually:","o":["The fund manager","The investment objective","The exit option / liquidity","The asset class"],"a":2,"e":"It is an operational feature: exit.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 1 · Liquidity risk","q":"A fixed income investment pays its promised return only if:","o":["It is unrated","Held to maturity","Interest rates rise","Sold in the secondary market"],"a":1,"e":"Selling before maturity exposes the investor to market price changes.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 5 · Updates","q":"When fundamental attributes change, the SID is revised:","o":["Before sending notice","Never","Immediately after completion of the exit option period","After one year"],"a":2,"e":"An addendum is issued immediately; the SID is revised after the exit window.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 9 · Third party","q":"A third-party payment is one made from a bank account:","o":["Of the AMC","Other than that of the first holder of the folio","Of any joint holder in the folio","In the first holder's name"],"a":1,"e":"Payment from a joint account is not third-party if the first holder is one of the account holders.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 4 · Advertisement code","q":"The additional benchmark for Index funds, ETFs and FoFs is:","o":["An appropriate benchmark based on underlying asset allocation","None","Always the 10-year GoI","Always Sensex"],"a":0,"e":"They use the benchmark matching their underlying asset allocation.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 4 · Advertisement code","q":"Logs of employee use of blogs and chat forums at intermediaries must be:","o":["Treated as records and maintained","Ignored","Deleted daily","Sent to SEBI monthly"],"a":0,"e":"Such logs are records to be maintained per the applicable regulations.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 9 · Payments","q":"For NRI investment via cheque from an NRE account, which document certifies the source of funds?","o":["Passport","PAN","Visa","FIRC or account debit certificate"],"a":3,"e":"Foreign Inward Remittance Certificate or bank debit certificate.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 1 · Asset classes","q":"Gold is often seen as:","o":["A lending asset","Risk-free","A safe haven asset","A source of regular income"],"a":2,"e":"Gold is viewed as a safe haven, though it earns no current income.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 4 · Advertisement code","q":"\"Since inception\" performance is computed from:","o":["The SEBI approval date","1 April of the launch year","The date of allotment of units","The NFO opening date"],"a":2,"e":"SEBI clarified since-inception performance is from the allotment date.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 11 · Standard benchmarks","q":"The standard additional benchmark for Equity Savings funds is:","o":["10-year GoI security","Nifty Midcap","Sensex","1-year T-bill"],"a":0,"e":"Equity savings and conservative hybrid: 10-year GoI.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 9 · Payments","q":"AEPS authorises transactions using:","o":["Signature","OTP","Credit card PIN","Aadhaar number and fingerprint"],"a":3,"e":"Aadhaar Enabled Payment Service uses biometric authentication.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 5 · Updates","q":"Who receives addenda so they can attach them to KIMs and SIDs in stock?","o":["Only trustees","Only SEBI","Only custodians","Distributors, brokers and ISCs"],"a":3,"e":"Addenda are circulated to distributors, brokers and ISCs.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 12 · Strategy","q":"Investors should choose close-ended funds only if:","o":["They need daily liquidity","They want guaranteed returns","They are short-term traders","They can keep the money locked in till maturity"],"a":3,"e":"Exit before maturity may not be viable.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 7 · Expenses","q":"A FoF's own TER over the underlying weighted TER cannot exceed:","o":["Three times","Two times the underlying weighted average TER","The same as the underlying","0.25%"],"a":1,"e":"It cannot exceed two times the weighted average TER of the underlying schemes, within overall ceilings.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 7 · Expenses","q":"For equity-oriented open-ended schemes, the TER slab for the next Rs 250 crore after the first Rs 500 crore is:","o":["1.60%","2.10%","1.90%","1.65%"],"a":2,"e":"Next Rs 250 cr: 1.90% (equity) and 1.65% (others).","c":7},{"t":"Ch 10 · Styles","q":"A share priced at Rs 5 can still be overvalued if:","o":["It is a small cap","Its earnings do not justify the price","It is listed on NSE","It pays dividends"],"a":1,"e":"Valuation is relative to earnings or book value, not absolute price.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 9 · Transactions","q":"If a redemption would reduce holdings below the scheme's minimum balance:","o":["All units may be redeemed and the folio closed","SEBI is informed","The investor must top up","Redemption is rejected"],"a":0,"e":"The MF may redeem all units and close the folio.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 8 · Dividends","q":"Under the old DDT regime, the drop in NAV on dividend payout equalled:","o":["Dividend paid plus dividend distribution tax","DDT only","Dividend paid only","Nothing"],"a":0,"e":"NAV fell by dividend plus DDT; investors got dividend net of DDT.","c":8},{"t":"Ch 12 · Strategy","q":"Growth-style stocks in a market downturn tend to:","o":["Fall less","Rise","Fall much more","Stay flat"],"a":2,"e":"Making them riskier.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 7 · Expenses","q":"Storage and handling expenses of gold can be charged in:","o":["Arbitrage funds","Gold ETFs","Liquid funds","Equity funds"],"a":1,"e":"Gold (and silver) ETF schemes may charge storage and handling costs.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 10 · Styles","q":"The top-down approach mainly helps avoid:","o":["Large exposure to a poor sector","High expenses","Taxes","Picking a single bad stock"],"a":0,"e":"Top-down starts with the economy and sectors.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 2 · Structure","q":"A downside of ETFs mentioned in the workbook is:","o":["Prices may fluctuate and deviate from NAV","Lock-in of 3 years","Very high minimum investment","No liquidity"],"a":0,"e":"Intraday prices can swing and gap from NAV, so investors must watch the price.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 5 · Disclosures","q":"A scheme performance dashboard on the AMC website allows investors to:","o":["Buy units","Compare schemes of the mutual fund easily","Change distributors","File complaints"],"a":1,"e":"The dashboard enables easy comparison across the fund's schemes.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 1 · Asset classes","q":"Which financial product is classified under Commodities in Table 1.1?","o":["PPF","REITs","Sovereign bonds of India","Gold ETF"],"a":3,"e":"Gold funds and commodity ETFs sit under Commodities as financial assets.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 4 · Advertisement code","q":"The standard additional benchmark for equity schemes in ads is:","o":["1-year T-bill","Gold","10-year GoI security","Sensex or Nifty"],"a":3,"e":"Equity schemes: Sensex/Nifty.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 5 · KIM","q":"After updating, the KIM is filed with SEBI:","o":["By courier","Forthwith, through electronic mode only","Annually in print","Not at all"],"a":1,"e":"KIM is filed with SEBI forthwith electronically.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 8 · Dividends","q":"From 1 April 2021, when declaring IDCW, mutual funds must show:","o":["How much is income distribution and how much is capital distribution","The fund manager's name","Only the record date","Only the dividend rate"],"a":0,"e":"Distinction between NAV appreciation and capital distribution is required.","c":8},{"t":"Ch 4 · Advertisement code","q":"Performance of other schemes managed by the same fund manager is shown as CAGR for:","o":["1, 3 and 5 years","10 years","Since inception only","1 year only"],"a":0,"e":"Other schemes of the fund manager: CAGR for 1, 3 and 5 years with benchmarks.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 6 · Empanelment","q":"Why might a small agent prefer working under an established distributor?","o":["To avoid NISM exam","It is mandatory","AMCs may link commission levels to volumes","To avoid KYD"],"a":2,"e":"Commission tiers linked to volume can make working under a larger distributor beneficial.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 9 · Transactions","q":"Pool accounts of stock brokers and clearing members for MF transactions are:","o":["Discontinued","Allowed only for ETFs","Mandatory","Optional"],"a":0,"e":"Units and funds now move directly to/from investor accounts.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 1 · Credit risk","q":"The possible outcomes for a lender regarding repayment are:","o":["Only default","Pay on time, pay late, or default","Bonus payment","Only on-time payment"],"a":1,"e":"A borrower may pay on time, delay, or default.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 3 · Custodian","q":"Custodians must register with SEBI under the:","o":["SEBI (Custodian) Regulations, 1996","SEBI (RTA) Regulations, 1993","Companies Act","SEBI (MF) Regulations only"],"a":0,"e":"All custodians register under the SEBI (Custodian) Regulations, 1996.","c":3},{"t":"Ch 5 · Disclosures","q":"The NAV of an open-ended scheme is calculated:","o":["Weekly","Monthly","Only on redemption","For all business days"],"a":3,"e":"Open-ended NAVs are calculated for all business days.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 6 · Empanelment","q":"Which undertaking is typically part of the AMC empanelment declaration?","o":["To buy units of the AMC","To guarantee returns","Not to issue advertisements other than those provided or pre-approved by the AMC","To offer rebates to investors"],"a":2,"e":"Distributors undertake to use AMC-provided or approved material only.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 2 · Active vs passive","q":"A passive fund tracking the Sensex will:","o":["Pick the best 10 Sensex stocks","Hold any large caps","Try to beat the Sensex","Hold Sensex stocks in the index weights"],"a":3,"e":"Passive funds replicate the index composition and weights.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 6 · Prerequisites","q":"After completing KYD, existing ARN holders must send the acknowledgement to:","o":["NISM","All AMCs they are empanelled with","Their investors","SEBI"],"a":1,"e":"A copy of the KYD acknowledgement goes to all empanelled AMCs.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 8 · ELSS","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["Some old retirement funds also give the 80C benefit, with a 3-year lock-in.","The 3-year ELSS lock-in runs separately from each SIP instalment.","The ELSS tax benefit goes to the first holder.","Under the new tax regime, ELSS is just an equity fund with a lock-in."],"a":0,"e":"Old retirement funds that give 80C carry a 5-year lock-in; the other three statements are true."},{"t":"Ch 6 · Prerequisites","q":"For non-individual distributors, KYD biometrics are done on:","o":["The CEO only","No one","All employees","Specified authorised persons"],"a":3,"e":"Biometrics are conducted on specified authorised persons.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 12 · Strategy","q":"Value funds tend to:","o":["Hold only growth stocks","Outperform in falling markets and benefit over longer periods","Outperform in bull markets","Have high turnover"],"a":1,"e":"Value orientation helps in falling markets.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 5 · Commodity risk score","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The risk score for a commodity is based on its annualised volatility.\nII. The commodity risk score is reviewed monthly.\nIII. A commodity with annualised volatility of 12% gets a risk score of 4.","o":["Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III","Only I and III"],"a":3,"e":"I is true. II is false: it is reviewed quarterly, using 15 years of prices. III is true: volatility of 10 to 15% scores 4."},{"t":"Ch 7 · Expenses","q":"On the Rs 40,000 crore slab, TER reduces by:","o":["0.25% flat","0.10% for every Rs 10,000 crore","Nothing","0.05% for every Rs 5,000 crore increase"],"a":3,"e":"A reduction of 0.05% for every Rs 5,000 crore of daily net assets or part thereof.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 10 · Return methods","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: An assured-return scheme must name a guarantor in its SID, and no assured-return product has been launched so far.\nStatement 2: When computing returns with dividends, SEBI's method reinvests the dividend at the cum-dividend NAV.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: assured-return schemes need a named guarantor, and only capital protection-oriented funds have been launched. Statement 2 is false: the reinvestment method reinvests at the ex-dividend NAV."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Cash and OTM","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Cash investments, subject to conditions, are allowed for resident individuals, sole proprietors and minors.\nII. Redemption proceeds of cash investments can be paid out in cash.\nIII. A One-Time Mandate is registered at folio level and takes 3 to 4 weeks to register.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: cash is allowed only for KYC-compliant resident individuals, sole proprietors and minors. II is false: payouts are made only via bank. III is true: OTM is at folio level, runs on NACH and takes 3 to 4 weeks."},{"t":"Ch 7 · Expenses","q":"For an open-ended debt scheme with Rs 500 crore daily net assets, the maximum TER is:","o":["1.85%","2.00%","2.10%","0.90%"],"a":0,"e":"Non-equity schemes: 1.85% on the first Rs 500 crore.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 6 · Prerequisites","q":"Documents for KYD are submitted at:","o":["CAMS Points of Service","Any bank","SEBI offices","AMFI head office"],"a":0,"e":"KYD documents and biometrics are done at CAMS PoS.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 9 · Payment modes","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. NACH, run by NPCI, has replaced ECS.\nII. AEPS uses Aadhaar along with the investor's fingerprint.\nIII. Payment through USSD *99# requires an internet connection.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: NACH by NPCI replaced ECS. II is true: AEPS uses Aadhaar plus fingerprint. III is false: USSD *99# works without internet."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Other benchmarks","q":"Real estate indices in India are described as:","o":["Having shorter histories and less acceptance","Mandatory for MFs","Maintained by SEBI","Widely accepted with long histories"],"a":0,"e":"They are yet to earn wide acceptance.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 1 · Inflation and DIY","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The hidden costs of do-it-yourself investing are time and mistakes.\nII. A 2% annual fee on a Rs 10 lakh portfolio costs about Rs 20,000 a year.\nIII. If prices rise 8% a year, an amount that buys 100 units today will buy only about 46 units after 5 years.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: DIY costs time and mistakes. II is true: 2% of Rs 10 lakh is about Rs 20,000 a year. III is false: at 8% it buys about 68 units after 5 years and about 46 after 10 years."},{"t":"Ch 2 · Structure","q":"After the NFO of a close-ended scheme, an investor who wants to buy units must:","o":["Apply to the AMC","Apply to SEBI","Wait for the next NFO","Buy from another investor on the stock exchange"],"a":3,"e":"Post-NFO, units are bought and sold on the exchange between investors.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 10 · Gold and accrual","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. A weaker rupee lowers the returns of a gold fund.\nII. Liquid, ultra-short and floater funds focus on accrual.\nIII. There are currently no mutual fund real estate schemes.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":3,"e":"I is false: gold returns = global price + rupee, and a weaker rupee means a higher return. II is true: these funds focus on accrual. III is true: there are no MF real estate schemes currently; REITs are gaining popularity."},{"t":"Ch 2 · Structure","q":"NFO stands for:","o":["New Folio Opening","Net Fund Obligation","National Fund Order","New Fund Offer"],"a":3,"e":"NFO is the launch of a scheme to the public for the first time.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 2 · Passive funds","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. MF Lite, under Chapter X of the MF Regulations 2026, is a lighter framework for passive schemes only.\nII. Index funds and ETFs must invest at least 95% in the securities of the underlying index.\nIII. A fund of funds must invest at least 80% in its underlying fund.","o":["Only I and II","Only I","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: MF Lite (Chapter X) covers passive schemes only. II is true: index funds/ETFs need 95% in index securities. III is false: a FoF also needs 95% in its underlying fund."},{"t":"Ch 3 · Custodian","q":"An independent custodian is an important control because it ensures:","o":["Securities are actually held in the scheme for investors","Faster NAV calculation","Higher returns","Lower expense ratios"],"a":0,"e":"Independent custody confirms the securities are really held for investors.","c":3},{"t":"Ch 4 · Investor entitlements","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["If no percentage is given among nominees, the entire amount goes to the first nominee","An investor can inspect the Trust Deed and the Investment Management Agreement","An investor can name up to 10 nominees","A prospective investor does not have the rights available to unitholders"],"a":0,"e":"A is not correct: when no percentages are given, nominees share equally. B, C and D are correct: these documents can be inspected, up to 10 nominees are allowed, and a prospective investor has no unitholder rights."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Debt selection","q":"Short duration funds are described as suitable for:","o":["The core portfolio of an investor with low risk-taking ability","Speculation","Only corporates","High-risk investors only"],"a":0,"e":"Low volatility and liquidity.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 4 · Fund manager disclosure","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A performance advertisement must also show the performance of other schemes managed by the same fund manager, as CAGR for 1, 3 and 5 years.\nStatement 2: If the fund manager manages more than 6 schemes, the ad shows the count plus the top 3 and bottom 3 schemes.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: the ad shows the same fund manager's schemes (CAGR for 1, 3, 5 years), and for more than 6 schemes the count plus the top 3 and bottom 3."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Treynor","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: For risk-adjusted measures, \"calculations are arithmetic; evaluation is an art\".\nStatement 2: A scheme with a return of 8%, a T-bill return of 5% and a beta of 1.2 has a Treynor ratio of 3.6.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: the notes say calculations are arithmetic while evaluation is an art. Statement 2 is false: Treynor = (8 − 5) ÷ 1.2 = 2.5; 3.6 wrongly multiplies by beta."},{"t":"Ch 7 · TER slabs","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements about open-ended scheme TER slabs and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: For the balance AUM beyond the specified slabs, the limit is 0.95% for equity schemes and 0.70% for other schemes.\nStatement 2: On the next Rs 3,000 cr after the first Rs 2,000 cr, an equity scheme can charge up to 1.40%.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: the balance slab is 0.95% equity / 0.70% other. Statement 2 is false: the next Rs 3,000 cr slab (after Rs 500 + 250 + 1,250 cr) is 1.50% equity (1.25% other); 1.40% applies to the following Rs 5,000 cr."},{"t":"Ch 3 · AMC removal","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. An AMC can be removed by a majority of the trustees.\nII. An AMC can be removed by 75% of the unitholders.\nIII. The custodian registers under the SEBI (Custodian) Regulations, 1996.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"e":"All are true: an AMC can be removed by a majority of trustees or by 75% of unitholders, and the custodian registers under the SEBI (Custodian) Regulations, 1996."},{"t":"Ch 4 · IDCW and yield ads","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. An IDCW advertisement must show the payout in Rs per unit, the face value and the prevailing NAV.\nII. Just below the payout figure, the ad must state that the NAV will rise by the payout and statutory levy.\nIII. Overnight, liquid and money market schemes may show a simple annualised yield if data for 7, 15 and 30 days exists.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true. II is false: the ad must state that the NAV will fall by the payout and statutory levy. III is true: simple annualised yield is allowed with 7, 15 and 30-day data."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Redemption","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Units are redeemed on a last-in, first-out (LIFO) basis.","If a redemption leaves a balance below the scheme minimum, the whole folio may be redeemed.","Redemption of units held in demat form is submitted directly to the AMC.","MITRA, the platform to trace inactive and unclaimed folios, is run by AMFI."],"a":1,"e":"If the balance falls below the minimum the whole folio may be redeemed. Redemption follows FIFO, demat redemption goes through the DP, and MITRA is run by CAMS and KFintech."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Strategy","q":"An investor in an active fund bears higher cost and risk in the hope of:","o":["Guaranteed return","Lower tax","Matching the benchmark","Beating the benchmark"],"a":3,"e":"The higher return is not guaranteed.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 11 · Risk measures","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The risk-free rate used in risk-adjusted ratios is the T-bill return.\nII. A positive alpha indicates outperformance.\nIII. For an active fund, a higher tracking error means it beats its benchmark more consistently.","o":["Only I and II","Only II and III","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: the risk-free rate is the T-bill return. II is true: positive alpha means outperformance. III is false: lower tracking error means more consistent outperformance."},{"t":"Ch 12 · IDCW option","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. An IDCW payout is assured regardless of the scheme's distributable surplus.\nII. IDCW is taxed at the investor's slab rate.\nIII. IDCW suits only tax-exempt investors, while the growth option compounds pre-tax.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is false: IDCW is not assured, it needs distributable surplus. II is true: IDCW is taxed at slab. III is true: IDCW suits only tax-exempt investors; growth compounds pre-tax."},{"t":"Ch 5 · BER notice and dashboard","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Any change in the Base Expense Ratio must be communicated by email/SMS and the website at least 3 working days before it takes effect.\nStatement 2: The scheme dashboard on the AMC website shows AUM, TER, fund manager, benchmark and exit load.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: BER changes need at least 3 working days' notice, and the scheme dashboard shows AUM, TER, fund manager, benchmark, exit load, portfolio and performance."},{"t":"Ch 6 · Distributor nomination","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Investors get 30 days to object to the AMC when AUM is transferred to a deceased distributor's nominee","The deceased distributor's ARN must have been valid and not suspended on the date of death","New SIPs can be registered under a deceased distributor's ARN","Commission to a deceased distributor's nominee stops after 12 months"],"a":1,"e":"The ARN must be valid and not suspended on the date of death. Investors get 15 days to object, no new business or SIPs are allowed under the deceased's ARN, and commission continues until the investor changes the ARN or AUM becomes nil."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Look-back, turnover","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["Long-term debt funds should be evaluated over at least 3 years.","Liquid funds are evaluated over 7 days, 15 days and 1 month.","Medium to long duration funds carry less interest rate risk than long duration funds.","Short-term debt funds need a look-back at least as long as long-term debt funds."],"a":3,"e":"Short-term debt funds use a shorter look-back than long-term debt (at least 3 years), so the last statement is wrong. Long-term debt needs at least 3 years, liquid funds use 7 days, 15 days and 1 month, and medium to long duration sits just below long duration in interest rate risk."},{"t":"Ch 9 · SIP registration","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Registration of a new SIP can take 15 to 30 days.\nStatement 2: An SIP may be cancelled if the investor's account has insufficient funds.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: SIP registration takes 15 to 30 days, and an SIP may be cancelled for insufficient funds."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Gold funds","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A Gold ETF tracks the price of gold.\nII. A gold sector fund holds shares of gold mining companies.\nIII. A gold sector fund rises and falls less than the gold price because company factors smooth its returns.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and II"],"a":2,"e":"I and II are true: a Gold ETF tracks gold; a gold sector fund holds mining shares. III is false: mining shares rise and fall more than gold and move with company factors."},{"t":"Ch 7 · TER disclosure","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Schemes that charge an exit load can still add an extra 0.05% to their TER.\nII. TER is disclosed daily on the AMC and AMFI websites.\nIII. Distribution commission is paid by the AMC in the manner SEBI specifies.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and III","Only II and III"],"a":3,"e":"I is false: the old extra 0.05% for schemes with exit load is gone. II is true: TER is disclosed daily on the AMC and AMFI sites. III is true: the AMC pays distribution commission as SEBI specifies."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Bias and allocation","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: The concept of loss aversion is associated with Kahneman and Tversky.\nStatement 2: Tactical or dynamic asset allocation aims to improve risk-adjusted returns.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: loss aversion is associated with Kahneman and Tversky, and tactical/dynamic allocation aims to improve risk-adjusted return."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Floater, duration","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Duration-managed debt funds are less volatile than other debt funds.\nStatement 2: The NAV of a floater fund stays relatively steady.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: duration-managed debt funds are more volatile. Statement 2 is true: floater NAV stays steady."}],"ver":2},"mock4":{"id":"mock4","label":"Mock 4 · Full exam","short":"Mock 4","kind":"mock","weight":0,"qs":[{"t":"Ch 1 · Interest rate risk","q":"If interest rates fall, the prices of existing bonds will:","o":["Rise","Remain unchanged","Become zero","Fall"],"a":0,"e":"Bond prices move inversely to interest rates.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 11 · Two-tier","q":"Under two-tier benchmarking, all benchmarks must be:","o":["Price Return Indices","Custom AMC indices","Total Return Indices","Global indices"],"a":2,"e":"All benchmarks are TRI.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 5 · Risk-o-meter","q":"While disclosing performance against benchmark, AMCs must also show:","o":["Distributor commissions","Fund manager photographs","Investor count","Risk-o-meter of the scheme and the benchmark"],"a":3,"e":"Risk-o-meters of both scheme and benchmark are disclosed.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 11 · Measures","q":"A good measure of the risk-free rate is the:","o":["Nifty 50 return","Inflation rate","T-Bill index return","Bank savings rate"],"a":2,"e":"T-bill index is a good proxy.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 12 · Within category","q":"Rankings and ratings by research agencies are based on:","o":["Distributor feedback","Future projections","SEBI directives","Historical performance"],"a":3,"e":"May or may not be repeated.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 5 · Risk-o-meter","q":"The Risk-o-meter depicts the risk to:","o":["The benchmark","The distributor's commission","The principal invested","The AMC"],"a":2,"e":"It shows the risk to the principal invested.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 1 · Market risk","q":"Fear of war causing all stocks to fall is an example of:","o":["Company-specific risk","Credit risk","Liquidity risk","Market-wide risk"],"a":3,"e":"Market-wide events affect all securities and cannot be diversified away.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 4 · Investor rights","q":"Pending investor complaints can lead SEBI to:","o":["Cancel the AMC's registration immediately","Refuse permission to launch new schemes","Reduce the NAV","Fine the distributor"],"a":1,"e":"Pending complaints can be a ground to refuse new scheme launches.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 12 · Within category","q":"Long-term debt fund performance should be evaluated over at least:","o":["1 month","3 years","1 year","10 years"],"a":1,"e":"At least 3 years; equity at least 5.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 9 · Cut-off","q":"Same week: liquid fund purchase; funds realised at 3 pm Monday. Applicable NAV:","o":["Monday","Sunday","Wednesday","Tuesday"],"a":3,"e":"After 1:30 pm cut-off: NAV of the day before the next business day (Wednesday) = Tuesday.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 9 · Third party","q":"Which of these is NOT among the third-party payment exceptions in the workbook?","o":["A parent paying up to Rs 50,000 for a minor","An employer paying through payroll deduction","A custodian paying on behalf of an FPI","An AMC paying its distributor's commission in scheme units"],"a":3,"e":"The March 2026 workbook lists only three exceptions: parent/related person for a minor (≤Rs 50,000), employer payroll, and custodian for FPIs.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 10 · Variance and SD","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Variance measures the fluctuation of returns around their own average.\nStatement 2: A reliable standard deviation needs at least 30 observations.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 1 is true: variance is fluctuation versus its own average. Statement 2 is true: SD needs at least 30 observations."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Cut-off","q":"Liquid fund redemption through IAF before cut-off is processed at:","o":["Lower of previous calendar day NAV and same day NAV","Next day NAV","Higher of the two","Face value"],"a":0,"e":"Lower of (a) previous calendar day NAV and (b) NAV of the day of receipt.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 10 · Debt","q":"A liquid or floater fund focuses on:","o":["Equity dividends","Gold prices","Capital gains from duration calls","Accrual (interest income)"],"a":3,"e":"Short-maturity funds focus on accrual with low duration.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 12 · Within category","q":"A large fund size helps with:","o":["Better diversification and economies of scale","Flexibility in small caps","Higher TER","Higher exit loads"],"a":0,"e":"A small fund is more flexible.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 9 · Bank accounts","q":"For non-resident investors, redemption payments are made by the AMC in:","o":["Gold","Investor's local currency","US dollars","Indian rupees"],"a":3,"e":"Payment is in rupees; conversion costs are the investor's.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 6 · GST","q":"GST registration is required when a supplier's annual turnover exceeds:","o":["Rs 10 lakh","Rs 40 lakh","Rs 1 crore","Rs 20 lakh"],"a":3,"e":"The workbook cites the Rs 20 lakh threshold.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 9 · Cut-off","q":"In the workbook example, Monday is a business day and Tuesday is not. Liquid fund redemption submitted Monday before 3 pm gets which NAV?","o":["Sunday","Wednesday","Tuesday","Monday"],"a":2,"e":"NAV of the day before the next business day (Wednesday) = Tuesday.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 7 · Dividends","q":"Who decides the quantum of dividend and the record date?","o":["The RTA","The trustees","SEBI","The AMC's CEO alone"],"a":1,"e":"Trustees decide; for up to monthly frequency they may delegate to AMC officials, ratified at the next Board meeting.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 2 · Debt schemes","q":"An Overnight fund invests in securities with maturity of:","o":["91 days","1 year","1 day","7 days"],"a":2,"e":"Overnight funds invest in securities maturing in one business day.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 4 · Investor rights","q":"Trustees must obtain unitholders' consent when:","o":["75% of unitholders require it, SEBI requires it, or trustees decide to wind up a scheme","NAV falls","A new distributor is added","The fund manager changes"],"a":0,"e":"These are the three occasions listed in the workbook.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 8 · Stamp duty","q":"An investor invests Rs 1,00,000. Stamp duty at 0.005% is:","o":["Rs 50","Rs 500","Rs 5","Rs 0.50"],"a":2,"e":"1,00,000 × 0.005% = Rs 5; units are allotted on Rs 99,995.","c":8},{"t":"Ch 10 · Debt","q":"Floating rate securities tend to hold their value when rates change because:","o":["They are unrated","They are equity","Their coupon resets with market rates","They have no maturity"],"a":2,"e":"Interest adjusts with the market.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 7 · AT1 bonds","q":"If an issuer does not exercise the call on an AT1 bond, valuation assumes maturity of:","o":["10 years","The next call date","The call date","100 years from issue"],"a":3,"e":"For AT1, 100 years from issue; for AT2, contractual maturity.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 9 · KYC","q":"Micro-SIP PAN exemption applies when annual SIP investment does not exceed:","o":["Rs 25,000","Rs 2 lakh","Rs 50,000","Rs 1 lakh"],"a":2,"e":"Rs 50,000 per year (rolling 12 months or FY), a composite limit with lump sum.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 3 · AMC functions","q":"Which AMC team calculates NAV daily?","o":["Sales team","Accounts team","HR team","Fund accounting team"],"a":3,"e":"Fund accounting maintains scheme books and computes NAV; the accounts team handles the AMC's own finances.","c":3},{"t":"Ch 2 · Active vs passive","q":"Actively managed funds have higher expenses because:","o":["The fund manager has a larger role","They are listed","They pay higher taxes","They must hold cash"],"a":0,"e":"Active management needs research and fund manager discretion, costing more.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 12 · Within category","q":"A \"short-term debt fund\" taking significant long-term debt exposure may be:","o":["Tax-efficient","Safer","Taking more risk than investors expect","Guaranteed"],"a":2,"e":"Not true to label.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 5 · Risk-o-meter","q":"A commodity with annualised volatility of 8% gets which risk value?","o":["4 (Moderately High)","5 (High)","3 (Moderate)","6 (Very High)"],"a":2,"e":"Less than 10% volatility = risk value 3.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 10 · Returns","q":"An investment earned 5% in 6 months. Its annualised return is:","o":["12.5%","10%","7.5%","5%"],"a":1,"e":"5% × 12 ÷ 6 = 10%.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 12 · Hybrid selection","q":"In a hybrid fund, the equity component provides ___ and the debt component provides ___.","o":["Appreciation; stability","Nothing; everything","Liquidity; tax benefit","Stability; appreciation"],"a":0,"e":"Equity for growth, debt for stability.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 1 · Credit risk","q":"Compared with its own government's bonds, other bonds in a country generally offer:","o":["Lower interest rates","No interest","The same rate","Higher interest rates"],"a":3,"e":"Own-government bonds are safest and pay the lowest rate; all others pay more.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 5 · Risk-band","q":"An SIF's risk level is first assigned:","o":["After one year","At each redemption","By AMFI","At NFO"],"a":3,"e":"The AMC assigns the risk level at the time of NFO.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 1 · Measuring risk","q":"Which measure is used for volatility, not credit risk?","o":["Credit spread","Standard deviation","Credit rating","Default history"],"a":1,"e":"Volatility is measured by variance, SD, beta and modified duration.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 6 · Commission","q":"Trail commission is calculated as a percentage of:","o":["The scheme's total AUM","The AMC's profit","Net assets attributable to units sold by the distributor","The amount originally invested"],"a":2,"e":"It is on net assets from units the distributor sold, computed on daily balances.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 5 · Risk-o-meter","q":"According to the workbook, the Risk-o-meter should be treated as:","o":["Irrelevant","The only basis for decisions","The first step in analysis, not the only factor","A return guarantee"],"a":2,"e":"It is a first step; investors should research further or consult an MFD or RIA.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 12 · Within category","q":"Portfolio turnover ratio is calculated as:","o":["Purchases or sales (whichever is lower) ÷ average net assets","Dividend ÷ NAV","NAV ÷ units","TER × AUM"],"a":0,"e":"Value of purchase or sale of securities (whichever is lower) ÷ average net assets.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 5 · Risk-band","q":"Why do SIFs use a Risk-Band instead of the Risk-o-meter?","o":["They are unregulated","They are less risky","SEBI exempts them from risk disclosure","They are riskier than other MF schemes, so a separate scale avoids confusion"],"a":3,"e":"SIFs are riskier; a distinct Risk-Band avoids confusion.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 9 · IAF","q":"The IAF limit applies:","o":["Per folio for lifetime","Per year per AMC","Per month per investor","Per day per scheme per investor"],"a":3,"e":"Rs 50,000 or 90% of value, per day per scheme per investor.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 11 · Measures","q":"Scheme A has Sharpe 0.9, Scheme B (same category) has Sharpe 0.6. Which is better on risk-adjusted basis?","o":["Equal","B","Cannot say","A"],"a":3,"e":"Higher Sharpe is better.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 4 · Investor rights","q":"If a unitholder specifies no percentage split among multiple nominees:","o":["Equal distribution is presumed","The first nominee gets all","The AMC decides","The legal heir decides"],"a":0,"e":"Equal distribution is presumed.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 8 · Set-off","q":"A capital loss can be set off against salary income:","o":["Up to Rs 50,000","No","Only for short-term losses","Yes, fully"],"a":1,"e":"Capital losses cannot be set off against other heads of income.","c":8},{"t":"Ch 7 · Dividends","q":"Why are valuation gains excluded from distributable reserves?","o":["They are taxed","SEBI owns them","They may never be realised","They are too small"],"a":2,"e":"Unrealised gains may not turn into real gains, unlike accrued income.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 2 · Equity schemes","q":"A Large & Mid Cap fund must invest at least:","o":["65% in mid caps","80% in large caps","25% each in large, mid and small caps","35% in large caps and 35% in mid caps"],"a":3,"e":"Large & Mid Cap: minimum 35% each in large and mid caps.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 2 · Equity schemes","q":"A Flexi Cap fund must invest at least ___ in equity, with freedom across market caps.","o":["65%","80%","50%","75%"],"a":0,"e":"Flexi cap: minimum 65% equity, dynamic across large, mid and small caps.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 5 · Risk-o-meter","q":"When a scheme's risk level changes, unitholders are informed through:","o":["A newspaper ad only","A notice-cum-addendum and email or SMS","The next annual report","Nothing"],"a":1,"e":"Notice-cum-addendum plus email/SMS to unitholders.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 11 · Measures","q":"The difference between scheme return and risk-free return is called:","o":["Beta","Tracking error","Alpha","Risk premium"],"a":3,"e":"Rs − Rf = risk premium.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 12 · Debt selection","q":"Some debt funds lower portfolio quality to earn higher interest. Investors should:","o":["Be comfortable with this strategy before investing","Ignore it","Always prefer them","Report them to SEBI"],"a":0,"e":"Strategy must match investor comfort.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 4 · Investor rights","q":"The status of complaints redressed is published by the AMC in its:","o":["Annual report, website and on AMFI's website","Monthly factsheet only","SID only","Newspaper ads"],"a":0,"e":"It appears in the annual report and on the MF and AMFI websites.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 4 · Investor rights","q":"Mutual fund units can be pledged. This is usually to:","o":["Transfer ownership","Change the distributor","Save tax","Offer security to a financier"],"a":3,"e":"Pledging offers units as security to a lender.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 9 · KYC","q":"PAN-exempt investors quote which reference issued by the KRA?","o":["PEKRN","EUIN","CAN","CKYC number"],"a":0,"e":"PAN Exempt KYC Reference Number.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 9 · Time stamping","q":"Opening a time-stamping machine for repairs must be:","o":["Done by any staff","Documented and reported to the trustees","Avoided entirely","Reported to AMFI"],"a":1,"e":"Only vendors or nominated persons; documented and reported to trustees.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 1 · Behavioural biases","q":"Buying a stock because everyone in the office is buying it is:","o":["Availability heuristic","Overconfidence","Herd mentality","Confirmation bias"],"a":2,"e":"Following the crowd is herd mentality.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 4 · Investor rights","q":"The Annual Report must disclose, for each scheme:","o":["Distributor names","Future returns","Unclaimed amounts and number of such investors","Employee salaries"],"a":2,"e":"The annual report mentions unclaimed amounts and investor count per scheme.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 6 · Disclosure","q":"Consolidated data on commissions paid to large distributors is disclosed on:","o":["AMFI's website","SEBI's website","NSE's website","Distributor websites"],"a":0,"e":"AMCs submit data to AMFI, which discloses consolidated data.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 8 · Bonus stripping","q":"Under bonus stripping, a disallowed capital loss is treated as:","o":["A gift","Cost of acquisition of the bonus units","Lost forever","A deduction from salary"],"a":1,"e":"It is treated as the cost of acquisition of bonus units.","c":8},{"t":"Ch 9 · Bank accounts","q":"Before deleting the current default bank account, the investor must:","o":["Get the distributor's NOC","Close the folio","Designate another registered account as default","Inform SEBI"],"a":2,"e":"Another account must be designated default first.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 3 · Service providers","q":"Which entity generates the account statement an investor receives?","o":["Trustee","Custodian","RTA","Auditor"],"a":2,"e":"The RTA maintains investor records and generates account statements.","c":3},{"t":"Ch 4 · Investor rights","q":"In the demat form, what serves as the Statement of Account?","o":["Nothing","The demat statement from the Depository Participant","The CAS from the AMC only","The contract note"],"a":1,"e":"The DP's demat statement is treated as compliance with the SoA requirement.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 12 · Gold selection","q":"Gold sector fund share prices can fall even when gold rises because of:","o":["SEBI rules","Stamp duty","Company-specific factors like management concerns","ETF tracking error"],"a":2,"e":"Mining stocks are driven by other factors too.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 12 · Within category","q":"Frequent churning of the portfolio indicates:","o":["Lower risk","Tax savings","Higher broking costs and possibly unsteady management","Steady management"],"a":2,"e":"Portfolio turnover must be seen against the style.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 2 · Categorisation","q":"The objective of SEBI's categorisation and rationalisation circular was to:","o":["Increase the number of schemes","Bring uniformity so investors can compare similar schemes","Ban hybrid funds","Remove debt funds"],"a":1,"e":"It standardised categories across fund houses for easier comparison.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 2 · Other schemes","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A Target Maturity Fund matures on a set date, is listed and reduces reinvestment risk.\nII. An FMP guarantees its returns because its portfolio matches its maturity.\nIII. An infrastructure debt fund (IDF) invests at least 90% in infrastructure debt.","o":["Only I","Only III","Only II","Only I and II"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: a TMF matures on a set date (2-14 years), is listed and reduces reinvestment risk. II is false: FMP returns are clearer but not guaranteed. III is true: an IDF invests at least 90% in infra debt."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Bank accounts","q":"A non-individual investor can register up to how many bank accounts?","o":["Unlimited","5","10","3"],"a":2,"e":"Individuals 5; non-individuals 10.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 7 · AT1 bonds","q":"A mutual fund (all schemes) may own at most what share of AT1/AT2 bonds issued by a single issuer?","o":["5%","10%","15%","25%"],"a":1,"e":"No mutual fund shall own more than 10% of such bonds of one issuer.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 12 · Within category","q":"In a small category with few schemes, the workbook suggests staying in the:","o":["Any scheme","Bottom 3","Top 3 to 5","Top 1"],"a":2,"e":"Top few consistently.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 10 · Investor's risk view","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Liquid and ultra-short funds faced credit events in 2018 and 2019.\nII. Arbitrage funds that use paired arbitrage, alpha hedging or merger arbitrage are risk-free.\nIII. Schemes being wound up are listed under Clause 37(d) of the MF Regulations.","o":["Only I","Only III","Only I and III","Only II"],"a":3,"e":"I is true: low risk is not zero risk; these funds faced credit events in 2018 and 2019. II is false: such strategies take views, so the funds are not risk-free. III is true: schemes being wound up are listed under Clause 37(d)."},{"t":"Ch 7 · AT1 bonds","q":"Why can close-ended debt schemes not invest in perpetual bonds?","o":["They are equity","They are unrated","They must invest in instruments maturing before the scheme ends, and perpetuals are deemed 100-year","SEBI bans all bonds"],"a":2,"e":"Deemed maturity of 100 years exceeds the scheme's life.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 9 · Time-stamp machine","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Opening of a time-stamping machine must be reported to the trustees.\nStatement 2: Serial numbers on time-stamped applications restart every day.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: machines are tamper-proof and opening one is reported to trustees. Statement 2 is false: serial numbers don't restart daily."},{"t":"Ch 5 · Risk-o-meter","q":"\"Low risk\" on the Risk-o-meter is shown in:","o":["Chartreuse","Irish Green","Neon Yellow","Red"],"a":1,"e":"Low: Irish Green (#08A04B).","c":5},{"t":"Ch 6 · Commission","q":"Under Regulation 66(6) of the SEBI MF Regulations 2026, distribution commission is paid by:","o":["The trustees","SEBI","The investor","The AMC"],"a":3,"e":"The AMC pays distribution-related charges as SEBI specifies.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 8 · STT, LTCG","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. STT on redemption of equity fund units to the mutual fund is 0.001%, paid by the seller.\nII. There is no STT on debt funds.\nIII. An equity LTCG of Rs 90,000 in a year is taxed in full.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: 0.001% on redemption to the MF, paid by the seller. II is true: no STT on debt funds. III is false: equity LTCG is exempt up to Rs 1.25 lakh a year, so Rs 90,000 attracts no tax."},{"t":"Ch 7 · Expenses","q":"The TER limit for a close-ended debt scheme is:","o":["1.00%","0.80%","1.85%","0.70%"],"a":1,"e":"Close-ended/interval non-equity schemes: 0.80%.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 9 · Liquid cut-off","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. For a liquid scheme purchase where funds are received before the 1:30 pm cut-off, the NAV of the day before receipt applies.\nII. For a liquid scheme purchase where funds are received after the cut-off, the NAV of the day before the next business day applies.\nIII. For non-financial requests, only the date needs to be stamped.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":3,"e":"All are true: a liquid purchase before cut-off gets the NAV of the day before receipt, after cut-off the NAV of the day before the next business day, and non-financial requests need only the date."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Debt","q":"A 9.5% AAA debenture has 3 years left. New 3-year AAA debentures yield 8.5%. The old debenture will trade at:","o":["A premium to face value","Par","Zero","A discount"],"a":0,"e":"Its higher coupon makes it more valuable when market yields are lower.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 6 · Commission","q":"Additional expenses allowed for promoting MFs can reward distributors for mobilising funds from:","o":["Foreign investors","B-30 locations (beyond the top 30 cities)","Corporate clients only","Top 5 cities"],"a":1,"e":"Higher commission is allowed for B-30 inflows.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 7 · IDCW rules","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Trustees may delegate monthly or more frequent IDCW decisions to AMC officials, to be ratified at the next board meeting.\nII. The IDCW notice must state that the NAV will fall after the distribution.\nIII. An IDCW payout can never include any part of the investor's capital.","o":["Only I and II","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: monthly or more frequent payouts can be delegated to AMC officials and ratified at the next board meeting. II is true: the notice says NAV will fall. III is false: a payout may include capital from the equalisation reserve, and the split must be disclosed."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Within category","q":"An investor buying into a scheme is essentially buying into its:","o":["AMC's shares","Portfolio","Trustee company","Distributor's business"],"a":1,"e":"Hence evaluate the portfolio against the objective.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 9 · Rejected payments","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["Post-dated cheques are accepted for lump sum purchases.","A third-party payer must be KYC-compliant with PAN and must give a declaration.","A demand draft issued against cash of Rs 50,000 or more is rejected.","Outstation cheques are not accepted."],"a":0,"e":"Post-dated cheques are not accepted except for SIP, so the first statement is false. Third-party payers need KYC with PAN plus a declaration, DDs against cash of Rs 50,000 or more are rejected, and outstation cheques are not accepted."},{"t":"Ch 5 · Using the risk-o-meter","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: The risk-o-meter can be the sole basis for an investment decision.\nStatement 2: Mutual funds may use derivatives to hedge, but cannot take open derivative positions.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: the risk-o-meter is only the first step, not the sole basis for decisions. Statement 2 is true."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Managing risk","q":"\"Avoid investments you do not understand\" is which risk management strategy?","o":["Avoidance","Taking a position","Diversification","Hedging"],"a":0,"e":"The three strategies are avoid, take a position and diversify.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 6 · Code change","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. A distributor-initiated change of code is allowed on a merger or acquisition within the group.\nII. On a distributor-initiated code change, part of the AUM may be moved to the new ARN.\nIII. On a distributor-initiated code change, the old ARN must be surrendered.","o":["Only II","Only III","Only I and II","Only II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: merger/acquisition within the group is a permitted reason. II is false: the entire AUM must move, to a valid KYD ARN. III is true: the old ARN is surrendered."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Do's","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Investors may book gains or losses to set them off against others.\nStatement 2: Sticking to the asset allocation is one of the workbook's do's in scheme selection.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: booking gains or losses for set-off is suggested, and sticking to asset allocation is listed among the do's."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Social media","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. MFDs must display their registered name and registration number on their social media home page.\nII. In videos, the registration number need be shown only at the end of the content.\nIII. The code of conduct prohibits excessive concentration of business with brokers or with a few investors.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: name and registration number go on the social media home page. II is false: they must appear at the start of every video/content. III is true: the Fourth Schedule bars excessive concentration with brokers or few investors."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Fourth Schedule","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: The code of conduct for AMCs and trustees is in the Fifth Schedule.\nStatement 2: The code requires equitable treatment of all classes of unitholders.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: the code of conduct for AMCs and trustees is the Fourth Schedule. Statement 2 is true: it requires equitable treatment of all unitholder classes."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Behavioural biases","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: Preferring the 'known devil' over the 'unknown angel' is confirmation bias.\nStatement 2: Over-allocating to equity after a bull run is loss aversion.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":1,"e":"Both are false: the known-devil preference is familiarity bias, and over-allocating after a bull run is recency bias."},{"t":"Ch 7 · AT1 and AT2","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements about bank perpetual AT1/AT2 bonds is/are FALSE?\nI. A mutual fund cannot hold more than 10% of one issuer's such bonds.\nII. A scheme cannot invest more than 10% of its assets in such bonds, and not more than 5% in one issuer.\nIII. AT1 bonds are valued on yield to maturity.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and II","Only III"],"a":3,"e":"I is true: MF ≤10% of one issuer's bonds. II is true: scheme ≤10% of assets and ≤5% in one issuer. III is false: AT1 bonds are valued on yield to call."},{"t":"Ch 3 · Service providers","q":"A distributor can be empanelled with:","o":["More than one AMC","Only AMFI","Only one AMC","Only bank-sponsored AMCs"],"a":0,"e":"Distributors can work with multiple AMCs.","c":3},{"t":"Ch 4 · Fiduciary duty","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. An MFD acts as a fiduciary and the investor's interest is paramount.\nII. An MFD may share an indicative portfolio or yield to help the client compare schemes.\nIII. To judge suitability, an MFD asks about the client's financial status, experience and objectives.","o":["Only II","Only I and II","Only II and III","None of them"],"a":0,"e":"I is true. II is false: the MFD code bars indicative portfolios, indicative yields and assured returns. III is true: these are the suitability questions."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Two-tier","q":"A Tier-2 benchmark for a large cap fund might be:","o":["CRISIL Liquid Index","Gold","S&P BSE 100","Nifty 50"],"a":3,"e":"Tier 1 e.g. BSE 100/Nifty 100; Tier 2 bespoke e.g. Nifty 50.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 4 · Trade disclosure","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Debt trades are disclosed daily, with a 15-day lag.\nII. If an illiquid security is later recovered substantially within 2 years, the money goes to the old investors.\nIII. If not, the recovered money goes to the AMC's own account.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only II and III","Only I and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: daily disclosure with a 15-day lag. II is true: substantial recovery within 2 years goes to old investors. III is false: otherwise it goes to the Investor Education Fund."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Real estate","q":"Real estate is described as:","o":["A money market instrument","A local asset driven by local factors","A commodity","A global asset"],"a":1,"e":"Its value depends on local economy, infrastructure and interest rates.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 3 · AMC functions","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is FALSE?","o":["The Compliance Officer reports non-compliance directly to SEBI.","The Compliance Officer signs the due-diligence certificate on new scheme documents.","The fund accounting team calculates the NAV daily.","Dealers place orders, while fund managers own the scheme's performance."],"a":0,"e":"The Compliance Officer reports to the AMC head and flags non-compliance directly to the trustees, not SEBI. The other three statements are correct."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Attribute change","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: When fundamental attributes change, the written communication to unitholders must be a physical letter; email or SMS is not allowed.\nStatement 2: Unitholders get an exit at NAV with no exit load, with a window of at least 30 days.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: email or SMS is allowed for the written communication. Statement 2 is true: exit is at NAV with no load, for at least 30 days."},{"t":"Ch 5 · SIF disclosures","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["The SIF Risk-Band has 5 levels","SIF portfolios are disclosed every month, within 10 days","The SIF Risk-Band is set at NFO and reviewed monthly","An SIF's ISID must include scenario analysis"],"a":1,"e":"B is not correct: SIF portfolios are disclosed every alternate month (May, Jul, Sep, Nov, Jan, Mar), within 10 days. A, C and D are correct."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Macro readings","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["A high fiscal deficit pushes interest rates up","Inflation above the comfort level brings monetary easing","Low global interest rates pull flows into emerging markets","Oil prices move the currency"],"a":1,"e":"Inflation above the comfort level brings monetary tightening, not easing. A high fiscal deficit pushes rates up, low global rates pull flows into emerging markets, and oil prices move the currency."},{"t":"Ch 9 · NRI payments","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A repatriable NRI investment made from an NRE or FCNR account needs a FIRC.\nStatement 2: Units bought with money from an NRO account can have their payouts sent to an NRE account.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: repatriable investment comes from NRE/FCNR with FIRC. Statement 2 is false: NRO-paid investments get NRO payouts only; NRE-paid ones can go to NRO or NRE."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Factsheet","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. The factsheet is published monthly.\nII. Publishing a factsheet is mandatory for every AMC.\nIII. Advertisement rules apply to the factsheet.","o":["Only II","Only III","Only I and II","Only II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: the factsheet is monthly. II is false: the factsheet is not mandatory. III is true: advertisement rules apply to it."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Do's and don'ts","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Investors should chase the funds with the best recent returns.","Ensuring suitability is linked to the PFUTP regulations.","The selection method may change each time, as long as the reasons are noted.","Taxes and loads need not be considered when redeeming."],"a":1,"e":"Suitability is linked to PFUTP. The workbook says don't chase past performance, keep a written and consistent selection method, and watch taxes and loads on redemption."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Rebalancing","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements:\nStatement 1: A 50:50 portfolio has Rs 1,000 each in assets A and B. If A rises 20% and B falls 5%, the investor should sell Rs 125 of A to restore the 50:50 mix.\nStatement 2: Rs 50 lakh invested at 8% grows to about Rs 1.08 cr only after 20 years.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: the portfolio becomes Rs 1,200 + Rs 950 = Rs 2,150, so each half should be Rs 1,075, meaning sell Rs 125 of A. Statement 2 is false: Rs 50 lakh at 8% reaches Rs 1,07,94,625 (about Rs 1.08 cr) in 10 years, not 20."}],"ver":2},"mock5":{"id":"mock5","label":"Mock 5 · Full exam","short":"Mock 5","kind":"mock","weight":0,"qs":[{"t":"Ch 11 · Measures","q":"Treynor ratio uses which risk measure?","o":["Variance","Modified duration","Beta","Standard deviation"],"a":2,"e":"Treynor = risk premium ÷ beta.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 1 · Behavioural biases","q":"Which emotions dominate investor behaviour, per the workbook?","o":["Envy, pride and guilt","Trust and doubt","Joy, anger and sadness","Fear, greed and hope"],"a":3,"e":"Fear, greed and hope drive most investment mistakes.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 8 · ETFs, exit load","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: For Equity ETFs and Gold ETFs, LTCG applies only after a holding of 24 months.\nStatement 2: GST on exit load is deducted from the load before it is credited to the scheme.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: the workbook table shows Equity and Gold ETFs as long-term after 12 months (as printed), taxed MSR / 12.5%. Statement 2 is true: GST on exit load comes out of the load before crediting the scheme."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Codes of conduct","q":"Compared with the SEBI code (Fourth Schedule), the AMFI Code of Ethics:","o":["Replaces it","Is weaker","Applies only to trustees","Is more explicit and encourages higher standards"],"a":3,"e":"ACE supplements the Fourth Schedule with more explicit, higher standards.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 10 · Returns","q":"For holding periods of less than one year, returns are typically shown as:","o":["XIRR","Rolling returns","CAGR","Simple absolute returns"],"a":3,"e":"Absolute for under one year; CAGR above one year.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 6 · Due diligence","q":"Due diligence requires that customer risk evaluation and scheme appropriateness processes be:","o":["Outsourced to AMFI","Skipped for HNIs","Handled by the sales team","Delinked from sales and relationship management"],"a":3,"e":"These processes must be separated from sales personnel.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 8 · STT","q":"STT on non-delivery-based sale of equity fund units on an exchange is:","o":["0.001%","0.1%","Nil","0.025%"],"a":3,"e":"Non-delivery sale: 0.025%, paid by seller.","c":8},{"t":"Ch 9 · Nomination","q":"Which entity can be a nominee for MF units?","o":["A partnership firm","The Karta of an HUF","A religious or charitable trust","A private company"],"a":2,"e":"Trusts cannot be nominees except religious or charitable trusts.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 10 · Credit events","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Creating a segregated portfolio is mandatory on any downgrade","Unrated debt can be segregated on a downgrade","A credit event means a default, delay or downgrade","Enabling provisions in the SID are needed only for schemes launched before 7 Nov 2019"],"a":2,"e":"Only the credit-event definition is true. Segregation is optional, unrated debt can be segregated only on actual default, and enabling provisions are required for new schemes launched after 7 Nov 2019."},{"t":"Ch 5 · Portfolio disclosure","q":"SIF portfolios are disclosed:","o":["Half-yearly","Quarterly","Monthly","Every alternate month (May, Jul, Sep, Nov, Jan, Mar)"],"a":3,"e":"As on the last day of every alternate month, within 10 days.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 12 · Scenario","q":"A client needs Rs 3 lakh in about 2 years for a car and wants low volatility. Most suitable:","o":["Long duration gilt fund","Sector fund","Small cap fund","Short duration debt fund"],"a":3,"e":"A 1–3 year horizon with low risk suits short duration debt funds.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 12 · Scenario","q":"An investor wants his FMP-like predictability but with the option to exit through the AMC. He should consider:","o":["A sector fund","ELSS","A Target Maturity Fund following an index","A close-ended equity fund"],"a":2,"e":"TMFs offer liquidity.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 11 · Measures","q":"According to the workbook, scheme evaluation using quantitative measures is:","o":["Purely arithmetic","An art that needs expertise; calculations are arithmetic","Only for SEBI","Not useful"],"a":1,"e":"Blind belief in measures without understanding is dangerous.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 3 · AMFI","q":"Which is NOT an objective of AMFI?","o":["Represent the industry to SEBI and government","Regulate the securities market","Promote best business practices","Undertake investor awareness programmes"],"a":1,"e":"SEBI regulates the securities market; AMFI is an industry association.","c":3},{"t":"Ch 8 · GST","q":"GST on investment management and advisory fees is charged:","o":["To the distributor","Not at all","Over and above the TER limit","Within the TER limit"],"a":2,"e":"GST on management fee is in addition to TER; GST on other fees is within TER.","c":8},{"t":"Ch 5 · Factsheet","q":"The monthly factsheet is published:","o":["Because SEBI mandates it","Only for NFOs","Voluntarily as market practice","Only by bank-sponsored AMCs"],"a":2,"e":"It is voluntary; publishing it monthly is part of AMFI's best practices guidelines.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 7 · Loads","q":"An investor redeems 1,000 units at NAV Rs 30 with a 2% exit load. He receives:","o":["Rs 29,400","Rs 30,600","Rs 29,700","Rs 30,000"],"a":0,"e":"1,000 × 30 × 0.98 = Rs 29,400.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 9 · Transmission","q":"In a jointly held folio with a nominee, if the first holder dies, the units go to:","o":["The AMC","The surviving joint holder","The nominee","The legal heirs"],"a":1,"e":"Joint holders' rights take precedence over nominees.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 11 · Disclosures","q":"A product label identifies the scheme objective as one of:","o":["Tax saving only","Wealth creation, regular income, or liquidity","Growth only","Speculation"],"a":1,"e":"Also the asset class, holding period and risk-o-meter.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 9 · DTP","q":"To register a Dividend Transfer Plan, the investor must hold units in:","o":["Direct plan only","ETF","IDCW option","Growth option"],"a":2,"e":"DTP needs the IDCW option; it overrides payout or reinvestment instructions.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 7 · Segregated portfolio","q":"The NAV of a segregated portfolio is declared:","o":["Daily","Only on recovery","Annually","Monthly"],"a":0,"e":"Segregated portfolio NAV is declared daily.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 10 · Returns","q":"Investor returns can differ from scheme returns mainly because of:","o":["Loads, taxes and the timing of investor cash flows","Different NAVs for each investor","Distributor skill","Different portfolios"],"a":0,"e":"Loads and taxes pull investor returns below scheme returns.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 9 · KYC","q":"For NRI KYC, the sole identification number is:","o":["OCI number","PAN","Passport number","Aadhaar"],"a":1,"e":"PAN, with passport/PIO/OCI copy and overseas address proof.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 9 · STP","q":"Compared with doing a separate redemption and fresh purchase, an STP avoids:","o":["KYC","All taxes","Idle money and double paperwork between the two legs","Exit loads"],"a":2,"e":"STP avoids the gap and two sets of paperwork.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 12 · Options","q":"SWP from an equity scheme may attract:","o":["Only stamp duty","No tax at all","Only GST","STT and capital gains tax"],"a":3,"e":"STT (equity) and capital gains (equity and debt).","c":12},{"t":"Ch 9 · Pledge","q":"Once units are pledged, the unitholder:","o":["Must transfer units to the lender","Cannot sell or switch until the pledgee gives a written NOC","Loses ownership","Can redeem freely"],"a":1,"e":"Pledged units are frozen until release.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 1 · Behavioural biases","q":"An investor believes his stock-picking is far better than the market's. This is:","o":["Familiarity","Loss aversion","Overconfidence","Herd mentality"],"a":2,"e":"Overconfidence is believing one's judgement is much better than others'.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 2 · Life Cycle Funds","q":"In a Life Cycle Fund, as the fund moves towards maturity:","o":["Equity moves up, debt comes down","Equity moves down, debt goes up","Equity moves up, gold comes down","Equity comes down, gold moves up"],"a":1,"e":"It follows a glide path: in the workbook's 15-year example, equity falls from 65–80% to 5–20% and debt rises from 5–25% to 25–65% (workbook sample Q5).","c":2},{"t":"Ch 7 · Dividends","q":"Realised gains included in the sale price of new units are credited to the:","o":["Equalisation reserve","Exit load account","Unit capital","AMC profit"],"a":0,"e":"They go to an equalisation reserve that can be used for dividends; investors must be told dividends may include capital.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 5 · Change of control","q":"While seeking SEBI approval for change of AMC control, the MF must file:","o":["New SIDs","The draft letter/email to unitholders and the draft newspaper ad","Distributor consents","A new trust deed"],"a":1,"e":"The draft communication and ad are filed with SEBI.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 4 · MFD code","q":"MFD training for representatives should cover:","o":["Only product returns","Stock tips","Only sales targets","Fiduciary obligations, fraud prevention and responsible social media use"],"a":3,"e":"These three areas are specified in the code.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 9 · KYC","q":"For a PoA-based investment, KYC must be completed by:","o":["Only the investor","Only the PoA holder","Both the investor and the PoA holder","Neither"],"a":2,"e":"Both; the PoA holder cannot apply for KYC on behalf of the issuer.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 4 · MFD code","q":"Commissions subject to clawback must be refunded by the MFD:","o":["Only to SEBI","Only if the investor complains","Never","By set-off against future commission or by payment"],"a":3,"e":"MFDs refund clawback incentives by set-off or payment.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 4 · MFD code","q":"To assess suitability, an MFD should seek information on the client's:","o":["Political views","Religion","Financial status, investment experience and objectives","Employer's profits"],"a":2,"e":"Suitability needs financial status, experience and objectives.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 6 · Advisory","q":"\"Appropriateness\" in the advisory model means selling:","o":["The highest-commission product","Any product the client wants","Only the product best suited within the investor's risk ceiling","Only index funds"],"a":2,"e":"Appropriateness: best suited product within a defined upper ceiling of risk appetite.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 12 · Scenario","q":"An investor wants debt exposure where the fund manager actively calls interest rates. He should consider:","o":["Liquid fund","Dynamic bond fund","Overnight fund","Floater fund"],"a":1,"e":"Dynamic bond funds position portfolios on rate views.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 7 · Dividends","q":"The public notice for a dividend must state that:","o":["Units will be bonus-issued","The NAV will decline after the payout and statutory levy","Dividend is guaranteed","The NAV will rise"],"a":1,"e":"It must clearly say the NAV will fall after the payout.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 4 · MFD code","q":"MFDs must quote a valid ___ and ___ in transactions to receive commission.","o":["ARN; EUIN","PAN; Aadhaar","CIN; GSTIN","KRA; CKYC"],"a":0,"e":"A valid ARN and EUIN are needed to place Regular Plan transactions and earn commission.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 5 · Factsheet","q":"For debt funds, the factsheet typically shows:","o":["Rating profile, average maturity and duration","Dividend yield of stocks","PE ratio and beta","Sector allocation only"],"a":0,"e":"Debt fund factsheets show credit rating profile, maturity and duration.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 1 · Asset allocation","q":"Strategic asset allocation means:","o":["Changing the mix to exploit market opportunities","Investing only in equity","Maintaining a target mix based on goals, horizon and risk profile","Avoiding rebalancing"],"a":2,"e":"Strategic allocation holds a target mix; tactical allocation shifts it.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 3 · Platforms","q":"The mutual fund segment on NSE is called:","o":["BSE StAR MF","NMF-II","MF Central","MF Utilities"],"a":1,"e":"NSE: NMF-II; BSE: BSE StAR MF.","c":3},{"t":"Ch 7 · Loads","q":"Can a scheme charge a lower exit load to investors who invested larger amounts?","o":["Yes","Only with trustee approval","Only for HNIs","No; no distinction by amount of subscription"],"a":3,"e":"No distinction among unitholders based on amount.","c":7},{"t":"Ch 11 · Measures","q":"Alpha is the difference between a scheme's actual return and its:","o":["Optimal (beta-expected) return","Expense ratio","Risk-free rate","Benchmark dividend yield"],"a":0,"e":"Positive alpha suggests out-performance.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 12 · Do's and don'ts","q":"Understanding the scheme's investment objective and strategy helps the investor know:","o":["The distributor's commission","The future NAV","Tax rate","What to expect from the scheme"],"a":3,"e":"As per section 12.6.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 2 · Debt schemes","q":"Under the 2026 categorisation, which debt fund invests across durations dynamically?","o":["Floating Interest Rates Fund","Gilt Fund","Liquid Fund","Dynamic Term Fund"],"a":3,"e":"The Dynamic Term Fund (earlier Dynamic Bond) invests across duration as the fund manager sees fit.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 1 · Risk profiling","q":"An investor can afford risk and has a long horizon but gets anxious with volatility. Which risk element is low?","o":["Capacity","Willingness","Need","Ability"],"a":1,"e":"Willingness is the psychological comfort with risk.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 9 · SIP","q":"A minor's SIP registered for 10 years will run until:","o":["The full 10 years","Guardian's retirement","The date the minor attains majority","The AMC decides"],"a":2,"e":"SIPs in a minor's folio are registered only till majority.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 12 · Do's and don'ts","q":"The advertisement disclaimer \"Past performance may or may not be sustained in future\" warns against:","o":["Diversifying","Chasing past performance","Asset allocation","KYC"],"a":1,"e":"Top performers change over time.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 6 · Advisory","q":"A mutual fund distributor is exempt from RIA registration as long as:","o":["He is over 50","His AUM is under Rs 100 crore","He charges a fee","Any advice is incidental to his primary activity of distribution"],"a":3,"e":"The IA Regulations 2013 exempt MFDs giving incidental advice.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 2 · SIF","q":"Specialised Investment Funds are meant to bridge the gap between:","o":["ETFs and index funds","PMS and AIFs","Banks and NBFCs","Mutual funds and PMS"],"a":3,"e":"SIF sits between MFs and PMS in portfolio flexibility.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 7 · Loads","q":"Entry load is:","o":["Allowed for HNIs","1% maximum","Allowed for NFOs","Banned by SEBI"],"a":3,"e":"SEBI banned entry loads; sale price = NAV (plus stamp duty).","c":7},{"t":"Ch 7 · Segregation and valuation","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["Unrated debt can be segregated only on actual default","The good-faith valuation of non-traded securities is reviewed by AMFI","The NAV of a segregated portfolio is declared daily","Exit load collected is credited back to the scheme"],"a":1,"e":"The second is wrong: good-faith valuations of non-traded securities are reviewed by the trustees, and auditors report them as fair and reasonable. The rest are correct: unrated debt is segregated only on actual default, segregated NAV is declared daily, and exit load is credited back to the scheme."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Codes of conduct","q":"The AMFI Code of Ethics (ACE) applies to:","o":["Distributors","AMCs in their operations and dealings","SEBI staff","Investors"],"a":1,"e":"ACE sets standards for AMCs; AGNI covers intermediaries.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 4 · MFD code","q":"Encouraging an investor to switch schemes frequently to earn more commission is:","o":["Allowed if disclosed","Good portfolio management","Churning, which is prohibited","Required by AMCs"],"a":2,"e":"Over-transacting and churning to earn commission is a malpractice.","c":4},{"t":"Ch 6 · Change of distributor","q":"A distributor can initiate a change of distributor code in client folios when:","o":["He wants to earn more","A client complains","He converts his individual business into a partnership firm","The market falls"],"a":2,"e":"Change in name/legal status is one of four allowed reasons.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 5 · Financial results","q":"Unaudited half-yearly financial results must be:","o":["Hosted on the website within one month of the half-year, with unitholders informed by email/SMS","Advertised only in a Hindi daily","Sent only by post","Published once a year"],"a":0,"e":"Within one month of 31 March and 30 September, in SEBI's format; unitholders get a written communication (incl. email/SMS) about their availability.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 9 · Triggers","q":"Once a trigger is activated and the action completed, the trigger:","o":["Becomes an SIP","Extinguishes (one-time facility)","Resets automatically","Continues monthly"],"a":1,"e":"A trigger is a one-time facility.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 9 · KYC","q":"Individual accounts opened on or after which date must have KYC uploaded to CKYCR?","o":["1 April 2015","1 August 2016","1 January 2020","26 November 2015"],"a":1,"e":"From 1 August 2016.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 5 · Portfolio disclosure","q":"Portfolio disclosures must include which identifier for each security?","o":["ISIN","LEI","GSTIN","CIN"],"a":0,"e":"Portfolios are disclosed along with ISIN.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 11 · Measures","q":"For a consistently out-performing active fund, tracking error should be:","o":["Low","Irrelevant","Negative","High"],"a":0,"e":"Low tracking error indicates consistency.","c":11},{"t":"Ch 9 · KYC","q":"In-Person Verification (IPV) for KYC is:","o":["Only for NRIs","Mandatory","Optional","Only above Rs 50,000"],"a":1,"e":"IPV is mandatory; IPV by a scheduled commercial bank is acceptable.","c":9},{"t":"Ch 4 · Regulatory rules","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The Potential Risk Class matrix for debt schemes became effective from 1 December 2021.\nII. Inter-scheme transfers are allowed only on conditions set by SEBI.\nIII. Carry-forward transactions are freely allowed for mutual fund schemes.","o":["Only I and II","Only II","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is true: the PRC matrix applies from 1 Dec 2021. II is true: inter-scheme transfers follow SEBI's conditions. III is false: no carry-forward deals are allowed except as SEBI permits."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Risk measures","q":"A diversified market index, by definition, has a beta of:","o":["2","1","0","0.5"],"a":1,"e":"Market beta = 1.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 2 · Debt schemes","q":"A Floating Interest Rates Fund (earlier Floater fund) must invest at least what share in floating rate instruments?","o":["50%","80%","95%","65%"],"a":3,"e":"Minimum 65% in floating rate instruments (including fixed-rate instruments swapped to floating).","c":2},{"t":"Ch 12 · Do's and don'ts","q":"At the core of scheme selection should be:","o":["The investor's asset allocation","The highest recent return","The highest commission","The newest NFO"],"a":0,"e":"Asset allocation is based on situation and goals.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 1 · Asset allocation","q":"Tactical asset allocation is most suitable for:","o":["First-time investors","Seasoned investors with a large investible surplus","Retirees needing income","Minors"],"a":1,"e":"Tactical allocation needs experience and surplus money.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 4 · MFD obligations","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["Clawback commissions may be retained by the MFD","An MFD must immediately report a Representative's arrest or moral turpitude to the AMC and AMFI","The MFD tagline must be printed in a font size of at least 10","The tagline to be used is \"SEBI-registered Mutual Fund Distributor\""],"a":1,"e":"B is true: such events go to the AMC and AMFI immediately. A is false: clawback commissions are refunded to AMCs. C and D are false: the tagline is \"AMFI-registered Mutual Fund Distributor\" in a font of at least 12 in print."},{"t":"Ch 6 · Due diligence","q":"Which is a due diligence factor for distributors?","o":["Number of family members","Record of fines, penalties and legal suits","Social media followers","Distributor's age"],"a":1,"e":"Fit-and-proper checks include regulatory fines, penalties, suits and customer compensation.","c":6},{"t":"Ch 5 · Portfolio disclosure","q":"The SIF's Investment Strategy Information Document (ISID) must include:","o":["Scenario analysis of expected loss from market movements","Celebrity endorsements","A return guarantee","Distributor list"],"a":0,"e":"SIFs must include scenario analysis in the ISID.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 12 · Options","q":"Dividend income from mutual funds is taxed:","o":["At the investor's applicable slab rate","At a flat 10%","Never","At 20% flat"],"a":0,"e":"Which reduces returns for most investors.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 7 · Sale price","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Since entry load is banned, the sale price of units is the NAV (plus stamp duty).\nStatement 2: A segregated portfolio is charged a management fee, while its other expenses are charged only on recovery.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: with entry load banned, sale price = NAV (+ stamp duty). Statement 2 is false: a segregated portfolio carries no management fee; other TER is charged only pro-rata on recovery."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Scenario","q":"An investor already holds a large cap fund and wants to add mid-cap exposure without managing allocations himself. He could choose:","o":["A large & mid cap fund or a flexi/multi cap fund","A gilt fund","A liquid fund","An FMP"],"a":0,"e":"These combine segments under the fund manager.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 3 · Service providers","q":"Credit ratings are used by fund managers as:","o":["Irrelevant","An initial input before their own due diligence","A replacement for research","The final investment decision"],"a":1,"e":"Ratings are an initial input; fund managers do their own due diligence.","c":3},{"t":"Ch 1 · Behavioural biases","q":"Relying on examples that come to mind easily instead of research is:","o":["Availability heuristic","Loss aversion","Familiarity bias","Recency bias"],"a":0,"e":"The availability heuristic uses readily recalled examples as a shortcut.","c":1},{"t":"Ch 9 · Change requests","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: A change of name, address or PAN is processed through the KRA.\nStatement 2: A change of bank mandate is processed through the KRA.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true: name, address, PAN and contact changes go via the KRA. Statement 2 is false: bank, mode of holding and nomination changes go to each mutual fund."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Scenario","q":"An investor with Rs 10 lakh in a debt fund wants to move gradually into equity over 10 months. Best facility:","o":["SWP","Switch in one go","Dividend payout","STP"],"a":3,"e":"STP moves money in instalments.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 5 · Portfolio disclosure","q":"Investors with registered emails receive monthly and half-yearly portfolio statements within:","o":["10 days","15 days","30 days","5 days"],"a":0,"e":"Emailed within 10 days of month or half-year end.","c":5},{"t":"Ch 9 · Transmission","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. If the first holder of a joint folio dies, the second holder becomes the first holder.\nII. A nominee ranks above a surviving joint holder in transmission.\nIII. Transmission requires KYC, the death certificate and an indemnity.","o":["Only I","Only I and III","Only II and III","I, II and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the second holder becomes first holder. II is false: a joint holder ranks above the nominee. III is true: transmission needs KYC, death certificate and indemnity."},{"t":"Ch 6 · Due diligence and mis-selling","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Portfolio turnover of more than 2 times the industry average triggers extra due diligence of a distributor.\nII. Executing an unsuitable transaction as execution only needs a written warning acknowledged by the investor and a written \"execution only\" confirmation.\nIII. Mis-selling by distributors is covered under the SEBI (Investment Advisers) Regulations.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and II","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: turnover above 2x the industry average triggers extra due diligence. II is true: a written warning acknowledged by the investor plus a written execution-only confirmation is needed. III is false: mis-selling falls under PFUTP 2003."},{"t":"Ch 5 · SID content","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. The SID cover page shows the face value and the NFO dates.\nII. The investment objective is also shown in the Highlights section.\nIII. A close-ended scheme must declare its NAV every business day.","o":["Only I","Only II and III","Only I and III","Only I and II"],"a":3,"e":"I and II are true. III is false: a close-ended scheme declares NAV at least weekly."},{"t":"Ch 9 · Non-individual KYC","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Identification of beneficial owners (UBO) also applies to listed companies.\nII. KYC for a non-individual investor requires a board resolution and details of authorised signatories.\nIII. KYC for an NRI requires an overseas address.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only I and II","Only II and III"],"a":0,"e":"I is false: UBO identification does not apply to listed companies. II is true: non-individual KYC covers eligibility, board resolution, authorised signatories and UBO. III is true: NRI KYC needs PAN, passport/PIO/OCI and an overseas address."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Do's and don'ts","q":"Periodically comparing tax on IDCW vs growth options is part of:","o":["Ranking","Keeping an eye on taxes and loads","KYC","Chasing performance"],"a":1,"e":"Both taxes and loads reduce returns.","c":12},{"t":"Ch 1 · Asset classes","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["Physical gold does not give a regular income","Rs 30,000 invested at 6% grows to about Rs 96,214 in 20 years","Fixed income is the only asset class that gives no intermittent cash flow","The Sensex growth of about 15.4% p.a. is measured before dividends"],"a":2,"e":"Commodities, not fixed income, are the only asset class with no intermittent cash flow. The other three are correct: physical gold gives no regular income, Rs 30,000 at 6% becomes about Rs 96,214 in 20 years, and the ~15.4% p.a. Sensex growth is before dividends."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Category benchmarks","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. Multi cap schemes are benchmarked to the Nifty 500 Multicap 50:25:25 Index / BSE 500.\nII. Large & mid cap schemes are benchmarked to the NIFTY LargeMidcap 250 / BSE 250 LargeMidCap.\nIII. Money market schemes are benchmarked to the NIFTY 1D Rate Index.","o":["Only I","Only II and III","Only I and II","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: multi cap uses Nifty 500 Multicap 50:25:25 / BSE 500. II is true: large & mid cap uses LargeMidcap 250. III is false: money market schemes use a Money Market A-I index; NIFTY 1D Rate is for overnight schemes."},{"t":"Ch 4 · Client data and ARN","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: An MFD may share client data with group companies for cross-selling if it improves service.\nStatement 2: A sub-distributor must hold a valid ARN.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: client data must not be shared with group companies for cross-selling. Statement 2 is true: sub-distributors need a valid ARN."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Turnover calc","y":"stmt","q":"A scheme's purchases were Rs 300 cr and sales Rs 450 cr during the year; its average net assets were Rs 600 cr. What is its portfolio turnover?","o":["75%","50%","125%","150%"],"a":1,"e":"Turnover uses the lower of purchases or sales as the numerator: Rs 300 cr / Rs 600 cr = 50%. 75% wrongly uses sales (the higher figure), 125% adds both, and 150% divides sales by purchases."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Risk measures","q":"Mathematically, standard deviation equals:","o":["Square root of variance","Average return","Beta × variance","Variance squared"],"a":0,"e":"SD = √variance.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 12 · Strategy facts","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is NOT correct?","o":["A balanced fund's tax status depends on its equity share.","Liquid funds carry the lowest risk and compare to a savings account.","Investors should understand a scheme's objective and strategy before investing.","Small cap funds carry less risk than multi cap funds."],"a":3,"e":"Equity risk falls small, mid, multi, large & mid, large, so small cap funds are riskier than multi cap funds and the last statement is wrong. A balanced fund's tax status depends on its equity share, liquid funds are lowest-risk and compare to a savings account, and investors should understand objective and strategy."},{"t":"Ch 12 · International, closed","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: A stronger dollar helps an Indian investor in an international fund.\nStatement 2: A close-ended fund should be chosen only if the money can stay locked in.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: international fund returns include currency, so a stronger dollar helps; close-ended funds suit only money that can stay locked in."},{"t":"Ch 5 · Reports and value","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Annual report availability is advertised in English and Hindi national dailies.\nII. The current value of a holding is units multiplied by NAV.\nIII. Investors cannot ask for a printed copy of the SAI.","o":["Only I","Only III","Only II and III","None of them"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: the ad appears in English and Hindi national dailies. II is true: current value = units x NAV. III is false: investors can ask for a printed SAI."},{"t":"Ch 10 · Risk measures","q":"Which measure is preferred by professional debt fund managers for interest rate sensitivity?","o":["Dividend yield","Weighted average maturity","Beta","Modified duration"],"a":3,"e":"Modified duration is a better measure than WAM.","c":10},{"t":"Ch 10 · Segregation costs","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are TRUE?\nI. No management fee is charged on a segregated portfolio.\nII. Legal costs of a segregated portfolio beyond its TER may be charged to the main portfolio.\nIII. Historically, a 10-year holding has shown almost no negative returns.","o":["Only I","Only I and II","Only I and III","I, II and III"],"a":2,"e":"I is true: no management fee on a segregated portfolio. II is false: excess legal costs are borne by the AMC and are never charged to the main portfolio. III is true: 10-year holdings have historically shown almost no negative returns."},{"t":"Ch 8 · TDS","q":"Redemption proceeds paid to a resident individual investor attract TDS of:","o":["Nil","10%","20%","1%"],"a":0,"e":"No TDS on repurchase proceeds for residents.","c":8},{"t":"Ch 9 · KYC process","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is TRUE?","o":["An address proof in the spouse's name is not accepted for KYC.","CERSAI began accepting uploads for individual accounts on 26 Nov 2015.","A change in FATCA/CRS status must be reported within 90 days.","For KRA KYC, in-person verification (IPV) done by a bank is accepted."],"a":3,"e":"IPV is mandatory and a bank's IPV is accepted. A spouse's address proof is accepted, CERSAI was notified on 26 Nov 2015 with uploads for individuals from 1 Aug 2016, and FATCA/CRS changes are reported within 30 days."},{"t":"Ch 2 · Other categories","q":"Infrastructure Debt Funds set up as mutual funds must invest at least ___ in infrastructure debt.","o":["90%","95%","75%","65%"],"a":0,"e":"An IDF is a close-ended scheme investing minimum 90% in infrastructure debt.","c":2},{"t":"Ch 3 · Valuation and demat","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Fair valuation of non-traded debt uses CRISIL and ICRA, which are appointed by SEBI.\nStatement 2: ETF units must be held in demat form.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":3,"e":"Statement 1 is false: CRISIL and ICRA are appointed by AMFI. Statement 2 is true: ETF units must be in demat."},{"t":"Ch 1 · Asset allocation","y":"stmt","q":"Which of the following statements is/are FALSE?\nI. Strategic asset allocation follows a fixed target mix.\nII. Real return is the return before adjusting for inflation.\nIII. Rs 10,000 invested at 8% grows to about Rs 1,00,627 in 30 years.","o":["Only I","Only II","Only III","Only I and III"],"a":1,"e":"I is true: strategic allocation means a fixed target mix. II is false: real return is after inflation; nominal return is before inflation. III is true: Rs 10,000 at 8% becomes about Rs 1,00,627 in 30 years."},{"t":"Ch 12 · Debt hierarchies","y":"stmt","q":"Statement 1: Banking & PSU funds carry less credit risk than corporate bond funds.\nStatement 2: Ultra-short duration funds carry less interest rate risk than low duration funds.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Both are true: credit risk rises gilt, banking & PSU, corporate bond, credit risk fund; interest rate risk rises overnight, liquid, ultra-short, low, short, medium, medium-long, long."},{"t":"Ch 2 · SIF","y":"stmt","q":"Consider the following statements about Specialised Investment Funds (SIF):\nStatement 1: A SIF fills the gap between mutual funds and PMS, with a minimum investment of Rs 10 lakh across strategies of the same AMC.\nStatement 2: A SIF Sector Rotation strategy can invest in a maximum of 6 sectors.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":2,"e":"Statement 1 is true. Statement 2 is false: Sector Rotation invests at least 80% in equity across a maximum of 4 sectors."},{"t":"Ch 11 · Data sources","y":"stmt","q":"Read the two statements and choose the correct option.\nStatement 1: Morningstar and Value Research are data vendors for scheme performance.\nStatement 2: Liquid schemes are benchmarked to a short-term money market index such as the CRISIL Liquid Fund Index.","o":["Both statements are true","Both statements are false","Statement 1 is true and Statement 2 is false","Statement 1 is false and Statement 2 is true"],"a":0,"e":"Statement 1 is true: Morningstar and Value Research are performance data vendors. Statement 2 is true: liquid schemes use a short-term money market benchmark such as NSE's Liquid or CRISIL Liquid Fund Index."}],"ver":2}};
